Hence, through this holistic approach, we have introduced an efficient and nuanced
solution for adapting NMT models to new tasks (languages, domains, and styles) either
by employing a parameter-efficient task-switching strategy, interactively adapting their do-
main expertise, and finally, by incorporating new knowledge into these low-rank matrices
using a specifically designed method to mitigate the effects of the catastrophic forgetting
phenomenon in this scenario.
4. Experimentation and Results
4.1 Experimental Setup
This section elaborates on the experimental setup for our study on tackling the continual
learning problem in neural machine translation (NMT) using low-rank adaptation (LoRA)
techniques. We outline the datasets used, evaluation metrics, and implementation details,
providing a comprehensive view of the experimental environment.
4.1.1 Dataset Description
The datasets selected for this work are diverse in nature, encompassing multiple languages,
domains, and stylistic variances (See Table 1).
Table 1:Datasets:To control for potential domain overexposure bias and accelerate our
experimentation, we employed reduced dataset versions (approximately 100,000
sentences per dataset, 30,000 for Multi30k). Details are provided in Subsection 4.2.
