of the model-estimated probabilities. Since the hu-
man annotation is unavailable at test time, we ap-
proximate it with a set of model-generated “sup-
port hypotheses” and select the hypothesis with
the highest expected utility against this set.
We define the utility function as a similar-
ity function over hypotheses pairs and consider
both sentence-level and span-level variants. The
proposed sentence-level utility function measures
similarity based on the difference between the
severity-weighted error span counts of the two hy-
potheses. For the span-level utility function, we
adopt the official F1(·,·)metric from the WMT
QE Shared Task (Blain et al., 2023), as well as
SOFTF1(·,·), an improved utility function that we
propose to address a defect in F1(·,·).
Experimental results on the WMT24 Metrics
Shared Task show that our MBR decoding, when
instantiated with the proposed SOFTF1(·,·)utility
function and applied with sufficiently large candi-
date sets, significantly improves span-level perfor-
mance and generally matches or outperforms both
the MAP and Majority V oting baselines at the sys-
tem and sentence levels. To avoid the high com-
putational cost of MBR decoding, we further dis-
till its decisions into a model decoded via greedy
search, removing the inference-time latency bot-
tleneck.
2 Related Work
To our knowledge, our work is the first to apply
reranking techniques to MT metrics.
2.1 MT Automatic Metrics
Early MT metrics such as BLEU (Papineni et al.,
2002), METEOR (Banerjee and Lavie, 2005), and
chrF (Popovi´c, 2015) rely on surface-level match-
ing and output a single scalar score per sentence.
Neural metrics, including BertScore (Zhang et al.,
2020), BLEURT (Sellam et al., 2020), COMET
(Rei et al., 2020), and MetricX (Juraska et al.,
2024), show higher correlations with human judg-
ments (Freitag et al., 2022b; Freitag et al., 2023b;
Freitag et al., 2024) but usually still produce
sentence-level scores only, limiting their useful-
ness for applications such as post-editing.
As human evaluation has shifted from scalar
scores (e.g., Direct Assessment) to multidimen-
sional annotations such as MQM (Freitag et al.,
2021a), evaluation has moved toward finer-
grained, error-localizing assessment. This task
is known as ESD (also referred to as WQE); it
underpins downstream uses such as AI-assisted
human evaluation (Zouhar et al., 2025) and hu-
man post-editing (Sarti et al., 2025a). Approaches
to it span a spectrum: unsupervised methods
that read error signals from the internal states of
translation models (Fomicheva et al., 2020; Sarti
et al., 2025b), supervised token-classification met-
rics such as xCOMET (Guerreiro et al., 2024),
and generative methods such as GEMBA-MQM
(Kocmi and Federmann, 2023) and AutoMQM
(Fernandes et al., 2023). Among these, LLM-
based generative approaches have attracted grow-
ing attention for their flexibility—often requiring
only prompt changes—and their competitive, of-
ten state-of-the-art performance (Lu et al., 2025;
Huang et al., 2024; Xu et al., 2023; Baswani et al.,
2023; Feng et al., 2025; Freitag et al., 2024).
2.2 Evaluation Metrics for ESD
The alignment between model-predicted ESD an-
notations and the human annotation can be evalu-
ated at the system, sentence, and span levels.
Soft Pairwise Accuracy (SPA) (Thompson
et al., 2024) and Pairwise Accuracy with Tie Cal-
ibration (Acc ∗eq) (Deutsch et al., 2023) measure
ranking consistency. Specifically, SPA operates
at the system level by evaluating pairwise rank-
ings of different MT systems based on aggregated
scores, whereas Acc ∗eq functions at the sentence
level, assessing the relative ordering of individual
translation sentence pairs.
At the span level, to our knowledge, the only
existing metric for ESD is the official metric of
the WMT QE Shared Task (Blain et al., 2023),
F1(·,·), which computes character-level precision
and recall between a hypothesis and a reference
annotation. This metric can also be used as an
MBR utility function when the reference is a sup-
port hypothesis. However, as we detail in §5.2.2,
F1(·,·)has a critical defect in its handling of
empty annotations, which motivates the soft alter-
native SOFTF1(·,·)that we propose in §5.2.3.
2.3 MBR Decoding
MBR decoding has a long history in speech recog-
nition (Stolcke et al., 1997; Goel and Byrne,
2000), word alignment (Kumar and Byrne, 2002),
and statistical MT (Kumar and Byrne, 2004;
Tromble et al., 2008). Recently, several stud-
ies have revisited MBR decoding for text gener-
ation, demonstrating promising results (Wu et al.,
2025; Heineman et al., 2024; Lyu et al., 2025).
These studies suggest that MBR decoding can
help overcome some of the limitations inherent
in MAP. Furthermore, the properties (Müller and
Sennrich, 2021; Kamigaito et al., 2025) and ef-
ficiency (Cheng and Vlachos, 2023; Vamvas and
Sennrich, 2024; Deguchi et al., 2024) of MBR de-
coding remain active areas of research.
3 MAP and MBR Decoding
In this section, we describe MAP and MBR decod-
ing in the context of general conditional generative
models. Given an inputx, a conditional genera-
tive modelM(·|x)defines a probability distribu-
tion over the hypothesis space. Decoding for this
model can be viewed as consisting of two phases:
hypothesis generationanddecision. In the hypoth-
esis generation phase, a procedure such as sam-
pling or beam search is used to produceNcan-
didate hypothesesC={h 0, . . . , hN−1 }from the
modelM(· |x). In the decision phase, a deci-
sion rule assigns a scorescore h to eachh∈ Cand
returnsargmax
h∈C
scoreh as the final output.
3.1 MAP Decoding
Formally, letHdenote the entire hypothesis space.
MAP decoding selects the hypothesis with the
highest model-estimated probability fromH:
hMAP = argmax
h∈H
scoreMAPh ,
scoreMAPh =M(h|x).
Finding the global maximizer overHis typically
intractable due to the enormous size of the space.
In practice, MAP decoding is approximated by re-
stricting the search to a finite set of candidate hy-
pothesesC ⊂ H:
hMAP ≈argmax
