scoreMBRh ≈ 1|S| Xhs∈S u(hc, hs).(3)
4 Error Span Detection with MAP
Decoding
4.1 Generative ESD Task
We focus on the ESD task in the reference-free
setting: given an inputx= (s, t)consisting of
a source sentencesand its translationt, the model
outputs an error annotation (a set of error spans
with associated severities).
LetEdenote a hypothesis containing a finite
number of major and minor error spans, and let
HESD represent the space of all admissible hy-
potheses. We denote the distribution of the (un-
known) ground-truth human annotation asY ESD.
The generative ESD model,M ESD(E|x), esti-
mates the probability of a hypothesisE∈ HESD.
For notation, we useE c to denote a candidate
hypothesis,E s for a support hypothesis, andE y
for the human annotation. Similarly,C ESD and
SESD represent the sets of candidate and support
hypotheses, respectively.
4.2 MAP Decoding for ESD
Under MAP decoding, the decision score for a
hypothesisE∈ C ESD is defined as its model-
estimated probability:
scoreMAPE =M ESD(E|x).
As discussed in §1, the MAP objective implic-
itly assumes that model-estimated probabilities are
perfectly correlated with similarity to the human
annotation. However, as illustrated by the example
in Table 1 (§7.3), this assumption frequently does
not hold for ESD. This discrepancy motivates the
adoption of MBR decoding as a robust alternative.
