We specify the utility functionsu(·,·)below.
5.2 Utility Functions of MBR Decoding in
ESD
In this section, we describe three instantiations of
MBR decoding for generative ESD models. The
utility functions fall into two categories: sentence
and span level.
Notably, although SPA and Acc ∗eq (mentioned
in §2.2) are standard metrics for ESD, they cannot
serve as MBR utility functions. This is because
they measure ranking consistency over a dataset
rather than computing a similarity score for a sin-
gle pair of error annotations.
5.2.1 MBR-SCORESIM
We introduceMBR-SCORESIM, which employs
our proposed SCORESIM(·,·)as the utility func-
tion. SCORESIM(·,·)is a sentence-level utility
function that measures the similarity between two
hypotheses based on severity-weighted error span
counts.
LetN maj(·)andN min(·)denote the counts of
major and minor error spans inE, respectively.
Following common practice (Freitag et al., 2021a;
Juraska et al., 2024), we first convert the error
spans into a scalar sentence-level score using the
standard MQM scoring function:
SCORE(E) = max(wmajN maj(E) +wminN min(E), α). (6)
Here,w maj andw min are negative severity weights
(w <0). These negative values reflect the subtrac-
tive nature of the MQM protocol (Freitag et al.,
2021a), which penalizes the accumulation of error
spans rather than rewarding quality. Finally,α <0
defines a lower bound on the total penalty.
We then define the similarity betweenE c and
Es as one minus the normalized absolute differ-
ence between their scores, using|α|for normal-
ization to yield a value in[0,1]:
SCORESIM(E c, Es) = 1−|SCORE(E c)−SCORE(E s)||α| .
5.2.2 MBR-F1
For span-level utility, we adoptMBR-F1, which
employs the standard F1(·,·)metric from the
WMT QE Shared Task. This function calculates
the harmonic mean of character-level precision
and recall between two annotations (see Appendix
A.1 for details).
While F1(·,·)is widely used, we identify a crit-
ical defect in its handling of empty annotations.
Specifically, if the support hypothesisE s is empty
(i.e., contains no errors), F1(·, E s)assigns the
minimum utility (0) to any non-empty candidate
Ec, regardless of how minor the error inE c might
be (see Appendix A.1 for the derivation). This
“all-or-nothing” behavior is problematic for two
reasons. First, as an evaluation metric, it fails to
provide a soft penalty when the human annotation
is error-free, ignoring the nuance that a hypoth-
esis with a single minor error is arguably better—
and more similar to an error-free translation—than
one with multiple major errors. Second, as an
MBR utility function, it assigns minimum utility
to any non-empty candidate whenever the support
hypothesis is empty. Given the diversity of sup-
port hypotheses (Kamigaito et al., 2025) and the
growing prevalence of perfect translations (Freitag
et al., 2024), cases with empty human annotations
or empty support hypotheses are likely to be fre-
quent. We therefore regard this behavior as a sig-
nificant limitation, which motivates our soft alter-
native, SOFTF1(·,·), introduced in §5.2.3.
5.2.3 MBR-SOFTF1
We introduceMBR-SOFTF1, an instance of
MBR decoding using our proposed SOFTF1(·,·)
as the utility function. SOFTF1(·,·)provides a
continuous, “soft” evaluation of mismatch, en-
suring robustness even when comparing against
empty support hypotheses.
Formally, letLbe the number of characters in
translationt. An error annotationEconsists of
two index setsE maj, Emin ⊆ {1, . . . , L}, repre-
senting the character positions of major and minor
errors. We represent this annotation not as discrete
sets, but as a dense severity vectorv∈R L. For
each character positioni∈ {1, . . . , L}, the vec-
tor elementv (i) represents the aggregated sever-
ity:v (i) =β·I[i∈E maj] +γ·I[i∈E min],
whereβandγrepresent the penalties for major
and minor errors, respectively. The total error
severity of an annotation is theℓ 1 norm of its vec-
tor,∥v∥ 1 = PLi=1 |v(i)|.
We measure the discrepancy between a candi-
date hypothesisE c (with vectorv c) and a support
hypothesisE s (with vectorv s) using theℓ 1 dis-
tance,∥v c −v s∥1. Based on these vector repre-
sentations, we define Soft Precision (SOFTP) and
Soft Recall (SOFTR) as:
SOFTP(E c, Es) = 1−∥vc −v s∥1L+∥v c∥1 ,
SOFTR(E c, Es) = 1−∥vc −v s∥1L+∥v s∥1 .
Here,Lis included in the denominator for nor-
malization. Finally, SOFTF1(E c, Es)is com-
puted as the harmonic mean of SOFTP(E c, Es)
and SOFTR(E c, Es).
Unlike the standard F1(·,·), SOFTF1(·,·)re-
turns a continuous value reflecting the degree of
mismatch. Crucially, even if the support hypoth-
esis is empty (v s =0), a candidate with a small
error vectorv c will yield a high utility score closer
to1, rather than collapsing to0(see proof in
Appendix A.2). Furthermore, a synthetic meta-
evaluation on human annotations (Appendix A.3)
confirms that SOFTF1(·,·)faithfully tracks fine-
grained quality changes that F1(·,·)misses in ex-
actly this regime. This property enables MBR de-
coding to meaningfully rank candidates even when
the consensus among support hypotheses is that
the translation is error-free.
6 Experiments on MBR Decoding
6.1 Experimental Setup
Datasets and ModelsWe used the MQM
annotations from the WMT24 Metrics Shared
Task as our test set, which covers three translation
directions: English→German, English→Spanish,
and Japanese→Chinese. To perform zero-
shot,reference-freeESD using a GEMBA-
MQM–style prompt (Kocmi and Federmann,
2023), we prompted four popular LLMs,
i.e.,Llama-3.3-70B-Inst(Grattafiori
et al., 2024),Gemma-2-27b-it(Team
et al., 2024),Phi-4(Abdin et al., 2024), and
Mistral-Large-Inst2. We verified the
training data cutoff or release dates for all models
to ensure no overlap with the test set, thereby
mitigating potential data leakage risks (Grattafiori
et al., 2024; Team et al., 2024; Abdin et al., 2024).
Hypothesis Generation and DecisionFor hy-
pothesis generation, the model generatesN∈
2https://huggingface.co/mistralai/
Mistral-Large-Instruct-2407
{16,64,256}hypotheses for each input, with Top-
K(K= 10) sampling (Fan et al., 2018) and
a temperature of2.0. This high temperature
promotes diversity among candidate hypotheses,
which is expected to indirectly elevate utility vari-
ance and thereby reduce utility estimation error
(Kamigaito et al., 2025). For the decision phase,
we compared the three MBR decoding methods
introduced in §5.2 against two baselines: MAP
and Majority V oting. Majority V oting selects the
candidate hypothesis that occurs most frequently
in the candidate setC ESD. Following prior work
(Freitag et al., 2021a; Juraska et al., 2024; Blain
et al., 2023), we merged “critical” errors into the
“major” category and adopt their recommended
parameter values for Eq. 6:w maj =−5,w min =
−1,α=−25,β= 1andγ= 0.5. We used
guided generation (Willard and Louf, 2023) to en-
sure that each hypothesis adheres to the specified
JSON format. Furthermore, due to the presence
of empty translations (L= 0) in the WMT Met-
rics Shared Task dataset that do not conform to the
ESD task definition, we added1to the denomi-
nators of SOFTP(E c, Es)and SOFTR(E c, Es)in
practice to prevent division by zero.
EvaluationFollowing the WMT Metrics Shared
Task, we used SPA (Thompson et al., 2024) and
Acc∗eq (Deutsch et al., 2023) for system- and
sentence-level evaluation, respectively. For the
span level, we used SOFTF1(·, E y)as the metric
and reported the average scores over test instances.
We selected SOFTF1(·, E y)over F1(·, E y)be-
cause, as discussed in §5.2.3, SOFTF1(·,·)has
better mathematical properties; in particular, it
avoids the zero-credit plateau of F1(·,·)when
handling empty or zero-overlap annotations. Our
synthetic meta-evaluation (Appendix A.3) further
confirms that SOFTF1(·, E y)tracks fine-grained
changes in annotation quality that F1(·, Ey)fails
to register. For the SPA and Acc ∗eq, we em-
ployed the PERM-BOTH test (Deutsch et al.,
2021). For SOFTF1 3, since it operates at the in-
stance level, we used paired bootstrap resampling
(Koehn, 2004). We used both MAP and Majority
V oting as baselines for all statistical significance
tests; a method is marked with † only when it sig-
nificantly outperforms both baselines. For all ESD
methods, sentence-level scores were derived using
3SOFTF1 indicates the average of the return values by
SOFTF1(·, Ey)during evaluation across the entire dataset.
Similar definitions apply to other functions.
Eq. 6, system-level scores were calculated by av-
eraging all sentence-level scores within that MT
system.
6.2 Experimental Results
Drawing on the results presented in Table 2 and 4,
we compare the performance of our proposed
MBR decoding instantiations against the MAP
and Majority V oting baselines. Our key findings
are detailed below.
Performance of MBR-SCORESIMAtN≥64,
MBR-SCORESIMachieved significantly higher
Acc∗eq scores compared to both MAP and Ma-
jority V oting, but lacks consistent improvement
on SPA and SOFTF1. This indicates a trade-off:
MBR-SCORESIMis highly effective at the specific
level it optimizes (sentence-level) but struggles to
generalize to system or span-level improvements.
Nevertheless, it remains valuable for applications
that rely on sentence-level metrics, such as rerank-
ing for MT.
Performance of MBR-F1MBR-F1 performed
comparably to both MAP and Majority V oting
with only marginal fluctuations. We attribute
this stagnation to the empty-annotation defect in
F1(·,·)(§5.2.2), which hinders its effectiveness as
an optimization objective and confirms the need
for our soft alternative.
Performance of MBR-SOFTF1AtN= 256,
MBR-SOFTF1 consistently improves SOFTF1
over both MAP and Majority V oting, with
these improvements being statistically signifi-
cant in every setup; for SPA and Acc ∗eq, MBR-
SOFTF1 typically matches or outperforms both
baselines, although these gains are not always
statistically significant. For example, with
Mistral-Large-Inst, the SPA and Acc ∗eq
scores under MBR-SOFTF1 are numerically close
to those of MAP and Majority V oting, and the
differences are not significant. Furthermore, at
N= 16, MBR-SOFTF1 performed comparably to
MAP with only marginal fluctuations, highlight-
ing a key limitation: MBR-SOFTF1 effectively
trades computational cost for quality, requiring
large sets of hypotheses (N). To address this re-
source dependency, we investigate MBR distilla-
tion in §8.
ConclusionWhile MBR-SCORESIMand MBR-
F1 are constrained by optimization trade-offs or
