Finally, F1(Ec, Es) = 2PR/(P+R)if P+R>0, and F1(E c, Es) = 0otherwise. Under this convention,
the empty-empty match receives score1, while any non-empty comparison with zero true-positive credit
receives score0.
A Defect in F1(·,·)The key limitation of F1(·,·)is not restricted to empty annotations. Whenever
S(Ec) +S(Es)>0, the metric can be written as F1(E c, Es) = 2C/(S(Ec) +S(Es)), with value0
whenC= 0. Therefore, all zero-credit annotation pairs collapse to the same minimum value:C=
0andS(E c) +S(Es)>0 =⇒F1(E c, Es) = 0. This includes at least three cases:E c ̸=∅, Es =∅;
Ec =∅, Es ̸=∅; andE c ̸=∅, Es ̸=∅with no character-level credit. Thus, F1(·,·)assigns the same
score to a candidate that marks only one minor-error character and to a candidate that marks a large
disjoint span, as long as both receive zero true-positive credit.
A.2 SOFTF1(·,·): Non-empty vs. Empty Spans
IfE c ̸=∅andE s =∅, then:
SOFTP(E c, Es) = 1− ∥vc∥1L+∥v c∥1 = LL+∥v c∥1 >0,SOFTR(E c, Es) = 1−∥vc∥1L >0.
which implies SOFTF1(E c, Es =∅)>0. This behavior differs from F1(E c, Es =∅)and highlights a
desirable property of SOFTF1(E c, Es): when only one ofE c andE s is empty, it does not simply return
0or1, but a decimal value between0and1that reflects the degree of the match.
A.3 Synthetic Meta-Evaluation
The analysis above shows analytically that SOFTF1(·,·)avoids a defect of F1(·,·); we now verify em-
pirically that this yields a more faithful response to fine-grained quality changes on real annotations.
SetupOn the entire WMT24 Metrics Shared Task, we perturb each human annotationE y with two
operations.Addintroduces a spurious error: we pick a substring not covered by any error inE y and
label a randomly sized portion of it (length drawn uniformly from[1,max(8, ℓ)]for a chosen region of
lengthℓ) as a major or minor error span.Deleteremoves a randomly chosen error span fromE y and
thus applies only whenE y is non-empty. Each edit moves the annotation strictly farther fromE y, so a
faithful metric should strictly decrease. For each sample we apply up to six consecutive edits of a single
operation, each building on the previously edited annotation.
ResultsTable 9 reports the results. WhenE y is non-empty, both F1(·,·)and SOFTF1(·,·)decline
