3.2. Building an Ensemble of Dialect
Classifiers
We adopted an ensemble approach (Arango et al.,
2024) to enhance robustness and stability of the
dialect classifierC. We fine-tuned five distinct Ko-
rean pre-trained language models56789 using a set
of shared hyperparameters, with a batch size of 16,
a learning rate of 4e-5, a weight decay of 0.01, 4
training epochs, a maximum input length of 64, and
a seed of 1337 for reproducibility.
After fine-tuning each model, we searched
for the optimal ensemble scenario among the
31 combinations. While the lowest-performing
case yielded an accuracy of 92.92%, we found
that BM-K/KoSimCSE-roberta7 combined with
beomi/KcELECTRA-base9 performed best. As
showninTable2,thisensembleachievedanoverall
accuracy of 94.92% with balanced performance.
3.3. DIA-REFINE Framework for Dialect
Translation
We operate ourDIA-REFINE on the trained en-
semble dialect classifier. First, an LLM generates a
candidate translationy following the given instruc-
tion, thenC determines whether they aligns with
the intended target dialectdtgt. If it was classified
