These results point to a mismatch between the
standard word alignment evaluation that treats each
word in the input as equally important and label
projection for translation-based XLT that requires
correct alignments on a subset of the input sentence.
Commonly, labeled spans in downstream evalua-
tion span words that carry meaning (e.g., named
entities). We thus additionally report the align-
ment results by excluding stopwords—words with
little semantic meaning—from the evaluation. Re-
sults presented in Table 2 (bottom half) support our
hypothesis: not accounting for the (accuracy of)
stopword alignment, TransAlign outperforms both
baselines consistently: this means it produces more
accurate alignments between content words, which
explains why it yields downstream XLT gains.
