A.6 Standard Meta-Evaluation after Error-Density Training
In our analysis, density normalization is used only to test whether length bias arises from
the supervision signal (Sections 5.1 and 5.3); it removes the length-conditioned skew during
training, and predictions are rescaled to raw error ratings at inference.
To verify that this intervention does not harm standard evaluation quality, we evaluate the
density-trained model on standard WMT meta-evaluation at sentence and blob granularities.
Table 8 shows that it remains within 1.5 points of the unnormalized baseline on both
Soft Pairwise Accuracy (SPA) and Acc-23 across all granularities and closely matches the
baseline at the sentence level. Thus, the reduction in length bias does not come at the cost of
agreement with human judgments.
