3.2 Roman Urdu
Roman Urdu refers to the representation of Urdu
using the Latin script, commonly used in informal
digital communication such as texting, social media,
and online forums. Unlike Native Urdu, Roman
Urdu lacks standardized orthography, resulting in
substantial spelling variation and increased lexical
diversity, which pose challenges for translation and
evaluation systems.
For this work, the Roman Urdu portion of the
dataset was constructed to mirror the Native Urdu
split. Starting from the 2,000 Native Urdu id­
ioms and their accompanying sentences, we utilized
GPT­55 to produce the initial transliterated drafts.
To ensure the quality of this synthetic starting point,
every entry was subjected to a thorough manual
review by three native speakers.
These reviewers post­edited the GPT­5 transliter­
ations where each entry was corrected and refined
until it reflected natural, real­world usage patterns,
including the informal phonetic spellings typical of
digital Urdu. Entries for which consensus on nat­
uralness and accuracy could not be reached were
discarded. The final Roman Urdu dataset consists
of 2,000 high­quality idiom–sentence pairs aligned
with their Native Urdu and English counterparts, en­
abling controlled cross­script comparisons in down­
stream evaluation.
3.3 Dataset Statistics
Table 2 presents a quantitative comparison of the cu­
rated Native Urdu and Roman Urdu idiom–sentence
datasets. Each split contains 2,000 idiom–sentence
