Table 3: Summary of full prompts used in the experiments.
system role was explicitly set to “You are an expert
translator fluent in both Urdu and English.”
The Few­Shot translation prompt contains two
variations: 5­Shot and 10­Shot with 5 and 10
Urdu­English translated examples provided in the
prompts respectively.
Few­Shot Example Selection. To prevent in­
context contamination, the few­shot examples were
drawn exclusively from a held­out pool that is en­
tirely disjoint from the 4,000­instance test set used
in all evaluations. For each iteration, examples
were selected randomly without replacement from
this held­out pool. No similarity­based retrieval
was applied; examples were not chosen based on
semantic proximity to the test instance. The same
sampling procedure was applied consistently across
all models to ensure fair comparison. This design
ensures that performance gains observed in few­
shot settings reflect genuine in­context learning
from representative idiom–translation pairs rather
than any overlap with test inputs.
4.2 Models
We evaluate a mix of closed­source and open­
source LLMs and Neural Machine Translation
(NMT) models. The LLMs include Gemini­3 6,
GPT­OSS­20B (Kumar et al. , 2025), Qwen 3­8B
(Y ang et al., 2025), DeepSeek R1­Distill­Llama­8B
(Guo et al., 2025), Gemma 3­1B (Team et al., 2025),
Mistral Instruct­7B (Jiang et al., 2023), and LLama­
3.2­3B­Instruct (Grattafiori et al. , 2024). For NMT
baselines, nllb­200­3.3B, nllb­200­distilled­600M
(Team et al., 2022), and Google Translate 7.
The model temperature was set to 0.8 with a max­
imum output limit of 500 tokens. To account for
models that generated conversational fillers, reason­
ing, or explanations alongside the translation, we
employed a multi­step regular expression (regex)
pipeline to programmatically isolate and extract the
final translated output for evaluation. The extrac­
tion procedure applied the following steps in order:
(1) remove chain­of­thought reasoning blocks en­
closed in<think>...</think> tags, as produced
by reasoning­oriented models such as DeepSeek­
R1; (2) strip surrounding quotation marks ( """",'',
«»), leading and trailing whitespace, and markdown
formatting markers (e.g., **bold**).
6https://gemini.google.com/
7https://translate.google.com/
4.3 Human Evaluation
To establish a gold­standard benchmark, we con­
ducted a human evaluation of 1,000 translations
(100 samples per model) generated from the Cul­
tural Translation experiment. The dataset consisted
of 500 Native Urdu and 500 Roman Urdu sentences.
Three MSc students with native Urdu proficiency
and strong command of English volunteered for the
evaluation; who hold postgraduate qualifications
and have extensive experience in Urdu–English
bilingual contexts, making them well­suited for
assessing figurative and cultural translation qual­
ity. The annotators were blinded to system identity
using anonymous labels (System A–J) with ran­
domised sample order per annotator.
Each translation was evaluated on a 5­point Lik­
ert scale across three core dimensions:
• Semantic Accuracy: Evaluates whether the
translation captures the underlying figurative
and metaphorical meaning of the idiom rather
than providing a literal rendering.
• Fluency: Assesses the grammatical correct­
ness, naturalness, and readability of the output
within an English linguistic context.
• Cultural Equivalence: Measures how well
the translation resonates as a functional equiva­
lent in English, determining if the output feels
like a natural expression or idiom rather than
an explained concept.
The final Human Score for each sample was calcu­
lated as the arithmetic mean of these dimensions.
4.4 Automated Evaluation Metrics
We employ a combination of lexical, semantic, and
idiomatic evaluation metrics to comprehensively
assess translation quality. BLEU ( Papineni et al. ,
2002) and ChrF ( Popović, 2015) measure surface­
level n­gram overlap between the model output and
reference translations. BERTScore ( Zhang et al. ,
2020), COMET (Rei et al., 2020), and XCOMET
(Guerreiro et al. , 2024) capture semantic similarity
and cross­lingual adequacy. ROUGE ( Lin, 2004) is
used for paraphrase evaluation, while Levenshtein
distance and unigram overlap assess idiom­level
variation and lexical preservation. Additionally,
cosine similarity computed using multilingual em­
beddings (LASER, LaBSE, and USE­multilingual)
quantifies semantic fidelity across scripts.
5 Results
5.1 Human Evaluation Results
To assess the reliability of the human evaluation, we
computed inter­annotator agreement using Fleiss’
kappa ( κ). Agreement was calculated separately
for each evaluation dimension, namely Semantic
Accuracy, Fluency, and Cultural Equivalence, as
well as for the aggregated Human Score. Table 4
reports the resulting κ values.
Evaluation Dimension Fleiss’ κ
Semantic Accuracy 0.66
Fluency 0.75
Cultural Equivalence 0.71
Human Score 0.68
Table 4: Inter­annotator agreement measured using
Fleiss’ kappa across evaluation dimensions.
The inter­annotator agreement results in Table 4
demonstrate substantial consensus across all dimen­
sions. The overall Human Score achieved a Fleiss’
κ of 0.68, while Fluency reached the highest align­
ment at 0.75. Semantic Accuracy (0.66) and Cul­
tural Equivalence (0.71) also showed consistent
substantial agreement. These robust values vali­
date the human evaluation as a reliable ground truth,
confirming that the scoring rubric was applied con­
sistently across evaluators.
A representative example of the evaluation scor­
ing process is provided in Table 5, demonstrating
how a model’s literal translation is penalized for se­
mantic and cultural divergence despite maintaining
grammatical fluency. The final Human Score for
each sample was calculated as the arithmetic mean
of these three dimensions.
Table 6 reports pairwise unweighted Cohen’s κ
between each pair of annotators across all evalua­
tion dimensions. Annotators are anonymised as A,
B, and C. All pairs achieve substantial agreement
across every dimension, confirming that the rating
rubric was applied consistently regardless of which
annotator pair is considered.
5.2 Correlation Analysis
To assess the reliability of automated metrics for
Urdu idiomatic translation, we calculated Spear­
man’s rank correlation coefficient ( ρ) between the
automated scores and the averaged human ratings
across 10 models.
