6 Conclusion
We present the first systematic benchmark for
Urdu–English idiomatic translation across Native
and Roman Urdu, covering translation, paraphras­
ing, idiom span detection, and back­translation.
Central to this study is our curated dataset of 4,000
aligned idiom–sentence pairs, which provides a
high­quality foundation for cross­script compar­
isons through manual verification by native speak­
ers. Our findings indicate that large LLMs, partic­
ularly Gemini­3, GPT­OSS­20B, and Qwen 3­8B,
consistently outperform NMT systems.
We found that cultural and idiomatic prompts
yield the highest semantic fidelity by activating
broader associations that better preserve the idiom’s
intent. Few­shot prompting provides additional
gains, confirming that in­context learning further
improves idiomatic translation quality over zero­
shot settings. Cross­script analysis reveals that
while semantic metrics like COMET and XCOMET
remain relatively stable across scripts, the lack of
standardized orthography and phonetic ambigu­
ity in Roman Urdu continues to pose challenges
for model robustness, with lexical metrics such as
BLEU and ChrF showing the steepest cross­script
degradation. Furthermore, our idiom span detection
results emphasize that the accurate identification
of figurative expressions is a critical prerequisite
for high­quality downstream translation. Overall,
this study offers a unified multi­metric evaluation
framework for idiomatic Urdu–English translation,
providing insights for LLM development, prompt
engineering, and the broader challenge of figurative
language processing in low­resource settings.
Limitations
While this study provides a comprehensive bench­
mark for idiomatic translation, several limitations
should be acknowledged. The dataset focuses ex­
clusively on the Urdu–English language pair. Al­
though this enables an in­depth analysis of these
languages, the findings may not directly general­
ize to other low­resource languages or different
linguistic families. The dataset comprises 4,000
idiom–sentence pairs. Although the dataset was
carefully validated by multiple native speakers, its
partially AI­generated template remains a poten­
tial limitation, as it may introduce evaluation bias
that favors models similar to the one used dur­
ing data generation. Moreover, the sample may
not fully capture the diversity and variability of
Urdu idioms in real­world usage. The lack of stan­
dardized orthography in Roman Urdu introduces
phonetic ambiguity and spelling inconsistencies,
posing challenges for both idiom detection and au­
tomated evaluation. Human evaluation was con­
ducted exclusively under the Cultural Translation
prompting condition; extending human evaluation
to other strategies, such as Literal Translation and
Few­Shot prompting, would enable direct compar­
ison of model performance across different con­
ditions and represents an important direction for
future work. In addition, idiom span detection is
evaluated using lexical­overlap metrics, such as
ROUGE and unigram overlap, which do not ad­
equately account for spelling variation in Roman
Urdu. Future work should incorporate phonetic
normalization or fuzzy string matching to enable
more robust and fair cross­script span detection
evaluation. Finally, the current study is limited to
Urdu–English translation, and extending the bench­
mark to other low­resource languages remains an
important direction for assessing the broader appli­
cability and generalizability of the proposed evalu­
ation framework.
