A Appendix
A.1 Resources
All experiments were performed on machines
equipped with NVIDIA A100 40GB GPUs.
A.2 Roman Urdu Pitfalls
Roman Urdu often leads to lower translation qual­
ity compared to Native Urdu due to inconsistent
spelling, phonetic ambiguity, and lack of standard­
ization. For example, the Urdu idiom ﻝوھپریپھتاہ 
اناج can appear as haath pair phool jana , hath pair
phool jana, or even haath pair phool ja na , causing
models to misinterpret or fail to detect idiomatic
meaning.
Other idioms show even greater variation in Ro­
manization:
• انرکںیتابےسﻥامسﺁ (to daydream) → aasman se
baatein karna / asman sy baatein krna
• اناجﺏﻭڈںیمتیسگرن (to become self­absorbed)
→ nargisiyat mein doob jana / nargisiat me
doob jana
These variations increase lexical ambiguity and
make it harder for models to match idioms with
their English equivalents.
A.3 Comparative Analysis: Native vs. Roman
Urdu
We analyze the impact of orthographic representa­
tion on model performance by comparing Native
Urdu (Perso­Arabic) against Roman Urdu (translit­
erated Latin) across core translation tasks. Figure 4
and Figure 5 provide a holistic visualization of
the ”Script Gap” using neural evaluation metrics,
which prioritize semantic adequacy over surface­
level string matching.
The empirical results confirm that Native Urdu
yields consistently superior semantic fidelity across
all neural benchmarks. As illustrated in the radar
charts, the performance delta is most pronounced
in tasks requiring high­precision mapping, such
as Direct Translation and Idiomatic Translation.
The visual ”contraction” of the Roman Urdu series
relative to the Native baseline represents the perfor­
mance tax imposed by phonetic noise and subword
fragmentation during tokenization.
These findings reinforce the conclusion that
while modern Large Language Models (LLMs) act
as a robust semantic anchor for informal Urdu, Na­
tive Urdu remains the gold standard for achiev­
ing peak semantic adequacy in culturally dense,
non­compositional translation tasks. The narrowest
gaps observed in Literal and Cultural prompting
suggest that instruction­tuning provides a degree
of resilience against script­based degradation that
is less present in traditional machine translation
frameworks.
