B. Handling Large Instances with
SLIDE
The sliding-window adaptation for LaBSE con-
structsdocumentembeddingsby(i)splittingadocu-
mentintosentences,(ii)formingoverlapping,token-
bounded windows of whole sentences, (iii) encod-
ing each window with LaBSE, and (iv) aggregating
window embeddings into a single L2-normalized
documentvectorusingweightsthatcompensatefor
sentence overlap. This approach allows each sen-
tence to be represented in multiple local contexts
while respecting LaBSE’s input-size constraint, pro-
ducing document-level embeddings suitable for
cosine-similarity comparisons.
At a high level, the procedure is:
1. Split the document D into sentences S =
[s0, . . . , sn−1].
2. Measure token lengthsts for each sentence
with the model tokenizer.
3. Greedily pack consecutive sentences into win-
dows so each window’s concatenated tokens
do not exceed the token budgetT.
4. Slide the window with overlap (windows share
sentences), encode each window with LaBSE
to obtain embeddings ek, and record which
sentence indices each window covers.
5. Compute de-overlap weights that apportion
each sentence’s token mass across the win-
dows that include it, aggregate the weighted
window embeddings and L2-normalize to ob-
tain the final document embeddingED.
Mathematical formulation.Let ts be the token
length of sentences, letIk be the set of sentence
indices covered by windowk, and letek ∈R d be
the embedding of windowk. Define the coverage
count
cs = #{k:s∈I k},
and the per-window de-overlap weight
wk =
