4 Experiments
4.1 Evaluation Metrics
To achieve a holistic and nuanced assessment, we
employ a diverse collection of metrics that span
surface-level text similarity, semantic understand-
ing, learned human-aligned judgments, and cutting-
edge LLM-based evaluation.
Lexical and n-gram Overlap Metrics.These
metrics quantify direct textual correspondences be-
tween hypotheses and references. Our set includes
BLEU (1-4) (Papineni et al., 2002), sentBLEU
(Chen and Cherry, 2014), SacreBLEU (Post, 2018),
GLEU (Mutton et al., 2007), NIST (Doddington,
2002), LEPOR (Han et al., 2012), ChrF (Popovi´c,
2015), ChrF++ (Popovi´c, 2017), METEOR (Baner-
jee and Lavie, 2005), and a comprehensive suite of
(a) Text Summarization
 (b) Machine Translation
Figure 2: Distribution of human evaluation scores across evaluation aspects.
Figure 3: Pearson correlation network of human evalua-
tion aspects.
ROUGE variants (Lin, 2004). Specifically, we em-
ploy eight ROUGE variants—ROUGE-1, ROUGE-
2, ROUGE-3, ROUGE-4, ROUGE-L, ROUGE-W,
ROUGE-S4, and ROUGE-SU4. Each variant is
evaluated under two stopword settings (with/with-
out stopwords) and three scoring modes (precision,
recall, F1-score), resulting in 48 distinct configura-
tions.
Embedding-based Semantic Metrics.To cap-
ture semantic alignment beyond surface matches,
we utilize embedding-based approaches, includ-
ing BERTScore (Zhang et al., 2020), BARTScore
(Yuan et al., 2021), MoverScore (Zhao et al.,
2019), Embedding Averaging (Landauer and Du-
mais, 1997), Greedy Matching (Rus and Lintean,
2012), Vector Extrema (Forgues et al., 2014),
LaBSE (Feng et al., 2022), and LASER (Artetxe
and Schwenk, 2019). BERTScore is computed
using four models: IndicBERT (Kakwani et al.,
2020), mBERT cased, mBERT uncased (Devlin
et al., 2019), and XLM-RoBERTa (Conneau et al.,
2020), across all 12 layers and using precision,
recall, and F-score, yielding 144 distinct configura-
tions.
Neural Learned Metrics.Metrics specifically
trained to emulate human judgment include
BLEURT (Sellam et al., 2020), COMET (Rei et al.,
2020), and xCOMET (Guerreiro et al., 2024), of-
fering refined evaluation of nuanced language un-
derstanding.
LLM-based Evaluation.At the forefront, we har-
ness the reasoning and discourse-aware capabilities
of large language models for evaluation. GPTScore
(Fu et al., 2024), implemented with Flan-T5-XXL,
evaluates outputs along six predefined dimensions,
providing diagnostic insights that extend beyond
overall scores. We additionally report MetricX-24
(Juraska et al., 2024) and GEMBA (Kocmi and Fe-
dermann, 2023), with GEMBA instantiated using
GPT-4.1. Complementing this, we utilize state-
of-the-art LLMs—including GPT-4.1, LLaMA-3.3
(Grattafiori et al., 2024), DeepSeek V3 (DeepSeek-
AI et al., 2025), and Gemini 2.5 (Comanici et al.,
2025)—through designed prompting protocols (full
prompt details in Appendix B.4).
Full descriptions and implementation details for
all metrics are available in Appendix B.
4.2 Outliers Detection
We characterize outliers as observations that devi-
ate markedly from the rest of the dataset (Barnett
et al., 1994). Given Pearson correlation’s sensitiv-
ity to such anomalies, we applied a robust detec-
tion method on the human scores for each evalua-
tion aspect. Rather than relying on standardization,
which is vulnerable to extreme values due to its
dependence on the mean and standard deviation,
we employed the median and Median Absolute
Deviation (MAD) for greater resilience (Iglewicz
and Hoaglin, 1993; Leys et al., 2013). For a set of
human scoress, MAD was computed as:
MAD= 1.483×median(|s−median(s)|)
A robustz-score was then derived using:
z= s−median(s)MAD
Observations with |z|>3.5 were flagged as out-
liers (Mathur et al., 2020). Table 5 reports the
counts of detected outliers across languages and
indicators.
