Figure 8: Construction of Building the bilingual Han–Vietnamese pipeline.
Appendix A: Alignment Pipeline and OCR
Fine-Tuning Impact
In this appendix, we provide a detailed description
of the Han–Vietnamese sentence alignment process and
demonstrate how fine-tuning the PaddleOCRv5 model
improves alignment quality. This section complements
the main paper by highlighting technical details of
preprocessing, alignment, and empirical results. For our
experiments, we use the exemplary historical workKhâm
Định Việt Sử Thông Giám Cương Mục[1], based on PDF
scans of Sino-Nom texts:
•Original Han text: Stored at the National Library of
Vietnam, in PDF image format, carved in 1884.
•Vietnamese translation 6: Provided by the Vietnam
Institute of Historical Studies. Official translation,
Education Publishing House, Hanoi, 1998.
A.1 Alignment Pipeline
The construction of the bilingual Han–Vietnamese corpus
follows three main stages (see Figure 8):
1.Han text processing:PDF scans of 51 historical
volumes are converted into page images, preprocessed
(resizing, denoising, binarization), and processed with
OCR. Post-processing involves box correction, line
merging, and sequence ordering according to traditional
vertical writing (right-to-left, top-to-bottom). The result
is a cleaned sequence of Han sentencesH=
{h1, h2, . . . , hN }.
2.Vietnamese translation processing:PDF translations
are extracted and cleaned to remove noise (titles,
footnotes, page numbers) and split into sentencesV=
{v1, v2, . . . , vM }, ensuring consistent structure suitable
for alignment.
3.Sentence alignment:We applyBertalign[6] to map
Han sentences to Vietnamese sentences. Bertalign
computes semantic embeddings for each sentence and
uses dynamic programming to find the optimal 1–1, 1–n,
6https://quangduc.com/a4672/kham-dinh-su-viet-thong-giam-
cuong-muc-pdf
or n–1 alignment:
alignment(H,V) = arg min
X
c(x, y)
wherec(x, y)is the cost function based on cosine
similarity between embedding vectors, adjusted for
sentence count in multi-sentence segments. The output
is a set of aligned sentence pairsA={(h i, vj)}with
confidence scoress ij ∈[0,1].
A.2 Integration of OCR Fine-Tuning
OCR errors in Han text significantly affect alignment
quality. Misrecognized characters can lead to incorrect
semantic embeddings, resulting in lower alignment
confidence or mismatched sentence pairs. To address
this, we fine-tuned the character recognition module of
PaddleOCRv5(see Section II in main paper).
After fine-tuning, the OCR output exhibits higher
character accuracy and confidence, which directly improves
downstream alignment. Specifically:
•Error reduction:Character misrecognition in low-
quality scanned pages decreases from 62.5% to 50%.
•Alignment robustness:More sentences are correctly
matched to their Vietnamese counterparts, particularly in
cases involving rare or archaic characters.
A.3 Experimental Results
We conducted experiments comparing alignment
performance using OCR outputs from thebaseline
andfine-tunedmodels. Table 3 summarizes the findings.
Metrics include:
•Aligned sentence ratio:Proportion of Han sentences
successfully mapped to Vietnamese.
•Average confidence:Mean Bertalign alignment score
across all sentence pairs.
•Exact 1–1 alignment rate:Fraction of sentences aligned
in a strict 1–1 manner.
The results indicate a clear improvement in both the quantity
and quality of aligned sentence pairs after fine-tuning OCR.
Notably:
