from the prompt source along with one test sen-
tence from the test set. Next, these examples are
labeled according to the criteria (1, 0, -1). Then,
the leaf node with the best label is selected and ex-
panded using K-NN. Figure 3 illustrates the TreeP-
rompt procedure.
Performing this process for every test sentence
would be computationally expensive and inefficient.
Therefore, we find high-quality examples once for
a set of test sentences rather than individually for
each one. To ensure diversity, the test sentence used
for labeling is changed in each iteration—meaning
the LLM labels examples based on a different ran-
domly selected test sentence from the test set each
time.
3.1 Complexity Analysis
We analyze the computational complexity of the
TreePromptalgorithm in terms of both time and
space.
Let:
•nbe the size of the prompt source corpusP,
•m be the number of examples initially sam-
pled fromP,
•k be the number of neighbors retrieved per
iteration,
•d be the dimensionality of the embedding
space (e.g., RoBERTa),
•t be the number of while-loop iterations un-
til the desired number of positively labeled
examples is collected,
•c be the cost of querying the LLM to label a
single example.
3.2 Time Complexity
The algorithm consists of the following main steps:
1. Initialization: Randomly sampling m exam-
ples fromPtakesO(m)time.
2. Initial Labeling: Querying the LLM to label
the initial m examples incurs a cost of O(m·
c).
3.While Loop(repeated fortiterations):
• Leaf Selection: Selecting the most
promising node from the current candi-
date set E takes O(|E|) =O(m+t·k)
time.
• KNN Retrieval: Using brute-force KNN
based on d-dimensional embeddings in-
curs a cost of O(n·d) . This can be im-
proved using approximate methods (e.g.,
FAISS).
• LLM Labeling of New Examples:
Querying k new examples takes O(k·c)
time.
• Update Candidate Set: Adding new ex-
amples toEtakesO(k)time.
Therefore, the overall time complexity of the
algorithm is:
O(m·c+t·(n·d+k·c))
If an approximate nearest neighbour (ANN)
search algorithm is used instead of brute-force
KNN, the complexity can improve to:
O(m·c+t·(logn+k·c))
3.3 Space Complexity
The space requirements are as follows:
•O(n·d) to store embeddings for the entire
corpusP,
•O(m+t·k) for maintaining the candidate set
E.
Hence, the total space complexity is:
O(n·d+m+t·k)
3.4 Summary
The main costs in the TreePrompt algorithm are
LLM calls and similarity search over the prompt
corpus. Utilizing KNN in a computationally ef-
ficient way, as well as batching or caching LLM
requests, can significantly reduce real-world run-
time.
4 Experimental Setup
This section describes the model configurations,
sampling methods, evaluation criteria, and datasets
used in our experiments.
4.1 Language Models
We evaluated our approach using four large lan-
guage models:
GPT-4o: The latest multimodal model developed
by OpenAI, known for its superior performance in
multilingual tasks, including machine translation
(OpenAI, 2024).
GPT-3.5-turbo: An efficient and faster variant
of GPT-3.5 that demonstrates strong translation
capabilities (OpenAI, 2023).
DeepSeek-V2: An open-source competitive
large language model that produces high-quality
outputs for machine translation tasks (AI, 2024).
4.2 Tree Expansion via KNN-RoBERTa
To augment the tree structure in our proposed
TreePrompt method, we utilize the K-Nearest
Neighbors (KNN) algorithm applied to sentence
embeddings generated by the RoBERTa model.
Starting from a labeled example, KNN-RoBERTa
allows us to retrieve semantically similar samples
from the prompt source corpus, which are subse-
quently considered for further labeling and integra-
tion into the tree.
We selected RoBERTa due to its superior ability
to capture semantic similarity compared to other
embedding models such as BERT or Sentence-
BERT. Empirical evaluations demonstrate that re-
trieval based on RoBERTa embeddings consis-
tently yields higher-quality example expansions,
thereby improving the effectiveness of few-shot
prompts in downstream translation tasks (Reimers
and Gurevych, 2019b). The embedding strategy
employed here plays a critical role in guiding the
LLM toward selecting more contextually relevant
translation examples.
