content relevant to topic t. The LLM issues a Google Search query, retrieves relevant snippets, and
extracts the most on-topic content from the returned results. To ensure grounding in real web content,
we request that the LLM pair each extracted item with a source URL for provenance.
Estimating difficulty.Once a sample x with source text s is drawn, we generate model outputs
and score their quality. Our framework requires a quality estimator that, given a model’s output
o=m(s) for input s, assigns a difficulty score dx ∈[0,100] , where higher values indicate greater
model failure. We average this score across a set of models M to obtain the difficulty of input
s: ds = 1|M| · Pm∈M qe(s, m(s)). The framework is agnostic to the choice of quality estimator;
different tasks require different instantiations. Formachine translation, a topic t is, e.g., “1990s
business news”; a sample x is a source-language text; and dx is the difficulty score. We use GEMBA
[7] based on Gemini-2.5-Pro, an LLM-as-a-judge metric that assigns a scalar quality score for an
input–output pair (s, o); difficulty is computed as 100 minus the quality score (see Section J.3 for
the quality estimation prompt). This approximation via multiple model outputs is known as artificial
crowd [8]. Forknowledge question answering, a topic t is a knowledge domain, a sample x is a
factual question-answer pair generated from that domain, and dx is the model’s error rate, averaged
across multiple model configurations (see Section 3).
Together, drawing a sample and estimating its difficulty constitutes a single query with unit cost; the
search algorithms described next decide which topics to query and how to allocate a fixed budget
across them.
2.3 Finding Difficult Topics as a Multi-Armed Bandit
A topic t is a distribution. A sample from a topic x∼t is a piece of input text and has an associated
difficulty score dx. Drawing a single sample x from topic t, generating a model output for it, and
estimating its difficulty has a cost of 1, and t⋆ denotes the set of samples that have been drawn from t.
The difficult topic search task is: given T={t} |T|j=1, find top-kt∈T E[dx|x∼t] at example budget
B, so Pt∈T |t⋆| ≤B. We evaluate any algorithm that selects some ˆT⊆T,| ˆT|=k with budget B
by 1k · Pˆt∈ ˆT E[dx|x∼ ˆt](i.e. selects thektopics with the highest average difficulty), where higheris better. The concrete instantiations of t, x, and dx for machine translation and knowledge QA are
defined in the previous subsection.
3
2.4 Search Algorithms
The general form of finding the best t∈T is shown in Algorithm 1. Repeatedly, until we reach a
budget, we select a topic to sample from (pull an arm), and at the end select the topic with the highest
observed difficulty. For our task, we use a series of increasingly complex selection methods:
Brute-force (Algorithm 2).The most basic approach samples from a random topic at each step.
All methods share a hyperparameter c that caps the maximum number of samples from a single topic
to prevent budget waste on a single “honeypot” arm. This approach is uninformed and wastes budget
on topics that do not look promising.
Greedy (Algorithm 3).Selects the topic that currently has the highest observed difficulty (pure
exploitation). This requires all topics to be sampled at least once before exploitation can commence,
which can be prohibitively expensive.
ϵ-Greedy (Algorithm 4).Stochastically switches between exploration (sampling never-sampled
topics, with probability ϵ) and exploitation (sampling the current best topic, with probability 1−ϵ ).
This enables exploitation to begin before all topics have been scored, making it the most budget-
efficient method in practice.
We also consider a batched version of these algorithms by replacing steps 2 and 3 in Algorithm 1
with choosing top-btopics at once. This helps avoid local minima through regularization.
Contextual Bandit.We also explore a contextual bandit variant that interpolates difficulty from
neighboring topics using keyword similarity (Jaccard index), allowing the algorithm to skip topics
whose neighbors are all easy. Although promising, ϵ-greedy matches or outperforms this variant in
our experiments (Section G), suggesting limited local difficulty correlation; we leave richer context
representations as future work.
3 Experiments
We first compare the proposed search algorithms on both tasks, then analyze the discovered topics
relative to existing benchmarks, validate robustness across scale, languages, models, and proxy
signals, and report cost. We use the framework on two tasks that exemplify benchmark saturation.
Machine translation.We search for English source texts and evaluate translation into Czech, Chinese,
German, and Ukrainian using Google Translate,2 Gemini 2.5 Pro [9], and Gemma 3 [10], covering
three language families and diverse model types (see Sections J.2 to J.4 for sampling, translation,
and quality estimation prompts). The topic set contains ∼3,000 topics, each with up to 25 samples.
Difficulty is measured by GEMBA-SQA score [7] averaged across models and languages.
Knowledge question answering.We generate 6,000 knowledge domains, each containing 25
subtopics. For each topic, search retrieves Google snippets, and the model synthesizes question-
answer pairs with definitive answers (see Sections J.5 to J.7 for prompts; Appendix D provides full
details). After filtering context-dependent questions, we retain 5,160 domains ( ∼130k subtopics).
Difficulty is estimated by averaging errors across three Gemini configurations: without thinking, with
thinking, and with search enabled.
In both tasks the goal is to efficiently identify top- k most difficult topics within a topic set T of
thousands of candidates.
3.1 Search Algorithm Comparison
We evaluate the search algorithms on both tasks by the difficulty of the final selected topic at each
budget level (Figure 2).
Machine translation.Brute search is near unusable, confirming that exploitation is essential.
Greedy works but requires sampling every topic at least once before exploitation can begin, which
is prohibitively expensive. ϵ-greedy begins exploiting early and outperforms the greedy-on-subset
baseline, which can miss difficult topics by chance. The unconstrained greedy variant (c=∞) gets
stuck on a locally optimal topic, confirming the value of capping exploitation. ϵ-greedy reaches
