A Ablation: Top-1 Topic Performance
In the main paper (Figure 2), we report search algorithm performance using top-10 difficulty. Here
we complement that analysis with top-1 results on both tasks (Figure 6), which measure how well
each algorithm identifies the single most difficult topic.
The trends are consistent with the top-10 results: ϵ-greedy dominates across both tasks, reaching
near-oracle difficulty with a fraction of the budget. Notably, top-1 performance is noisier than
top-10 because it depends on a single topic rather than an average over ten, making the advantage
of ϵ-greedy’s balanced exploration-exploitation even more pronounced. On machine translation,
ϵ-greedy converges to within ∆<1 of the oracle by step 5,000. On knowledge QA, the same pattern
holds, with ϵ-greedy recovering oracle-level difficulty by exploring only ∼10% of the subtopic space.
These results confirm that the findings from the main paper (Figure 2) are robust to the choice of k
and generalize across both evaluation tasks.
B Robustness Across Languages and Models
In this section, we provide detailed per-language and per-model breakdowns of the search algorithm
performance, complementing the aggregate results in the main paper (Section 3).
Per-language results.Figure 7 shows algorithm performance separately for English →Czech,
English→Chinese, English →German, and English →Ukrainian. ϵ-greedy consistently identifies
near-optimal challenge topics across all four language directions, including the high-resource
English→Chinese pair where baseline performance is already strong. The convergence rate is
similar across languages, confirming that the search framework does not favor particular language
families.
Per-model results.Figure 8 breaks down performance by translation model: Gemini-2.5-Pro,
Gemma3-27B, and Google Translate. The relative algorithm ordering is preserved across all three
models, with ϵ-greedy dominating in each case. Notably, even against Gemini-2.5-Pro—a frontier
model that is also used as our quality estimator—the search successfully identifies genuinely chal-
lenging topics, ruling out the concern that the framework merely finds topics where the estimator
model struggles.
C Relationship to Active Learning and Curriculum Learning
Our framework is fundamentally distinct from both active learning and curriculum learning. Active
learning [34] selects examples from afixedpool to label; our method searches anunboundedspace—
the Internet—todiscovernew examples. Curriculum learning [ 35] orders known training examples
by difficulty; our method identifieswhich topics are difficultin the first place. The difficult examples
we discover could serve as inputs to either approach, making them complementary. Our MAB
formulation corresponds to Best Arm Identification [6]; we emphasize that the contribution is the
