When LLMs Benchmark Themselves: Deconstructing Self-Bias in Automated Evaluation,"When LLMs Benchmark Themselves: Deconstructing Self-Bias in
Automated Evaluation
Wenda Xu*1 Sweta Agrawal1 Vilém Zouhar2 Markus Freitag1 Daniel Deutsch1
1Google 2ETH Zurich
Abstract
As LLMs rapidly saturate existing bench-
marks, automated benchmark creation us-
ing LLMs (LLM-as-a-benchmark)—where a
model generates test inputs (LLM-as-a-testset)
and evaluates outputs (LLM-as-an-evaluator)—
has gained traction as a cheap alternative to hu-
man curation. We show that this paradigm has
a fundamental problem:LLM-generated bench-
marks systematically favor the model that cre-
ated them. Using machine translation as our pri-
mary testbed, we find that self-bias arises from
two compounding sources, LLM-as-a-testset
and LLM-as-an-evaluator, and their combina-
tion amplifies the effect. Crucially, even when
test data is generated with explicit diversity con-
trols, each model’s implicit stylistic tendencies
produce homogeneous, model-specific outputs
that inflate its own scores. Increasing source
text diversity, using our proposed diversity met-
ric, partially mitigates this bias. Self-bias is
strong enough to cause each model to rank it-
self first, overriding the peer-consensus order-
ing. We confirm that the phenomenon extends
to open-ended generation on the Chatbot Arena
task.
1 Introduction
Keeping NLP benchmarks current is harder than
it used to be. LLMs saturate leaderboards within
months of a benchmark’s release, yet building a
new human-curated test set is expensive and slow—
and for languages or domains where labeled data
is scarce, it may simply not be feasible. Auto-
mated benchmark creation using LLMs (LLM-as-a-
benchmark) (Farchi et al., 2024; Maheshwari et al.,
2024; Pombal et al., 2025a) has emerged as a prac-
tical response: a single model generates test inputs
(LLM-as-a-testset) and scores the outputs (LLM-as-
an-evaluator), producing benchmarks on demand
at low cost.
*Email Correspondence: {wendax, swetaagrawal,
freitag, dandeutsch}@google.com ,
vzouhar@ethz.ch
The problem is that this convenience comes
with a hidden cost. Prior work has shown that
LLM-based evaluators tend to favor their own out-
puts (Panickssery et al., 2025; Chen et al., 2025;
Xu et al., 2024), but the bias introduced by the test
set generation step—and how it compounds with
evaluator bias—has not been carefully studied. If
the source texts a model generates are already tai-
lored to its own strengths, performance scores will
reflect the model’s idiosyncrasies rather than its
actual quality.
We study this problem and make three contribu-
tions.(1)We formally define self-bias using the
estimator-bias framework (Xu et al., 2024), decom-
posing it into LLM-as-a-testset and LLM-as-an-
evaluator components and showing that their com-
bination compounds the effect. Self-bias is strong
enough that every model ranks itself first, overrid-
ing the peer-consensus ordering as estimated by the
remaining models.(2)We trace self-bias to a plau-
sible mechanistic cause: even under high-quality
prompts with explicit diversity controls (Pombal
et al., 2025b), each model generates repetitive,
model-specific source texts—text it can then trans-
late and score more easily. This effect is strongest
in low-resource languages, where limited genera-
tion capacity causes outputs to converge into dis-
tinctive degeneration patterns. We also verify that
self-bias appears in LLM-as-a-benchmark on the
Chatbot Arena task, showing the problem is not
specific to translation.(3)We provide practical
guidance for reducing self-bias: filtering source
texts using our proposed diversity metric reduces
bias, though the effectiveness varies across mod-
els; generating benchmark data in languages where
models have high proficiency minimizes model-
specific signatures; and using frontier models to
benchmark open-source models remains reliable
when the quality gap is large.
arXiv:2509.26600v3  [cs.CL]  30 Aug 2026
2 Preliminaries and Self-Bias Definition
LLM-as-a-testsetWe automatically generate a test
set using a generator model, Mtest. For a given in-
struction s, Mtest produces a source text x and an
optional reference text y′. The source text x is then
used as a prompt for the target model under evalua-
tion, Mtarget, which generates an output y. Finally,
an evaluation metric computes a quality score for
y by comparing it against source text x. We inves-
tigate testset generation self-bias: a generated test
set may systematically favor its generator model
(Mtest), inflating that model’s scores.
LLM-as-an-evaluatorAn evaluator model,
Mevaluator, assesses the quality of an output y
generated by a target model Mtarget in response to a
prompt x , assigning it a score based on predefined
criteria. In our experimental setup, the prompts
x are sourced from human-authored benchmark
datasets. We investigate evaluator self-bias: an
LLM-based evaluator may assign higher scores to
its own model’s outputs than warranted.
LLM-as-a-benchmarkcombines LLM-as-
a-testset and LLM-as-an-evaluator: a single
model generates source text x, prompts a target
model Mtarget to produce output y, and then
evaluates y. By unifying both roles in one model,
LLM-as-a-benchmark inherits—and potentially
compounds—the biases of each component.
We operationalize self-bias as follows:
biasMi =θ Mi,Mi
