where M is a set of models, Mi is the model
being evaluated, and Mo are peer models. θMi,Mo
is therankingof model Mi as determined by model
Mo, averaged across segments. We use rankings
rather than raw scores so that self-bias is directly
comparable across settings where quality metrics
operate on different scales. θMi,Mi is the self-
ranking and 1|M|−1 PMo̸=Mi θMi,Mo is the peer-
consensus ranking, which we treat as a proxy for
true performance. Since peer models may carry
their own biases, our framework measuresrelative
self-preference rather than deviation from an ab-
solute ground truth. A negative biasMi indicates
that Mi ranks itself more favorably than its peers
do; a more negative value reflects stronger self-
preference.
In our study, Mo plays the role of test set gen-
erator (LLM-as-a-testset), evaluator (LLM-as-an-
evaluator), or both (LLM-as-a-benchmark).
3 Experimental Setup
We study self-bias through machine translation,
which offers established quality metrics (Juraska
et al., 2024; Kocmi and Federmann, 2023; Xu et al.,
2023; Lavie et al., 2025), annually updated test
sets with official human rankings for our mod-
els (Kocmi et al., 2025) as ground truth, and the
ability to study self-bias across diverse language
pairs. We validate the generality of our findings
on open-ended generation via the Chatbot Arena
task (Section 6.2). For translation, our experiments
encompass six low-to-medium resource language
directions: Bemba −English, Kurdish −English,
Aymara−English, Luo−English, English−Bemba,
and English−Aymara. We focus on low-to-medium
resource languages where model quality differ-
ences are larger; on high-resource pairs, all models
score near-perfectly and self-bias becomes diffi-
cult to measure (See Appendix Section E for de-
tailed discussion). For the Chatbot Arena task, we
contrast high-resource (English, Korean) and low-
resource (Bemba, Aymara) prompt languages.
Model selection.We select Gemini 2.5 Pro, GPT-
4.1, and Claude Opus 4 for two reasons. First, they
are the top-performing systems at WMT25 (Kocmi
et al., 2025), for which official human rankings can
still distinguish their translation quality. Second, all
three have been used in the zero-shot benchmarking
framework of Pombal et al. (2025a), demonstrat-
ing their capability as LLM-as-a-benchmark gen-
erators. In preliminary experiments, multilingual
open-source models (Gemma3-27B, Mistral-Large-
2411, Qwen3-32B) failed to generate high-quality
benchmark data for low-resource languages.
Evaluation setup.We evaluate models under
the three conditions defined in Section 2. For
LLM-as-a-testset, translation quality is measured
by MetricX-QE (Juraska et al., 2024), a SOTA
reference-free translation metric where lower
scores indicate higher quality. For LLM-as-
an-evaluator, source texts are drawn from the
FLORES-200 benchmark (Team et al., 2022) to
ensure human-authored test set inputs, so that the
LLM acts solely as an evaluator. For LLM-as-a-
benchmark, models generate source texts and eval-
uate translations; a fully reference-free generation
variant is analyzed in Section 5. We additionally
use chrF (Popovi´c, 2015) to measure textual sim-
ilarity across source texts generated by different
models. As described in Section 2, all raw scores
are normalized into per-segment system rankings
to ensure comparability across settings.
For LLM-as-a-testset and LLM-as-a-benchmark,
we generate 200 source texts per language direc-
tion; for LLM-as-an-evaluator, we sample 200
FLORES examples per direction. In all cases, trans-
lations are obtained from all three LLMs.All eval-
uations are reference-free.Temperature is set to
0 for all API calls. All prompts are provided in
Appendix Section C. Our sample size of N=200
is well justified: bootstrap resampling (B=10,000)
shows that confidence intervals for self-rankings
and peer-rankings do not overlap, confirming sta-
tistically significant self-bias at this sample size
(Appendix Section A). We further assess statisti-
cal significance via permutation tests ( 10,000 re-
samples) throughout; significance at p <0.05 is
marked with ∗ in all tables.
4 Does the use of LLM-as-a-benchmark
introduce self-bias?
We investigate self-bias using machine translation,
where source and target languages can be precisely
controlled. To generate our benchmark data, we
employ the high-quality prompt proposed by Pom-
bal et al. (2025a) in their LLM-as-a-benchmark
framework. This prompt explicitly controls for
diversity and quality by instructing models to gen-
erate source-reference text pairs covering diverse
topics, varying in length and complexity, and tar-
geting different styles (see Appendix Section C for
details). Despite these explicit controls, we find
that self-bias persists due to implicit model-specific
features in the generated texts.
Table 1 quantifies self-bias for Gemini-2.5-pro,
GPT-4.1, and Claude-Opus-4 across XX→En direc-
