Decomposition of self-bias.To understand
what drives self-bias, we decompose LLM-as-a-
benchmark into its LLM-as-a-testset component
(Table 3), where models generate test sets and trans-
lation quality is scored by MetricX-QE, an indepen-
dent neural metric unrelated to any of the evaluated
LLMs. Permutation tests (p <0.05) confirm that
LLM-as-a-benchmark bias is significantly larger
than LLM-as-a-testset bias alone for all three mod-
els, demonstrating that testset generation bias and
evaluator bias compound—the combination ampli-
fies the distortion beyond what testset generation
produces in isolation. We also verify that LLM-
as-an-evaluator, where models act as judges for
translations of human-authored FLORES test data,
independently exhibits self-bias (see Appendix Sec-
tion F).
Where does the self-bias stem from?Co-
generating source texts and translation references,
as in (Pombal et al., 2025b), can introduce two bi-
ases: (1) atranslatability bias, where the model
avoids source texts it cannot translate well, and
(2) ageneration bias, where each model generates
text with its own model-specific patterns. We con-
firm translatability bias by comparing MetricX-QE
scores for src-only vs. src+ref generation (Table 4):
src+ref consistently yields better scores, indicating
thatco-generation tailors sources to the model’s
translation strengths.
Our primary interest is the more fundamental
generation bias. Each LLM generates text with
its own model-specific patterns—characteristic n-
gram distributions and stylistic tendencies. These
patterns advantage the same model in two ways:
(1) the model translates text matching its own pat-
terns more accurately, and (2) since LLMs can rec-
ognize their own outputs (Panickssery et al., 2025),
they score them more favorably as evaluators. In
high-resource languages, all models produce di-
verse text and these model-specific patterns are
minimal. In low-resource languages, each model
falls back on a narrow set of patterns, making its
outputs distinctive and amplifying self-bias. To
isolate generation bias from the translatability con-
found, we study source-only generation in subse-
quent sections.
5 How does source text generation impact
self-bias?
This section traces self-bias to the properties of
generated source texts. We show that src-only gen-
eration still exhibits self-bias (Section 5.1), that
each model’s implicit stylistic tendencies produce
homogeneous outputs despite explicit diversity con-
trols (Section 5.2), that this lack of diversity drives
self-bias (Section 5.3), and that improving diversity
partially mitigates it (Section 5.4). We focus on
Aymara→English and Bemba→English.
5.1 Source text generation leads to self-bias
As Table 5 shows, a measurable self-bias persists
even when the data generation pipeline is con-
strained to produce only source texts without co-
generated references. The negative bias scores ap-
pear exclusively on the diagonal, confirming that
self-bias is inherent to each model’s generated texts.
Source-only generation thus does not eliminate self-
bias; the model’s inherent stylistic tendencies in
monolingual generation are sufficient to create eval-
uation advantages for its own translation system.
5.2 What makes source text generation
exhibit bias?
We investigate how each LLM’s source text char-
acteristics contribute to self-bias. Each model’s
source texts are more similar to its own other gener-
ated texts—even those for entirely different topics—
than to texts from other models. To quantify this
within-model versus cross-model similarity, we de-
fine a chrF@K metric.
