2 Natural Language Processing
et al. 2020), domain-specialized models (Beltagy et al. 2019; Huang et al. 2019b; Lee et al. 2020;
Chalkidis et al. 2020), multilingual encoders designed for cross-lingual transfer (Pires et al. 2019;
Wu and Dredze 2019; Conneau et al. 2020), and refinements to attention, positional encoding and
normalization that relax its quadratic cost and 512-token limit (Beltagy et al. 2020; Dao et al. 2022;
Su et al. 2024; Xiong et al. 2020; Zhang and Sennrich 2019). These accumulated improvements
were synthesized into ModernBERT, an updated encoder-only architecture that incorporates rotary
positional embeddings, FlashAttention technology, alternating attention patterns, and refined pre-
training procedures (Warner et al. 2025). Its architecture supports context windows up to 8,192
tokens, sixteen times BERT’s original context length of 512, with improved computational effi-
ciency that addresses limitations stemming from training instability and restricted sequence length
(Xiong et al. 2020; Beltagy et al. 2020).
While these architectural advances have been widely adopted for high-resource languages,
Turkish NLP remains primarily centered on earlier architectural paradigms. BERTurk (Schweter
2020) laid the foundation for transformer-based Turkish NLP as, to our knowledge, the first mono-
lingual Turkish BERT model pretrained from scratch and publicly released. Its widespread adoption
across diverse downstream tasks has made it the de facto standard for Turkish language understand-
ing. Subsequently, models such as ConvBERTurk and various ELECTRA adaptations have emerged
on community platforms, although they frequently prioritized immediate availability over technical
documentation. Critical specifications regarding corpus composition, preprocessing pipelines, and
systematic evaluation protocols are often underspecified, which impedes reproducibility and limits
the ability of the research community to build systematically on prior work.
Recent large-scale multilingual efforts have incorporated Turkish alongside other languages.
Multilingual BERT (mBERT) (Devlin et al. 2019) and XLM-R (Conneau et al. 2020) share
parameters and vocabulary across typologically diverse languages, which dilutes representational
capacity for individual languages (Conneau et al. 2020; Chang et al. 2024). For morphologically
rich languages, multilingual tokenizers produce inefficient segmentations, leading to increased
token fertility, longer effective sequences, and reduced vocabulary utilization (Rust et al. 2021).
Although generative large language models now dominate the research agenda, encoder-only
models remain the workhorse of a large class of applications. Text classification, named entity
recognition, part-of-speech tagging, semantic similarity and dense retrieval require contextual
or pooled representations rather than autoregressive generation, and they are typically deployed
in high-throughput or latency-sensitive settings where the cost per item matters (Antoun et al.
2025). Encoders are also a component of generative systems rather than an alternative to them,
serving as the retrieval stage of retrieval-augmented generation pipelines (Lewis et al. 2020). The
economics favor them by more than an order of magnitude: Luccioni et al. (2024) measure task-
specific text classification at a mean of 0.002 kWh per 1,000 queries against 0.047 kWh for text
generation, and conclude that they ‘do not see convincing evidence for the necessity of [generative
models’] deployment in contexts where tasks are well-defined’. Nor is the quality gap what the
difference in size suggests: with matched data and architecture, a fine-tuned 400M-parameter
encoder outperforms a fine-tuned 1B-parameter decoder on MNLI (91.3 against 89.9), and its
authors conclude that at 1B parameters or below ‘encoders will continue to outperform all others in
their size range (and even ones above it)’ (Weller et al. 2026). A 149M-parameter Turkish encoder
therefore answers a practical need: efficient, low-latency classification, tagging and retrieval for
Turkish, which a multilingual encoder serves at a higher token cost and a generative model at a far
higher inference cost.
This work identifies and addresses two distinct gaps in Turkish NLP research. First, amod-
ernization gapexists: no monolingual Turkish encoder has been trained from scratch with the
efficiency, stability and long-context improvements consolidated in ModernBERT, so these proper-
ties have been unavailable to Turkish NLP. Second, anevaluation gappersists due to the lack of
a standardized, reproducible benchmarking framework, hindering systematic comparison across
model generations and architectural paradigms.
