We address these gaps by introducing TabiBERT, a monolingual Turkish encoder, based on
the ModernBERT architecture, and TabiBench, a unified evaluation benchmark with standardized
protocols. Across TabiBench, TabiBERT leads the Turkish models in five of eight task categories
and BERTurk, the previous best, in six of eight; its overall average of 77.28 (a GLUE-style
macro-average on a 0–100 scale) exceeds BERTurk’s 75.66. TabiBERT’s technical specifications
and results compared to prior Turkish BERT models are summarized in Table 1. The gains
concentrate where the benchmark is not saturated: +9.55 F1 on question answering and +2.41
NDCG@10 on code retrieval, with smaller leads on academic understanding (+0.77 macro-F1) and
information retrieval (+0.60 NDCG@10), whereas on the four short-text categories the leading
Turkish models lie within 1 of one another. Against the previous best result for each category
individually, TabiBERT is 1.48 higher on average, indicating consistent generalization across
domains with a single general-purpose encoder. Beyond accuracy, TabiBERT supports a context
length of 8,192 tokens, sixteen times that of prior Turkish BERT models, which cannot process such
inputs at all; as a ModernBERT-architecture model it also inherits that architecture’s long-context
efficiency, which Warner et al. (2025) report as up to 2.65× faster inference than the next-fastest
encoder at 8,192 tokens.
For completeness, we also evaluate against mmBERT (Marone et al. 2026), a recent large-scale
multilingual encoder with 307M parameters (against TabiBERT’s 149M) and a 256,000-token
vocabulary that achieves 78.98 on TabiBench. Our work focuses on developing an efficient,
monolingual encoder that incorporates modern architectural advances at a scale suitable for
widespread research and practical deployment; Section 5.3.3 sets out when each of TabiBERT,
BERTurk and mmBERT is the appropriate choice.
The main contributions of this work are as follows:
– TabiBERT (Türker et al. 2025c), a monolingual Turkish encoder pretrained from scratch on
a corpus we composed and curated for this purpose: 86.58B tokens (426 GB) of web text,
academic articles, graduate theses, parliamentary transcripts, source code and mathematics, to
our knowledge the first Turkish encoder corpus to include the last five. Pretrained for 1T tokens
with the ModernBERT architecture and recipe, it is the first monolingual Turkish encoder with
an 8,192-token context and achieves state-of-the-art results among Turkish-specific encoders on
five of eight benchmark categories.
– TabiBench (Türker et al. 2025b), a unified evaluation framework of 27 datasets across eight
task categories with fixed public splits, a documented split-generation rule and an identical
fine-tuning protocol for every compared model, covering domains such as code retrieval and
academic text understanding that are absent from prior Turkish benchmarks.
– A reproducible release and two methodological findings: model weights, training configurations
and evaluation code are public (Türker et al. 2025d); and we document that ModernBERT’s
published pretraining throughput depends on a pre-tokenization step not documented in its paper
or codebase, and that token-classification comparisons across tokenizers require word-level
rather than subword-level evaluation.
4 Natural Language Processing
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section 2 reviews prior work on encoder
architectures, Turkish language models and evaluation benchmarks; Section 3 describes the
TabiBERT model and the pretraining procedure; Section 4 specifies the TabiBench evaluation
framework; Section 5 reports the evaluation results of TabiBERT on the TabiBench benchmark;
Section 6 discusses the results; Section 7 presents some limitations of our work; and Section 8
concludes the paper and outlines directions for future work.
2. Related Work
The introduction of Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT) (Devlin
et al. 2019) was a significant advancement in encoder-only transformer models. These models
were trained on large English corpora and established strong performance baselines across diverse
natural language processing tasks. They provided a pretrained model that could be fine-tuned for
various downstream tasks.
2.1 Encoder-only Transformer Models and Architectural Evolution
Following BERT, encoder-only transformer models were improved by various architectural inno-
vations or training techniques, including changes to pretraining objectives, optimization strategies,
and large-scale data and model scaling, leading to consistent performance gains across a wide
range of downstream NLP tasks. RoBERTa (Liu et al. 2019) scaled the corpus and training
regime, ALBERT (Lan et al. 2020) reduced model size through parameter sharing and factorized
embeddings, ELECTRA (Clark et al. 2020) introduced the more sample-efficient replaced-token-
detection objective, and the DeBERTa family (He et al. 2021; Microsoft 2020; He et al. 2023) added
disentangled attention and, in its third version, ELECTRA-style training. Later work improved
pretraining efficiency rather than the attention mechanism: MosaicBERT (Portes et al. 2023) com-
bined FlashAttention (Dao et al. 2022), ALiBi (Press et al. 2022), gated linear units (Shazeer
2020), dynamic unpadding (Zeng et al. 2022) and low-precision normalization into a fast training
recipe, RetroMAE (Xiao et al. 2022) redesigned masked autoencoding for dense retrieval, and
FlexiBERT (Tuli et al. 2023) searched heterogeneous encoder configurations with neural architec-
ture search. Renewed interest in encoder-only models has led to modernized successors to BERT.
ModernBERT (Warner et al. 2025) revisited the original BERT model, extending context length
and updating architectural elements and design choices. NeoBERT (Le Breton et al. 2025) revisited
the depth-to-width ratio and extended the context to 4,096 tokens with deployment in mind, and
OptiBERT (Dervishi et al. 2025) trained compute-optimal BERT-style models.
2.2 Monolingual TurkishBERTModels
The success of transformer-encoder models motivated a series of BERT models known as
BERTurk to be trained on Turkish data (Schweter 2020). Several variations of BERT, such
as DistilBERT (Sanh et al. 2019), ConvBERTurk (Schweter 2025), and ELECTRA (Clark et al.
2020), were also trained on Turkish data by the same authors (Schweter 2020). These models were
generally trained on a 35 GB corpus consisting of 4.4B tokens drawn from the Turkish OSCAR
corpus (Ortiz Suárez et al. 2019; Abadji et al. 2022), a Wikipedia dump, a various OPUS cor-
pora (Tiedemann 2012) and a corpus provided by Kemal Oflazer. Some models, like ConvBERTurk
and ELECTRA, were also trained on the Turkish portion of the mC4 corpus (Xue et al. 2021;
Schweter 2020), which is a cleaned version of the public web crawl data of Common Crawl.
These models have been evaluated on various downstream tasks (such as part-of-speech tagging
