TabiBERT: A Large-Scale ModernBERTFoundation Model and A Unified Benchmark for Turkish5
and named entity recognition) where they generally outperform their multilingual counterparts
(Schweter 2020; Conneau et al. 2020). Despite their popularity, these models were limited in
transparency as they were not products of academic publications.
Subsequently, two Turkish BERT models were introduced with academic publications.YTU-
Cosmos-BERT (Kesgin et al. 2023) utilized an uncased Turkish tokenizer and was trained on a
corpus of an unknown number of tokens reported to be around 75 GB in size, which consists of
web crawls (OSCAR and mC4), Turkish Wikipedia, news and novels. TurkishBERTweet (Najafi
and Varol 2024) was tailored for social media. It also employed an uncased tokenizer, specifically
designed for social media texts, by the addition of special tokens that can encode special information
in tweets like hashtags, mentions, and URLs. It was pretrained with a corpus of around 110 GB in
uncompressed form, containing nearly 894 million tweets of at least 10 tokens each, that is, at least
roughly 9B tokens.
2.3 MultilingualBERTModels
The development of multilingual pretrained language models began with Multilingual BERT
(mBERT) (Devlin et al. 2019), which extended the original BERT architecture to 104 languages
by pretraining on a corpus obtained by concatenation of Wikipedia corpora. Despite lacking
explicit cross-lingual objectives, mBERT demonstrated surprising zero-shot cross-lingual transfer
capabilities (Pires et al. 2019). XLM (Conneau and Lample 2019) worked on enhancing cross-
lingual alignment and introduced Translation Language Modeling (TLM) that leverages parallel
corpora. XLM-RoBERTa (Conneau et al. 2020) further scaled multilingual pretraining to 2.5
TB of Common Crawl data across 100 languages, achieving state-of-the-art performance using
only masked language modeling without parallel data. Later work varied the objective or the
architecture: cross-lingual contrastive learning in InfoXLM (Chi et al. 2021), back-translation
masked language modeling in ERNIE-M (Ouyang et al. 2021), combined cross-lingual objectives
in Unicoder (Huang et al. 2019a), decoupled input and output embeddings in RemBERT (Chung
et al. 2021), disentangled attention with ELECTRA-style pretraining in mDeBERTa (He et al.
2023), and improved vocabulary allocation across languages in XLM-V (Liang et al. 2023).
More recent work has further expanded the landscape of multilingual encoders.
EuroBERT (Boizard et al. 2025) introduced multilingual encoders covering European and sev-
eral widely spoken global languages. Similarly, mmBERT (Marone et al. 2026), building on
ModernBERT, incorporated long-context attention and efficient training strategies. It demonstrated
strong performance on classification, embedding, and retrieval tasks, particularly for low-resource
languages, while largely preserving the inference efficiency of ModernBERT despite its large
vocabulary of 256,000 tokens.
However, these broad multilingual approaches often struggle with the linguistic specificity
required for morphologically complex languages, motivating the development of language-family-
specific or language-specific models.
2.3.1 Curse of Multilinguality
A key challenge in multilingual modeling is thecurse of multilinguality, a phenomenon where
adding more languages to a fixed-capacity model eventually degrades per-language perfor-
mance (Conneau et al. 2020); it is attributed to negative interference between languages competing
for shared parameters (Wang et al. 2020), has been observed across 10,000 models and 250 lan-
guages (Chang et al. 2024), and is mitigated by modular adapters (Pfeiffer et al. 2022) or experts
trained on language subsets (Blevins et al. 2024). These findings motivate the development of more
targeted models that focus on either monolingual models or models that focus on related language
families rather than attempting to cover all languages in a single model.
6 Natural Language Processing
2.4 ModernBERT
ModernBERT is one of the recent and most significant bidirectional encoder-only transformer
models. It upgrades the original BERT architecture by utilizing contemporary enhancements
to support longer context lengths and improve efficiency and performance in natural language
understanding tasks. It was pretrained on a massive corpus of 2 trillion tokens of English text
and code, enabling stronger generalization and suitability for both language and code understand-
ing. ModernBERT introduces several architectural techniques aimed at improving efficiency,
including Rotary Positional Embeddings (RoPE) (Su et al. 2024), FlashAttention (Dao et al.
2022), unpadding (Zeng et al. 2022), also adopted by mGTE (Zhang et al. 2024), GeGLU acti-
vations (Shazeer 2020), and alternating attention (Gemma Team 2024). Together, these design
choices reduce memory usage and inference time compared to conventional encoder models, while
preserving strong performance on downstream tasks. These features make ModernBERT effective
for classification, retrieval, semantic search, and other NLU tasks, and it serves as an efficient
alternative to older BERT variants in research and production settings.
2.4.1 Language-Specific Variants
The success of ModernBERT has inspired several language-specific adaptations, demonstrating the
effectiveness of specialized training over multilingual approaches. For example, ModernGBERT
is a family of German encoder models (134M, 1B parameters) trained from scratch, incorpo-
rating ModernBERT’s architectural innovations (Wunderle et al. 2026). Their work showed
that dedicated encoders outperform encoders converted from German decoder-only LLMs via
LLM2Vec (BehnamGhader et al. 2024) in terms of both performance and parameter efficiency,
validating the approach of language-specific model development.
LLM-JP-MODERNBERTis a similar ModernBERT-based model trained on a large-scale Japanese
corpus with 8,192-token context length (Sugiura et al. 2025). It achieves good results on fill-mask
evaluations but does not surpass existing baselines on downstream tasks, and its authors leave the
cause open.
2.4.2 Domain-Specific Variants
ModernBERT has been adapted to specialized domains that require long-context understanding:
Clinical ModernBERT was pretrained on biomedical literature, clinical notes and medical ontolo-
gies (Lee et al. 2025), and BioClinical ModernBERT continued pretraining on a multi-institution
corpus of more than 53.5 billion tokens, outperforming existing biomedical and clinical encoders
on four downstream tasks (Sounack et al. 2025). In Japanese radiology report classification,
ModernBERT trained and ran faster than BERT at comparable in-distribution accuracy, although
it generalized less well to an external set of reports (Yamagishi et al. 2026); these adaptations
motivate its use as a base for further domain- and language-adapted encoders.
2.4.3 Architectural Analysis and Comparisons
In a controlled study designed to isolate architectural effects from differences in pretraining
data, ModernBERT was compared with DeBERTaV3 when both models were pretrained on the
same dataset. The results indicate that while ModernBERT offers faster training and inference
speeds attributable to its architectural optimizations, the earlier DeBERTaV3 architecture main-
tained superior sample efficiency and overall benchmark performance under identical pretraining
conditions (Antoun et al. 2025).
TabiBERT: A Large-Scale ModernBERTFoundation Model and A Unified Benchmark for Turkish7
2.5 Turkish NLP Benchmarks
Early evaluation of Turkish NLP models has predominantly relied on individual, task-specific
datasets, developed independently for problems such as named entity recognition (NER), sentiment
analysis, dependency parsing, or question answering. Examples include Turkish NER datasets
derived from Wikipedia (Rahimi et al. 2019) and news (Tür et al. 2003), sentiment datasets collected
from the web (Amasyali et al. 2018), and syntactic resources such as Universal Dependencies
Turkish treebanks (Zeman et al. 2019; Türk et al. 2023; Mar¸ san et al. 2023). Even though these
datasets have been useful in enabling steady and consistent progress on task-specific problems,
they often differ substantially in annotation quality, domain coverage, and evaluation protocols,
and many lack widely adopted, fixed train/validation/test splits, limiting the comparability of
reported results across studies (Çakıcı et al. 2025). As transformer-based models became dominant,
evaluation practices for Turkish largely continued to follow this fragmented paradigm. Recent
works frequently assembled their own evaluation datasets or introduced task-specific splits tailored
to a single study. For instance,YTU-Cosmos-BERT evaluated models on tasks such as sentiment
analysis, news classification, mask prediction, and zero-shot classification using custom-constructed
datasets and internally defined splits, rather than established benchmark splits (Kesgin et al. 2023).
TurkishBERTweet focused on sentiment analysis and hate speech detection in social media,
similarly relying on task-specific tweet datasets and cross-validation-based evaluation protocols
rather than standardized benchmark test sets (Najafi and Varol 2024). Because each of these works
evaluates its model on a self-selected set of datasets under its own splits or cross-validation folds, the
reported results are not comparable across models: differences in data, splits and protocol confound
any cross-study comparison, and a cross-validation study of Turkish sentiment analysis datasets
and tools documents how strongly reported outcomes depend on exactly these choices (Çakıcı et al.
2025).
In response to these limitations, several Turkish NLP benchmark efforts have emerged that aim
to consolidate multiple tasks under unified evaluation frameworks. Mukayese aggregated diverse
Turkish NLP tasks, including language modeling, machine translation, NER, summarization, and
text classification, into a single benchmark suite, providing common baselines (Safaya et al. 2022).
TrGLUE adapted the GLUE paradigm to Turkish natural language understanding tasks, though
its scope remained largely limited to sentence-level NLU (Altinok 2025). Cetvel, a large-scale
LLM-oriented evaluation benchmark, covered a broader set of discriminative and generative
tasks while emphasizing cultural and linguistic relevance for Turkish (Kesen et al. 2024). More
recently, TR-MTEB introduced a Turkish adaptation of the Massive Text Embedding Benchmark
(MTEB), focusing on embedding and retrieval tasks (Baysan and Güngör 2025) by utilizing
machine translation.
Despite recent advances, a systematic quality assessment of 17 widely used Turkish benchmark
datasets against six criteria found that 70 per cent of them fail to meet the authors’ heuristic
standards for linguistic correctness, coherence and cultural appropriateness (Umutlu et al. 2025),
underscoring the need for stricter dataset construction and validation standards. Last but not least,
most existing benchmarks focus on traditional NLP tasks, with limited coverage of modern NLP
tasks such as academic text understanding, retrieval, and code-related tasks, which have become
central to contemporary transformer and LLM evaluation. No existing Turkish benchmark combines
this task coverage with fixed, public splits and a uniform fine-tuning protocol across the compared
encoders.
3. TabiBERT: Model Architecture and Pretraining
In this section, we present a comprehensive overview of the pretraining procedure of the TabiBERT
model, in terms of the pretraining dataset, tokenizer details, the pretraining objectives, and the
model architecture.
8 Natural Language Processing
3.1 Pretraining Data
We compiled a comprehensive dataset primarily consisting of Turkish monolingual text, supple-
mented with a small portion of English, coding and math data, to pretrain our model. To address
significant size disparities and differences in quality across corpora and ensure balanced repre-
sentation of different text types, we employed strategic oversampling of smaller but high-quality
datasets. The constituent corpora total 84.88B raw tokens (sum of Table 2); weighting by sampling
rate yields the 86.58B-token pretraining corpus, of which 13 per cent is from non-Turkish sources.
Throughout the paper, including the abstract, all references to the size of the pretraining corpus
include the effects of oversampling. The details of each corpus are explained in the following
subsections, and the training corpora statistics, as well as the sampling rates, are summarized in
Table 2. To our knowledge, this is the first Turkish encoder pretraining corpus to include academic
articles, graduate theses, parliamentary transcripts, source code and mathematical text; prior Turkish
encoders were trained on web crawls (OSCAR, mC4), Wikipedia, OPUS, news, novels or tweets.
Web corpora.We extracted the tur_Latn subset of FineWeb-2 (Penedo et al. 2025) as the main
Turkish web corpus, with a knowledge cutoff date of April 2024. We also included a sample of 5
million English web pages from the FineWeb-2 English dataset to provide English language context
during pretraining. Moreover, we included a Turkish Wikipedia dataset comprising articles scraped
from Wikipedia’s Turkish-language pages in August 2024. This dataset provides encyclopedic
knowledge covering a wide range of topics written in a formal and informative style.
Scientific corpora.We used the Dergipark and Yöktez datasets from TURNA (Uludo ˘gan et al.
2024). Dergipark is a corpus of Turkish academic articles, and Yöktez is a corpus of Turkish
graduate theses.
Book corpus.We used the Book Corpus dataset from TURNA (Uludo ˘gan et al. 2024), a
compilation of Turkish fiction and non-fiction books.
Bilkent Creative Writings.TheBilkent Creative Writings(Yılmaz 2025) corpus comprises creative
writings produced by Bilkent University students in Turkish writing courses.
ParlaMint-TR.TheParlaMint-TRcorpus originates from the CLARIN Flagship project, b and
contains transcripts of Turkish parliamentary debates within the broader European ParlaMint
initiative.
Code corpus.TheCodecorpus consists of English code repositories, documentation and
instructions obtained by randomly selecting a subset of 2.5M examples from several coding
corpora (Replete-AI 2024; CodeParrot 2022; Bisht 2023; Pham 2023).
Math corpus.TheMathcorpus comprises mathematical questions and solutions in English,
collected from well-known academic and public datasets (Amini et al. 2019; Hendrycks et al.
2021b; Gao et al. 2025; Yue et al. 2024; Yu et al. 2024; Gallifant 2024).
