Against the best Turkish baseline in each category (the last two rows of Table 5), TabiBERT
leads in five of the eight; in those five it exceeds the closest competitor by 2.70 on average, with
the large gains in question answering (+9.55 F1) and code retrieval (+2.41 NDCG@10) over
BERTurk, while the leads on NLI (+0.18 macro-F1), information retrieval (+0.60 NDCG@10)
and academic understanding (+0.77 macro-F1) are below 1. Where it trails, on text classification
(−0.81 macro-F1), STS (−0.59 Pearson) and token classification (−0.25 micro-F1), the deficits
are likewise below 1. Its mean of 77.28 exceeds the mean of the per-category previous bests, 75.80,
by 1.48.
The pattern is therefore a decomposition rather than a uniform advantage. On the four short-text
categories (text classification, token classification, STS, NLI) the leading Turkish models lie within
1 of one another and the aggregate is near saturation for base-sized encoders, whereas question
answering and code retrieval separate the models clearly on their respective metrics (9.55 F1 and
2.41 NDCG@10). The context-length ablation (Appendix A.2) shows that most of the question
answering gain persists when TabiBERT is capped at 512 tokens, so it is a model effect rather
than a context-window effect alone.
5.3.2 Performance by Task Category
Text understanding tasks.On text classification, results converge among top models (except
TurkishBERTweet).YTU-Cosmos-BERT leads (84.25), followed closely by TabiBERT (83.44)
and BERTurk (83.42). Margins under 1 macro-F1 suggest approaching saturation.
On token classification, all Turkish models exceed 92 micro-F1. BERTurk (93.67),YTU-
Cosmos-BERT (93.60), and TabiBERT (93.42) cluster within 0.25 micro-F1, indicating strong
performance across models with limited improvement headroom.
On semantic textual similarity, top models (excluding TurkishBERTweet) score a Pearson
correlation of 84.68–85.33. BERTurk leads (85.33), TabiBERT follows (84.74), thenYTU-Cosmos-
BERT (84.68).
On natural language inference, TabiBERT achieves the highest Turkish model score (84.51),
marginally exceeding BERTurk (84.33) andYTU-Cosmos-BERT (84.16).
Question answering.TabiBERT shows the largest improvement over Turkish baselines: 69.71 F1
versus BERTurk’s 60.16 (+9.55 F1, about 16 per cent relative gain). Of the two datasets in this
20 Natural Language Processing
benchmark, only TQuAD’s 95th-percentile length (1,024 tokens) exceeds the 512-token limit of
BERTurk,YTU-Cosmos-BERT, and TurkishBERTweet, forcing these three baselines to truncate
its longer passages, whereas XQuAD’s 95th percentile (384 tokens) stays under this limit and is
unaffected; TabiBERT and mmBERT instead share an 8,192-token window, processing TQuAD
without truncation. A context-length ablation (Appendix A.2) decomposes TabiBERT’s TQuAD
gain into these two components: capping TabiBERT at 512 tokens on TQuAD still yields 70.31
F1, well above BERTurk’s 63.30, indicating that context length accounts for only about 2 of the
9.04 F1, while the remaining roughly 7 F1 reflect a genuine modeling advantage independent of
context.
Academic understanding tasks.TabiBERT leads Turkish models (70.06), exceedingYTU-Cosmos-
BERT (69.29) and BERTurk (69.00). It is particularly strong on Thesis-Abstract-Classification-
11K (187-class task with specialized language and limited per-class data), where pretraining
on scientific articles and theses provides a domain advantage, in addition to the impact of its
long-context support.
Information retrieval.TabiBERT achieves 75.44 NDCG@10 across the six information retrieval
datasets, surpassing BERTurk (74.84). The gain is modest and we do not attribute it to any single
factor.
Code retrieval.TabiBERT substantially outperforms Turkish models: 56.95 NDCG@10 versus
BERTurk (54.54, +2.41) andYTU-Cosmos-BERT (53.80, +3.15). We attribute the improvement to
exposure to code during pretraining and to the tokenizer’s treatment of code syntax, and possibly
to the longer context for long code snippets; we did not ablate these factors separately, so the gain
is measurable but its attribution among them is not.
5.3.3 Comparison with Multilingual mmBERT
mmBERT achieves 78.98, 1.70 higher than TabiBERT on the 0–100 average. This reflects funda-
mental resource differences rather than architectural superiority, as both share the ModernBERT
architecture with different specialization strategies.
Resource differences.mmBERT has 2.1 times as many parameters as TabiBERT (307M versus
149M). Both are 22-layer ModernBERT-base models with approximately110M non-embedding
parameters each. The difference lies almost entirely in the embedding matrix, which makes up
about 38.5M of TabiBERT’s parameters ( 50,176× 768) versus about 196.6M of mmBERT’s
(256,000× 768). mmBERT’s larger embedding matrix, needed to accommodate 1,800 languages,
accounts for its larger overall parameter count and higher memory requirements.
In tokenization, mmBERT’s tokenizer shows 41 per cent higher fertility on Turkish text, requir-
ing 41 per cent more tokens for identical content. This increases computational cost and reduces
effective context by about 29 per cent (equivalent to a reduction from 8,192 to approximately
5,800 tokens of Turkish text). Finer tokenization may capture morphological nuances via character
n-gram fragmentation but sacrifices efficiency and effective context length.
In training scale, mmBERT was pretrained on 3T tokens against TabiBERT’s 1T. Its mas-
sive multilingual corpus ( 1,800 languages) enables cross-lingual transfer, particularly from
high-resource languages like English.
Practical trade-offs.Despite mmBERT’s higher scores, TabiBERT offers advantages for Turkish-
focused applications: fewer tokens per Turkish input and hence less computation per input (not a
measured speedup), 51 per cent fewer parameters (149M against 307M), and about 41 per cent
longer effective Turkish context (mmBERT’s is about 29 per cent shorter). For Turkish-only
deployment under memory or latency constraints, or for inputs longer than 512 tokens, TabiBERT
is the appropriate choice; where cross-lingual transfer or coverage of other languages is needed
and the larger model is affordable, mmBERT scores higher; BERTurk remains a strong choice
for sentence-level classification, tagging and similarity on short inputs, where it is within 1 of
TabiBERT: A Large-Scale ModernBERTFoundation Model and A Unified Benchmark for Turkish21
TabiBERT, but it cannot process inputs beyond 512 tokens, has no code or mathematics in its
pretraining data, and trails by 9.55 F1 on question answering and 2.41 NDCG@10 on code retrieval.
6. Discussion
Figure 2 visualizes performance patterns across TabiBench. This section interprets these results,
discusses architectural trade-offs, and addresses methodological considerations that informed our
evaluation design.
6.1 Interpreting Performance Patterns
Task saturation versus architectural advantages.On the four text-understanding categories (classi-
fication, token classification, STS, NLI) the leading Turkish models lie within 1 of one another, and
these benchmarks approach saturation for base-sized encoders (Section 5.3). BERTurk’s durability
is itself informative: it has the smallest pretraining corpus of the baselines (35 GB against 75
GB forYTU-Cosmos-BERT and 110 GB for TurkishBERTweet; Table 1) yet outscores both,
which points to corpus curation rather than data volume as the source of its strength. Critically,
TabiBERT’s competitive performance on these tasks demonstrates that long-context optimization
does not compromise short-text understanding, a key validation of ModernBERT’s architectural
design.
The gains on question answering (+9.55 F1) and code retrieval (+2.41 NDCG@10) are where
extended context, code in pretraining and the tokenizer provide measurable advantages, while the
leads on academic understanding (+0.77 macro-F1) and information retrieval (+0.60 NDCG@10)
are below 1 and we do not read them as decisive. The context-length ablation in Appendix A.2
shows that this question answering gain is only partly a context-length effect; most of it persists
even when TabiBERT is capped at the same 512-token context as the baseline models.
Architectural innovations transfer to Turkish.TabiBERT’s results indicate that ModernBERT’s
innovations transfer to a morphologically rich, agglutinative language: the model matches or
exceeds the Turkish baselines while inheriting the architecture’s efficiency at long context and a
sixteenfold longer context window than earlier BERT-based Turkish models (Warner et al. 2025).
6.2 Methodological Considerations and Design Decisions
Tokenization-agnostic evaluation for token classification.For named entity recognition and part-
of-speech tagging, we adopted word-level rather than subword-level evaluation. This addresses
a fundamental comparability issue: different tokenizers fragment words into varying numbers of
subword tokens. A higher-fertility tokenizer would achieve artificially inflated subword-level scores
by correctly predicting more fragmented tokens per word, even with identical word-level accuracy.
Word-level evaluation aggregates predictions at word boundaries before computing met-
rics, ensuring fair comparison regardless of tokenization strategy. While subword-level metrics
may be appropriate for analyzing tokenizer-specific behavior, cross-model comparisons require
tokenization-agnostic metrics. This principle is broadly relevant in multilingual NLP, where
tokenization strategies vary substantially.
Pretraining infrastructure and reproducibility documentation.During initial pretraining, we
observed training throughput of about 30 per cent of that reported by Warner et al. (2025) on
8×H100 SXM GPUs. Experiments with 8 ×A100 SXM showed only marginal decreases, sug-
gesting data loading bottlenecks rather than GPU compute limitations. We traced this with the
ModernBERT maintainers via a GitHub issue, e where we learned that the reported speeds relied
egithub.com/AnswerDotAI/ModernBERT/issues/236
22 Natural Language Processing
on a pre-tokenized dataset, an implementation detail not documented in the paper or codebase;
the maintainers have since pointed to data-preparation documentation in their follow-up Ettin
repository (Weller et al. 2026). Implementing pre-tokenization resolved the throughput issue.
Implementation decisions that seem obvious to a model’s developers must be stated explicitly if
others are to reproduce their results; we document ours in Section 3.
7. Limitations
We acknowledge limitations constraining this work’s scope and indicating areas for future research.
7.1 Computational Resource Constraints
Model scale.We pretrained only base-sized models (149M parameters) due to budget limita-
tions. ModernBERT includes a large variant (395M parameters). Scaling experiments with the
ModernBERT-large configuration could yield stronger performance, particularly on tasks requiring
greater capacity (complex reasoning, low-resource specialized domains).
Corpus size and training duration.Our pretraining corpus (86.58B tokens) is modest compared
to large-scale corpora like FineWeb (15T tokens) (Penedo et al. 2024). TabiBERT was pretrained
for 1T tokens versus ModernBERT (2T) and mmBERT (3T). Limited Turkish text availability
necessitated 11.55 epochs over the same data, raising potential overfitting concerns. However, we
hypothesize that base model capacity (149M parameters) substantially mitigates memorization
risks; that is, the model likely lacks sufficient capacity to memorize the corpus. Larger, more diverse
Turkish corpora would directly address this limitation.
7.2 Evaluation Constraints
Statistical significance.Every score in this paper is a single fine-tuning run at the configuration
selected on the validation split (Section 5.2, seed 25), for each of the 27 datasets and five models.
A full multi-seed repetition was beyond our budget, since the published grid already amounts to
on the order of 2,000 fine-tuning runs; we did, however, rerun the closest-margin categories with
five seeds (Appendix A.3): the NLI lead holds at +0.11 and the token-classification deficit narrows
to −0.06, neither of which is significant against the seed spread, and the TabiBERT-only reruns
move the STS, information retrieval and academic understanding scores by at most 0.36. Margins
below 1 on the 0–100 scale, which is where all differences on the four short-text categories and
three of TabiBERT’s five leads fall, should therefore be read as indicative rather than established;
the question answering and code retrieval gains (9.55 F1 and 2.41 NDCG@10) are an order of
magnitude larger than those margins.
Long-context evaluation gap.TabiBERT’s 8,192-token window is sixteen times that of the earlier
Turkish models, yet TabiBench gives limited opportunity to exercise it: most inputs are sentences
or paragraphs (text and token classification, NLI, STS) or query-document pairs (retrieval). The
two datasets in which a material share of inputs exceeds 512 tokens are TQuAD (11–14 per cent
of examples per split under the Turkish tokenizers) and Thesis-Abstract-Classification-11K (95th
percentile 1,152 tokens); the context-length ablation in Appendix A.2 isolates the effect on the
former. Document-level QA, multi-document reasoning and legislative analysis are absent from
Turkish evaluation infrastructure, a gap of the field rather than of this work, but one that constrains
validation of the long-context capability.
TabiBERT: A Large-Scale ModernBERTFoundation Model and A Unified Benchmark for Turkish23
7.3 Translation-Based Datasets: Rationale and Limitations
Fifteen of the twenty-seven TabiBench datasets are machine-translated and two are human-
translated (Table 3 gives the translating agent and source of each): six were translated by us
using GPT-4.1 (the four code retrieval and two academic understanding datasets); four information
retrieval datasets by the TR-MTEB group usingaya-expanse-8b (Baysan and Güngör 2025) and
MsMarco-TR by prior work (Kesgin et al. 2023); and SNLI-TR, MultiNLI-TR (Budur et al. 2020),
STSb-TR (Beken Fikri et al. 2021), and SICK-TR (Dehghan and Amasyali 2025) by their original
authors; XQuAD (Artetxe et al. 2020) was translated professionally as part of its original construc-
tion, and BiText combines professionally translated WMT16 news text with the SETIMES2 parallel
corpus (Bojar et al. 2016). The translation systems behind these resources differ considerably:
NLI-TR was produced with Amazon Translate and validated by bilingual experts (Budur et al.
2020), STSb-TR and SICK-TR with the Google Cloud Translation API, the former checked by
BLEU against reference translations and the latter unverified (Beken Fikri et al. 2021; Dehghan
and Amasyali 2025), the four TR-MTEB sets with the 8B-parameter aya-expanse model (Baysan
and Güngör 2025), and MsMarco-TR with a system that is not documented; for our own six
datasets we used GPT-4.1, a frontier general-purpose model substantially larger and more recent
than any of these, and we validated the result as described below. We opted to rely on translations
from a pragmatic stance. High-quality Turkish datasets for retrieval and specialized domains are
scarce or non-existent, yet these tasks are critical for comprehensive encoder evaluation. For code
retrieval, only natural language components are translated while code remains unchanged. To
substantiate the quality of the six translations we produced ourselves rather than assert it, we ran a
native-speaker adequacy audit. Three native Turkish speakers, graduate and undergraduate students
of computer engineering at Bo˘gaziçi University who are not authors of this paper, independently
rated a stratified random sample of 120 test-split items (20 per dataset) against the English source
on a three-level scale (meaning preserved; meaning preserved but flawed; meaning changed) and
flagged whether the gold label or gold document still held for the Turkish version; the protocol is
given in Appendix A.5. By majority vote, the translation preserved the meaning of the source in
108 of the 120 items (90.0 per cent; 95 per cent Wilson interval 83.3–94.2) and the gold label or
gold document still held for 119 (99.2 per cent; 95.4–99.9): 92 items (76.7 per cent) were rated
fully adequate, 16 (13.3 per cent) preserved meaning with lesser flaws such as awkward phrasing
or a non-standard term, and 12 (10.0 per cent; 5.8–16.7) contained a meaning-changing error,
between one and three per dataset (three-way splits, three items, were resolved to the more severe
label). Agreement on whether meaning was preserved was moderate (Fleiss’ κ=0.51 ; 0.24 on
the three-level scale, where one rater applied a markedly more lenient threshold for lesser flaws).
The meaning-changing errors were mostly omitted titles or sections in long inputs, altered numeric
masks in PubMedRCT-10K-TR and domain-terminology slips, and they concentrate in outputs of
unusual length: a separately sampled stratum of 17 length-ratio outliers had a 65 per cent error rate,
so a simple length-ratio check identifies most gross failures. We report these figures rather than
re-translate the flagged items, so that the released datasets remain the ones evaluated here, and we
release the audit scripts and anonymized ratings with the benchmark.
Machine-translated content may fail to capture linguistic nuance, idiomatic expressions or
cultural context. However, in our case, all of the content is technical or formal in nature, minimizing
such risks. Moreover, omitting such task categories with the aim of avoiding machine-translated
content would severely limit the evaluation scope.
8. Conclusions and Future Work
This work introduces two resources for Turkish NLP: TabiBERT, a ModernBERT-based encoder
demonstrating that recent architectural advances transfer effectively to Turkish; and TabiBench, a
standardized evaluation benchmark addressing reproducibility challenges in Turkish NLP research.
24 Natural Language Processing
8.1 Key Contributions
TabiBERT.We developed a Turkish encoder on the ModernBERT architecture, pretrained for 1T
tokens on an 86.58B-token corpus of web, scientific, parliamentary and academic text supplemented
with bilingual, code and mathematical data, with a Turkish-specialized tokenizer. TabiBERT
achieves an average of 77.28 on TabiBench, leading the Turkish models in five of eight task
categories and BERTurk in six of eight, with its largest advantages on question answering (+9.55
F1) and code retrieval (+2.41 NDCG@10). It offers an 8,192-token context, sixteen times that of
earlier Turkish models, with 51 per cent fewer parameters than mmBERT (149M against 307M)
and, because mmBERT needs 41 per cent more tokens for Turkish, about 41 per cent longer
effective Turkish context; ModernBERT-based encoders also offer faster inference at long context
lengths (Warner et al. 2025). While mmBERT scores higher (78.98), this gap of 1.70 reflects 2.1 ×
parameters and 3×training tokens, not architectural differences.
TabiBench.We developed a standardized benchmark of 27 curated datasets across eight task
categories with fixed splits, task-appropriate metrics and a fair comparison protocol under which
every model is fine-tuned identically, and we release it as an open resource and as a template for
similar efforts in other lower-resource languages.
Methodological insights.Documenting the pretraining infrastructure challenges we encoun-
tered highlights the importance of reporting implementation details for reproducibility. Adopting
tokenization-agnostic metrics (word-level evaluation for token classification) addresses a compara-
bility issue relevant across multilingual NLP.
8.2 Broader Implications
Beyond Turkish, the methodology (systematic dataset quality review, language-specific optimiza-
tion and transparent reporting of constraints) generalizes to other languages that lack standardized
evaluation frameworks, and the results support targeted, resource-efficient development as a com-
plement to large-scale multilingual approaches, the choice between them depending on application
requirements rather than on universal superiority.
8.3 Future Directions
The most significant gap is the absence of Turkish datasets that evaluate long-context understanding
(long and multi-document question answering, legislative analysis); developing them would allow
the 8,192-token context to be assessed directly. Comprehensive multi-seed significance testing
and robustness analysis under input perturbations (typos, morphological variation, domain shift)
would formalize the comparisons reported here. Pretraining a larger variant (ModernBERT-large,
395M parameters) and training beyond 1T tokens on expanded corpora would show whether
the advantages scale with capacity and data. Continued training from TabiBERT optimized for
retrieval could provide Turkish NLP with a specialized embedding model potentially superior
to multilingual alternatives, and continued pretraining for specialized domains (legal, medical,
scientific) could reveal domain-specific benefits. A controlled comparison of Turkish-specific
and multilingual ModernBERT variants at matched compute and corpus quality would quantify
the benefits of monolingual specialization against cross-lingual transfer. Finally, TabiBench can
expand to further task types, such as mathematical understanding, and be maintained as a living
benchmark with versioned releases.
TabiBERT and TabiBench contribute to Turkish NLP by providing both an efficient, modern
encoder model and a standardized evaluation benchmark. These resources address infrastructure
gaps that have hindered progress, and we believe they will facilitate more rigorous and reproducible
research in Turkish language technology.
