A.2 Context-Length Ablation on Question Answering
Among all benchmark tasks, question answering shows the largest gap between TabiBERT and the next-best Turkish model:
on TQuAD specifically, TabiBERT scores 72.34 against BERTurk’s 63.30, a difference of 9.04 F1. Since TabiBERT and
mmBERT are the only models fine-tuned on TQuAD with a 1,024-token cap rather than 512 (Section 5.2), part of this gap
could simply reflect their access to a longer context rather than a genuine modeling advantage. This section decomposes the
gap into a context-length component and a model component.
We first measure how much of the input is actually affected by a 512-token cap. Since all models were fine-tuned on
XQuAD with the same 384-token cap (Section 5) and only 1.35 per cent of its examples exceed 512 tokens, XQuAD offers no
context-length differential between models and is therefore excluded from this analysis. We instead focus on TQuAD, where
TabiBERT and mmBERT were fine-tuned with the longer cap and thus could plausibly benefit from it. For each of the five
models’ tokenizers, we compute the fraction of TQuAD examples whose question and context together, exactly as fed to the
model when only the context is truncated (the question is always kept intact), exceed 512 tokens. Table 14 reports these shares
per split. 10.83–13.98 per cent of examples exceed 512 tokens for the four Turkish-oriented tokenizers, with mmBERT’s
multilingual tokenizer pushing 14.96–25.35 per cent of examples over the limit due to higher subword fertility on Turkish text.
We therefore isolate the context-length effect on TQuAD, where it is material. We fine-tune TabiBERT and mmBERT
again on TQuAD with the maximum context length capped at512 tokens, the limit supported by BERTurk, TurkishBERTweet,
andYTU-Cosmos-BERT, whose published scores are already obtained under this cap and require no new run. For this ablation,
we reuse each model’s original best hyperparameter configuration selected during the main hyperparameter search (Section 5)
rather than repeating hyperparameter tuning at the new context length, since running a full second search for this ablation alone
was not computationally feasible. Table 15 reports the resulting scores alongside the originally published, uncapped ones.
Capping the context at 512 tokens lowers TabiBERT’s TQuAD F1 from 72.34 to 70.31 (−2.03 F1) and mmBERT’s
from 71.55 to 69.26 (−2.29 F1), confirming that some of both models’ advantage does come from their longer supported
context. Crucially, however, the512-capped TabiBERT still scores 70.31, well above BERTurk’s 63.30. Decomposing the
original 9.04 F1 TQuAD gap between TabiBERT and BERTurk accordingly, about 2 F1 ( 72.34→70.31 ) are attributable to
context length, and the remaining roughly 7 F1 (70.31→63.30 ) reflect a genuine modeling advantage independent of context.
The same pattern holds for mmBERT ( 512-capped score 69.26, still 5.96 F1 above BERTurk), confirming that the higher
performance of TabiBERT and mmBERT on question answering is only partially explained by context length; most of the
gap is a model effect.
This context effect is specific to long-passage datasets such as TQuAD and does not arise on XQuAD, whose 384-token
cap already accommodates essentially all examples for every model.
