whereNdenotes the maximum number of retrievals allowed.
If accuracy alone is considered, NR does not penalize overconfidence, as increasing eNR alone does not affect
the NR accuracy which always remains aNR. In fact, overconfidence can even be implicitly rewarded in
NR, as higher eNR may result in increased aNR due to lucky guesses. Conversely, AR is highly sensitive to
overconfidence: large values of eARi can substantially decrease AR accuracy, since the weighting coefficients
fora ARi decrease exponentially asiincreases.
To illustrate this, consider a simplified case where aNR =a , aARi =a for all i≥0 , and eNR =e , eARi =e for
all i≥0 . Let N→ ∞, then the accuracy of NR is simply a, while the accuracy of AR becomes the sum of a
geometric series:
