glass. Two light bulbs, 40 Watts each, lit the cell. The toilet bowl sunk into the floor was located 40
centimetres from the nearest bed. It was cleaned with cold water without detergent. Prisoners were
given three litres of water per day, it was not allowed to boil it. There was not enough hot water for
all of them and they took turns skipping the shower. There was no calendar or clock in the cell. Nor
was there a radio, television, books or newspapers. The applicant did not speak the Uzbek language
and could not communicate with his cellmates. Prisoners were taken outdoors in groups of thirty.
The yard was small, six by four metres, so they huddled together. Their report corroborated the
applicant’s description of his conditions of detention. In May 2012 the applicant developed pulmonary
tuberculosis. Upon his discharge, he took advantage of the absence of police escort and absconded.
