A Appendix
A.1 System Architecture and Implementation
The entire system was developed in Python 3.13
and structured using modular components for inde-
pendent RAG pipelines, a Neo4j-based Knowledge
Graph (KG), and an orchestration layer. Each RAG
module corresponds to a distinct legal domain—
Constitution and Legal Acts,Past Cases, andIn-
dian Penal Code (IPC)—and exposes a uniform
API interface for document ingestion, retrieval,
and generation. All vector embeddings are gener-
ated using the all-MiniLM-L6-v2 model from the
SentenceTransformers library, producing 384-
dimensional representations optimized for cosine
similarity. Embedding storage and search are
implemented through ChromaDB using an HNSW
(Hierarchical Navigable Small World) index for
fast approximate nearest-neighbor retrieval. The
Neo4j graph is accessed using the py2neo connec-
tor through the Bolt protocol, while the orchestra-
tion logic is built atop LangChain and asyncio for
asynchronous API calls.
A.2 Prompt Templates and Retrieval Flow
Each RAG module follows a two-stage pipeline:
(1) evidence retrieval and (2) grounded generation
via Gemini 2.5 Flash. Prompts are dynamically
constructed using retrieved text chunks and meta-
data such as statute titles, section numbers, and
case identifiers. A typical retrieval prompt is for-
matted as:
“Using only the retrieved legal excerpts
below, answer the question concisely and
cite relevant Articles or IPC Sections.
Avoid speculation. ”
To reduce hallucination, each prompt includes ex-
plicit grounding constraints and reference indica-
tors (e.g., “Article 14” or “Section 302”). Tem-
perature is fixed at 0.1 and the maximum context
window is set to 8192 tokens for deterministic be-
havior.
A.3 Knowledge Graph Schema
The Neo4j-based KG currently integrates over
2586 nodes and 5056 relationships across three do-
mains. Entity types include Case, Judge, Article,
IPCSection, Offense, and Punishment. Rela-
tionships comprise:
•HEARD_IN— connects cases to courts,
•DECIDED_BY— links cases to judges,
•GOVERNED_BY — connects cases to relevant
IPC sections,
•REFERS_TO — captures cross-citations be-
tween cases or statutes,
•APPLIES_TO — associates IPC sections with
offenses.
A.4 Dataset and Preprocessing Pipeline
The system utilizes four primary datasets:
1. The Constitution of India(of India, 2024):
parsed from PDF using PyMuPDF, segmented
by article headers.
2. Central Legal Acts Dataset(Data, 2021):
imported from structured CSVs with meta-
data fields (act number , enactment year , entity
name).
3. Indian Penal Code Dataset(Akshit2605,
2023): 3,481 IPC records from Kaggle, nor-
malized into text templates of the form “Sec-
tion X: Offense – Punishment”.
4. Supreme Court Case Records(Zenodo,
2020): 2,900+ case documents tokenized by
section headers (Facts,Issues,Held) using
regex-based chunking.
Markdown syntax, non-ASCII characters, and typo-
graphic inconsistencies were removed during pre-
processing. Documents were chunked into 1,000-
character segments with a 200-character overlap to
preserve contextual continuity.
A.5 Evaluation Metrics and Scripts
Retrieval performance is measured using Preci-
sion@k, Recall@k, and F1@k computed from em-
bedding cosine similarities. Generation quality is
assessed using ROUGE-L and Token Overlap (Lin,
2004). Each evaluation script supports batch pro-
cessing of test queries across the three RAG in-
stances. Evaluation parameters and configurations
are version-controlled to ensure consistent runs.
