paradigms rely heavily on Chain-of-Thought (CoT)
prompting (Wei et al., 2022), where the model
“thinks” in the same natural language (NL) space
used for user interaction. We posit that this uncon-
strained usage of NL for internal reasoning intro-
duces a fundamental inefficiency: a misalignment
between thecommunicativeutility of language and
thecomputationalrequirements of reasoning.
Natural language is evolutionarily optimized for
communication. It prioritizes redundancy, polite-
ness, and ambiguity management to facilitate hu-
man understanding. In contrast, not only reasoning
tasks (e.g., mathematics, coding), but also almost
all question answering tasks that require critical
thinking are governed by strict structures, determin-
istic rules, and high information density. As illus-
trated in Table 1, when LLMs use conversational
NL to process structured problems, they expend
1
arXiv:2512.15358v1  [cs.CL]  17 Dec 2025
significant computational resources on syntactic
sugar that contributes little to the actual state tran-
sition of the reasoning process. This observation
suggests that while NL is essential for the interface
(answering), it is a suboptimal representation for
the inference process (reasoning) in domains that
admit formal structure.
Motivated by this distinction, we argue that the
ideal inference framework should decouple the
representation of reasoning from the representa-
tion of the final response. To this end, we intro-
duce a novel information-theoretic framework and
proposeDenser: Dual-density inference. Unlike
generic acceleration methods, Denser is explicitly
designed to exploit the categorization and structural
patterns inherent in reasoning tasks. The frame-
work operates via three components: (1) a Query
Analysis Module that identifies the structural re-
quirements of the input; (2) a High-density Reason-
ing Module where the model employs telegraphic,
symbol-rich language to perform intermediate de-
ductions; and (3) a Low-density Answering Mod-
ule that expands the compressed reasoning path
into a user-friendly, natural language explanation.
We evaluate Denser across a comprehensive
suite of benchmarks spanning math, logic, and cod-
ing, domains where reasoning patterns are most
structured and thus most amenable to density opti-
mization. Our empirical results demonstrate that by
aligning the information density of the reasoning
chain with the structural nature of the task, Denser
reduces token consumption by up to 62% while
preserving, and in some cases enhancing, accuracy
compared to standard CoT. This confirms that for
complex, multi-step reasoning problems, the ver-
bosity of natural language is not merely a cost issue,
but a representational burden that can be optimized
away. Our contributions are as follows:
• We propose a theoretical perspective that distin-
guishes thecomputationalfunction of language
(internal reasoning) from itscommunicativefunc-
tion (external answering), identifying a represen-
tational mismatch in current CoT approaches for
structured tasks.
• We develop Denser, a dual-density inference sys-
tem that dynamically modulates language density,
employing compressed symbolic representations
for intermediate steps and natural language for
final outputs.
• We provide extensive empirical evidence on
reasoning-intensive benchmarks, demonstrating
that Denser significantly improves computational
efficiency without sacrificing performance, vali-
dating the efficacy of representational alignment
in structured problem solving.
2 Related Work
Reasoning Strategies.Chain-of-Thought (CoT)
prompting (Wei et al., 2022; Kojima et al., 2022)
has established itself as the dominant approach for
eliciting step-by-step reasoning from LLMs. This
technique has been extended in various directions:
Self-consistency (Wang et al., 2022) aggregates
solutions from multiple reasoning paths, Tree-of-
Thought (Yao et al., 2023) explores multiple reason-
ing trajectories, Least-to-Most (Zhou et al., 2022)
breaks complex problems into simpler subprob-
lems, and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (Lewis
et al., 2020; Shi et al., 2023) incorporates external
knowledge. While these approaches improve rea-
soning accuracy, they typically increase rather than
decrease token consumption, often generating even
more verbose explanations.
Efficient Reasoning.Efficiency optimization
in language models has primarily focused on
architectural improvements (Dao et al., 2022;
Shazeer, 2019) or inference acceleration techniques
(Leviathan et al., 2023; Cai et al., 2024). From a
theoretical perspective, works on computational
complexity in transformers (Poli et al., 2023; Lu
et al., 2022) analyze the computational costs of dif-
ferent operations but rarely address the information-
theoretic aspects of generated content. Other ap-
proaches include distillation (Chen et al., 2025;
Liao et al., 2025) and quantization (Jin et al., 2024),
which compress models rather than optimizing rea-
soning strategies. Our work introduces a novel
perspective by identifying the different information
density between reasoning and answering. Besides,
we provide a detailed theoretical discussion on the
scalability of symbolic versus natural language rep-
resentations, and the generalization scope of our
method, in Appendix A.
3 Empirical Analysis of Information
Density in LLM Reasoning
While we identify the theoretical distinction be-
tween computational and communicative functions
of language in LLM reasoning, this section pro-
vides empirical evidence for this distinction and
reveals specific patterns that our method exploits.
We analyze how current reasoning approaches dis-
