105 ÷ 3 = 35, so 3 is a factor. Then 35 ÷ 5 = 7, so 5 is a factor. And 7 is prime. Therefore, 210 = 2 × 3 × 5 × 7. Now
I need to use Euler’s totient function. For any integer n with prime factorization n=p a11 ×p a22 ×. . .×pakk , we have
ϕ(n) =n×(1− 1p1 )×(1− 1p2 )×. . .×(1− 1pk ). So ϕ(210) = 210×(1− 12 )×(1− 13 )×(1− 15 )×(1− 17 ) =
210× 12 × 23 × 45 × 67 = 210× 1×2×4×62×3×5×7 = 210× 48210 = 48. This gives us the count for one complete period of 210. Sincewe want numbers less than 1000, I need to consider how many complete periods of 210 fit into 1000, and handle the remainder
separately. We have 1000 ÷ 210 = 4 remainder 160. So there are 4 complete periods, each contributing 48 numbers. For the
remainder (numbers from 841 to 999), I need to count those relatively prime to 210.
Denser (HD reasoning):210 = 2·3·5·7ϕ(210) = 210·(1− 12 )(1− 13 )(1− 15 )(1− 17 ) = 210·1·2·4·62·3·5·7 = 481000 = 4·210 + 160Complete periods:4·48 = 192
