2.2.1 Problem Formulation: Coordinate-Based Discourse Representation
To enable this coordinate-based operation (Figure 2(a)), we first segment the input documentD into a
sequence of atomic building blocks termedElementary Discourse Units (EDUs). Formally, the document
is represented as a sequenceU={e 1,e 2, . . . ,eN}, where each unite i constitutes a triplet:
ei = (ti,pos i,id i) (1)
Here, ti represents the textual content (typically a coherent clause or sentence), posi denotes its physical
grounding (character offsets), and crucially, idi acts as a unique sequential index. This index establishes
aCoordinate System, allowing the system to reference content by pointers. The core objective is to
learn a decomposition function f:E → Tthat maps the linear EDU sequence to a hierarchical tree T
(Figure 2(c)). Each noden j ∈ Tacts as a compressed semantic capsule defined as:
nj =
