The mask𝑚𝑡 is the crucial component that enforces the action-
observation separation. During trajectory construction, our frame-
work distinguishes between tokens generated by the agent and
tokens returned by the environment. The loss is computed only on
agent-generated tokens by applying a binary loss mask𝑚𝑡 :
• Agent’s Actions (𝑚𝑡 = 1): tokens are generated by the agent
as part of its decision-making process. This includes the
Planner’s reasoning and <task> generation, as well as the
Executor’s reasoning and<search>generation.
• Environmental Observations (𝑚𝑡 = 0): tokens represent in-
formation from the environment. For the Planner, the out-
put in the <result> . . . </result> block is an observa-
tion. For the Executor, the search result in the <documents>
. . . </documents>block is also an observation.
3.3.4 Composite Reward Design.To guide the model towards mas-
tering the complex behaviors required by our framework, we de-
signed a composite reward function𝑅(G)that simultaneously en-
courages three distinct objectives: answer correctness, structural
compliance, and information refinement quality, where total reward
for a complete trajectory groupGis a weighted sum of them.
1. Answer Correctness (𝑅ans).The primary objective is to produce
a factually correct answer. This reward is based on the F1 score
between the final answer generated by the Planner𝐴pred, and the
set of ground-truth answers𝐺. We linearly transform this score to
a range of[−3,3]to provide a strong, scaled reward signal.
𝑆ans =max𝑔∈𝐺 F1(𝐴pred,𝑔)(4)
𝑅ans(G)=6·𝑆 ans−3(5)
2. Format Compliance (𝑅format).To ensure the agent adheres to the
Plan-then-Execute protocol, we reward the generation of correctly
formatted XML-style tags. This component is composed of two
parts, one for the Planner and one for the Executor.
𝑅format(G)=𝐼 𝑃(G)+𝐼 𝐸(G)(6)
Here,𝐼𝑃(G) is an indicator function that equals 1 if the Planner’s
final output in the group is structurally correct (i.e. proper use
of <task> or <answer>). Similarly,𝐼𝐸(G) is an indicator function
that equals 1 if all of the Executor’s outputs within the group are
correctly formatted (i.e. proper use of<search>or<result>)
3. Refinement Quality (𝑅refine).A crucial capability of the Execu-
tor is to distill critical information from noisy search results. To
incentivize not only the act of summarization but also its factual
accuracy, the refinement reward is granted only if two conditions
are met: a non-empty summary is produced, and this summary
contains the ground-truth answer.
Let𝑠refine(𝜏𝑒) be the set of all string contents extracted from
<refine> tags across all Executor trajectories within a group G.
We first concatenate these contents into a single, unified string,
