III. METHODOLOGY
A. Integration of Knowledge Graphs with Language Models
KG-BERT is used to enrich LLMs (Claude, Mistral IA,
GPT-4) by injecting structured knowledge from KGs into
their embeddings, enhancing entity-level reasoning and factual
precision, especially for complex tasks.
1) Preparation of KGs:KGs such as Wikidata, Freebase, or
domain-specific graphs are preprocessed into triples (subject,
relation, object), encoded into vectors via KG-BERT. Entities
are aligned with LLM tokens to ensure semantic coherence.
2) Enrichment of Embeddings:Entity and relation vectors
from KGs are injected into intermediate layers by merging
with token embeddings. A gating mechanism balances KG
input with contextual signals from the model.
3) Fusion of Information:Factual data is integrated through
attention-based mechanisms, refining the model’s interpreta-
tion of complex entity relations and mitigating hallucinations
from purely text-based pretraining.
Figure 1 illustrates how structured knowledge from knowl-
edge graphs is integrated into language models for better
performance and precision in tasks.
Fig. 1. Knowledge Graph Integration Process in Language Models via KG-
BERT.
B. Specific Approach for Each Model
The integration of KG-BERT is tailored to the architectural
specifics of each language model:
1) Claude:An additional KG-dedicated attention layer is
added to Claude, preserving efficiency while improving factual
management and logical inference.
2) Mistral IA:In Mistral IA, KG-BERT is modularized
and connected via cross-layers, enabling efficient integration
with minimal overhead. A lightweight aggregation strategy
maintains responsiveness in constrained environments.
3) GPT-4:For GPT-4, KG-BERT vectors are integrated
using a dedicated attention head and merged before decoding,
allowing deep reasoning and factual correction at scale.
C. Experimentation and Testing
We benchmarked the enriched models against their baselines
to assess accuracy, speed, and robustness.
1) Datasets:Standardized benchmarks such as Bench-
marks included GLUE, SQuAD, and Natural Questions for
QA; CommonGen and WikiText for generation. MedQA and
legal corpora tested domain-specific understanding.
2) Experimental Setups:Training was performed on GPU
clusters. Hyperparameters were adjusted per model to optimize
convergence and performance.
3) Evaluation Metrics:QA tasks used accuracy, recall, and
F1-score. Generation was evaluated via BLEU, ROUGE, and
perplexity. Factual accuracy was assessed by comparing model
outputs against verified KG content.
IV. EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS
A. Datasets
We evaluated KG-BERT-enhanced LLMs on standard and
specialized benchmarks to assess generalization and domain-
specific reasoning.
1) GLUE (General Language Understanding Evalua-
tion) [10]:General-purpose NLP benchmark covering clas-
sification, NER, and sentiment tasks.
2) SQuAD (Stanford Question Answering Dataset) [11]:
Contextual QA dataset evaluating precise answer extraction.
3) Natural Questions [12]:Real-world QA dataset from
Google queries, emphasizing open-domain complexity.
4) MedQA [13]:Focused on medical knowledge, testing
factual accuracy in a high-stakes domain.
5) LegalQA [14]:Assesses legal reasoning and compre-
hension through domain-specific QA.
B. Experimental Configurations
Extended models were trained on GPU clusters using opti-
mized configurations.
1) Hardware:All training used NVIDIA Tesla V100
GPUs, chosen for memory capacity and compute efficiency.
2) Software:Models were implemented in TensorFlow and
PyTorch. KG handling relied on RDFLib and DGL.
3) Hyperparameters:
•Learning rate: Tuned per model and dataset for stable
convergence.
•Batch size: 16 for GPT-4, 32 for Mistral IA, adjusted to
memory limits.
•Epochs: 3–10 based on task complexity and overfitting
risk.
C. Results
Performance gains were observed across QA and text gen-
eration tasks. Table II shows the performance of the models
on question-answering tasks, while Table III presents the
performance on text generation tasks.
