KBQA-R1: Reinforcing Large Language Models for Knowledge Base Question Answering
A. Extended Related Work
A.1. Knowledge Base Question Answering (KBQA).
Before the rise of LLMs, KBQA studies are commonly categorized into information-retrieval-based (IR-based) methods
(Sun et al., 2018; 2019; Zhang et al., 2022; He et al., 2021; Saxena et al., 2020) and semantic-parsing-based (SP-based)
methods (Ye et al., 2022; Shu et al., 2022; Gu & Su, 2022; Zhang et al., 2023a). With LLMs, three paradigms have emerged:
(i)end-to-end approachesthat directly generate logical forms via in-context learning or fine-tuning (Li et al., 2023; Nie
et al., 2024; Luo et al., 2024; Jiang et al., 2023); (ii)step-by-step (agentic) approachesthat interleave reasoning with graph
exploration and tool use (Gu et al., 2023b; Huang et al., 2024; Sun et al., 2023; Luo et al., 2023; Xiong et al., 2024; Chen
et al., 2024; Jiang et al., 2024); and (iii)search-augmented approachesthat leverage tree search algorithms such as Monte
Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) for systematic exploration (Luo et al., 2025c).
While MCTS-based methods like KBQA-o1 (Luo et al., 2025c) achieve strong performance through heuristic exploration,
they exhibit two key limitations.First, they incur significant computational overhead from multiple rollouts per
query and require separate policy and reward models during inference. Second, their reasoning traces are often
template-driven(e.g., “At this step, we should find the relation...”) rather than genuinely analytical—the model
announceswhataction to take without explainingwhybased on observations. In contrast, we train a single policy via
RL with outcome-based rewards, encouraging the model to developadaptive reasoningthat analyzes environmental
feedback and justifies action choices, while eliminating test-time search overhead.
A.2. LLMs, tool use, and agentic reasoning.
Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting improves reasoning by eliciting intermediate steps (Wei et al., 2022); ReAct (Yao
et al., 2022) interleaves “think” and “act” to ground reasoning in environment feedback; and heuristic search has been
applied to agent traces (e.g., MCTS-style selection in (Hao et al., 2023) and tree-structured deliberation in (Yao et al., 2023)).
Recent graph-augmented approaches such as Plan-on-Graph (Chen et al., 2024) incorporate self-correcting mechanisms with
dynamic memory for adaptive planning on knowledge graphs. While these methods expand the search space or stabilize
multi-step reasoning, free-form thoughts can overfit prompt templates and do not guarantee executability. We keep the
interleaved think-act design but require typed, schema-aware actions with validators and an executor, turning traces into
verifiable computations rather than narrative justifications.
A.3. Retrieval-augmented generation and search-as-a-tool.
Classical RAG pipelines retrieve text snippets and feed them to the model for generation (Lewis et al., 2020). Recent work
moves toward search-as-a-tool, prompting or training LLMs to issue search calls and iterate (Trivedi et al., 2022; Yao et al.,
2022; Schick et al., 2023; Li et al., 2025; Jin et al., 2025; Luo et al., 2025a). GraphRAG and graph-decoding approaches
(He et al., 2024; Ma et al., 2025; Mavromatis & Karypis, 2025; Li et al., 2024b;a; Luo et al., 2025a;b) further integrate
graph retrieval with LLM reasoning, enabling tighter coupling between structured knowledge and text-based evidence.
These approaches reduce hallucination but depend heavily on retrieval quality, local subgraph construction, or constrained
decoding at inference time. Our setting differs fundamentally by treating aknowledge graphas the environment: actions are
typed and executable against the KB schema, observations are structure-grounded entity sets rather than text passages, and
step-wise executability can be validated programmatically rather than inferred from unstructured documents.
B. Experimental Details
B.1. Datasets and Statistics
We conduct experiments on three widely-used KBQA benchmarks, each designed to evaluate different aspects of model
generalization and reasoning capabilities. All datasets are grounded on Freebase (Bollacker et al., 2008).GrailQA(Gu
et al., 2021) is a large-scale dataset specifically designed to evaluate KBQA models across three generalization levels:
i.i.d.,compositional, andzero-shot. It contains 64,331 questions in total, with 44,337 training questions, 13,231 validation
questions and 6,763 test questions. Following prior work (Luo et al., 2024; 2025c), we use the dev set for evaluation. The
compositional and zero-shot settings are particularly challenging, requiring models to handle unseen combinations of entities
and relations.WebQSP(Yih et al., 2016) is an enriched version of WebQuestions, providing semantic parses for 4,737
questions. The dataset is split into 3,098 training questions and 1,639 test questions.GraphQuestions(Su et al., 2016) tests
14
KBQA-R1: Reinforcing Large Language Models for Knowledge Base Question Answering
KBQA models on complex graph-structured reasoning. It contains 5,166 questions in total, with 2,508 for training and
2,658 for testing. The dataset challenges models to navigate multi-hop relationships.
B.2. Baselines (Detailed Descriptions)
We compare KBQA-R1 with bothfine-tune-basedandprompting-basedKBQA methods.
Fine-tune-based methods.These methods are trained on the full supervision of the corresponding benchmark and are
included as upper-bound references.
• RnG-KBQA(Ye et al., 2022): a retrieve-and-generate framework that first retrieves relevant KB evidence and then
generates executable logical forms.
• DecAF(Yu et al., 2022): a multi-granular retrieval and refinement approach that progressively improves retrieved
knowledge for robust KBQA.
• TIARA(Shu et al., 2022): a semantic parsing method that maps questions to structured queries through iterative
refinement.
• CoTKR(Wu et al., 2024): a Chain-of-Thought enhanced knowledge rewriting method that trains a knowledge rewriter
via SFT and DPO to convert KB triples into natural language reasoning text for improved QA.
• KBQA-o1(Luo et al., 2025c): an agentic KBQA approach based on Monte-Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) with policy
and reward models for heuristic search.
For GraphQuestions, following the setup in KBQA-o1 (Luo et al., 2025c), we additionally report results ofSPARQA(Sun
et al., 2020),BERT+Ranking(Gu et al., 2021), andArcaneQA(Gu & Su, 2022).
Prompting-based methods.These methods operate under limited or no task-specific gradient updates and are compared
under a similar low-annotation constraint.
• KB-BINDER(Li et al., 2023): an in-context learning method (GPT-3.5-turbo) that binds questions to KB entities and
relations.
• KB-Coder(Nie et al., 2024): a code-style in-context learning method (GPT-3.5-turbo) that generates executable logical
forms.
• ARG-KBQA(Tian et al., 2024): a prompting approach (GPT-3.5-turbo) that uses augmented reasoning graphs to
improve KBQA.
• Interactive-KBQA(Xiong et al., 2024): an interactive framework that decomposes complex questions into sub-
questions and iteratively queries the KB to gather evidence for answer generation.
• ToG (Think-on-Graph)(Sun et al., 2023): a beam search-based method that prompts LLMs to explore multiple
reasoning paths on the knowledge graph, selecting the most promising path at each step.
• PoG (Plan-on-Graph)(Chen et al., 2024): an advanced graph reasoning method that first generates a reasoning plan
and then executes it on the knowledge graph with self-correction capabilities.
• RoG(Luo et al., 2023): a reasoning-on-graphs framework that asks LLMs to reason over graph-derived paths and
evidence.
• GNN-RAG(Mavromatis & Karypis, 2025): a graph neural retrieval method that retrieves question-relevant graph
paths before LLM reasoning.
• SubgraphRAG(Li et al., 2024b): a graph-centric RAG method that retrieves compact subgraphs for LLM answer
prediction.
• DoG(Li et al., 2024a): a constrained graph-decoding method that encourages LLMs to generate well-formed chains on
knowledge graphs.
15
KBQA-R1: Reinforcing Large Language Models for Knowledge Base Question Answering
B.3. Training Hyperparameters and Infrastructure
B.3.1. GRPO CONFIGURATION
We adopt the GRPO algorithm (Shao et al., 2024) with the following hyperparameters: (1)Rollout sampling:n= 5 responses
per prompt with temperature τ= 1.0 and top-p= 1.0 ; (2)Clipping: asymmetric clip ratios ϵlow = 0.2, ϵhigh = 0.28
following DAPO (Yu et al., 2025); (3)KL regularization: KL loss coefficient β= 0.001; (4)Reward weights and gating: we
set λoutcome = 1.0and λformat = 0.1, and use RRCG confidence thresholds τhigh = 0.95and τlow = 0.3across all datasets;
(5)Batch configuration: train batch size 256, PPO mini-batch size 128, with dynamic micro-batching enabled.
B.3.2. INFRASTRUCTURE
Training is conducted on 8×NVIDIA A100-80GB GPUs with FSDP (Zhao et al., 2023) for model sharding. The Freebase
KB backend uses Virtuoso (Erling & Mikhailov, 2009) with ODBC connection pooling (pool size 48, query timeout 600s).
