2 Problem Setup
We study metric-fair learning on a domainX endowed with a similarity metric d:X × X →[0,1]. A
learning algorithm receives d and a set of i.i.d. samples from a distribution D over labeled examples
(x, y)∈ X × {±1}and outputs a classifier. To accommodate fairness, we focus on probabilistic
classifiers h:X →[0,1] and interpret h(x) as the probability of label +1 (so the probability of −1
equals1−h(x)). We refer to such probabilistic classifiers aspredictors.
We consider fairness as treating similar individuals similarly: two individuals that are close under d
should receive similar predictions. To this end, we consider learning score function f:X →R that
is Lipschitz in the following sense.
Definition 1(Lipschitz mapping).Let D be some metric on R. A mapping f:X →R satisfies the
(D, d)-Lipschitz property if for allx, x′ ∈ X,
D
␀
f(x), f(x′) ≤d(x, x ′).(1)
