MODEL ANSWER:
Search for potential reasoning traces: - Psoriasiform lesions
on the trunk with involvement of palms and soles. - Asymmetric
non-suppurative joint inflammation. - Bilateral ocular redness.
- Erosions on the glans penis. - History of risky sexual contact
20 days before. - These features suggest a sexually transmitted
infection with a reactive or disseminated phase. - Secondary
syphilis is known to present with a rash, mucocutaneous lesions,
and systemic symptoms. - Reiter’s syndrome is a reactive arthri-
tis that follows a genitourinary infection but does not typically
present with psoriasiform lesions or mucocutaneous erosions.
- Erythema multiforme is a hypersensitivity reaction and does
not typically involve the glans penis or joints. - HIV infection
may present with systemic symptoms but does not typically
include the described mucocutaneous and ocular features.
Reasoning process: 1. Gustavo presents with a combination
of skin lesions, joint inflammation, ocular redness, and
mucocutaneous erosions. 2. The psoriasiform rash on the trunk
and palms/soles is a key feature of secondary syphilis. 3. The
mucocutaneous erosion on the glans penis is also consistent
with secondary syphilis. 4. The bilateral ocular redness and
asymmetric joint inflammation are additional features of
secondary syphilis. 5. The history of a risky sexual contact
20 days before is consistent with the incubation period of
syphilis. 6. Reiter’s syndrome is a reactive arthritis that follows
a genitourinary infection but does not typically present with
psoriasiform lesions or mucocutaneous erosions. 7. Erythema
multiforme is a hypersensitivity reaction and does not typically
involve the glans penis or joints. 8. HIV infection may present
with systemic symptoms but does not typically include the
described mucocutaneous and ocular features.
Conclusion: 2
