with the Wikipedia corpus (Karpukhin et al., 2020).
While MusWikiDB contains fewer pages (0.14M vs
3.2M) and has a smaller vocabulary size (7.5M vs
21.5M), it consists exclusively of music-specialized
text information.
3.2. ArtistMus
Whilepreviousbenchmarkshavemainlyaddressed
general music understanding, none has focused
on music metadata, particularly the artist, which
is crucial in music listening contexts (Lee, 2010;
Doh et al., 2024). Therefore, we introduce Artist-
Mus, a multiple-choice benchmark dataset on mu-
sic artists, to evaluate the performance of LLMs
in artist-related QA using artist-specific data from
MusWikiDB.
Example Questions of ArtistMus
Metadata:
Artist: Robyn
Country: Sweden
Debut Year: 1989
Genre: Pop
Topic: Artistry
Factual Question:
""What vocal range does Robyn have?""
A. Alto
B. Soprano
C. Tenor
D. Baritone
Answer:B
Contextual Question:
""How did Robyn’s approach to music change
while writing her eighth studio album, Honey,
according to the text?""
A. Robyn explored music that was hypnotic
and lacked a traditional structure.
B. Robyn aimed to replicate the success of
her previous hits.
C. Robyn focused on creating more main-
stream pop hits.
D. Robyn returned to her R&B roots with a
focus on tidy pop songs.
Answer:A
3.2.1. Data Collection
We selected music artists from MusWikiDB_raw
whose infoboxes contained bothgenreandyearin-
formation. ThesectionsfromtheirWikipediapages
werethenrankedbyfrequency, andthetopfivetop-
ics—biography,career,discography,artistry, and
collaborations—were chosen to capture the broad-
est range of artist-related content. To balance ef-
ficiency and contextual richness, we retained text
passages with token lengths from 500 to 2000.
For genre normalization (Schreiber, 2015), all
genre labels were converted to lowercase, and
spaces, hyphens (-), and slashes (/) were removed.
We used the 48 root genres defined in (Schreiber,
2016) as our initial genre taxonomy. After filtering
to retain data corresponding to the 300 most fre-
quent genres, each was mapped to one of 20 final
genre labels.
To extract artists’ regional information, we
provided the first paragraph (abstract) of each
Wikipedia page to Llama 3.1 8B Instruct (Grattafiori
et al., 2024), prompting it to identify the country as-
sociated with each artist. The list of valid countries
was obtained from thepycountry2 library.
3.2.2. Data Selection
We selected a diverse set of 500 artists based on
theirtopic,genre,debut year, andcountry. Among
these factors,countrywas given the highest pri-
ority, with a focus on including artists from under-
represented regions. Specifically, we applied an
inverse frequency strategy on MusWikiDB, prioritiz-
ing countries with lower representation to ensure
geographical balance. This approach enabled the
inclusion ofartistsfromminor regionssuch asMau-
ritius, Senegal, and Azerbaijan.
Figure 2 illustrates the regional distribution of the
artists included in ArtistMus.3 Unlike many existing
datasetsthatareheavilybiasedtowardtheU.S.and
Europe, ArtistMus deliberately incorporates artists
from 163 countries (or regions), ensuring broad
global diversity and representation. Approximately
43% of the artists originate from outside the U.S.
and Europe, indicating balanced representation
beyond Western and European cultural centers.
To further enhance diversity and balance, we re-
placed dominant genres and topics with less com-
mon ones. Instead of focusing on widely repre-
sented genres such asPoporRock, we prioritized
underrepresentedgenressuchasWorldMusicand
Latin. Similarly, rather than overrepresented top-
ics likeBiography, we emphasized less frequent
onessuchasArtistry. Inaddition,debutyearswere
selected to achieve a more uniform temporal dis-
tribution. The distribution of debut years for the
artists in ArtistMus is shown in Figure 3.
For each artist, we generated one factual and
one contextual question to evaluate the LLM’s
factual accuracy and contextual reasoning ability.
To construct these questions, we provided GPT-
4o (Achiam et al., 2023) with the corresponding
section text. Factual questions target verifiable de-
tails such as dates, names, and events, whereas
contextual questions require reasoning or synthe-
sis across multiple pieces of information within the
passage.
3.2.3. Data Validation
To ensure benchmark quality, we applied a two-
stage validation process. We validate the gener-
