Appendix G. Implementation Details of S-expression Core Cali-
bration
To ensure robustness against syntactic errors and semantic hallucina-
tions in LLM-generated S-expressions, we implemented a rigorous calibra-
tion pipeline. This pipeline consists of ﬁve key operations: (1) Parenthesis
Matching, (2) Entity Identiﬁcation & Replacement, (3) Relation Identiﬁca-
tion & Replacement, (4) Type Constraint Enforcement, and (5) Expression
Variant Generation.
Appendix G.1. Recursive Syntactic Reconstruction Rules
LLMs often generate S-expressions with unbalanced parentheses or in-
correct nesting structures. To address this, we employ a Recursive Re-
construction strategy that rebuilds the syntax tree from ﬂat tokens. The
procedure is as follows:
1. Tokenization & Cleaning: The input S-expression is ﬁrst stripped
of all existing parentheses to eliminate structural noise and split into a
sequence of atomic tokens.
2. Arity-based Parsing: A recursive parser processes the token stream.
For each function token encountered, the parser recursively consumes
a speciﬁc number of subsequent arguments based on the function’s
deﬁnition:
• IS_TRUE: Consumes 3 arguments (Subject, Predicate, Object).
• JOIN: Consumes 2 arguments (Relation/Entity, Entity).
• R: Consumes 1 argument (Relation).
• AND: Consumes a variable number of arguments until the end
of the scope.
3. Reconstruction: The parser wraps the consumed arguments in cor-
rectly balanced parentheses (e.g., (Function Arg1 Arg2) ) before re-
turning the substructure. This method guarantees that the output
S-expression is syntactically valid regardless of the original depth of
nesting or missing brackets.
51
Appendix G.2. Execution-guided Semantic Alignment Algorithm
After syntactic correction, the expression contains surface names (e.g.,
“sorbitol_dehydrogenase”) that need to be grounded to Knowledge Graph
(KG) identiﬁers. We employ an execution-guided search algorithm:
1. Candidate Retrieval: For each surface form token (entity or rela-
tion), we retrieve the top- k candidates using a dense retriever based on
semantic similarity.
2. Constraint Injection: We detect implicit constraints such as instance_of
and strictly map them to the Wikidata property P31 (“instance of”) to
ﬁlter entity candidates by type.
3. Cartesian Product Generation: We generate a set of candidate S-
expressions by computing the Cartesian product of all possible entity
and relation candidates.
4. Structural Variation: To handle potential directionality errors in
relations, we automatically generate variants with reversed relations
(e.g., swapping Subject and Object).
5. Execution Veriﬁcation: Each candidate variant is translated into
SPARQL and executed against the KG. The search terminates at the
ﬁrst variant that returns a non-empty result , which is then selected
as the ﬁnal calibrated core.
Appendix G.3. Qualitative Case Study
Table G.6 demonstrates the robustness of the calibration module. The
LLM initially predicted an expression with incorrect relation surface forms
(e.g., involved_in) and unlinked entities. The calibration module success-
fully corrected the syntax, mapped ambiguous relations to speciﬁc properties
(e.g., mapping involved_in to P703 based on the context of the entity), and
enforced type constraints.
Appendix H. Robustness Analysis on Out-of-Distribution Data
To evaluate the ’retraining-free adaptation’ capability of the self-evolving
mechanism, we conducted additional ablation studies on a constructed Out-
of-Distribution (OOD) test set. The construction process was designed to
simulate real-world scenarios where user queries often deviate from train-
ing patterns. Speciﬁcally, we utilized the 3,971 samples from the SPICE
dataset (used in the main experiment) as the original knowledge source.
