C. Semantic Chunking with Structural Fidelity
When a sheet is labeledMulti-Header, SQuARE builds and
uses only the semantic indexI c (Stage A, line 4). We segment
at header boundaries so that each blockB i is a coherent region
rather than a fixed window, and we attach explicit metadata
meta(Bi) = (Hi, Yi, Ui),
whereH i is the ordered header path (outer to inner labels),
Yi are the time labels present in the block, andU i records the
unit string when available.
Each block receives a brief two-sentence descriptionD i that
states what the block contains and which years or units appear.
We embedD i toe i and form the index entries(e i, Bi)∈ Ic.
At query time (Stage C, lines 17–19), the algorithm retrieves
a listCof the top-kblocksB i ranked by cosine similarity
to the query embedding; we forward at most three blocks
to the answer model to keep the context length stable while
preserving numerical fidelity.
A quality gate (Stage C, lines 20–22) rejects weak contexts.
If the retrieved blocks pass, the system answers using only
the corresponding cells. If they do not, control follows Al-
gorithm 1 to the alternate path or to merging on Flat sheets
(Stage D, lines 23–31), including summarization if the merged
context exceeds the token budget.
D. Structured SQL Retrieval for Flat Spreadsheets
For Flat sheets, Algorithm 1(lines 5-7)materializes a small
relational viewI s then infers a schemaS={(c j, tj)}with
cleaned column names and types, and persists any detected
units in a dedicated field. In thesqlbranch Algorithm 1 ((lines
11-14)), an LLM proposesQ SQL from(q,S,SampleRows),
which we execute and refine once or twice for errors or empty
results. Returned rowsRthen serve as evidence. We reject ill-
shaped outputs (for example, empty rows for scalar questions)
in the subsequent quality checks.
Constrained SQL : We restrict generation to
SELECT{FROM{WHERE{GROUP BY{ORDER BY{LIMIT
over a whitelisted schema, with a strict row cap and no
DDL/DML. Empty or ill-shaped results fail the same quality
gate used for chunks.
E. Agent-Based Intelligent Retrieval Selection
The agent in Algorithm 1(lines 9)chooses betweenchunk
andsqlusing the sheet label and cues in the question.
Aggregations with explicit filters tend to route to SQL on Flat
sheets. Queries that refer to layout or nested headers route to
chunk retrieval.
We apply a strict acceptance test quality check to the context
returned by the chosen mode. If the primary attempt fails this
test, Stage D executes the alternate path when the sheet is
Flat. If both paths produce partial confidence scores, we merge
them into a single context, summarize if the token budgetT
