B. Question Taxonomy and Evaluation Protocol
Questions are grouped by retrieval pathway: header driven
(multi-header and merged WB), SQL-driven (flat tables with
tiers), and hybrid (merge step when the primary mode yields
low confidence). We report exact-match accuracy for numeric
and categorical answers, as well as retrieval recall separately
for chunk retrieval and SQL results, isolating retrieval quality
from answer generation.
Difficulty rubricEasy: direct lookup;Medium: one ag-
gregate or range filter;Hard: multi-predicate filters and/or
comparative aggregates across entities/years.
V . Evaluation and Results
We evaluateSQuAREacross three settings: multi header cor-
porate spreadsheets, a complex merged World Bank workbook,
and flat public tables – and compare against strong baselines,
including ChatGPT-4o.
A. Evaluation Protocol
We report exact match accuracy for numeric and categori-
cal answers. Exact match avoids semantic scoring ambiguity
(e.g., “1 billion” vs. “1,000 million”) where metrics such as
BERTScore [18] are not faithful to numeric correctness. To
isolate retrieval quality from answer generation, we addition-
ally report retrieval recall (R@k). For ChatGPT-4o, we report
a tool-free, out of the box baseline (no external retrieval or
SQL tools) to reflect default assistant behavior.
Models and controls.We instantiate SQuARE with
Gemma 3:12B [19] and Llama 3.2:11B [20] (the same model
is used throughout routing and answering). For the ChatGPT-
4o baseline, we issue the same questions via the publicly avail-
able ChatGPT-4o web interface, with no tool use, browsing, or
external retrieval, to reflect an out-of-the-box setting. Across
all runs, we use temperature 0 and a fixed top-kfor retrieval.
All outputs were manually verified due to the prevalence of
numeric answers.
B. Results on Multi-Header and Merged Benchmarks
Figure 2 summarizes accuracy per task and model. On
multi header corporate balance sheets, SQuARE(Gemma) at-
tains91.3%, outperforming SQuARE(Llama) (80.7%) and
ChatGPT-4o (28.7%). On the merged World Bank workbook,
SQuARE(Gemma) reaches86.0%, ahead of SQuARE(Llama)
(74.0%) and ChatGPT-4o (54.0%). These gains are consistent
with the design goal of preserving header paths and units
when the layout is complex and presenting verifiable rows as
evidence.
C. Results on Flat, Single Header Tables
On five flat datasets (Health, Public Debt, GEP, Energy,
Education; 450 QA total with 30 Easy/30 Medium/30 Hard per
dataset), SQuARE’s dual indexing (vector + SQL) and rout-
ing deliver strong performance. SQuARE(Gemma) achieves
93.3%overall, with87%on the Hard tier; ChatGPT-4o
is competitive on Easy/Medium but trails on Hard (81%),
where exact filters, groupings, and multi-column predicates
favor SQL-grounded retrieval. SQuARE(Llama) is consistently
behind Gemma but benefits from the same routing.
