Lowlikelihood of Incorrect responses.We demonstrate the likelihood of correct and incorrect responses in Figure 9a.
As shown, after step 80, the likelihood of incorrect responses is consistently lower than that of correct responses. This
provides evidence that incorrect trajectories lie in a low-probability regime of the policy, which causes their token-level
prediction errors to be assigned disproportionately large weights in the GRPO gradient. As a result, these low-likelihood
incorrect responses exert an outsized negative influence on similar correct trajectories, suppressing the likelihood of correct
actions and accelerating Lazy Likelihood Displacement. This observation is consistent with the theoretical analysis that
low-probability negative samples amplify gradient interference and contribute directly to the onset of the LLD death spiral.
