B. Example of AI doctor-AI patient conversation
Example MEDPI conversation between an LLM doctor (Gemini 2.5 Pro) and an AI PATIENT. The encounter reason is
seizure disorderand the encounter objective istreatment advice. We show only the first six messages (three turns); the
full interaction spans 18 messages.
DR. AI
Hello, Eleanor Rutherford. I'm Doctor AI, and I'll be your medical assistant for this consultation 
today.

I can see from the information you provided that you'd like to discuss a seizure disorder.

To start, could you please tell me a bit more about what's on your mind today and how I can best 
help you?
AI PATIENT
Sure. I've noticed my seizures are happening more often, especially mid-morning, and I'm worried 
about how this could affect my memory and work. I'm looking to talk through longterm options-
things like neurostimulation devices, dietary therapies, even surgery. Mostly, I want a plan that'll 
help me stay independent and keep my job.
DR. AI
Thank you for sharing that with me, Eleanor. It's completely understandable that you're 
concerned about an increase in seizures and the potential impact on your memory, work, and 
independence. It’s also very proactive of you to be thinking about long-term treatment strategies.

To help me understand your situation better and to explore those options with you, I need to 
gather a bit more information first.

Could you start by describing what one of these recent seizures is like for you? For instance, 
what do you experience right before it happens, what happens during the seizure itself, and how 
do you feel afterward?
DR. AI
Thank you for describing that so clearly, Eleanor. That sounds like a very disorienting and 
unsettling experience. The feelings you have right before the seizure—the rising sensation, the 
taste, the difficulty with words—are a very important part of the picture. I'm glad to hear your 
daughter is there to support you through them.

You mentioned the seizures are happening more often. To help me understand this change, could 
you give me a sense of the numbers? For example, how many of these episodes were you having 
per month a few months ago, and how many have you been having more recently?

You also noted the mid-morning timing. Have you noticed any other potential triggers that might 
bring on a seizure, such as stress, lack of sleep, or anything else?
AI PATIENT
Right before a seizure, I usually get a tight, rising feeling and sometimes have trouble finding 
words. I also get queasy and taste something metallic. During the seizure, I’m mostly confused, 
and afterward I feel pretty foggy and vulnerable for a bit—my daughter’s usually on the phone 
with me when it happens, which helps.
AI PATIENT
A few months ago, I was having maybe one or two seizures a month. Lately it’s been more like 
four or five, mostly mid-morning. I know poor sleep, bright screens, and stress definitely make 
them worse—so I try to keep my routine really steady.
