Perplexity-based capacity.We measure how evidence changes answer log-likelihood:
ˆCeff =  logp θ(A∗ |Q, E)−logp θ(A∗ |Q)  ·w cons,(4)
where A∗ is the model’s top answer and wcons ∈[0,1] penalizes contradictory evidence. This
difference quantifies how much the evidence supports the answer—positive values indicate support,
near-zero values suggest evidence is being ignored, and negative values indicate conflict.
Perplexity decomposition.Beyond aggregate capacity, we extract features that decomposehow
models use evidence:
•L Q = logp θ(A∗ |Q): Answer likelihood without evidence
•L QE = logp θ(A∗ |Q, E): Answer likelihood with evidence
•∆L=L QE −L Q: Capacity lift (how much evidence helps)
• ratio=L QE/LQ: Normalized capacity measure
•p max: Maximum token probability (model confidence)
These features enable the model to learn fine-grained patterns about evidence utilization that aggregate
measures miss.
3.5 Calibrated Hallucination Model
Guided by the entropy–capacity design prior established above, we now define the operational
detector. Given feature vector x= (H, Ceff, LQ, LQE,∆L,ratio, pmax)⊤ and labeled data, we fit a
logistic regression model:
