4.2. Task Taxonomy
Given S from Section 3.3, we construct QA pairs that require
spatio-temporal reasoning over gaze transitions. Examples
of these tasks are illustrated in Figure 1, and further details of
each task construction process are provided in Appendix A.4.
4.2.1. Past Task
Past tasks focus on temporal reasoning over gaze and capture
the dynamic characteristics of the user’s gaze, modeling how
attention shifts across objects over time.
• Non-Fixated Object Identification (NFI). Evaluates
implicit visual awareness by identifying objects that were
visible but never directly fixated. For each timestamp
t, we sample one never-gazed object from Ooutt as the
5
correct answer and three visible objects from Ofovt as
distractors.
• Object Transition Prediction (OTP).Assesses tempo-
ral continuity in gaze behavior by predicting the next
object to be fixated. Given the current fixation on object
group Ofovi within the scanpath S, the correct answer is
the next newly attended object Ofovi+1, while distractors
are sampled from the global object pool excluding the
current group.
• Gaze Sequence Matching (GSM). Measures how well
models capture human-like scanpath patterns through
sequential gaze transitions. We extract triplets of consec-
utive fixation groups Ofovi−1 → Ofovi → Ofovi+1 as correct
transitions, while negative options include shuffled or-
derings of the same groups or randomly sampled triplets
that preserve transition structure.
• Scene Recall (SR). Tests contextual memory by recall-
ing background objects previously visible during a fix-
ation. At each fixation Ofovi , we sample the correct an-
swer from background objects visible at other fixations
Oout\i = Sj̸=i Ooutj but not at the current one, with three
visible background objects as distractors.
4.2.2. Present Task
Present tasks capture the user’s perceptual state and intention.
We control question difficulty using both Ofovi and Oouti .
• Object Identification (OI, Easy/Hard). Evaluates
recognition of the currently attended object within the
fixation region Ofovi . The correct answer corresponds to
the fixated object itself. For the easy setting, distractors
are randomly sampled from earlier object pools, while
for the hard setting, distractors are chosen from visually
similar Oouti appearing in the same frame.
• Object Attribute Recognition (OAR). Assesses fine-
grained perceptual understanding by predicting visual
attributes (e.g., color, shape, or texture) of the currently
fixated object within Ofovi . To avoid overlapping or am-
biguous attribute options, we use Qwen3-VL-30B [30] to
generate distinct and contextually consistent distractors.
• Future Action Prediction (FAP).Models intention in-
ference by predicting the user’s next action from the re-
cent gaze-conditioned context {Ofovi−k, . . . ,Ofovi }, where
k < idenotes the number of past fixation steps con-
sidered. Ground-truth future actions are derived from
fine-grained human action captions aligned with gaze
sequences in the original video sources. Semantically
related but incorrect action descriptions are generated by
Qwen3-VL-30B [30] to serve as distractors.
4.2.3. Proactive Task
Proactive tasks go beyond passive perception by requir-
ing model-initiated interventions [ 15]. The model must
determine when to act (e.g., issue an alert), rather than
merely predict future actions. Unlike prior proactive set-
tings [13, 15, 37], we leverage gaze as an anticipatory signal
for upcoming attention and object events.
• Gaze-Triggered Alert (GTA). Triggers an alert when
the user gaze a specified object within Rfovi . This task
evaluates the model’s ability to indicate user’s fixation.
• Object Appearance Alert (OAA). Triggers an alert
when the specified object first appears in the periph-
eral region Routi . This task assesses whether the model
can proactively recognize and react to newly emerging
objects in streaming video.
Overall, we filter out ambiguous questions through both
Qwen3-VL-30B-based validation [30] and human verifica-
tion. Please refer to Appendix A.4 for more details.
5. Experiments
5.1. Experimental Setup
Baselines. We evaluate four categories of existing mod-
els in a zero-shot setting on STREAM GAZE: (1) Closed-
source MLLMs, including GPT-4o [ 17], Claude Sonnet4
and Opus4 [ 18]; (2) Gaze-based models , including As-
sistGaze [7]; (3) Open-source MLLMs, including Qwen2.5-
VL [1], InternVL3.5 [26], MiniCPM-V [33], Kangaroo [14],
EgoGPT [31] and VITA-1.5 [3]; (4) Open-source stream-
ing MLLMs , including Flash-VStream [ 35], VideoLLM-
Online [2], Dispider [21] and ViSpeak [4]. Following prior
streaming benchmarks [13, 15], because non-streaming mod-
els cannot ingest live video streams, we convert all streaming
tasks into offline inference by providing each model with
the corresponding video clip. We additionally report human
oracle performance for all tasks.
Gaze input strategy. To incorporate gaze into each
model, we employ a visual prompting strategy inspired
by [19]. For each frame It, we overlay (i) a green dot indi-
cating the instantaneous gaze coordinate (xt, yt) and (ii) a
red circular region representing FOV . We also prepend an
instruction prompt that explicitly explains these cues to the
model, e.g., “The user’s gaze center is indicated by a green
dot. The red circle represents the area where the user’s gaze
is focused. ”Full prompts are provided in the Appendix B.
Evaluation metrics. For past and present tasks, which
are multiple-choice questions, we use Accuracy based on
exact matching (with optional fuzzy matching for semanti-
cally equivalent responses). In particular, for VideoLLM-
online [2], which generates free-form text responses, we
applied keyword-based matching by checking if the ground
truth option keyword appeared in the response using reg-
ular expressions. For proactive remind tasks, we adopt a
multi-triggering query protocol following [13, 15] to simu-
late online decision-making. At each timestamp, the model
is prompted with the cumulative video observed so far and
must answer “Yes” if the target object has appeared in the
field of view and “No” otherwise. We use accuracy as the
