3.2 End-to-End Reinforcement Learning
While MCTS provides a principled mechanism for exploring reason-
ing trajectories, its effectiveness ultimately depends on how well
the explored paths are evaluated and refined. To fully exploit this
process, we leverage the information collected during MCTS to fine
tune the reasoning policy 𝜋𝜃 through end-to-end reinforcement
learning. Specifically, we adopt Group Relative Policy Optimization
(GRPO) [21], an advanced policy optimization algorithm that di-
rectly optimizes𝜋𝜃 with respect to the paths discovered by MCTS.
For each trajectory, we introduce an outcome-directed reward func-
tion 𝑅(𝜏) , which jointly measures the structural validity of the
reasoning process and its semantic alignment with the original
query. This design ensures that the policy is guided toward produc-
ing both faithful and well-structured answers.
More specifically, given a dataset of questions𝑞∈D 𝑄, the agent
interacts with the text-attribute graph 𝐺 via MCTS to generate
a collection of reasoning trajectories {𝜏𝑖}𝑁𝑖=1 ⊆ T𝑞, where each
trajectory is defined as𝜏𝑖 = ␀(𝑠(𝑖)0 ,𝑎(𝑖)0 ),(𝑠 (𝑖)1 ,𝑎(𝑖)1 ),...,(𝑠 (𝑖)𝑇 ,𝑎(𝑖)𝑇 ).
