promising, they often face scalability issues—large models are ex-
pensive to retrain and fine-tune as data and task complexity increase.
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a practical
alternative [22]. Instead of retraining, RAG brings in external knowl-
edge at inference time, which reduces hallucinations and improves
factual accuracy. The simplest version, naive RAG [ 5], follows a
basic retrieve-and-generate workflow. More advanced methods im-
prove retrieval with query rewriting or expansion [24], and further
refine results through reranking [30]. In parallel, LLMs are being
used in agent-based frameworks. Chain-of-Thought prompting [10]
encourages step-by-step reasoning, while ReAct [29] combines rea-
soning with tool use. These ideas have inspired further work on
multi-agent collaboration [8] and stronger planning abilities.
Reinforcement learning for LLMs Optimization.Reinforcement
learning has emerged as a powerful paradigm for optimizing large
language models (LLMs), and several recent approaches extend be-
yond traditional human-in-the-loop methods such as PPO or DPO.
For example, MCTS-DPO [28] integrates Monte Carlo Tree Search
into DPO training by generating multiple candidate outputs and
treating sibling nodes as comparative training examples, with high-
Q-value nodes serving as positive samples to guide fine-tuning.
Iterative DPO [31] further advances this idea by introducing self-
rewarding language models, where the model generates and evalu-
ates its own outputs, thereby enhancing instruction following and
self-assessment without requiring external feedback. Reinforced
Self-Training (ReST) [7] adopts a grow-and-improve strategy, alter-
nating between generating candidate outputs and fine-tuning only
on those surpassing a gradually increasing quality threshold, which
ensures continuous improvement. Beyond optimizing final outputs,
reinforcement learning has also been applied to process-level su-
pervision, where intermediate reasoning steps are annotated and
rewarded. Lightman et al. [15] show the effectiveness of process
reward models (PRMs) with PRM800K, while automated alterna-
tives such as stronger external LLM annotation [17], Monte Carlo
simulation [25], and Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS)–based eval-
uation [18] reduce reliance on human labeling. Finally, Zhang et
al. [33] propose a self-refining loop that iteratively improves anno-
tations via PRM-guided MCTS, enabling reinforcement learning at
both output and process levels. Together, these approaches demon-
strate the growing role of reinforcement learning in advancing the
optimization of LLMs across diverse reasoning tasks.
Graph-O1: Monte Carlo Tree Search with Reinforcement Learning for Text-Attributed Graph Reasoning WWW ’23, May 1–5, 2023, Austin, TX, USA
6 Conclusion
This paper introducesGraph-O1, a framework that enables Large
Language Models (LLMs) to perform structured, step-by-step rea-
soning over text-attributed graphs. By integrating Monte Carlo Tree
Search (MCTS) with the generative capabilities of LLMs,Graph-O1
selectively and iteratively explores the graph, focusing only on
the most relevant nodes and textual information. This approach
improves both reasoning depth and computational efficiency. To
further enhance performance, we employ reinforcement learning
with Grouped Reinforcement Policy Optimization (GRPO), which
optimizes the model’s decision-making during graph traversal. Ex-
perimental results demonstrate thatGraph-O1achieves superior
performance compared to all baseline methods.
