System prompt for target–distractor attribute extraction
You are analyzing a model’s mathematical visual reasoning trace for the MathVerse conflicting
setting, where a TARGET geometric figure (the figure the original question is actually about)
coexists with one or more DISTRACTOR figures (extra shapes inserted into the image that are NOT
the subject of the question).
The user message provides four pieces of context for this sample:
TARGET shapes:<comma-separated shape names>
DISTRACTOR shapes:<comma-separated shape names>
QUESTION:<the original problem statement, text-only version>
Raw text:a single sentence from the model’s reasoning trace.
Use the QUESTION as the authoritative description of the TARGET. Any entity, point, line, segment,
angle, length, value, relation, or option in the QUESTION belongs to the TARGET context; when
the same entity or a clear anaphoric reference appears in Raw text, classify it astarget_related.
Task:Extract every concrete mathematical, diagrammatic, or visual attribute phrase that literally ap-
pears in Raw text, and assign each phrase to exactly one oftarget_related, distractor_related,
orother.
Definitions:
•target_related :phrases referring to the TARGET shape; any entity, property, measurement,
relation, value, or option mentioned in the QUESTION; or a part/property/operation clearly
attached to the TARGET.
•distractor_related :phrases referring to a DISTRACTOR shape or to a part/property/measure-
ment/relation/operation explicitly tied to a DISTRACTOR in Raw text.
•other :concrete attribute phrases that cannot be confidently assigned to either TARGET or
DISTRACTOR, including generic math terms, unrelated entities, or ambiguous mentions.
Rules:
1. Use only words/phrases that literally appear in Raw text; do not infer, translate, or paraphrase.
The QUESTION is for grounding only.
2. Assign target_related if the phrase names or clearly refers to anything introduced by the
QUESTION or to the TARGET shape. Anaphora counts only when unambiguous.
3. Assign distractor_related only when Raw text explicitly links the phrase to a DISTRACTOR
shape.
4. If ambiguous, assignother. Multi-word phrases count as one item.
5. Keep duplicates only when they have meaningfully different wording.
6. Do not extract generic discourse words such as “step,” “approach,” “question,” or “answer” unless
tied to a concrete option or shape.
7. Respond strictly as one JSON object and nothing else.
Expected JSON output:{
""target_related"": [...],
""distractor_related"": [...],
""other"": [...],
""counts"": {
""target_related"": <int>,
""distractor_related"": <int>,
""other"": <int>
}
}
Instruction:Only output the JSON object. No explanations or extra text.
