Sentiment-Aware Summarization for Unstructured Text
Despite these advances, sentiment analysis is rarely integrated with summarization in IS research.
Most approaches treat sentiment classification and summarization as separate tasks, overlooking
their joint potential to distill both the content and emotional tone of UGC. This disconnect is partic-
ularly limiting in settings where the emotional valence of content—such as frustration, enthusiasm,
or sarcasm—is key to interpreting user concerns or opportunities.
Background
Text Summarization
Text summarization aims to distill the essential content of a document into a concise version.
Existing methods are broadly categorized into extractive and abstractive approaches. Extractive
methods identify and select salient sentences from the input, ensuring grammaticality but often
including redundancy. Abstractive methods generate new sentences that capture the core meaning,
offering greater flexibility but requiring robust language modeling capabilities. Hybrid approaches
aim to balance the strengths of both.
Textrank
TextRank is a graph-based algorithm widely used for extractive summarization (Mihalcea and
Tarau 2004). It treats the sentences of a document as nodes in a graph, where edges represent
the similarity between sentences. The importance of each sentence is determined by its centrality
within the graph.
Formally, given a documentD={s 1, s2, . . . , sN }, a graphG= (V, E)is constructed, whereV
represents the sentences inDandErepresents the edges connecting similar sentences. The weight
of an edge between two sentencess i ands j, denoted asw ij, is calculated as:
