g∈G c(r)g
whereGdenotes the set of alln-grams in the reference summary,c (s)g andc (r)g are the counts of
gin the system-generated summary and reference summary, respectively. This metric reflects the
proportion of reference content successfully recalled by the generated output.
ROUGE-L is computed based on the length of the longest common subsequence (LCS) between
a system summarySand a reference summaryR, denoted byLCS(S, R). The recall-oriented
ROUGE-L is given by:
ROUGE-L= LCS(S, R)|R|
These formulations ensure a rigorous and interpretable comparison between generated and human-
authored summaries, balancing token-level and sequence-level alignment.
Our Model and Baselines
To evaluate the performance of our proposed models, ECPE-TextRank and Senti-UniLM, we com-
pare them against several well-established baseline methods.
TextRank (Mihalcea and Tarau 2004) is a classic graph-based extractive summarization method
that ranks sentences via an iterative algorithm on a similarity graph.
UniLM (Dong et al. 2019), a Transformer-based sequence-to-sequence model, leverages masked-
Workshop on Information Technologies and Systems, Nashville, USA 202510
Sentiment-Aware Summarization for Unstructured Text
token and sequence-level pretraining for effective abstractive summarization.
Pegasus (Zhang et al. 2020) uses Gap Sentences Generation, masking important sentences and
reconstructing them to enhance abstractive capability.
BART (Lewis et al. 2021), a denoising autoencoder, is pretrained by text corruption and recon-
struction, enabling coherent abstractive summaries.
