In LLMs, this expectation cannot be computed efficiently
because ˆzi,ˆyi are sequences of tokens from an exponentially
large space. Therefore, we approximate it by a single Monte
Carlo sample as
(∗)≈r(ˆy i, y⋆i )∇logπ(ˆzi,ˆyi |x i;θ),(5)
where ˆzi,ˆyi ∼π(· |x i, y⋆i ;θ (k−1)) is a single rationale-
answer sample. This is afilteredgradient update of the clas-
sic Monte-Carlo EM (Wei & Tanner, 1990; Neath, 2013),
where the reward r(ˆyi, y⋆i ) serves as a filter in broader pro-
posal distributions (Section 3).
2.2. Practical Meta-Algorithm
Exact sampling of ˆzi,ˆyi from the posterior distribution in
(5) is computationally infeasible in LLMs. This is because
sampling from the posterior requires inverting the generative
process in (1) to obtain reasoning paths ˆzi leading to correct
answers y⋆i . Moreover, ˆzi,ˆyi are sequences of tokens from
an exponentially large space.
We address this challenge by considering a genericrationale
proposal distribution q(ˆz,ˆy|x, y⋆;θ) and implement it by
promptingthe most recent model θ(k−1). Then we approx-
imate (5) by sampling ˆzi,ˆyi ∼q(· |xi, y⋆i ;θ (k−1)). More
specifically, for each data point, we sample ˆzi,ˆyi; keep it
only if the sampled answer is correct, ˆyi =y ⋆i ; and then
fine-tune on retained ˆzi,ˆyi. Because the reward r(ˆyi, y⋆i )
is binary, the rationales leading to incorrect answers are
filtered out in the gradient update.
The pseudo-code of our algorithm is given in Algorithm 1.
We call itfiltered EM ( FEM)due to reward filtering in (5).
The algorithm is iterative. In each iteration, it applies the
E- and M-steps (Section 2.1) to all question-answer pairs
sequentially: Line 3 is theE-stepapplied to dataset D and
Line 5 is the correspondingM-stepwith reward filtering.
For simplicity of exposition, we assume a batch size of 1.
The design of FEM is motivated bysupervised fine-tuning
(SFT). In particular, each iteration ofFEMcan be viewed as
an epoch in SFT, where the training data are adapted to the
learned model due to sampling ˆz(k)i ,ˆy(k)i in Line 3. It can
also be viewed as reward-weighted fine-tuning (Mukherjee
et al., 2025), where the model is updated to increase the
reward-weighted log-likelihood of the sampled rationale-
answer pairs.
The rationale proposal distribution q(· |x, y⋆;θ) is a design
decision and should have two properties. First, the probabil-
ity of event ˆy=y⋆ should be maximized to maximize the
amount of training data. Second, the quality of rationales ˆz
should be high to ensure that conditioning on x,ˆzleads to
better predicted answers ˆyand generalization. We provide
different instantiations ofqin Section 3.
2.3. Filtered EM and Reinforcement Learning
The filtered EM update can also be justified through its
relationship to reward-based maximization inreinforce-
ment learning (RL). To show this, we build on the work
of Mukherjee et al. (2025), which extends results of Ma
et al. (2019); Liang & Vlassis (2022) from offline logged
bandits to offline RL.
