sampling distribution q (E-step) and maximizing the lower
bound (M-step).
3. Rationale Sampling
Now we instantiate the rationale proposal distribution q(· |
x, y⋆;θ) in Algorithm 1. We focus on three representative
schemes: rejection sampling with a budget, self-taught rea-
soning, and prompt posterior sampling.
Rejection sampling with budget M (RS).A statistically
correct way of sampling z from the LVM in (1) is rejection
sampling (Neal & Hinton, 1998). In LLMs, it corresponds
to sampling reasoning-answer pairs conditioned on the ques-
tion until the correct answer is sampled, with a budget of
at most M. The underlying intuition is to create a virtuous
cycle: as the model improves, it is more likely to sample
correct answers, thereby providing more training data for
the nextM-step. This approach has been proposed before
under the names of rejection fine-tuning (Yuan et al., 2023;
Dong et al., 2023; Singh et al., 2024) and online rejection
sampling fine-tuning (Shao et al., 2024, Appendix A.1.3).
The pseudo-code of this method is given in Algorithm 2.
Prompt posterior sampling ( PPS).Modern LLMs are
instruction fine-tuned to follow human feedback (Ouyang
et al., 2022). Therefore, one natural way of implementing
posterior sampling is by conditioning on the correct answer
in the prompt, which we show in Figure 1. This directly
addresses the computational inefficiency of RS. Specifically,
because the correct answer is conditioned on, the model is
likely to generate it and in this way increase the fraction of
accepted rationale-answer pairs. We note that this does not
guaranteethat the model outputs the correct answer. This
also introduces a mismatch between training and testing. At
training time, the LLM generates rationales by conditioning
on both the question and answer, while at test time only the
