4.3. Ablation Studies
We investigate the robustness of our findings through three
ablation studies with Qwen 3B (Section C). First, we vary
the rejection sampling budget M∈ {1,3,5} in RS. While
increasing M sometimes improves performance through
increased data utilization, it also increases computation time
linearly in M, and still significantly underperforms PPS
and STaR. Second, we test the sensitivity of our results to
the prompt in Figure 1 by conditioning less explicitly. We
observe that less precise conditioning decreases absolute
performance of all sampling schemes. However, the relative
order remains the same, and PPS continues to outperform
all baselines. This shows that our results generalize beyond
a single prompt and highlights the importance of precise
instructions when conditioning. Finally, we also experiment
with smaller and larger training set sizes.
5. Related Work
Our filtered EM (FEM) framework unifies several recent ap-
proaches to self-improvement and reasoning under the lens
of inference. In this section, we explicate the connections to
specific EM-style algorithms, data filtering strategies, and
the broader RL as inference literature.
LLM self-improvement. FEM encompasses several recent
self-improvement algorithms for LLM reasoning. Notably,
ReSTEM(Singh et al., 2024) correspond to instantiating
the rationale proposal distribution q in Algorithm 1 with
standardrejection sampling(Neal & Hinton, 1998). This
relationship arises from a specific modeling choice: follow-
ing the variational perspective of Dayan & Hinton (1997),
Singh et al. (2024) introduce a binary optimality variable
O and maximize P(O= 1|x) , treating the entire trajec-
tory—rationale z and answer y—as a latent variable. Conse-
quently, theirE-stepfilters samples from a fixed distribution:
the LLM conditionedonlyon the question x. Such reliance
on a fixed sampling distribution persists in recent literature
as well. For instance,LaTRO(Chen et al., 2024) introduces
a variational framework optimizing a lower bound on correct
answer likelihood; Xu (2025) proposesEM Policy Gradient
to stabilize off-policy updates; andRAFT++(Xiong et al.,
2025) proposes a filtered version of PPO (Schulman et al.,
2017). In all these cases, the algorithms operate under a
fixed sampling policy π(z, y|x;θ) that conditions only on
the question.
Other works have explored alternative proposal distribu-
tions, but typically through complex probabilistic machin-
ery or separate inference networks.TRICE(Phan et al.,
2023) addresses the high variance of gradient estimators
in theM-stepusing control variates.BRiTE(Zhong et al.,
2025) targets theE-stepby jointly training a separate LLM
7
Learning to Reason in LLMs by Expectation Maximization
to approximate the posterior via reinforcement learning.
Similarly, Hu et al. (2024) propose amortizing intractable
posterior inference using GFlowNets (Bengio et al., 2023);
although they do not apply this to iterative LLM training, it
shares the limitation of requiring a separate trained network
to sample from the true posterior.
Our FEM explores a more lightweight alternative: leverag-
ing the LLM’s inherent reasoning and instruction-following
capabilities (Bai et al., 2022; Ouyang et al., 2022; Wei et al.,
2022; Kojima et al., 2022) to approximate q via prompt-
ing, thereby avoiding the complexity of training auxiliary
models. We demonstrate that in notable domains, such as
calibrating LLM-as-a-judge, this EM-based approach can be
vastly improved by simply changing the sampling distribu-
tion to that of rationales conditioned on the true answer. This
highlights that, as prior work has overlooked, examining the
sampling scheme offers a simple yet effective direction to
further improve the training efficiency of LLM reasoning.
Filtering and implicit objectives.Beyond iterative algo-
rithms, our work connects to the literature, viewing data fil-
tration as implicit objective modification. Shrivastava et al.
(2025) propose oversampling reasoning traces and applying
top-K filtering, arguing that such filtration acts as a form
of reward shaping. Similarly, Karan & Du (2025) shows
that inference-time sampling from a sharpened distribution
can outperform RL-based post-training. From a distribu-
tional perspective, methods likeQuark(Khalifa et al., 2021)
and others (Go et al., 2023; Kruszewski et al., 2025; Kim
et al., 2025) derive objectives by defining a target distribu-
tion that discards incorrect answers while preserving the
relative probabilities of correct ones, often approximated
via information projection (Amari, 1985; Nielsen, 2018).
Our work is complementary to this line of research: we
adopt a latent-variable perspective, treating such filtering as
an implicit target distribution over rationales and answers,
and study how different choices of thesamplingdistribu-
tion in theE-stepaffect learning via our FEM. Rather than
viewing filtering as a heuristic for dataset cleaning, FEM
frames it as a formal approximation of the posterior in a
latent variable model.
RL as probabilistic inference.Finally, the derivation of
FEM is grounded in the probabilistic formulation of RL,
also known as Control as Inference; see our discussions
with Lemma 1. The paradigm of addressing RL problems
via Expectation-Maximization is well established in con-
trol theory (Dayan & Hinton, 1997; Levine, 2018), having
been investigated through various lenses, including mixture
models (Vlassis & Toussaint, 2009) and MCMC approx-
imations (Hoffman et al., 2007). While this theoretical
equivalence is well-documented in general RL, our work
systematically operationalizes it for LLM reasoning. We
underscore that, in the context of LLMs, the choice of sam-
pling distribution is not merely a heuristic choice but a
central component of the variational objective.
6. Conclusions
We revisit reasoning as a latent-variable model, bridging the
gap between learning to reason, filtered EM, and reward-
based policy gradients. Our work highlights the critical role
of rationale sampling distributions, a factor that may have a
major impact on empirical performance. In the process, we
propose prompt posterior sampling, a simple yet effective
sampling scheme corresponding to the rationalization stage
of STaR. In our experiments, prompt posterior sampling
consistently outperforms other sampling schemes, including
rejection sampling and STaR, on two human-rating align-
ment tasks in HelpSteer2 and Summarize from Feedback
datasets.
Limitations.First, we establish that the sampling distribu-
tion is an important domain-specific hyperparameter. Our
results on alignment tasks provide a definitive proof of con-
cept: the “right” choice of the rationale sampling distribu-
tion in FEM leads to substantial empirical gains. In future
work, we plan to experiment with more complex tasks, such
as math reasoning, and to explore richer guiding signals.
Second, our current algorithm design is motivated by SFT,
in which each iteration is essentially a single epoch during
which the training data are adapted to the learned model
using sampled rationales. In future work, we plan to investi-
gate if our improvements in sampling generalize to online
reinforcement learning, such as GRPO (Shao et al., 2024).
When viewed in this way, our current design is reminiscent
of batch RL (Lange et al., 2012; Jaques et al., 2020; Levine,
2018), also with connections to prior literature on expert
iterations (Anthony et al., 2017; Anthony, 2021) Last but
not least, while all of our approximations in Section 2.1
are properly justified, we have not formally analyzed FEM,
which we also leave for future work. This is because we
focused on developing the algorithmic framework and em-
pirically validating it.
Impact Statement
This paper presents work whose goal is to advance the field
of machine learning. There are many potential societal
consequences of our work, none of which we feel must be
specifically highlighted here.
