Figure 3: Results of training Qwen2.5-7B inCombination Lock. Belief grading with a domain-specific grader
(dom-BG) allows ABBEL to surpass the performance of models trained with full context access (Full Ctx,
FC beliefs) and generate even more compact belief states. Shaded regions indicate SEM over 3 seeds.
Environment and Metrics.Combination Lockis a 3-character version ofWordleproposed by Arumugam
& Griffiths (2026). We train with a vocabulary of 10 digits and a 12-step horizon, and to test generalization
we evaluate on a disjoint vocabulary of 16 letters and a 16-step horizon. Each episode ends with reward
(H + 1−steps to find code)/H if the code was identified, and−1otherwise. We report the meanRegret,
which is the number of incorrect attempts made in an episode.
Experimental Setup.We train ABBEL with belief grading using a domain-specific grader (ABBEL-dom-
BG), as well as an ablation of grading by training only with outcome reward (ABBEL), and the full-context
settingsFC beliefsandFull Ctxdescribed in Section 2. For each framework we also measure zero-shot
(Zero) performance to evaluate the effect of RLFT. We finally train Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct to study the
scalability of our approach to larger models. We train Qwen2.5-7B and Qwen2.5-14B for 140 and 100 steps,
respectively, using three random seeds for each setting.
Belief Grader Function. InCombination Lock, the set of possible numbers at each position is a highly
informative statistic computable from the interaction history. To grade each generated belief statebt, we
computed these sets and compared them to the result of parsing the contents ofbt into the same format3,
generating a grade of 1 when they were identical and 0 otherwise (Algorithm 3). We stop grading each
trajectory after the first step with an incorrect belief, to avoid penalizing beliefs that were only incorrect due
to propagating errors from the previous step.
Results.For the 7B models, training ABBEL with belief grading is highly effective, resulting in ABBEL-
dom-BGoutperformingtheFull Ctxagent by 7% (Fig. 3a). The belief states remain concise, and even
decrease in length compared to ABBEL zero-shot (Fig. 3b, see Appendix D.1 for examples), resulting in a
60% reduction in memory usage compared toFull Ctx. Without grading, ABBEL’s Regret converges above
Full Ctxwhile using more memory than ABBEL-dom-BG (see Table 1). ComparingFull CtxwithFC
beliefs, we find that belief prompting helps initially, but the advantage disappears over training. All 14B
agents started with lower zero-shot Regret and Peak Token usage than their 7B counterparts (see Table 1),
but we again find that RLFT with belief grading significantly boosts ABBEL-dom-BG’s performance and
memory efficiency, reachingFull CtxRegret with about half the memory usage.
6.2 Belief Grading with Information Reconstruction
We demonstrate that belief grading generalizes to a less synthetic setting, using a general grader based on
reconstructing past information from the belief state.
