Belief Grader Function.We grade each generated beliefbt+1 by how wellπθ can reconstruct the most
recent observationot givenbt+1, bt and actionat, thus rewardingbt+1 for integrating information inot that
is not already in the priorbt. We compute this as the log probability underπθ of the tokens in the last
observationo t, conditioned on the environment instructionspI andb t,a t, andbt+1, i.e.,
fBG(bt+1) = logπθ(ot|p I,bt,at,bt+1).(5)
By application of Bayes’ rule, we derive that thebt+1-dependent component of this is proportional to
logπθ(bt+1|p I,bt,at,ot)−logπ θ(bt+1|p I,bt,at),(6)
where the second term encouragesbt+1 to contain new information relative topI, bt and at, while the first
encourages that new information to be explainable byot. See Algorithm 4 for details.
Results.Results are shown in Table 2. We find that reconstruction-based belief grading is effective: though
ABBEL-rec-BG does not outperformFull Ctx, it reduces ABBEL’s performance gap by about 2x and trains
in half the steps (50 vs 100), while remaining highly memory-efficient: its test pass rate is only7.7%lower
thanFull Ctxwhile using57%less memory. We observe that the grading helps ABBEL learn to add more
useful information to its beliefs, as ABBEL-rec-BG’s belief states were longer on average (see Appendix D.3
for examples). We observe zero-shot ABBEL andFull Ctxperform similarly due to ABBEL agents having
a helpful initial bias to ask for more clarifications before submitting an answer, as we observed in Section 4.2.
