Concrete example of a likely confounded comparisonTable 7 provides a direct illustration
of how published boundary-metric “improvements” can be driven by density regime shifts rather than
improved boundary placement. For example, on DialSeg711, the comparisonTextTiling vs. CSM
shows a substantial increase in W-F1 (∆W-F1= +0.220) accompanied by a large increase in boundary
density (∆BOR= +2.13), moving from a conservative regime (BOR< 1) to an aggressive regime
(BOR≫ 1). Under the failure taxonomy in Table 3, this pattern is consistent with recall-dominated
oversegmentation: higher tolerant boundary scores arise from emitting many more boundaries, not
necessarily from more accurate boundary localization at a matched granularity.
