D Boundary canonicalization
All datasets are converted to a canonical message sequencem1,...,m T per dialogue, where eachmt
corresponds to one dataset-provided utterance after filtering (below). Candidate boundary indices
are the between-message positionsi∈{1,...,T−1}.
FilteringWe (i) retain all dialogue turns from both speakers (user and agent/assistant), removing
only dataset-level metadata headers where present (e.g., meeting preambles in QMSum), (ii) preserve
speaker role labels but do not include them in utterance text, and (iii) remove empty or whitespace-
only utterances if any exist. After filtering, messages are reindexed so that all boundary indices refer
to positions in the filtered sequence.
Gold boundary extractionFor each dataset, we define the gold boundary setGd⊆{ 1,...,T d−1}
using the dataset’s native segment annotation as follows:
• Datasets with segment IDs per utterance:letℓt be the segment ID for messagemt; then
Gd ={i:ℓ i̸=ℓ i+1}.
• Datasets with explicit boundary markers:letbt∈{ 0, 1} indicate thatmt starts a new
segment; thenG d ={i:b i+1 = 1}.
• Domain-/state-labeled datasets:let labelt denote the dataset-provided label for message
mt; thenG d ={i:label i̸=label i+1}.
