high-level program behavior.
• We demonstrate that training with C-ing
Clearly also improves vulnerability detec-
tion performance, highlighting the potential
of our method for other code-related applica-
tions. We confirm our findings on different
model families as well (Qwen2.5-14B).
2 Method
In this section we introduce the prompting strate-
gies used in C-ing Clearly , define the tasks along
with their dataset construction, and provide model
training details.
2.1 Prompting strategies
The goal is to generate high-quality completions
from a pre-trained LLM Mgen for an assembly-
related task. Given a compilable C program, we
extract its corresponding assembly code and utilize
Mgen along with a prompt templateP.
The prompt template P contains two distinct
components: the context c and the task-specific
instruction I. We consider two data-generation
setups: C-ing Clearly (the context contains the
assembly codeand the original C code) and No
C-ing (the context is assembly code only). We
prompt the LLM withP(context, I) , which results
in a task-specific assembly output. In Figure 1, we
illustrate a high-level overview of the two setups.
The full set of prompts are listed in Tables 6-11 in
the Supplementary.
We propose an additional strategy we refer to as
Multi-turn C-ing Clearly . Instead of directly
outputting the assembly report, we engage Mgen
in a three-turn conversation. In the first turn, the
LLM analyzes the C code. The assembly code
is cross-referenced with the C code in the second
turn. Finally, Mgen integrates observations from
the previous two turns and outputs an assembly-
only analysis. Both setups prevent mentions of the
C code in the final output, but the final output of
Multi-turn C-ing Clearly may draw on the C
code explanations from the intermediate turns.
For both tasks, we use Mgen=LLama-3.1-
Nemotron-70B-Instruct (Wang et al., 2024b) to
generate the synthetic data.
2.2 Tasks
We use the synthetic data generated with the pre-
viously described prompting strategies to perform
supervised fine-tuning. We focus on two tasks:
Binary Code Summarization (BCS) and Vulnera-
bility Detection (VD). Our goal in BCS is to obtain
general analysisreports that split the code into as-
sembly blocks, highlight their functionality, and
conclude with an overview. For BCS, we fine-tune
using{task prefix + assembly, general analysis}
pairs. We hypothesize that retaining thegeneral
analysisin the output also benefits the VD task.
VD is typically a multi-label vulnerability clas-
sification task, performed over popular high-level
programming languages such as C++ or Java (Sun
et al., 2024). However, we are interested in en-
hancing the binary code analysis capabilities of an
LLM, therefore we cast this as anassembly →
vulnerability labelsgenerative problem. The vul-
nerability labels are based on existing taxonomies
