1. We fine-tune the model using{task prefix + as-
sembly, general analysis + vulnerability analysis}
pairs, where the vulnerability analysis is a report
containing potential CWE labels, security implica-
tions and the relevant assembly code where they
occur. In Figure 2 we depict the structure of the
supervised fine-tuning data for both tasks.
The full set of prompts used to generate the
training data is provided in both the code repos-
itory and the Supplementary for each method:
C-ing Clearly (Tables 6-7), Multi-turn C-ing
Clearly (Tables 8-9), and No C-ing (Tables 10-
11). Forvulnerability analysiswe use slightly dif-
ferent prompts for vulnerable and non-vulnerable
samples. For vulnerable samples we feed the
ground truth CWEs and their description, while for
non-vulnerable samples we specify that the code is
most likely benign.
2.3 Supervised fine-tuning datasets
We generate the supervised fine-tuning data from
both vulnerable and non-vulnerable code snippets.
Specifically, we start from two datasets, Diverse-
Vul (Chen et al., 2023) and VDISC (Russell et al.,
2018). They both contain C/C++ functions, col-
lected from open source projects, with vulnera-
bility annotations obtained either through static
analysis tools or by crawling security issues and
mapping the corresponding commits to affected
functions. DiverseVul contains 19K vulnerable
functions spanning 150 CWEs.
After discarding samples with length above
16,384, we obtain∼8.5K vulnerable samples. We
keep a held-out test set of 500 samples and use
1https://cwe.mitre.org
the other samples for training. From the VDISC
dataset, we use∼9K non-vulnerable samples for
training. For all the C samples, we generate the
corresponding assembly code by running the gcc
-S source.c command. We generate a supervised
fine-tuning dataset for each of the two tasks, as
described in subsection 2.2. Each dataset contains
examples from both DiverseVul and VDISC.
2.4 Models and training details
We note that training with C-ing Clearly
data is model agnostic and benefits any base
model that has sufficient knowledge of C, but
may lack assembly knowledge. We choose
Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (Grattafiori et al., 2024)
as the main base model for our experiments, which
we fully fine-tune on each dataset resulted from
the No C-ing , C-ing Clearly and Multi-turn
C-ing Clearly data generation methods. We fix
the training budget to 1B tokens for every experi-
ment.
We also use Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct as the
baseline in a zero-shot setting. To generate reports
that are similar to our fine-tuned models’ outputs,
we use the prompt in Table 12 in the Supplemen-
tary. The quality of these reports provides insight
into the model’s initial assembly capabilities.
For model training we set the maximum se-
quence length to 16,384 and discard examples
where the combined length of the prompt and com-
pletion exceeds this limit. On average, the assem-
bly code in the supervised datasets is approximately
2,530 tokens long. We set the global batch size to
160. The optimizer is AdamW with a base learning
rate of 3e-4. We use linear warmup with cosine
annealing, with an initial learning rate of 1e-5 and
