3 Binary code summarization
In this section, we evaluate how well
Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and the fine-tuned
models can analyze and explain the assembly code.
3.1 Evaluation
To evaluate report quality, we employ LLM-as-
a-judge (Zheng et al., 2023). More specifically,
we ask GPT-4o (Hurst et al., 2024) to select the
best out of two summaries (each from a different
model), based on how accurately it captures the
main purpose, high-level functionality and logic
of the assembly code. The evaluation prompt is
illustrated in Table 13 in the Supplementary. We
perform pairwise comparisons between all models.
For each of the 500 test samples, we generate
two different outputs from each BCS model, using
nucleus sampling (Holtzman et al., 2019) and a
temperature of 0.4. This results in a total of 2000
comparisons for each pair of models. The order of
the two reports in the evaluation prompt is random.
3.2 Results
The win rate matrix is reported in Table 1. The
model trained with C-ing Clearly data is pre-
ferred over all other models, achieving the high-
est average win rate. Most importantly, C-ing
Clearly outputs surpass those from the No C-ing
model, showcasing the importance of the C code
anchor in the synthetic data generation process.
Outputs from the Multi-turn C-ing Clearly
model, although frequently preferred over the base-
line outputs, are less competitive. Despite its sub-
optimal BCS performance, the multi-turn setup has
other advantages, discussed in section 4.4.
To better understand the summary structure and
check for possible LLM judge biases, we analyze
in Table 2 the average response statistics for the
different models in relation to their average win-
rates. C-ing Clearly outputs are both the highest
ranked and the shortest on average. In contrast,
while Multi-turn C-ing Clearly produces simi-
larly short summaries, the judge ranks them second
to last. This suggests that length is not a primary
factor in the judge’s decisions.
3.3 Qualitative analysis
We investigate the answers from the LLM judge to
gain insights into the differences between outputs.
A sample of the C-ing Clearly output is shown
in Table 14 in the Supplementary. In Figure 3 we
notice that the judge prefers the C-ing Clearly
output, highlighting aspects such as core function-
ality, accuracy and conciseness. The No C-ing out-
put on the other hand is comprehensive but doesn’t
capture high-level insights as effectively.
We next examine several key phrases that fre-
quently appear in the judge’s responses and are
invoked when ranking the outputs. For each phrase,
we count how often it occurs when the winning
report is produced by either C-ing Clearly or No
C-ing. These counts are then normalized by the
total number of occurrences of each phrase.
We notice in Figure 4 that phrases linked to high-
level functionality (’core functionality’, ’main pur-
pose’, ’more focused’) and correctness (’more cor-
rectly’) are more often linked to cases whereC-ing
Clearly is ranked first. Notably, when ’core func-
tionality’ is mentioned, C-ing Clearly outputs
