4.4 Multi-label classifier for VD
Since VD is a classification task, we can train
multi-label classifiers on top of assembly-level fea-
tures extracted from the input program. While
not specifically finetuned for assembly repre-
sentations, we can extract features from the
Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-based models evaluated
in Section 4.1.
Specifically, we encode the assembly using the
contextual embedding of size 4,096 corresponding
to the last token in the{task prefix + assembly}
instruction prompt. The label set spans 135 unique
CWEs in the training data. The classifier is a two-
hidden-layer MLP, for which we select the best
performing detection threshold based on a held-out
validation set. We extract features from both the
baseline and fine-tuned models.
The classification results averaged over 20 in-
dependent training runs are presented in Table 5.
As in the generative setup, the best performing
models are those trained on Multi-turn C-ing
Clearly and C-ing Clearly data, outperform-
ing Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and No C-ing . This
suggests that fine-tuning on synthetic data en-
hanced with C anchors results in more discrimi-
native assembly features for the VD task. While
this is not the focus of our work, it indicates a po-
tential direction for future research on optimizing
assembly embeddings.
5 Related Work
LLMs for assembly are typically designed to learn
meaningful assembly representations for tasks such
as Binary Code Similarity Detection or to assist
with binary analysis tasks such as Binary Code
Decompilation or Binary Code Summarization.
5.1 Models for learning semantic
representations of assembly
To learn assembly representations, both encoder
and decoder Transformer-based models (Vaswani
et al., 2017) are pretrained then fine-tuned con-
trastively with different signals. Encoders are
trained with Masked Language Modeling (MLM)
signal and specialized pretraining tasks such as
Jump Target Prediction (Wang et al., 2022).
For instance, Wang et al. 2024a introduce CLAP,
in which they supervise a RoBERTa encoder (Liu
et al., 2019) with natural language explanations
generated by an LLM from the original source
code. ASMA-tune (Wang et al., 2025) learns a
projector module to align the assembly encoder to
the decoder’s latent space, to benefit from both the
encoder’s representation learning and the decoder’s
explanation capability.
Jiang et al. 2023 add hierarchical self-attention
heads to a pre-trained decoder and train it with both
next token prediction loss and source code - assem-
bly contrastive loss. This model then transfers well
to both embedding tasks and binary analysis tasks
such as Binary Decompilation.
5.2 Models for binary analysis
LLMs have been more recently used for assembly
analysis tasks such as Binary Decompilation and
Binary Code Summarization, finetuned from gen-
eral LLMs (Grattafiori et al., 2024) or code LLMs
(Guo et al., 2024). Efforts to build a foundational
LLM for assembly have emerged, with Cummins
et al. 2024 adapting a code LLM (Roziere et al.,
2023) and further specializing it for downstream
tasks such as compiler flag optimization and disas-
sembly into LLVM-IR.
For Binary Decompilation, Tan et al. 2024 train
end-to-end models called LLM4Decompile to pro-
duce the decompiled output based on the assembly
input. They outperform classic decompilation tools
such as Ghidra (National Security Agency, 2025)
and can even further refine Ghidra’s output.
7
Compared to Source Code Summarization, Bi-
nary Code Summarization suffers from a scarcity
of high-quality training data. To address this, re-
cent papers have introduced large-scale datasets
and explored the use of LLMs: BinSum (Jin et al.,
2023), CAPYBARA (Al-Kaswan et al., 2023) and
CP-BCS (Ye et al., 2023). These datasets typically
provide function-level summaries by pairing source
code comments with the corresponding assembly
code. In contrast, Lu et al. 2024 construct MalS,
a malware-focused BCS dataset where summaries
are obtained with LLMs based on the malicious
source code. Our work follows a similar direc-
tion and focuses on producing more in-depth bi-
nary code summaries via LLMs rather than relying
on potentially terse and implementation-specific
source code comments.
5.3 Vulnerability detection
Prior to LLMs, a common approach to identifying
vulnerable code was to use features from a domain-
tuned Transformer (Thapa et al., 2022).
Despite leveraging LLMs, recent VD approaches
have not yet resulted in consistently high perfor-
mance (Ding et al., 2024). Through in-context
learning (Brown et al., 2020; Dong et al., 2022),
LLMs can be adapted using auxiliary information
in the prompt, such as data-flow (Zhang et al.,
2024a) or static analysis tools (Li et al., 2023).
DLAP (Yang et al., 2025) employs Retrieval-
Augmented-Generation (Liu et al., 2020) to in-
tegrate the most similar labeled code fragments
with static analysis outputs. This retrieved informa-
tion is further used to select a predefined Chain-of-
Thought (Wei et al., 2022; Kojima et al., 2022) to
guide the vulnerability analysis.
6 Conclusion
In this paper, we present a synthetic data gener-
ation method named C-ing Clearly to enhance
binary code analysis for LLMs. By providing both
assembly code and its corresponding C code, we
leverage the LLM’s in-context abilities to guide it
towards better outputs.
Experiments show that training with C-ing
Clearly improves binary code summaries, in
terms of correctness, conciseness and understand-
ing of high-level functionality. Moreover, our
method improves the performance on the vulnera-
bility detection task, highlighting its effectiveness
across related code analysis problems. Finally, ab-
lations on the vulnerability detection task demon-
strate that C-ing Clearly is robust across different
model families and can help models improve by
bootstrapping on self-generated data.
7 Limitations and Future Work
Our method leverages high-level C code to support
in-context learning of low-level machine represen-
tations. We acknowledge that other high-level lan-
guages may also aid in reasoning about low-level
code such as assembly or bytecode.
Our experiments are based on assembly pro-
grams compiled using gcc -S , which applies no
optimization. We hypothesize that our proposed
method could be also beneficial when applied to
optimized assembly code, where the C anchor may
help interpret more compact representations.
Additionally, our results are demonstrated on
the x86-64 instruction set, without covering other
instruction sets such as ARM or RISC-V .
Finally, a practical limitation lies in the size of
the assembly programs. These require substantial
context capacity from the LLM. We restricted our
experiments to inputs with a maximum sequence
length of 16K tokens. Future work should explore
the scalability and robustness of our approach on
longer binaries.
