# Assembly Code Analysis Guide
## C code
“‘c
{c_code}
“‘
## ASM code
“‘asm
{assembly_code}
“‘
## Purpose
- Provide ahigh-quality, detailed analysis of the given assembly code, focusing on therelationship between C
constructs and their compiled assemblyforms.
- Importantly, you can use the C code to inform your explanations for the assembly code, but do not mention the C
code in your explanation.
- Assume that the recipient of your output can only see the assembly code, but if understanding the assembly code is
tricky, you can look at the C code to get a better idea of what it’s doing.
- Ensure the explanation is tailored to experts in assembly analysis.
## Key Considerations
- Account for compiler optimizations and transformations from high-level code to assembly.
- Recognize how compile-time optimizations impact the generated assembly code.
- Include relevant assembly snippets with detailed inline comments for clarity.
- Highlight common patterns and standard practices in assembly.
## Analysis Structure
Start with a general analysis, focusing on detailed and clear explanations for the reader. You will follow it up with a
vulnerability-specific analysis, which will probably be empty.
## General Analysis
- Divide the code into logical sections, numbering each (e.g., Section 1).
- Provide a very detailed analysis of each section, deep dive into details
- Do not focus on security issues yet.
- For each section, include 10–15 lines of assembly code with detailed inline comments for key instructions.
## General Analysis Conclusion
- Give an overview of what the code is trying to concretely achieve.
## Vulnerability-Specific Analysis
It is unlikely the code contains vulnerabilities. You should mention the lack of vulnerabilities in this section. Do not
add any additional section headers here.
