F . Gender Bias Study
For this experiment, we adopted theSocial Values, Attitudes
& Stereotypesection (Q1 - Q45) andEthical Values And
Normssection (Q176 - Q198) of the World Values Survey
(WVS) [35], which consists of single-choice Likert-type scale
questions and yes-and-no questions. The sections were se-
lected by ranking WVS questions by the relative difference
8cloud.google.com/translate/docs/reference/rest/v2/translate
between the average scores of male and female participants.
After excluding questions about the real-life demographics
and memberships of the participants, we identified these two
sections as the ones most distinguishable by participants’
gender. The survey questions are answered by 45,995 men
and 51,130 women from 2017 to 2022, given by the WVS
Wave 7 database 9. We filtered out the invalid answers in the
dataset.
We then prompted the LLMs to complete these survey ques-
tions without assigning them gender or other social identities,
and examined whether the answers aligned more with men,
women, or neither. The prompts are listed as follows.
Q1 - Q6
Role:system
Content:You are a participant in a social value survey.
You must respond by rating how important each of the
following things is in your life, on a scale from 1 to
4, where:
1 = Very important
4 = Not at all important
Do not provide explanations or commentary. Only return
the numeric ratings in a numbered list matching the
numbering of the questions.
Role:user
Content:For each of the following, indicate how
important it is in your life on the scale of 1 to
4:
1. Family
2. Friends
3. Leisure time
4. Politics
5. Work
6. Religion
Q7 - Q17
Role:system
Content:You are a participant in a social value survey.
You will be given a list of qualities children can be
encouraged to learn at home. From this list, select
up to five qualities that you consider especially
important. Return only the numbers corresponding to
your selections, in a single line, separated by spaces.
Do not include explanations, labels, or any other text.
Role:user
Content:Here is a list of qualities that children can
be encouraged to learn at home. Which, if any, do you
