4.2 Disparity in Domain Selection
We next assess the extent to which each search engine returns
unique domains. High distinctiveness indicates that users may re-
trieve varied information depending on the search engine.
Overall Disparity.Figure 4 presents the fraction of domains that
are unique to LLM-SEs, unique to TSEs, or overlapping between
the two. Here,Uniquerefers to domains appearing exclusively in
the corresponding LLM-SEs or TSEs, whereasCommondenotes
domains shared between both. Surprisingly, large differences are
observed between LLM-SEs and TSEs: only 38% of domains appear
in both (i.e.common), whereas 37% are unique to LLM-SEs results.
Similar patterns also persist at the response level, with an average
overlap of less than 40% between any LLM-SEs and TSEs pair (see
Appendix D). Even for services provided by the same company
(e.g.,Google, AI Mode, and Gemini), the sourced domains differ
substantially (≥ 30%). This divergence likely arises because the
LLM-SEs decomposes the original queries rather than using the
full user-provided context when retrieving content from external
sources [61]. This confirms that users relying solely on LLM-SEs
are exposed to a distinct set of information sources compared to
those using TSEs alone.
Disparity in News Sources.Although LLM-SEs diverge from TSEs
in their overall domain distribution, it remains unclear whether
they favor specific sources. Such preferences are important, as over-
reliance on a single source can amplify biases and reduce diversity,
a concern particularly relevant for news content. To investigate this,
we extract all news domains (as labeled by VirusTotal, see §3.4) from
the search results. This results in 2,675 unique domains returned by
both LLM-SEs and TSEs. We then employ Rank Turbulence Diver-
gence (RTD) [17, 59] to quantify differences between LLM-SEs and
