Figure 6 illustrates the rank distribution of overlapping domains
in the TSEs results. Surprisingly, we observe heavy skew in overlap-
ping domains toward the top-1 TSE results. The sources LLM-SEs
most frequently cite are those ranked first by traditional search
engines 23.27% in Bing and 14.53% in Google. This suggests that for
the overlapped search results, LLM-SEs tends to cite the domains
that are also considered as the most important in TSEs.
Traffic-Based Popularity.We then use the Tranco ranking [58]
to assess the user popularity of the cited domains. Tranco has been
widely adopted in prior work [4, 25, 37, 71] and allows us to assess
whether LLM-SEs prioritize high-traffic websites or rely on less
popular sources. Here, a lower rank indicates higher popularity.
Table 2 reports the mean and median Tranco ranks for all domains
cited by each search engine. A Kruskal-Wallis test [48] is further
conducted to confirm statistical differences (𝐻= 58627.99,𝑝<
0.001). Post-hoc results show all LLM-SEs differ significantly from
TSEs (𝑝< 0.001). Overall, except for ChatGPT, the LLM-SEs tend
to return less popular domains (i.e.higher rank) than the TSEs.
Notably, Copilot and Gemini exhibit substantially higher average
Tranco ranks with over 22,000 above both TSEs, which indicates a
stronger inclination toward low-traffic domains. Such a tendency
may negatively affect user trust, as search behavior research [56]
shows that users typically perceive high-traffic domains as more
authoritative. Beside, lower-traffic domains may also be less reliable,
which motivates our investigation of domain credibility in §5.1.
