1) Multi-Modal Fusion via Shared Self-Attention. 𝑄(𝑙−1) and
𝐻(𝑙−1)𝐶 from the previous layer are concatenated and processed
by ashared self-attention layer, allowing them to exchange
information. For the first layer (𝑙= 1), the inputs are the initial
learnable queries𝑄and code text embeddings𝐻 𝐶:
[𝑄(𝑙)𝑠𝑎 ,𝐻(𝑙)𝐶,𝑠𝑎]=Self-Attn([𝑄 (𝑙−1),𝐻(𝑙−1)𝐶 ]).(4)
2) Graph Information Extraction via Cross-Attention. 𝑄(𝑙)𝑠𝑎
from the self-attention step then acts as the query to extract relevant
structural information from𝑥𝐺 viacross-attention:
𝑄(𝑙)𝑐𝑟𝑜𝑠𝑠 =Cross-Attn(𝑄=𝑄 (𝑙)𝑠𝑎 ,𝐾=𝑥 𝐺,𝑉=𝑥 𝐺).(5)
3) Feed-Forward Network.Finally, the outputs from the attention
layers are processed through separate Feed-Forward Networks
(FFN) to further refine their representations.
𝑄(𝑙) =FFN𝑄(𝑄(𝑙)𝑐𝑟𝑜𝑠𝑠), 𝐻 (𝑙)𝐶 =FFN𝐶(𝐻(𝑙)𝐶,𝑠𝑎).(6)
The final output of the Bridge Module is the set of contextualized
query embeddings from the last layer,𝐵𝑄 =𝑄(𝐿𝑏)∈R 𝑁𝑞×𝑑model. To
interface with the downstream LLM, these embeddings are linearly
projected into a soft prompt𝑃𝐺 =Proj LLM(𝐵𝑄), which will later be
used to guide task-specific generation.
3.2.2Training Procedure.This stage aligns the representations
of code graph-text pairs(𝑥𝐺,𝐶) within the Bridge module, inde-
pendent of the final LLM (Figure 3(b)). Following established multi-
modal learning methodologies [32, 36], we employ three comple-
mentary objectives:
Graph-Text Contrastive Learning (GTC).This objective aligns
global representations of graphs and texts. Given a batch of M pairs,
let BQ ={𝐵 𝑖𝑄}𝑀𝑖=1 denote the graph-query embeddings from the
Bridge module. Critically, let hcls ={ℎ𝑖𝑐𝑙𝑠}𝑀𝑖=1 denote the text-only
representations, where eachℎ𝑖𝑐𝑙𝑠 is the [CLS] token of𝐻(𝐿𝑏)𝐶 for
its sample. The symmetric InfoNCE loss [52] encourages aligned
pairs(𝐵𝑖𝑄,ℎ𝑖𝑐𝑙𝑠) to be similar and contrasts them against all other
negative pairs:
LGTC = 12 ␀InfoNCE(B Q,h cls,𝜏)+InfoNCE(h cls,B Q,𝜏)  .(7)
The similarity within InfoNCE function is computed via maximum
cosine similarity over all𝑁𝑞 query tokens, and the resulting scores
are then scaled by a learnable temperature parameter𝜏 (detailed in
Appendix B).
Graph-Text Matching (GTM).This objective trains a binary
classifier𝜙gtm, to predict whether a graph-text pair is a true match.
Given a pair(𝐵𝑖𝑄,ℎ𝑖𝑐𝑙𝑠), the predicted probability𝑝𝑖 =𝜙 gtm(𝐵𝑖𝑄,ℎ𝑖𝑐𝑙𝑠).
The loss is standard cross-entropy averaged over𝑀 samples, with
hard negative mining [10] applied to construct challenging non-
