Graph Construction.Following the refinement, we construct a
CPG for each snippet. We begin by parsing source code using tree-
sitter2, where each node corresponds to a named AST element and is
labeled with a predefined type (e.g., assignment, identifier). For
textual representation, high-level statement nodes retain only the
first line of their source text (e.g., the condition in an if statement),
while other nodes (e.g., expressions, identifiers) retain their
full span. Edges in the CPG encode structural and semantic rela-
tions and are categorized into AST, CFG, and DFG types. AST edges
capture syntactic hierarchy (e.g., has_name, contains). CFG and
DFG edges are both derived from the static analysis of the AST: CFG
edges encode control-flow semantics (e.g.,sequential_execution,
true_branch) by analyzing statement sequences and control struc-
tures, while DFG edges model data dependencies (e.g., flows_to,
contributes_to) based on variable definition and usage patterns.
Each edge is also assigned a textual attribute indicating its seman-
tic role. Finally, all node and edge textual attributes are encoded
into dense feature vectors using a pre-trained code encoder (e.g.,
UniXCoder), and the resulting graph is stored and processed using
the PyG [12] for downstream GNN computation.
4.2 Experimental Setup
Downstream Tasks.We evaluateCGBridgeon two represen-
tative code generation tasks that require deep structural under-
standing:Code Summarization, which involves identifying control
flows, data dependencies and functional roles to generate accurate
2https://tree-sitter.github.io/tree-sitter
natural language descriptions of code functionality;Code Trans-
lation, which re-expresses the same logic in a different language
while preserving semantics, relies heavily on the accurate modeling
of abstract syntax trees and complex program structures. These
tasks serve as strong testbeds forCGBridge’s to unify structural
comprehension with generative quality.
Metrics.Our evaluation protocol combines lexical and semantic
similarity, reference-free assessment, and functional correctness.
Specifically, for summarization, we adoptMETEOR[29] andROUGE-
L[ 34] for lexical similarity,BERTScore[64] andSentence-BERT cosine
similarity[ 44] for semantic similarity and theLLM-as-a-Judgepara-
digm [14, 26, 51, 56, 59], where an LLM rates generated summaries
from 0-4 on Coherence, Consistency, Fluency, and Relevance, which
are then aggregated into an overall quality rating. For translation,
we useCodeBLEU[ 45], Syntax Match, and Dataflow Match to eval-
uate structural alignment, withExecution Accuracyserving as the
primary metric for functional correctness of the translated code.
Baselines.We compareCGBridgeagainst the following three
baselines to demonstrate its effectiveness:
•Zero-shot.Base code LLMs without task-specific fine-tuning.
• +LoRA.Code LLMs fine-tuned with LoRA [ 23], a powerful and
widely-adopted Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) method,
which provides a primary text-only adaptation baseline.
• +GraphText.Following prior work [ 55, 65], we convert code
graphs into textual descriptions (listing nodes then edges) as
in-context information for LLMs.
The detailed implementations are referred to Appendixes D, E and F.
