4.3 Overall Performance
Table 1 presents the main experimental results of our proposed
CGBridgeframework on two downstream tasks.
Performance on Code Summarization.The results reveal a con-
sistent trend across all models. The +GraphText baseline exhibits
limited effectiveness and occasionally underperforms the Zero-shot
baseline, indicating that long serialized graph descriptions may
introduce noise and hinder the LLM’s ability to exploit structural
information. In comparison with the strong +LoRA baseline, our
+CGBridgeshows a consistent advantage. While +LoRA remains
competitive on lexical overlap metrics such asROUGE-L,CGBridge
consistently achieves superior performance on semantic metrics, in-
cludingBERTScoreandLLM-as-a-judgeevaluations, demonstrating
its capability to generate summaries with higher semantic fidelity
and overall quality.
Performance on Code Translation.This task demands both se-
mantic preservation and strict functional correctness, where the lim-
itations of serialization become particularly evident. The +Graph-
Text baseline performs poorly, with a substantial drop inExecution
Accuracy, suggesting that linearized graphs fail to convey essen-
tial program logic. Although both +LoRA and our +CGBridge
achieve strongCodeBLEUscores, our approach consistently out-
performs on fine-grained metrics such asSyntax Match,Dataflow
Match, andExecution Accuracy(e.g., 98.26% on Qwen2.5-Coder-7B).
These findings underscore the importance of a dedicated integration
mechanism—such as our Bridge module—for effectively translating
structural signals into functionally correct code.
4.4 Robustness to Variable Renaming
To test whether our framework alleviates the structural informa-
tion loss in text-only models, we perform a robustness evaluation
under code obfuscation. The code summarization test set is mod-
ified by replacing all function names and internal variables with
random identifiers (e.g.,get_price→fn_abc,cost→var_xyz),
while preserving external library calls [22]. As this transformation
invalidates lexical metrics such asROUGE, we adopt the semantic-
orientedLLM-as-a-Judge(LLM-J) score. Results in Table 2 show
pronounced degradation in text-only models, particularly in LoRA
variants (e.g., a 15.2% drop in the 7B LoRA model). In contrast, our
+CGBridgeremains highly stable, with changes of only -1.6% and
-0.3% for the 1.5B and 7B models. These findings demonstrate that
by leveraging explicit structural graphs, our framework captures
true program logic and data flow, effectively resisting superficial
identifier variations—a key weakness of text-only approaches.
4.5 Ablation Study
To validate the contribution of each key component within our
framework, we conduct two ablation studies on the code translation
task using the Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B.
Impact of Training Components.We evaluate the effectiveness
of our training strategies by comparing the fullCGBridgemodel
with five ablated variants:w/o Stage 1 (CGE Pre-training), which
skips self-supervised pre-training and randomly initializes the CGE
module;w/o Stage 2 (Cross-Modal Alignment), which omits the
entire cross-modal alignment and initialized Bridge module with
pre-trained BERT weights for the final adaptation;w/o GTMand
w/o GTG, which remove their respective alignment objectives from
Stage 2; andw/o Stage 3 (Task Adaptation), which performs only
Stage 1 and 2 without the final instruction-based adaptation. As
shown in Figure 4, removing any component degrades performance,
confirming that each part is essential. A strong initial graph repre-
sentation is critical, as skipping Stage 1 results in a sharp drop (CB:
69.81→57.20, EA: 80.91→71.77). Removing Stage 2 yields the worst
performance (69.61% EA), while removing Stage 3 also causes a
notable decline to 74.32% EA, demonstrating the importance of both
cross-modal alignment and task-specific tuning. Within Stage 2,
eliminating GTM or GTG objectives reduces performance on both
metrics, highlighting their complementary roles in establishing a
robust and precise bridge between modalities.
