3.2 LLM Critique
LLM critique is responsible for identifying the unfaithful content in the initial summaryˆy, providing a
hallucination scoresand a textual feedbackc. The hallucination scoresis used to determine if a summary
is faithful or not, and also to rank unfaithful summaries. Specifically,ˆyis considered to be unfaithful ifs >0.
On the other hand, the textual feedbackcfrom critique is used to guide the subsequent refinement process.
In this work, we explore two strategies for designing the LLM critique component.
Critique with binary feedback:LLM is prompted to output a simple yes/no response indicating whether
the summary contains any hallucinated information. Specifically, given an input documentxand an initial
summaryˆy, the hallucination scoresofˆyis defined as the log-likelihood ratio of “yes” and “no” tokens:
s= log π(yes|x,ˆy, pbincritique)π(no|x,ˆy, pbincritique) (3.1)
wherep bincritique denotes the prompt designed for the binary critique strategy. The textual feedbackcin this
case is “The summary is unfaithful.” ifs >0, and “The summary is faithful.”, otherwise.
Critique with fine-grained feedback:Inspired by the recent advancement in hallucination detection task
(Min et al., 2023; Scirè et al., 2024; Yang et al., 2024; Wan et al., 2024), we design a three-stage process
for LLM critique with fine-grained feedback. In the first stage, we decomposeˆyinto a list of atomic facts
