3.3 Semantic Tree Construction
Here, we detail the construction of current semantic tree T≤k from the set of leaf gist token rep-
resentations {h⟨GT⟩1 , . . . , h⟨GT⟩k }. The construction is scalable and incremental: when processing
sub-segment sk, the existing treeT<k (which summarizes preceding ⟨GT⟩1, . . . ,⟨GT⟩k−1) is partiallyreused to formT ≤k by integrating the new gist token’s representationh ⟨GT⟩k (obtained as describedin Sec. 3.4). The general bottom-up aggregation process is:
{h⟨GT⟩1 , h⟨GT⟩2 , . . . , h⟨GT⟩M }
Hierarchical Aggregation
− − − − − − − − − − − − − → TM .
4
While the tree structure can be flexible, we employ the binary tree. Meanwhile, to avoid introducing
extra consumption, we do not use padding tokens to keep tree balanced. Instead, any remaining
unpaired gist token is deferred to the aggregation at next level.
Once the structure is determined, the aggregation function is defined as: Agg:P(R d)→R d, where
P denotes the power set and Rd is the hidden state dimension. Starting from the leaf gist tokens,
the aggregator iteratively combines hidden states hu for all children u of node v to form the parent
node’s hidden stateh v:
hv = Agg({hu |u∈C v}),(5)
Here, Cv denotes the set of children of node v. For efficiency, the aggregation function Agg(·) is
implemented by a single-layer self-attention mechanism, followed by an averaging operation:
Agg(Cv) =Average(Self-Attn agg({hu |u∈C v};θ agg)),(6)
The trainable parameters size in θagg is significantly smaller than the entire LLM, and are only built
once during inference. Therefore, it does not affect the original inference complexity O((L/τ) 2).
Furthermore, the self-attention in aggregation mechanism further allows information from subsequent
segments to influence preceding ones, mitigating the unidirectional limitations of causal LLMs.
3.4 Tree-based Compression
This stage processes each sub-segment sk and its associated gist token ⟨GT⟩k by leveraging thesemantic summary T<k of preceding content (i.e., tree built from gist tokens ⟨GT⟩1, . . . ,⟨GT⟩k−1).
The primary goal is to generate contextualized representations for tokens in sk and for ⟨GT⟩k. Oncethe representation is computed, the key ( K⟨GT⟩k) and value (V⟨GT⟩k) representations of ⟨GT⟩k arealso stored to accelerate the construction of subsequent semantic tree (as per Sec. 3.3).
