Aij log AijQij .(4)
This objective measures the expected information-theoretic distance between the transition
dynamics, where the expectation is taken over the starting topics as defined by the context dis-
tributionp (c). The KL divergence (4) quantifies the information cost of encoding the transition
matrixAin terms of transition matrixQ[6]. As in our framework it is the Maxwell demonY
that eventually controls both matricesQandA, this information cost is carried by the Maxwell
demon.
Our proposed faithfulness metric, which we call theSemantic Faithfulness(SF) score and
denote asF S, is defined in terms of the minimal value of this KL divergence (4), obtained by
jointly optimizing overAandQsubject to all constraints:
FS := 11 +D min . D min := minA∈CA,Q∈CQ D(A∥Q) (5)
HereC A andC Q denote constraint sets for transition matricesAandQ, respectively, according
to Eqs.(1)-(3). AsD min can take values between zero and infinity, the SF scoreC A ranges
from zero to one. Values of one are attained when the two probability distributionsAandQ
coincide. This implies high faithfulness: the answer produces the same topical shifts that was
implied by the question with respect to the context. Conversely, a small score suggests the
answer’s topical focus diverges significantly from the question’s, indicating a potential lack of
faithfulness.
We hasten to stress here that the minimal KL divergenceD min defined in Eq.(5) is a well-
defined and unique quantity. This follows from the results of Csiszar and Tusn´ ady [4], who
showed that the problem of a joint minimization of KL divergenceD(A∥Q) has a solution as
long as setsC A andC Q are convex, see also [5]. Furthermore, they also provided a constructive
5
approach to find the solution by alternating minimization (AM) with respect to distributions
AandQ. When applied to the problem of joint minimization ofD(A∥Q) under convex
constraints, the AM method of Csiszar and Tusn´ ady is known as the Blahut-Arimoto (BA)
algorithm [3, 2]. Convergence of the BA algorithm to a global minimum follows from joint
convexity ofD(A∥Q) in both its arguments, see [6], p.30. In the following section, we will
present details of an AM scheme for our setting.
3 Computing Semantic Faithfulness (Think Like the Demon!)
This section details the computational algorithm for finding the optimal matricesAandQand
thus computing the faithfulness score.
3.1 The Alternating Minimization (AM) Algorithm
The overall optimization problem is to find the pair of matrices (A,Q) that minimizes the
objective functionD(A∥Q) subject to their respective constraint sets, which we denoteC A
andC Q. As mentioned above, this problem is jointly convex inAandQ, which provides
convergence of the AM algorithm to a global minimum irrespective of a starting point.
The Alternating Minimization (AM) algorithm [4, 5, 6] decomposes the joint optimization
into a sequence of simpler sub-problems. Starting with an initial guessQ (0) for one matrix, the
algorithm proceeds iteratively:
1.A-update Step:WithQ (k) held constant, solve forA (k+1):
A(k+1) = arg min
