{vijayv, gneubig}@andrew.cmu.edu
Abstract
The goal of text clustering is to organize and
understand a corpus, where the meaning of
each cluster matters as much as its membership.
Conventional methods focus on partitioning
a pre-trained embedding space geometrically,
and leave the interpretation of each cluster as
a post-hoc consideration. We invert this de-
sign and make a large language model the core
of clustering: CLUSTERFUSIONextracts ex-
plicit topic definitions and assigns each record
by conditioning on them, so its clusters are
interpretable by construction. It also adapts
to new domains by supplying domain knowl-
edge and user preferences through prompting,
without the costly fine-tuning that conventional
methods require. We further show that the
quality of the extracted topics is governed by
context organization: the exemplars shown to
the model and the order in which they appear,
where reordering the same inputs alone can
shift accuracy by up to 22%, an effect we at-
tribute to attention degradation over long con-
texts. Across three public benchmarks and two
new domain-specific datasets, CLUSTERFU-
SIONachieves state-of-the-art accuracy, with
pronounced gains in specialized domains and
substantially more accurate cluster interpreta-
tion than post-hoc labeling, without any train-
ing. To support future work, we release our
newly constructed dataset and results on all
benchmarks.1
1 Introduction
Text clustering has long been framed as partition-
ing a vector space into compact groups. Elegant
as this geometric view is, it overlooks why we
need clustering: to organize and understand a cor-
pus in service of a downstream goal, whether ex-
ploring patterns in data (Johnson et al., 2023) or
pseudo-labeling a corpus for a downstream task
1https://github.com/YimingXu1213/
clusterFusion/
such as retrieval (Charikar et al., 1997). This goal-
oriented nature imposes two requirements. First,
the method must be conditioned on the goal, since
the task is underspecified without knowing what
the user seeks and which aspects of the data matter
to them (Caruana, 2013). Second, its output must
be interpretable: what each cluster means should
be as clear as which records it contains.In this
paper, we describe an algorithm that (1) flexibly
adapts to a domain by conditioning on domain
knowledge and user preferences, and (2) pro-
duces clusters that are inherently interpretable
and causally related to their associated topics.
Text clustering today is typically done via a
two-step process of extracting embeddings from
a dataset (Muennighoff et al., 2022; Wang et al.,
2022) and applying traditional clustering algo-
rithms (MacQueen, 1967; Day and Edelsbrunner,
1984), yet this process satisfies neither requirement.
The resulting clusters are geometric regions whose
meaning must be recovered post hoc, and condition-
ing them on a new goal or domain demands costly
fine-tuning of the embedder, or semi-supervised
feedback whose annotation cost is impractical at
scale (Basu et al., 2002, 2004; Bae et al., 2020).
Large language models (LLMs) offer a natural
means to condition clustering on a goal stated in
language, and a growing body of work applies them
to clustering (De Raedt et al., 2023; Zhang et al.,
2023; Wang et al., 2023; Viswanathan et al., 2024;
Feng et al., 2024; Jo et al., 2025). In most of these
methods, however, embedding-space clustering re-
mains the primary driver of the output and the LLM
is confined to an auxiliary role, enriching repre-
sentations or refining boundaries post hoc. The
cluster’s interpretation thus remains opaque.
A nascent line of work instead generates clusters
end-to-end with an LLM (Huang and He, 2025), de-
composing clustering into two stages: (1)topic ex-
traction, where the LLM proposes candidate topics
from the corpus, and (2)topic assignment, where
arXiv:2512.04350v2  [cs.CL]  20 Oct 2026
it assigns each record to one of them. As currently
realized, this paradigm faces three limitations that
bear directly on the accuracy and interpretability
of the resulting clusters. First, despite the LLM’s
central role, the organization of its input—which
records it is shown, and in what order—has not
been studied systematically, even though it strongly
affects whether the extracted topics are stable and
coherent. Second, records are assigned to bare
topic names without explicit definitions, leaving
the clusters neither reliably interpretable nor sta-
bly assigned, an ambiguity this line of work itself
notes. Third, there is no systematic mechanism
for incorporating domain knowledge or user pref-
erences, leaving the LLM’s central advantage for
goal conditioning untapped.
We realize this design in CLUSTERFUSION,
which places the LLM at the core of clustering:
it extracts explicit topic definitions from the corpus
and assigns each record by conditioning on them.
Because a cluster’s members then share an explicit,
human-readable description, and it can further in-
corporate user preferences and domain knowledge
through prompting, CLUSTERFUSIONcan explic-
itly target a downstream goal or application. We
make the following contributions:
• A natively interpretable formulation of clus-
tering.Every cluster assignment is condi-
tioned exclusively on explicit topic definitions
mined from the dataset; the definition that de-
scribes a cluster is therefore the sole basis of
its membership, a property termedfaithful-
nessby Jacovi and Goldberg (2020). Unlike
post-hoc interpretation of embedding-based
clustering, our proposed algorithm is inter-
pretable by construction.
• A localization of the task’s difficulty, and
the role of context organization.Decom-
posing clustering into topic extraction and
assignment, we find that assignment is near-
saturated, whereas topic extraction is the bind-
ing constraint on cluster quality. We trace this
bottleneck tocontext organization, the selec-
tion and ordering of the exemplars shown to
the model: holding model, prompt, and sam-
ple fixed, ordering alone accounts for up to a
22% accuracy difference, which we attribute
to attention degradation over long contexts
and upper-bound with an oracle ordering.
• Controllable domain adaptation without
training.We show that domain knowledge
and user preferences can be supplied through
prompting to steer both topic extraction and
assignment, yielding large gains in special-
ized domains where fine-tuning is impractical
(e.g., accuracy on OpenAI Codex rises from
53.8 to 66.0). This is a form of control that
embedding-based methods do not offer.
• State-of-the-art results and new resources.
We instantiate these findings in CLUSTERFU-
SION, a training-free method that attains the
best accuracy across five datasets and yields
substantially more accurate cluster descrip-
tions than embedding-based labeling. We re-
lease a new human-annotated domain-specific
benchmark (OpenAI Codex), our prompt tem-
plates, and results on all benchmarks.
2 CLUSTERFUSIONframework
CLUSTERFUSIONtreats the LLM as the clustering
core by decomposing clustering into two stages:
(1) topic extraction, where the LLM extracts candi-
date topics with their definitions from the corpus,
and (2) topic assignment, where each record is as-
signed to one of the proposed topics. A fundamen-
tal bottleneck lies in the limited context window,
which is particularly restrictive for topic extraction
on large datasets. To mitigate this, we introduce
an embedding-guided subset partition stage that
selects and organizes the input viasamplingand
sorting, described below.
An overview of the framework is shown in Fig-
ure 1, and the pseudocode is provided in Algo-
rithm 1.
2.1 Embedding-guided subset partition
We leverage embedding-based methods to trans-
form the input dataset into a manageable, repre-
sentative, and well-organized sample. This stage
consists of two steps:
Grouping and balanced sampling.Given a
dataset DN ={x 1, x2, . . . , xN } with N records,
we first compute embedding representations:
Z={z 1, z2, . . . , zN }=Embedder(D N )(1)
where zi ∈R d is the embedding vector for record
xi.
We then apply a traditional clustering algorithm
such as KMeans to partition the embeddings into
Figure 1: Overview of the CLUSTERFUSIONframework
Mgroups:
G={G 1, G2, . . . , GM }=Group(Z, M)(2)
where each group Gm ={(x j, zj) :j∈ I m}
contains records and their embeddings assigned to
groupm.
To construct a representative sample DS of size
S, we perform balanced sampling by drawing
⌊S/M⌋ samples from each group. For groups
with sufficient samples (|Gm| ≥ ⌊S/M⌋), we sam-
ple without replacement to ensure diversity. For
smaller groups (|Gm|<⌊S/M⌋ ), we sample with
replacement to meet the target sample size:
DS =
M[
m=1
Sample(Gm,⌊S/M⌋)(3)
Sampling is necessary to respect the LLM’s context
window limit, while balanced sampling amplifies
the signal from low-frequency clusters and ensures
that minority groups remain represented.
Sorting.To improve coherence in the LLM
input, we arrange the sampled records DS =
{xs1, xs2, . . . , xsS } before concatenation. We eval-
uate two sorting strategies:
(i) Cluster-based ordering:Records are sorted
by their predicted group index:
πcluster = argsort({group(xsi)}Si=1)(4)
(ii) Similarity-based ordering:Records are
sorted by their embedding similarity (cosine or Eu-
clidean distance) to the first sampled record:
πsim = argsort({sim(zs1, zsi)}Si=1)(5)
where sim(·,·) denotes a similarity measure (e.g.,
cosine similarity or Euclidean distance).
The final sorted sample is then
D′S ={x sπ(1) , xsπ(2) , . . . , xsπ(S) }, where
π∈ {πcluster, πsim}. Our experiments demonstrate
that this pre-organization improves readability
and reduces fragmentation, enabling the LLM to
generate more consistent and higher-quality topics.
2.2 Summarization: topic extraction
The sampled and sorted records are concatenated
into a prompt for the LLM, which is instructed
to extract a compact set of candidate topics along
with concise definitions. These topics serve as the
proposed cluster labels. This step leverages the
LLM’s strong contextual reasoning abilities, and
also enables the flexible incorporation of domain
knowledge or user preferences directly into the
prompt (e.g., specifying whether ambiguous utter-
ances should be merged into one category or sepa-
rated into distinct clusters). The complete prompt
templates and domain-specific examples are pro-
vided in Appendix B.
2.3 Classification: topic assignment
Each record in the original dataset is then inde-
pendently assigned to one of the candidate topics
through a separate LLM call. The model is con-
strained to choose from the topic list produced in
theTopic Extractionstep; if it outputs an invalid
label, the call will be rerun. The full prompt tem-
plate for this classification step is also detailed in
Appendix B.
3 Experimental setting
3.1 Datasets
We evaluate CLUSTERFUSIONon three widely
used clustering benchmarks and two newly con-
structed domain-specific datasets. The generic
benchmarks allow comparison with prior work,
while the domain-specific corpora test the model’s
ability to adapt to specialized contexts. The exist-
ing generic benchmarks are:
