4https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FUq9qRwrDrI
• Adobe Lightroom: We collected 6,410 cus-
tomer reviews for Adobe Lightroom iOS, an-
notated into 40 categories. This dataset cap-
tures domain-specific terminology and nu-
anced user feedback. Due to Adobe company
policies, we are unable to release the raw data.
However, we include our evaluation results on
this dataset.
3.2 Metrics
Following prior work (Zhang et al., 2021), we eval-
uate the quality of the resulting clusters against
ground-truth labels using normalized mutual infor-
mation (NMI) and accuracy. Accuracy is computed
by identifying the optimal alignment between pre-
dicted clusters and ground-truth labels via the Hun-
garian algorithm (Kuhn, 1955). These metrics are
widely adopted in clustering evaluation as they cap-
ture both distributional similarity and label-level
correspondence.
3.3 Hyper-parameters and design choices
Following prior work, we assume the number
of clusters K is known (Zhang et al., 2023;
Viswanathan et al., 2024). During the embedding-
guided subset partition stage, we set the number of
groupsMto2K, enabling more diverse sampling
across the dataset. In general, choosing M > Kis
recommended as it enhances diversity in the sam-
pled records. In the topic extraction stage, we then
explicitly prompt the LLM to generate exactly K
topics.
The sample size S used for topic extraction is
bounded by the LLM’s context window. In our ex-
periments this bound is not binding for Bank77,
CLINC, Tweet, and Codex, so we use the full
dataset (S=N ); for the larger Adobe Lightroom
corpus (6,410 records) we set S= 1,000. Assign-
ment always covers every record in DN regardless
ofS.
To compare effectively with these approaches,
we use the same base encoders reported for
SCCL, ClusterLLM, Keyphrase Clustering and
LLMEdgeRefine: INSTRUCTOR (Su et al., 2022)
for Bank77 and CLINC, and DistilBERT 5 (Sanh
et al., 2019; Reimers and Gurevych, 2019) for
Tweet. For our newly constructed domain-specific
datasets, we adopt the INSTRUCTOR model.
5Following ClusterLLM and Keyphrase Clustering, we
used a version of DistilBERT finetuned for sentence similarity
classification.
For the LLM component, we use GPT-4o (Hurst
et al., 2024) as the backbone model for both topic
extraction and topic assignment. All GPT-4o calls
use the model’s default parameters, including its
default temperature and token settings. To exam-
ine the generalizability of our framework across
different LLMs, we additionally evaluate GPT-
5 (OpenAI, 2025) and Qwen3 (Qwen3-VL-30B-
A3B-Instruct) (Yang et al., 2025) on the Codex
dataset with default settings. To thoroughly evalu-
ate performance, we run each configuration 5 times
and report the mean and standard deviation.
4 Results
We evaluate CLUSTERFUSIONon three widely
used benchmarks (Bank77, CLINC, Tweet) and
two new domain-specific datasets (OpenAI Codex,
Adobe Lightroom). We compare CLUSTERFU-
SIONagainst strong baselines and state-of-the-art
methods, including DSE (Zhou et al., 2022), PCK-
Means, LLM Correction, and Keyphrase Clus-
tering (Viswanathan et al., 2024), SCCL (Zhang
et al., 2021), ClusterLLM (Zhang et al., 2023), and
LLMEdgeRefine (Feng et al., 2024).
4.1 Clustering performance comparison
Across all five datasets (Tables 1 and 2), CLUS-
TERFUSIONconsistently achieves the highest accu-
racy, with gains especially pronounced on domain-
specific data: on OpenAI Codex, accuracy im-
proves from 44.5 to 66.0 (48% relative increase).
NMI results are similarly strong, with substantial
boosts on OpenAI Codex (56.1 to 72.6, 29% im-
provement) and Adobe Lightroom (46.8 to 60.0,
28% improvement).
4.2 Beyond clustering accuracy: more
accurate interpretations
Standard clustering metrics, accuracy and NMI, as-
sess only whether records are grouped correctly,
not whether the resulting clusters are described
correctly. We therefore introduceinterpretation
accuracy, measuring how closely a method’s clus-
ter label matches the concept it should name:
the cosine similarity between the label and the
Hungarian-matched ground-truth label (Kuhn,
1955), computed with all-mpnet-base-v2 em-
beddings (Reimers and Gurevych, 2019).
On OpenAI Codex, CLUSTERFUSIONattains an
interpretation accuracy of 0.52, against 0.27–0.29
for embedding-based methods (Table 3). This gap
is structural. Because CLUSTERFUSIONextracts a
topic definition and then assigns to it, every cluster
is organized around an explicit description; embed-
ding methods cluster first and attach a label only
afterward, so their labels describe a group they
did not shape. The advantage is intrinsic to the
pipeline rather than to any dataset or ordering: the
score stays between 0.49 and 0.52 across all input
orderings we test.
On top of this structural advantage, the prompt-
based interface lets CLUSTERFUSIONsharpen its
definitions with information embeddings cannot
exploit (Appendix C). Given domain knowledge, it
separates a competitor mention (“Anthropic is in
trouble”) from the generic negative sentiment (“so
bad”) that KMeans conflates; given a user prefer-
ence, it consolidates off-topic presentation remarks
(a salad bowl, the presenter’s beard) into a single
low-priority cluster instead of fragmenting the fixed
cluster budget as Keyphrase does. We quantify the
accuracy gains from these interventions in §4.3.
4.3 Ablation study
Topic extraction limits performance.To dis-
entangle the contributions of topic extraction and
topic assignment, we skip the topic extraction stage
and directly provide ground-truth topics. As shown
in Tables 1 and 2, performance becomes nearly
saturated (accuracy and NMI exceed 85 points
on generic benchmarks and are around 80–90 on
domain-specific datasets), with extremely small
variance. This indicates that the assignment stage
is already highly reliable, and the primary opportu-
nity for improvement lies in topic extraction.
Ordering matters to topic extraction.During
our experiments, we observed that exemplar or-
dering plays a critical role in the quality of topic
extraction. As shown in Table 2, even the unsorted
baseline already outperforms traditional clustering
methods. However, performance improves consis-
tently when records are pre-organized—whether
by cluster assignments (e.g., KMeans) or by em-
bedding similarity (e.g., cosine distance)—as il-
lustrated in Figure 2. On the Tweet dataset, for
instance, accuracy rises by 22% (from 71.6 to 87.1)
solely due to improved ordering.
One explanation lies in how transformer-based
LLMs handle long contexts. While self-attention
allows tokens to interact globally, empirical stud-
ies show that attention effectiveness decays with
distance, making it harder to integrate scattered
