Pre-organizing records into semantically coherent
groups reduces this burden and allows the LLM
to better exploit its contextual reasoning. We fur-
ther validate this with an oracle ordering (sorted by
ground-truth topics): while infeasible in practice,
the consistent gap between heuristic and oracle or-
derings confirms that improved ordering heuristics
could unlock further gains.
The pipeline alone outperforms baselines; do-
main prompting adds further gains.We disen-
tangle CLUSTERFUSION’s gains with an ablation
on extra domain prompting (Table 4). The pipeline
alone is already decisive. With no extra domain
prompting, using only the minimal context avail-
able to baselines, CLUSTERFUSIONachieves the
best accuracy on all five datasets, by a wide mar-
gin on the domain-specific corpora (Codex 53.8 vs.
44.5; Lightroom 78.2 vs. 71.5). This advantage is
structural and needs no extra domain input. Addi-
tional domain prompting then adds a further boost.
Because the LLM is the clustering core, CLUSTER-
FUSIONcan naturally absorb background knowl-
edge and user preferences through prompting. This
boost is large where the LLM lacks domain knowl-
edge (Codex 53.8→66.0; Lightroom 78.2→83.5)
and negligible on the general benchmarks. 6 The
two gains are additive: on Codex, the pipeline con-
tributes +9.3 points and prompting a further+12.2.
It is also important to note that the extra prompting
stays high-level. It supplies background knowl-
edge and coarse organizing preferences (e.g., that
presentation-related comments should form one
cluster). It never enumerates the target clusters or
assigns any record. The model still extracts the
topics from the data and labels each record itself.
For full transparency, we provide the exact prompts
we use for Codex in Appendix B.2.
Best performance across LLM familiesTo eval-
uate generalizability, we additionally test GPT-5
(OpenAI, 2025) and Qwen3 (Yang et al., 2025)
on the Codex dataset. As shown in Table 5 (Ap-
pendix A), our method remains the best-performing
approach across all model families, with order-
ing consistently providing a substantial boost.
The “Assignment Only” setting varies little across
LLMs, while performance differences are more
pronounced in topic extraction, reinforcing that it
is the primary challenge.
4.4 Cost performance
While clustering accuracy is critical, practical de-
ployment also requires methods to be cost-efficient.
Unlike representation-learning approaches such as
SCCL (Zhang et al., 2021) or DSE (Zhou et al.,
2022), CLUSTERFUSIONis training-free: it re-
quires no fine-tuning or GPU-intensive optimiza-
tion, relying only on one-off clustering and prompt-
ing.
Compared to other training-free, LLM-based
methods, CLUSTERFUSIONincurs a cost profile
similar to the Keyphrase method (Viswanathan
et al., 2024), and its two stages scale complemen-
tarily. Topic extraction issues a single LLM call
over a fixed-size sample of S records, so its cost
is independent of the corpus size N; topic assign-
ment issues one call per record, so the dominant
cost is linear in N and, because records are labeled
independently, trivially parallelizable. CLUSTER-
FUSIONthus scales to large corpora at the asymp-
6Even a general benchmark can benefit from a preference
that encodes ground-truth granularity: on CLINC, one instruc-
tion to treat ambiguous responses (“yes”/“no”/“unsure”) as
separate intents rather than one group raises accuracy from
87.1 to 87.3.
totic cost of a single per-record LLM pass, and
when the representative sample exceeds the con-
text window, topic extraction extends naturally to
a batched variant (see Limitations). Crucially, at
comparable cost, CLUSTERFUSIONdelivers sub-
stantially higher accuracy, demonstrating superior
cost-effectiveness.
As shown in Figure 3, CLUSTERFUSIONconsis-
tently achieves higher accuracy at nearly identical
cost across all five datasets, with particularly pro-
nounced gains on OpenAI Codex (a 63.8% relative
accuracy improvement over Keyphrase with less
than $0.02 additional cost); the monetary cost we
report is computed from token-based API pricing
and therefore directly reflects token usage.
5 Conclusion
We recast text clustering with the LLM as its core:
rather than partitioning an embedding space and
labeling the result after the fact, CLUSTERFUSION
extracts explicit topic definitions and assigns each
record by conditioning on them. This renders the
clusters interpretable by construction, and we fur-
ther showed that the extracted definitions are ac-
curate descriptions of the underlying concepts, a
quality embedding-based partitions cannot provide.
Analyzing the paradigm, we found that its difficulty
lies not in assignment, which is near-saturated, but
in topic extraction, whose quality is governed by
context organization: reordering the same inputs
alone shifts accuracy by up to 22%. On this basis,
CLUSTERFUSIONadapts to specialized domains
through prompting alone, without any training, and
attains the best accuracy across five datasets. To-
gether, these results establish LLM-core cluster-
ing as an accurate and interpretable alternative to
embedding-based pipelines, and identify context
organization as the central lever for improving it
further.
Limitations
While CLUSTERFUSIONachieves strong perfor-
mance, several limitations remain. First, for ex-
tremely large datasets, our embedding-guided par-
tition naturally extends to batch processing: em-
beddings can organize samples both within each
batch (via ordering) and across batches (via bal-
anced sampling), enabling hierarchical or parallel
topic extraction while preserving the framework’s
core advantages. Second, our main experiments
assume the number of clusters K is known (Zhang
