et al., 2023; Viswanathan et al., 2024; Zhang et al.,
2021; Feng et al., 2024). This is a deliberate design
choice to enable fair comparison with prior work,
not a constraint of our method. Because topics are
extracted by the LLM, the point value of K can be
replaced with a user-specified range [Kmin, Kmax]
from which the model selects. This requires only
a one-line change to the topic extraction prompt,
and captures a user’s intended granularity better
than a brittle point estimate. This range variant
performs strongly in our internal experiments; we
report fixed-K throughout only because these base-
lines all require a known K, and adapting them for
an apples-to-apples range-based comparison is be-
yond our scope. A systematic study of range-based
and automaticKselection is a natural next step.
Ethics Statement
Data privacy.We use public benchmarks
(BANK77, CLINC, Tweet) and two newly con-
structed ones based on public Adobe Lightroom
and Codex reviews. All sources are public and
free of personally identifiable information (PII),
and comply with their respective terms of use. No
sensitive or proprietary data is involved.
Bias in LLMs.Our method uses GPT-4o (Hurst
et al., 2024) for topic extraction and assignment,
which may reflect biases from its training data.
While our balanced sampling strategy helps mit-
igate this, we acknowledge potential societal or
linguistic bias. Future work could explore fairness-
aware prompting or bias auditing.
Reproducibility.We document each step of our
pipeline in detail. Grouping uses open-source li-
braries (such as KMeans), and LLM-based steps
rely on modular, prompt-based methods. We also
release the dataset and raw experimental results to
support reproducibility.
Environmental impact.To reduce compute cost,
we limit LLM usage through lightweight grouping
and representative sampling. Nonetheless, we rec-
ognize the environmental footprint of LLM infer-
ence and encourage future work on more efficient
alternatives.
