schemes:Augmentationexpands the input context with auxiliary information;Organizationstructures the generation
process via planning; andReflectionrefines the output through self-evaluation and selection.
In theVanillasetting, the model receives a
brief instruction along with the document d and
generates the summary in a single pass, i.e., ˆs=
Msum(d).LRMs(e.g.,O1,O3, GPT-5) use the
same instruction, but rely on implicit, in-model
reasoning.
3.2 Augmentation-based Reasoning (CoT,
E2A, QAG, Cite).
In this scheme, the model is guided by theaugmen-
tation( A) that expands the input with intermediate
representations, such as stepwise reasoning cues,
extracted evidence, or question–answer pairs. We
define this generation process as:
ˆs=Msum(d;A),(3)
where A can be obtained by the following methods:
CoT (Chain-of-Thought).We adopt the CoT
reasoning pattern to summarization (Wei et al.,
2022; Adams et al., 2023; Zhang et al., 2024a).
The augmentation ACoT provides stepwise reason-
ing cues, directly embedded in the input prompt.
The model generates the summary in a single pass:
ˆs=Msum(d;A CoT).(4)
E2A (Extract-to-Abstract).We employ a two-
stage extract–abstract pipeline (Dou et al., 2021;
Zhang et al., 2023a). First, the model extracts K
salient sentences from the document to form the
augmentationA E2A:
AE2A ={e k}Kk=1 =M extract(d),(5)
where ek denotes each extracted sentence. Next,
the model generates the final summary with this
additional context:
ˆs=Msum(d;A E2A).(6)
QAG (Question–Answer Guided).Prior
works (Durmus et al., 2020; Liu et al., 2024; Sinha,
2025) have shown that expert-designed questions
can help models improve summarization faithful-
ness. We design a three-stage procedure that au-
tomatically generates questions. First, the model
generatesHdocument-specific questions:
{qh}Hh=1 =M question(d).(7)
Next, a new model answers each question:
ah =M answer(d, qh).(8)
Finally, the summary is generated conditioned on
the question–answer pairs, which form the augmen-
tationA QAG to generate the final summary:
AQAG ={(q h, ah)}Hh=1,ˆs=M sum(d;A QAG).
(9)
The number of questions H is decided by the ques-
tion model according to the document length.
Cite (Cited Summarization).Inspired by cited
summarization (Ou and Lapata, 2025), we propose
a single-stage, retrieval-augmented setup. The aug-
mentation ACite is a set of N citation units from
the document: ACite ={c i}Ni=1. Each citation unit
ci serves as a referenceable sentence. The model is
prompted to produce both the summary ˆsand a set
of evidence alignmentsR:
(ˆs,R) =Mcite(d;A Cite).(10)
Here, R links each summary sentence to its sup-
porting citations inA Cite.
3.3 Organization-based Reasoning (Deco,
Plan)
This reasoning scheme structures the summariza-
tion process through organization. We denote the
organizational guidance prompt as P . The model
generates the summary as:
ˆs=Msum(d;P),(11)
where P encodes the inferred structure or an ex-
plicit plan outlining the summary’s organization.
Deco (Decomposition).We implement a hier-
archical decomposition strategy designed for long-
document summarization (Chang et al., 2023). The
process has three stages. First, the model segments
the documentdintoJcoherent chunks:
{cj}Jj=1 =M seg(d).(12)
Second, the model generates a local summary uj
for each chunk:
uj =M sum(cj).(13)
Finally, the local summaries are combined into a
structural plan PDeco, which guides the final sum-
marization:
PDeco ={u j}Jj=1,ˆs=M sum(d;P Deco).
(14)
This method captures local information in each
chunk and uses it to improve the global summary.
Plan (Plan-then-Write).Following previous
work (Sun et al., 2024), we propose a plan then
write strategy by introducing a two-stage structural
framework. First, the model generates a compact
planP Plan:
PPlan =M plan(d),(15)
where PPlan includes elements such as
{domain,goal,style,salient_info} , which
outlines the summary’s focus and structure.
Second, the model generates the final summary
conditioned on this plan:
ˆs=Msum(d;P Plan).(16)
This method mimics the human outlining process
to organize key elements before abstraction.
3.4 Reflective Reasoning (IR, SC)
This reasoning scheme refines summaries through
iterative reflection or self-evaluation. It allows the
model to assess its own outputs and improve them
based on the feedback. We denote reflective feed-
back or evaluation cues asR. The process typically
involves three steps: generating one or more candi-
date summaries, evaluating them using R, and then
revising or selecting the final summaryˆs.
IR (Iterative Refine).We adopt the evaluate
then revise framework from prior work (Zhang
et al., 2023b; Lin et al., 2025). The model
iteratively improves its summary through self-
evaluation and targeted revision, with early stop-
ping to prevent over-refinement. The process starts
with an initial draft:
s(0) =M sum(d).(17)
For each iteration t∈ {1, . . . , T}, the model per-
forms a two-step reflective loop. First, an evaluator
module generates structured feedbackR (t)IR :
R(t)IR =M evaluate(d, s(t−1)).(18)
Second, a reviser module incorporates this feed-
back to produce an improved summary:
s(t) =M revise(d, s(t−1), R(t)IR ).(19)
The loop terminates when R(t)IR signals <STOP>, or
after T iterations. The final summary ˆs=s (T) is
taken from the last iteration.
SC (Self-Consistency).Building on self-
consistency from majority voting (Wang et al.,
2022; Chen et al., 2023; Li et al., 2024b), we pro-
pose a rubric-based selection framework for ab-
stractive summarization. First, the model samples
N candidate summaries from its conditional distri-
bution, using a non-zero decoding temperature:
s(q) ∼p M(s|d),(20)
