4 Experimental Setup
4.1 Datasets
As shown in Table 1, we use an evaluation suite
of eight representative datasets covering multiple
domains (Chu et al., 2025), which are categorized
into three groups based on their input structure and
task type. Full details on data pre-processing are
provided in Appendix A.
Short-Form Documents:(1)CNN/DM(Her-
mann et al., 2015) contains concise news articles
paired with human-written highlights. (2)SAM-
Sum(Gliwa et al., 2019) includes messenger-style
dialogues and short abstractive summaries. (3)
Reddit(Kim et al., 2019) features informal, user-
generated posts from the TIFU subreddit. (4)
WikiHow(Koupaee and Ganesan, 2018) provides
instructional articles with step-wise summaries.
These datasets show the single-document summa-
rization (SDS) setting, with short inputs (100–700
tokens) and moderate compression ratios.
Long-Form Documents:(5)ArXiv(Cohan et al.,
2018) consists of long scientific papers and their
abstracts. (6)Multi-News(Fabbri et al., 2019)
merges multiple related news articles into a single
summary, requiring cross-document reasoning. (7)
BookSum(Kry ´sci´nski et al., 2021) includes chap-
ters and narratives summarized by humans. These
datasets evaluate multi-document (MDS) and long-
form narrative summarization (LNS) capabilities,
with inputs up to around 6,500 tokens.
Table-to-Text:(8)SciGen(Moosavi et al., 2021)
pairs scientific tables with descriptive textual sum-
maries, testing structured-to-text generation (TTS).
4.2 Implementation Details
Models.Our experimental evaluation uses the
GPT family via Azure AI API 2. We utilize GPT-
4.1 (1,000K context window) as our main model.
We compare its performance against three LRMs:
O1 (200K context),O3 (200K context), and GPT-5
(400K context).
Decoding Parameters.All configurations run
once for each document with a fixed random
seed for consistency. We use temperature=0
and keep top_p, top_k, and other sampling
settings at their default values. For GPT-
4.1, we set max_output_tokens=1,000. For
the LRMs, we use think_ability=medium and
max_completion_tokens=10,000 because these
models include internal “thinking” tokens in their
completion budget.
4.3 Evaluation Metrics
We evaluate summaries using automatic metrics
grouped by assessment focus: reference similarity,
factual faithfulness, and LLM as a judge.Ref-
erence similarity:We report ROUGE (average
of ROUGE-1/2/L) (Lin, 2004), which measures n-
gram overlap and longest common subsequence be-
tween system and reference summaries. For seman-
tic similarity, we use BERTScore F1 (Zhang et al.,
2020), which computes contextual embedding sim-
ilarity between the two summaries.Factual faith-
fulness:We adopt SummaC (Laban et al., 2022),
which estimates sentence-level consistency via nat-
ural language inference, and AlignScore (Zha et al.,
