VI. Summary abstractiveness is negatively cor-
related with reference-based quality and fac-
tual faithfulness.As outlined in Section 3, we
group systems into five reasoning paradigms and
measure their average abstractiveness (Yuan and
Zhang, 2025). In Figure 5,VanillaandReflec-
tiveare the least abstract and achieve the highest
ROUGE scores.Augmentationmethods fall in the
middle on both metrics.Organizationmethods are
the most abstract and perform the worst on both
metrics. LRMs break this trend: they are relatively
extractive but trade lower ROUGE for higher Sum-
maC. This suggests that abstractiveness interacts
with model family and internal reasoning style.
We further conduct a qualitative case study to in-
vestigate specific model behaviors. As shown in Ta-
ble 2, Deco’s chunk-based aggregation loses global
conditional context, causing it to over-generalize a
conditional rule and apply the “2–3 inches” cook-
ing length to all julienne sticks, while E2A’s ex-
traction bottleneck excludes the final cutting step,
resulting in an incomplete procedure. By compari-
son, Vanilla and GPT-5 largely preserve the original
conditional structure. These examples suggest that
summarization requires accurate compression in-
stead of creative reasoning. Reasoning pipelines
that fragment or filter context risk driving models
to fill non-existent logical gaps or omit important
information. The complete case study table is pro-
vided in Appendix H.
VII. Risk of Over-Thinking: Increased reason-
ing reduces faithfulness without improving qual-
ity.We analyze the effect of a hyperparameter
named “think ability”, which controls the depth
of internal reasoning withinGPT-5. We select
one representative dataset from each of the three
groups: short-form (WikiHow), long-form (ArXiv),
and table-to-text (SciGen). As shown in Figure 6,
factual faithfulness consistently declines as think
ability increases:AlignScoreshows the steepest
drop, whileSummaCexhibits a similar but milder
trend. In contrast, reference-based quality metrics
(ROUGEandBERTScore) remain stable. This ob-
served drop suggests that the LRM can “over-think”
the source instead of maintaining faithful compres-
sion. Overall, too much internal reasoning poses a
clear risk. It undermines factual grounding without
improving summary quality.
VIII. Controlled validation beyond GPT: More
internal reasoning does not always help sum-
marization.Our main benchmark focuses on the
GPT family. To test whether this pattern also ap-
pears beyond GPT, we conduct an additional con-
trolled study onGemini 2.5 Flash. Specifically, we
vary its internalthinking budget( tb) while keep-
ing the summarization prompt fixed toVanilla. We
use the same evaluation protocol and metrics as in
Section 4. Table 3 reports results on three repre-
sentative datasets: short-formCNN/DM, long-form
ArXiv, and table-to-textSciGen.
We find that increasing the thinking budget does
not consistently improve summarization perfor-
mance.BERTScorestays nearly unchanged across
different budgets. For ROUGE, the clearest pat-
tern appears onArXiv, where performance drops
from 0.2284 at tb=0 to 0.1954 at tb=1024. On
CNN/DMandSciGen, the best ROUGE is obtained
at the middle setting tb=256, rather than at the
largest budget. The faithfulness metrics show a sim-
ilarly mixed pattern across datasets. Overall, these
results support our main conclusion that internal
reasoning is highly context-dependent in summa-
rization, and that simply allocating more reasoning
budget does not reliably lead to better summaries.
