H Case Study
To study the potential hallucination caused by the
reasoning (Chen et al., 2026b; Zhang et al., 2026b),
here we show the full example of the generated
summaries in Table 16 from the Wikihow dataset,
together with the golden reference.
I Human Evaluation Guidelines
Our human evaluation used two PhD students, each
independently rating summaries using the rubric
in Table 17 on a 1–5 scale for Conciseness, Com-
pleteness, and Faithfulness. We report the average
of their scores across examples. Here we list the
full human evaluation guidelines used for our man-
ual annotation study, including rating scales and
definitions.
J Emerging Reasoning Directions for
Future Adaptation
Beyond the methods benchmarked in this work,
several recent directions may be adapted to abstrac-
tive summarization in future studies. Integrating
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) (Zhang
et al., 2026a) with reasoning-enhanced summa-
rization represents a promising future direction,
wherein relevant external passages can be dynami-
cally retrieved to supplement key content segments
with additional factual detail (Zhang et al., 2024b).
Moreover, recent analyses of logical phase transi-
tions indicate that reasoning behavior can degrade
abruptly under certain regimes, which is relevant to
stability-aware summarization design (Zhang et al.,
2026c).
At the reasoning-policy level, neuron-level mix-
ture routing has been explored as a way to make
reasoning trajectories more explainable and con-
trollable (Dong et al., 2026), while error-driven
refinement strategies provide another perspective
on improving first-pass solution quality (Jiang
et al., 2026). Reasoning-path search approaches
are also promising for selecting stronger inference
traces before generation (Ling et al., 2026; Chen
et al., 2026a). Semantic-aware frameworks for log-
ical reasoning may further help control interme-
diate reasoning states during summary construc-
tion (Zhang et al., 2026e).
We also note that progress in parameter-efficient
adaptation may offer practical implementation ben-
efits when transferring new reasoning modules to
summarization settings (Dong et al., 2025). Mean-
while, advances in robust cross-modal retrieval
and alignment can provide transferable architec-
tural ideas, even when the source tasks are not
summarization-specific (Li et al., 2026a,b). More
broadly, work on long-horizon multi-agent dynam-
ics suggests another possible route for modeling
planner-decoder interactions in iterative summa-
rization pipelines (Zhang et al., 2026d).
