Appendix
Evaluation Guidelines for Expert Error-Based Summary Annotation
Objective:
Evaluate model-generated plain language summaries of biomedical research abstracts. Annotators assess each summary for
accuracy, completeness, readability, and reader appropriateness, and label specific errors based on the taxonomy below.
Materials Provided:
• Original abstract of the medical article
• Model-generated plain language summary
Evaluation Procedure:
1.Review the Abstract:Read the abstract carefully to understand its methodology, key findings, and context.
2.Evaluate the Summary:Compare the summary to the abstract and your medical expertise.
3. Annotate Errors:Highlight problematic segments in the summary, tag each with the corresponding error type, and
suggest corrections when necessary.
Error Categories:
1. New Information Errors (Expert Judgment Required)
•Incorrect Definitions:Inaccurate or misleading explanations of medical terms or concepts.
•Incorrect Synonyms:Inappropriate simplification or substitution that changes medical meaning.
•Incorrect Background Information:Medically inaccurate or misleading added context.
2. Inference Errors (Check Against Abstract)
•Contradiction:Information directly conflicts with the abstract.
•Omission:Important findings or details from the abstract are missing.
•Jumping to Conclusions:Unsupported generalizations or causal claims.
•Misinterpretation:Inaccurate paraphrasing or simplification of abstract content.
•Entity Errors:Factual errors in names, numbers, dosages, or statistics.
3. Structure and Readability Errors
•Structure Error:Poor organization or illogical flow.
•Grammatical Error:Issues in spelling, punctuation, or sentence structure.
•Persona Relevance:Language is too technical or too simplistic for the target reader.
4. Hallucinations
• Hallucination:Fabricated, irrelevant, or nonsensical content not supported by the abstract or general medical knowledge.
12/16
Annotation Instructions:
• Highlight the erroneous segment in the summary.
• Tag the segment with the appropriate error label (e.g.,Contradiction,Incorrect Definition).
• Provide a corrected version using either the abstract or medical expertise.
