3.5 Dual-Track Model Specialization - Mortgage Language Model (MLM v2)
Both expert models begin with the same foundation: Meta-LLaMA-3.1-8B base model undergoes Continued Pre-training
(CPT) on our mortgage corpus. However, the subsequent specialization paths diverge based on task requirements.
3.5.1 Track 1: Conversational Q&A Specialist via Instruction Residual
Figure 1: Architectural flow for Track 1 (Residual Learning).
Step 1 - Continued Pre-training:Our CPT journey begins with the foundational Meta-LLaMA-3.1-8Bs base model
(Wbase). This step is critical, as our initial baseline (MLM v1) revealed that supervised fine-tuning alone was insufficient
to imbue the model with deep domain knowledge. To address this, we perform CPT on our proprietary domain corpus
(Dmortgage), which consists of millions of specialized tokens. The objective is to shift the model’s internal representations
from general web text to the specific terminology, regulations, and contexts of the mortgage finance domain. This phase
produces a domain-specialized, non-instruction-following model, W (1)cpt , optimized via the standard causal language
modeling loss. The specific hyperparameters for this CPT stage are detailed in Table 6.
W (1)cpt = arg minW Xx∈Dmortgage Llm(x;W)(3)
whereL lm is the causal language modeling loss (next-token prediction).
Step 2 - Instruction Residual (IR):We adopt the instruction residual technique introduced by Jindal et al. [ 2], which
we term Instruction Residual (IR). This technique demonstrates that instruction-following capabilities can be represented
as an additive residual in weight space and transferred to domain-adapted models without requiring Instruction tuning.
Following their methodology, we compute the instruction residual from two reference models in the same architecture
family -W LLaMA-base (pretrained only) andW LLaMA-inst (instruction-tuned):
∆inst =W LLaMA-inst −W LLaMA-base (4)
This residual ∆inst captures the weight changes necessary to acquire instruction-following behavior. Following the
direct addition method proposed by Jindal et al. [2], we apply this residual to our domain-adapted model:
5
WIR =W (1)cpt + ∆inst (5)
Theoretical Foundation from Jindal et al.:The Instruction Residual approach is grounded in the observation that
continued pre-training and instruction fine-tuning operate on approximately orthogonal subspaces in the model’s weight
space [2]. This enables:
1. Transferability: The instruction residual ∆inst generalizes across different training distributions within the
same architecture family, as it encodes task-agnostic instruction-following behavior.
2. Compositionality: Domain knowledge acquired through CPT and instruction-following capabilities can be
linearly combined without significant catastrophic interference.
Jindal et al. demonstrate that this approach successfully balances domain expertise with general instruction-following,
which we validate in the mortgage finance domain.
Step 3 - Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)[9] with LoRA:Finally, we apply DPO using Low-Rank Adaptation
(LoRA) to align outputs with human preferences for conversational quality. This provides a computationally efficient
alignment, as detailed in Section 3.5.3, by freezing the WIR weights and training only low-rank adapters. The
hyperparameters for this DPO alignment are specified in Table 7. The objective is:
WQ&A = arg minW E(x,y+,y−)∼Dpref
