5.4 Efficiency Analysis
The self-routing classification step introduces minimal overhead (empirically measured at <100ms) as it reuses the
already-deployed WQ&A model. The primary efficiency bottleneck in a production environment is typically the inference
backend itself.
Traditional inference, implemented through the Hugging Face Transformers API, exhibits a batch-dependent perfor-
mance ceiling governed by synchronous processing. As batch size increases, throughput scales linearly until GPU
saturation, but latency expands proportionally. Our benchmarks of this static-batching approach (based on 100 total
requests) show throughput saturating at 9.5 req/s at a batch size of 128, but this comes at the cost of average latency
rising from 6.5s to over 10.4s per request. This reflects the intrinsic limitation of static batching—each generation call
must complete before subsequent requests are initiated.
To overcome this, our system uses the vLLM [10] runtime, which introduces a dynamic, streaming-oriented inference
paradigm. We benchmarked the vLLM [ 10] performance by sending 100 requests to a server configured with an
increasing number of concurrent workers, with results detailed in Table 9.
