prompt injection (PI) or jailbreaking, defenses target malicious instructions from the current user or
untrusted data.Middle:In CUP, a legitimate-looking instruction from one user can persist in shared
context and later affect another user, as shown in a real-world attack on Continua [Continua, 2025].
Right:Existing defenses do not cleanly transfer: jailbreak filtering reduces but does not eliminate
CUP, while instruction detectors for PI over-flag benign collaborative messages, reducing utility.
harmful instructions from the user it is currently serving, and (2) prompt injections (PI) test whether
an agent can distinguish trusted instructions from untrusted external data, such as retrieved documents
or tool outputs.CUP targets a different boundary: whether a shared-context agent can correctly
scope instructions across users and tasks.In CUP, an instruction, preference, or constraint issued
by one legitimate user is incorrectly applied to another user or an unrelated workflow. The attack is
harmful not necessarily because the instruction is malicious in isolation, but because it is executed
outside its intended scope. Table 1 summarizes this boundary-based distinction.
This vulnerability is not merely theoretical. Figure 1 illustrates CUP using a real-world attack
onContinua [2025], a collaborative language agent. Unlike jailbreaks, which target harmful user
instructions, or prompt injection, which targets untrusted external data, CUP arises when an instruction
from one legitimate user is applied outside its intended user/task scope. In Figure 1, Mallory’s
instruction could be legitimate for their reminders, but harmful when later applied to other users. This
also explains the defense gap: jailbreak filters only partially help because the instruction need not be
malicious in isolation, while prompt-injection detectors can over-flag benign collaborative messages.
We further validate this threat onElizaOS[AI16zDAO, 2025], where cross-user propagation can
affect higher-impact actions such as financial transactions, calendars, messages, and files.
To systematically study this threat, we introduceMURMUR(Multi-User Reasoning for Multi-task
Utility and Robustness), an evaluation framework that turns existing single-user agent benchmarks into
concurrent multi-user simulations. Rather than requiring entirely new benchmark suites, MURMUR
takes existing single-user benchmark tasks and then runs them as interleaved shared-context sessions
to measure both collaborative utility and vulnerability to cross-user poisoning.
Our findings show that shared-context agents face a security–utility tension.(1) Attack efficacy
and existing defense mismatch:CUP achieves high success rates even with input-filtering jailbreak
defenses, while instruction-detecting PI defenses over-flag benign collaborative messages and harm
utility.(2) Distinction from prompt injection:prompt-injection attacks are largely ineffective with
the same environments and same prompts, while CUP remains successful, showing that the user/task
boundary creates a different failure mode from the instruction–data boundary.(3) Propagation and
persistence:successful attacks carry across later interactions and compromise multiple concurrent
tasks.(4) Defenses:approaches with explicit user/task boundary separation substantially reduce
cross-user propagation, compared to generic input filtering and summarizing other users’ context.
Taken together, these findings highlight a gap between how LLM agents are increasingly deployed and
how they are typically evaluated: shared agents are embedded in multi-user workflows, but standard
safety evaluations largely assume a single user, a single task, and a single instruction boundary. As
agents move from private assistants to persistent collaborators in group chats, workspaces, and social
