2 Background and Related Work
We summarize the most relevant work and compare cross-user poisoning with other attacks in this
section. A full comprehensive survey is in Appendix A.
Single-user vs. multi-user agents.While most prior agent research has focused on single-user agents,
establishing benchmarks for evaluating tool use, reasoning, software engineering, and embodied
interaction [Yao et al., 2024, Lin et al., 2024, Hong et al., 2024, Qian et al., 2023, Laban et al.,
2026], the field is now moving toward multi-user agents. This emerging area is still nascent, with
early work focusing on design challenges, information gathering, and the social dynamics of shared
conversational spaces [Mao et al., 2024, Jhamtani et al., 2025, Wu et al., 2025, Houde et al., 2025].
Our work extends this line by studying its security implications. Closest to our setting, Yang
et al. [2026] formalize multi-user assistants as a multi-principal decision problem and stress-test
instruction prioritization under conflicting user goals, privacy preservation, and coordination. We
study a complementary failure: an instruction planted by one user persists in shared context and is
later executed as an unauthorized tool action for another user or task, which we measure through
benchmark-verified tool execution and persistence, contrast with matched prompt injections, and
mitigate with boundary-scoping defenses.
Agent security and security boundaries.Prior work studies instruction-following failures such as
jailbreaks [Perez and Ribeiro, 2022], prompt injection through retrieved data or tool outputs [Greshake
et al., 2023, Pasquini et al., 2024, Zhan et al., 2024], prompt infection in multi-agent systems [Lee
and Tiwari, 2024], and memory poisoning [Chen et al., 2024c, Xiang et al., 2024]. As shown in
Table 1, these attacks mainly target either the policy boundary, where the current user elicits harmful
behavior, or the instruction–data boundary, where untrusted external content is treated as instructions.
CUP instead targets the user/task boundary in shared-context agents: influence from one legitimate
user is incorrectly applied to another user or unrelated workflow. Thus, CUP is not only a failure to
separate data from instructions, but a failure to scope instructions to the correct user and task.
Why existing defenses do not directly address CUP.Jailbreak defenses target harmful user re-
quests [ProtectAI], while prompt-injection defenses sandbox, sanitize, or deprioritize untrusted
external data [Liu et al., 2025, Zhu et al., 2025, Meta, Zhan et al., 2024, Debenedetti et al., 2024,
Chennabasappa et al., 2025], and action-level guardrails screen individual tool calls [Mou et al.,
2026]. CUP is a distinct threat vector because the adversarial instruction enters through a legitimate
user channel and may be valid for that user’s own task. The challenge is therefore not simply to reject
malicious-looking content, but to determine which user and task an instruction is allowed to govern.
The agent must treat instructions from multiple users as equally valid, creating a unique vulnerability
that current security paradigms do not address. This motivates defenses that explicitly enforce user-
and task-level context separation rather than relying only on input filtering or instruction detection.
3
3 Formalizing and Demonstrating Cross-User Poisoning Attacks
3.1 Setup and Notation
An agent M interacts with a set of users U in a stateful environment E with database DB and
tool/action set A. Interactions proceed in turns. In auser turn, some user sends a message to the
agent. In the followingagent turn, the agent may execute one or more tool actions and then emits a
natural-language response. All turns are recorded in a global transcript Ht = (m1, . . . , mt) after t
messages, where each entry
mτ = (rτ , uτ , xτ , Wτ )
records the role rτ ∈ {USER,ASSISTANT}, speaker uτ ∈ U ∪ {AGENT}, message content xτ , and
tool traceW τ . For user messages,W τ =∅.
When a user ut sends mt = (USER, ut, xt,∅) , the agent conditions on the shared transcript Ht and
produces the next assistant message. During this agent turn, it may execute a sequence of St+1 tool
actions that updateDB. We denote the resulting tool trace by
Wt+1 =
