where as,t+1 ∈ Ais a tool action andys,t+1 is the corresponding observation. The agent then appends
mt+1 = (ASSISTANT,AGENT, x t+1, Wt+1) to the transcript. We say this assistant turnservesthe
user ut. The key difference from single-user agents is that Ht contains interleaved messages and tool
traces from multiple users and tasks.
This model captures common group-facing deployments, where Discord bots, Slack apps, and
customer-service agents observe shared channels while holding tools for moderation, messaging,
refunds, calendars, or account actions [Midjourney, Inc. / Discord, 2026, Slack, 2024, Salesforce,
2024]. In such systems, the same transcript can contain instructions from multiple users, while the
agent’s tools may affect specific users, resources, or workflows. We focus on suchshared-context
agents, where users interact with one persistent agent and build on each other’s conversations [An-
thropic, 2026, Continua, 2025, AI16zDAO, 2025]; architectures that strictly isolate per-user state are
a different interaction model.
3.2 Cross-User Poisoning
Definition.Cross-user poisoning (CUP) occurs when an instruction, preference, memory, or con-
straint introduced by one user is incorrectly applied while serving another user or unrelated task,
causing unauthorized, unintended, or user-inconsistent behavior. CUP is therefore a user/task-
boundary failure: shared agents must not let context from one user’s workflow govern another user’s
workflow, resources, or tool actions unless an explicit sharing or authorization relation permits it.
Threat model.We assume a subset of users Uadv ⊊U is adversarial and the remaining are benign
Ubenign =U \ Uadv. The attackers operate under the same constraints as normal users: they interact
with the agent through the public interface in a black-box manner and under the normal platform
constraints (e.g., token budgets, rate limits). Attackers can send up to B ordinary user messages, each
message triggers a normal agent response, and they cannot edit or delete the transcript history. The
attacker’s messages may be overtly malicious, subtly adversarial, or benign in isolation; the defining
failure is that they later affect other users or unrelated tasks.
Authorization failure and poisoning.CUP does not require mutually untrusted users: permission
to use a shared agent does not confer authority to set rules for other users’ workflows, files, or
tool actions.Authorization failureis the violated security property;cross-user poisoningis the
mechanism, in which an instruction planted in persistent shared state later governs actions taken for
other users, even after the attacker stops interacting (Section 5.2). Nor does CUP require malice: if
Alice tells the agent “CC the emails you analyze to alice@example.com” and the agent applies this
while summarizing Bob’s inbox, Bob’s email is disclosed. Model-level task disentanglement and
system-level authorization are thus complementary mitigations.
Attacker objective.The attacker’s goal is to make the agent execute a malicious action sequence
while serving a benign user. Let Smal = (˜a1, . . . ,˜aL) be the attacker-specified malicious action
template. Let ΠA(W) denote the action sequence in trace W , and let ⪯ denote pattern matching. A
CUP attack succeeds if
∃u∈ Ubenign,∃t ′ > t0 :S mal ⪯Π A(Wt′)
