Real-world demonstration.We validate CUP on two deployed multi-user agents, Continua [Con-
tinua, 2025] and ElizaOS [AI16zDAO, 2025], in private test environments with accounts we control.
Figure 1 already shows a real attack on Continua: Mallory introduces a reminder rule that is valid for
Mallory’s own workflow, but the agent later applies it while serving another user. The final reminder
sent to Zoorb contains Mallory’s link, so the malicious template
SmalCONTINUA = (create_reminder(...,url=...vulnweb.com))⪯Π A(W3)
ElizaOS demonstrates a higher-impact version of the same failure. ElizaOS is an extensible, multi-
platform agent framework for managing Discord/X communities, including moderation, announce-
ments, and participant incentivization. We create a mock airdrop community where users can request
transfers via the agent across different platforms. As shown in Figure 2, a malicious user Krimo first
tells the agent in Discord to send all future airdrops to an attacker-controlled hot wallet. Later, a
benign user on X requests a transfer to a different address, but the agent (represented by the account
Jos) applies Krimo’s prior rule and transfers funds to the attacker’s address:
SmalELIZAOS = (transfer(address=0x4a6b3D...))⪯Π A(Wt′).
These demonstrations show that CUP is not only a benchmark artifact. An instruction introduced by
one user can persist in the shared state and later parameterize privileged tool calls for another user,
potentially affecting financial actions (as in this case), calendars (as in Continua), messages, or files.
In practice, this attack vector can be exploited intentionally or unintentionally. In public or semi-
public forums such as Discord, adversaries can deliberately exploit it. In more trusted settings such
as Slack, workspaces, or group chats, even benign user preferences can persist beyond their intended
scope and interfere with other users’ interactions. The risk is therefore not limited to overtly malicious
prompts; it stems from the agent’s inability to maintain correct user and task boundaries.
4 MURMUR: Framework for Evaluating Multi-User Agents
Section 3 defines CUP as a user/task-boundary failure. To measure this failure systematically, we
introduce MURMUR (Figure 3), a modular framework for evaluating shared-context agents in
multi-user, multi-turn settings. MURMUR scales by lifting existing single-user agent benchmarks
into interleaved multi-user simulations: new domains can be added by plugging in existing tasks,
tools, environments, and verifiers rather than creating multi-user benchmarks from scratch. With
LLM-simulated users, this enables joint evaluation of benign task utility and cross-user robustness
across agents, attacks, and defenses.
5
4.1 Framework Design
Framework components.A MURMUR environment consists of four components:(1)a stateful
environment (E, DB)with structured data such as files, messages, user profiles, or reservations;(2)
a tool set A whose calls are deterministic state transitions over DB;(3)a benign task set, where each
task θ includes participating users, personas ρ, a task description D, and a reference action sequence
Aθ used by the benchmark verifier; and(4)an attack factory that pairs attacker objectives with target
action sequences Smal. This lets the same environment measure both benign task completion and
whether one user’s instruction propagates to other users or tasks.
