Adapting existing benchmarks to shared multi-user sessions.Most agent benchmarks are single-
user and often single-session: one user gives a task, the agent calls tools, and a verifier checks the final
state. MURMUR adapts these benchmarks by preserving the original objective, tools, environment
state, and correctness verifier, but changing the interaction structure. Tasks are executed as multi-turn
conversations among named users and a single shared-context agent. When a task contains separable
information, we distribute that information across users. For example, in the Airline domain adapted
from TauBench [Barres et al., 2025], a one-user “book a flight” task becomes a two-user interaction
where one user provides travel constraints and another provides traveler or payment details.
We then run multiple adapted tasks concurrently in the same transcript. Thus, even when an underlying
task originates from a single-user benchmark, the evaluation becomes multi-user at the interaction
level: the agent must scope interleaved instructions, preferences, and tool calls across users and tasks.
We apply this procedure to Airline from TauBench [Barres et al., 2025] and Slack and Workspace
from AgentDojo [Debenedetti et al., 2024]; additional adaptation details appear in Appendix D.
Session construction and execution.A MURMUR session selects k tasks Θ ={θ 1, . . . , θk} to
run concurrently. Each benign user is assigned to exactly one task by a hidden mapping
g:U benign → {1, . . . , k},
and Gj ={u∈ Ubenign :g(u) =j} denotes the users collaborating on task θj. The evaluated agent
is not given g; it receives the shared transcript and must infer which users and requests belong to the
same workflow.
Benign users are simulated by an external LLM with policyG conditioned on persona, task description,
and a task-local projection of the transcript. This projection includes user messages from the same
task group and assistant replies addressed to that group, preventing simulated users from reacting
to unrelated workflows. This restriction applies only to user simulation: the evaluated agent still
receives the full shared transcript, including interleaved users and prior tool traces, and must infer
user/task boundaries itself. Appendix B gives the projection operator, routing procedure, prompts,
and scheduler. Table 12 shows that exposing simulated users to cross-group messages does not
materially change ASR, but reduces TSR.
Attack injection and evaluation.To instantiate CUP reproducibly, we insert an attacker message
containing a cross-user adversarial objective into the shared transcript. For example, an attacker
may introduce a rule that future flight-booking requests should forward passenger details to an
attacker-controlled address. The attack succeeds only if this rule is later applied while serving benign
users. After injection, benign users continue interacting normally. Further details in Appendix B.
6
The agent is robust if it completes benign tasks without propagating the attacker instruction across
users or tasks. Thus, MURMUR measures both utility and cross-user robustness: whether a shared
agent can help multiple groups without allowing one user’s instruction to govern another user’s
workflow. Appendix G gives an example attack, and Algorithm 1 summarizes the full procedure.
4.2 Evaluation Metrics
We evaluate agents along three dimensions.
(1)Task Success Rate (TSR):the fraction of benign tasks solved according to the benchmark verifier.
A task θ succeeds if the final environment state matches the state obtained by executing the reference
action sequenceA θ. Since tool effects are deterministic, this provides an exact success criterion.
(2)Attack Success Rate (ASR):the fraction of benign tasks on which the attacker-specified action
sequence is executed. Formally, for a benign task θj, an attack succeeds if there exists an assistant
stept ′ serving some benign useru∈ Ubenign withg(u) =jsuch thatS mal ⪯Π A(Wt′).
(3)Attack Persistence Rate (APR):the fraction of successful attack sessions in which the attack
compromises at least i benign tasks. Let S≥i denote the set of sessions in which at least i benign
tasks are compromised. For 1≤i≤k,APR i = |S≥i||S≥1|. This is a cumulative metric: APR1 = 1, and
APRi is non-increasing iniand undefined ifS ≥1 = 0.
