user’s own task: asking an agent to remember a preference, include a link, or follow a workflow
rule can be legitimate for that user. The violation arises later, when the agent applies that instruction
outside its authorized user or task scope. Thus, jailbreak-style input filters provide only partial
protection.
Instruction-level prompt-injection defenses are mismatched for a different reason. In CUP, the
adversarial content enters through the same user channel used for ordinary collaboration, where
benign users routinely issue instructions. As a result, detectors that flag instruction-like messages
over-flag benign user messages, thus having a high false-positive rate: on benign user messages,
DataSentinel [Liu et al., 2025], MELON [Zhu et al., 2025], PromptGuard [Meta], and a GPT-5.4
judge flag 89.06%, 100.00%, 53.12%, and 35.53% of messages respectively (Appendix H). For
consistency, all our experiments use the main attacker-message format described in Appendix E;
however, the same appendix shows that CUP is not specific to this format, since alternative minimal
and natural-language attacker messages also retain substantial ASR.
Agent guardrails.We also test two guardrail components (Appendix H). With LlamaFirewall’s
PromptGuard 2 input filter [Chennabasappa et al., 2025], GPT-4.1 retains 42.7% CUP ASR, mirroring
ProtectAI. The action-level guard TS-Guard [Mou et al., 2026] trades security for utility: it detects
31.5% of successful CUP sessions while disrupting 1.20% of benign ones, or 85.4% while disrupting
27.93%. Violations depend on which user authorized an action, not its surface semantics.
Prompt injections are largely ineffective, but CUP remains effective.To test whether CUP is
merely a stronger prompt-injection template, we compare PI and CUP on theSlackandWorkspace
environments from AgentDojo [Debenedetti et al., 2024], along with their corresponding multi-user
versions in MURMUR. Airline is excluded because it does not have a corresponding PI baseline.The
same attack objectives, input templates, and inputs are used for both PI and CUP; the only difference
is the attack channel.For PI, the malicious string is introduced through retrieved data. For CUP, it is
introduced by another legitimate user in the shared session.
Table 4 shows that current frontier models achieve less than 5% PI ASR in these settings, while CUP
remains nontrivial and often high. This contrast is consistent with our boundary-based framing: PI
exploits the instruction–data boundary, whereas CUP exploits the user/task boundary. Thus, the same
attack objective is much more effective when it enters through another trusted user in a shared multi-
user context. This is also consistent with recent deployment trends: agent systems are increasingly
hardened against prompt injection using adversarial training and layered safeguards [OpenAI, 2025],
while analogous defenses for separating instructions across users remain underdeveloped.
5.2 Attack Persistence and Effects of Task Concurrency
We next evaluate whether a single attacker insertion can persist across later benign tasks. Unless
otherwise specified, persistence is measured with one attacker insertion and five benign task groups
(k= 5 ). We summarize propagation using Attack Persistence Rate (APR), which measures the
fraction of successful attack sessions that compromise at least i benign tasks, and the average number
of compromised tasks as concurrency varies. GPT-5.4 is omitted from persistence experiments due to
API cost constraints.
