Defended TSR is measured under attack; no-defense TSR is without attack or defense.
Successful attacks are highly persistent.At k= 5 , once a CUP insertion succeeds, it often carries
forward across later benign tasks even after the attacker stops interacting. In Slack, Workspace, and
Airline, respectively, successful attacks compromise four or more of the five benign task groups in
approximately 58%, 54%, and 60% of sessions with at least one successful CUP attack (Figure 5
left). This shows that CUP can become a persistent shared state rather than a one-turn failure.
Model-specific persistence counts appear in Appendix Tables 13, 14, and 15.
Concurrency increases the attack blast radius.Figure 5 (right) measures the average number of
compromised benign tasks as the number of concurrent tasks increases. Conditioned on at least one
successful compromise, attacks propagate to more tasks as concurrency grows. This is consistent with
our user/task-boundary framing: when more workflows are interleaved in the same shared context,
the agent has more opportunities to apply one user’s instruction outside its intended scope.
Utility under concurrency.Given the high ASR and APR above, we next ask whether CUP is due
to a broader difficulty for LLMs in handling concurrent multi-user contexts. Prior work shows that
LLMs degrade in multi-turn conversations, often making early assumptions, relying on prior mistakes,
and failing to recover [Laban et al., 2026]. Multi-user concurrency adds another challenge: the agent
must track not only dialogue state, but also which user and workflow each instruction belongs to. We
observe a similar pattern in benign MURMUR sessions. With GPT-4.1, TSR remains stable through
moderate concurrency but drops slightly at higher concurrency, while tool calls initially decrease and
then spike as more tasks are interleaved (Appendix I).
Interpretation.Together with the high ASR/APR, these trends suggest that current instruction-tuned
agents struggle to maintain fine-grained user and task boundaries in shared context. They condition
on coarse roles such asSYSTEM,USER, andASSISTANT, while multi-user transcripts require tracking
which specific user and workflow each instruction should govern. This motivates defenses that
explicitly isolate or scope context by user and task.
5.3 Boundary-Scoping Defenses
We evaluate three lightweight boundary-scoping baselines that modify how shared context is exposed
to the agent.(1)Task clusteringuses an LLM classifier to estimate task groups online and gives
the agent only messages and tool traces from the inferred cluster.(2)Context augmentationuses
the same inferred task groups, but unlike clustering, it does not remove any transcript entries.
Instead, it prepends each message with its inferred task-cluster ID and task summary, and instructs
the agent to apply prior instructions only within their authorized user/task scope.(3)Selective
Summarizationgives the agent the current user’s messages and agent replies, plus an LLM-generated
summary of other messages and tool calls the LLM finds relevant. These baselines compare context
isolation, explicit boundary annotation, and generic context compression while retaining realistic
errors introduced by model-generated routing and summarization.
Explicit boundary information helps.Because LLM-based routing and summarization are stochas-
tic, we repeat each defense evaluation three times and report 95% confidence intervals (Table 5).
Boundary-aware context management substantially reduces non-adaptive CUP. Task clustering yields
no successful attacks in any environment across all runs (95% upper bounds of 5.5%, 4.6%, and
9
7.6%), showing that removing unrelated task context is an effective containment strategy in our
evaluated setting. Context augmentation also yields zero observed ASR in Slack and Workspace, and
5.7% in Airline. These results support our main claim: CUP is driven by failures to preserve user/task
scope, and mechanisms that expose or enforce this scope can reduce cross-user poisoning.
Defenses induce distinct utility trade-offs.Clustering modestly reduces TSR, likely because the
classifier occasionally misroutes messages, whereas context augmentation preserves the full history
and raises TSR above the undefended baseline in Slack and Workspace. Clustering thus gives the
lowest observed ASR, while augmentation retains more utility. Since defended TSR is measured
under attack and the baseline is benign, these comparisons are conservative for the defenses.
Selective context summarization is insufficient.Summarization remains highly vulnerable (23–48%
ASR) and lowers TSR by 15–25 points, likely because useful context is lost. Summaries can preserve
the attacker instruction as apparently valid shared state while discarding surrounding evidence about
who issued it and when it should apply. Thus, context compression alone does not enforce user/task
boundaries; it can even carry poisoned instructions forward in a more authoritative form.
Adaptive attacks remain open.These defenses are useful building blocks, but they rely on ML/LLM
routing, labeling, and summarization. An adaptive attacker could try to enter the victim’s cluster,
spoof delegation or admin approval, or induce summaries that preserve poisoned instructions as
shared state. Thus, our results show that boundary information helps, but robust mitigation likely
requires stronger classifiers, trusted platform metadata, and tool-level authorization in addition to
model-based context management.
6 Discussion, Limitations, and Future Work
Our results expose a reliability gap in shared multi-user agents. These systems are useful because
they preserve context across users and workflows, but that same context lets one user’s instruction
persist, enter the agent’s working state, and later govern another user’s workflow. As agents move
into group chats, workspaces, customer-support systems, and social platforms with privileged tools,
they cannot be assumed to inherit the safety properties of single-user assistants.
CUP is not caused simply by transcripts exceeding the agent’s context capacity. Summarization
shortens context but can preserve attacker instructions as shared state while removing evidence about
their intended scope. In contrast, task clustering and context augmentation reduce ASR by making
user/task boundaries explicit: clustering removes unrelated workflows, while context augmentation
keeps the transcript but annotates it with inferred task IDs and summaries. Multi-user agents should
therefore organize context around user/task scope, rather than merely compressing or filtering it.
Our defense mechanisms, though, are practical first steps, not complete solutions. Following our
disclosure, Continua adopted context truncation and implemented a task-clustering-based mitigation,
and ElizaOS introduced stricter mappings between related tasks. This suggests that scoped context
management is actionable in deployed systems. Robust agents will likely need context selection,
trusted platform metadata, and tool-level authorization, so actions affecting users, accounts, files,
calendars, or wallets require approval from the affected user or an authorized delegate.
Future work should study adaptive attacks against boundary-scoping defenses, targeted in-cluster
attacks, retrieval-based memory architectures, and authorization policies for shared tools. Multi-user
agents also need platform protections such as authentication, rate limiting, and Sybil resistance, since
adversaries may create identities to infiltrate multiple task clusters.
7 Conclusion
We identified cross-user poisoning, in which one user’s instruction to a shared-context agent is
later applied while the agent serves another user or task. We demonstrated CUP on two deployed
agents and introduced MURMUR to measure it: CUP achieves high and persistent attack success,
outperforms matched prompt injections, and is not resolved by existing filters and guardrails, while
boundary-scoping defenses reduce it with distinct utility trade-offs. User/task scoping should be a
first-class security property of collaborative agents.
