C MURMUR algorithm
D Benchmark Adaptation Details
Overview.MURMUR adapts existing single-user, single-task agent benchmarks into shared multi-
user sessions without requiring entirely new task suites. The adaptation preserves each benchmark’s
original task objective, tools, environment state, and success verifier wherever possible. The main
change is the interaction structure: tasks are converted into multi-turn conversations among named
users and then interleaved with other tasks in a shared transcript. Table 2 summarizes the resulting
environments, tasks, users, and attack objectives, while Figure 3 illustrates the overall adaptation and
evaluation workflow.
Environment and tool conversion.For each source benchmark, we first port the structured
environment state and tool interface into the MURMUR execution platform. The initial database
state DB is kept consistent with the original benchmark whenever possible, and tools remain
deterministic read/write operations over this state. This allows benign task success to be evaluated
using the original benchmark verifier, while adding a shared multi-user interaction layer on top.
Converting tasks into multi-turn user interactions.Each task is converted into a user-facing
multi-turn interaction. When the task naturally contains separable pieces of information, we distribute
those pieces across multiple users. For example, in the Airline environment adapted from Barres
et al. [2025], a one-user “book a flight” task can be converted into a two-user interaction: a primary
customer provides the route and travel dates, while a companion provides traveler details or payment
information. The agent must communicate with both users to complete the same underlying booking
objective, which remains verifiable by the original benchmark’s evaluation criteria. An illustrative
