Airline task conversion is shown in Appendix F. If some task does not contain separable information,
such as a ""send email with summary of a.txt"" task from the Slack environment adapted from
[Debenedetti et al., 2024], we keep it as a single user task, but ensure that the user simulator makes it
a multi-turn conversation by not giving the entire task description in one go.
Interleaving tasks into shared sessions.After task conversion, MURMUR runs k >1 (or k= 1
with a malicious task) adapted tasks concurrently in the same shared transcript. The agent observes
interleaved messages from multiple users and must infer which requests and preferences, and therefore
which tool calls, belong to which user and task. This creates the user-boundary and task-boundary
challenge studied in our work. Importantly, this challenge does not require every individual task
to involve multiple users. Even when a task involves only one user because the original goal does
not naturally decompose across users, MURMUR evaluates it in a shared session by interleaving it
with other users’ tasks. The resulting evaluation tests whether one shared-context agent can maintain
boundaries while serving several users concurrently. Algorithm 1 gives the full session-generation
and execution procedure.
User personas.Across environments, we instantiate users with 30 personas spanning varied
interaction styles and constraints, including polite, curt, upset, weak-English, partial-information, and
differently cooperative users. These personas are used to generate more varied multi-turn interactions
while preserving the original task goals and verifiers. Representative persona examples and simulator
prompts are provided in Table 6.
Adapting adversarial objectives.If a source benchmark includes adversarial tasks or attack
objectives, MURMUR adds them to the attack factory together with their required tool-action
sequences. This allows the same environment to evaluate both benign task success and cross-user
