LlamaFirewall.We evaluate the PromptGuard 2 input classifier from LlamaFirewall [Chennabas-
appa et al., 2025] ( Llama-Prompt-Guard-2-86M) at its default threshold. With GPT-4.1, CUP
retains 42.7% ASR. As with ProtectAI, input filters are effective when a message resembles a
jailbreak, but CUP can be expressed as an ordinary instruction whose harm arises only when it is
applied outside the issuing user’s scope. We evaluate only this input-filtering component, not the full
LlamaFirewall framework.
ToolSafe.We evaluate TS-Guard, the step-level action guard from ToolSafe [Mou et al., 2026], at
two risk thresholds (Table 9). The conservative setting causes little benign disruption but detects
only 31.5% of successful CUP sessions; the aggressive setting detects 85.4% but disrupts 27.93%
of benign sessions. Like DataSentinel and MELON, TS-Guard is therefore mismatched to the
multi-user setting. It is weakest in Airline, where actions such as send_certificate for $50
