Figure 6: Utility and tool-use behavior under increasing task concurrency. TSR remains stable through
moderate concurrency, while tool calls initially decrease and then rebound at higher concurrency.
Multi-user sessions provide utility benefits.Shared sessions can reduce coordination overhead by
allowing the agent to query the relevant participant directly. For example, in flight-modification tasks,
one user may provide itinerary constraints while another provides reservation or payment details; the
agent can collect both within the same session instead of requiring users to coordinate over external
channels. We therefore evaluate how utility changes as task concurrency increases.
We measure benign sessions with varying concurrency (k∈ {1, . . . ,5}) using GPT-4.1. Figure 6
reports per-task task success rate (TSR) and average tool-call counts. More detailed results and
confidence intervals are provided in Tables 16 and 17.
Utility declines slightly with higher concurrency.Relative to the single-task case, agents can
sometimes improve performance at low concurrency by using shared context. Figure 6a shows that
per-task utility remains stable through moderate concurrency, but declines slightly as concurrency
increases further. This suggests that overlapping histories can be useful for coordination at low
concurrency, but induce interference and context confusion at higher concurrency. Overall, the values
remain close, indicating that multi-user task solving remains viable while still exposing boundary
failures.
Tool calls decrease at moderate concurrency, then rebound.As shown in Figure 6b, the average
number of tool calls per task initially decreases at moderate concurrency, then increases sharply at
five tasks. This pattern reflects two competing dynamics: at moderate concurrency, the agent can use
shared context from previous turns to avoid redundant tool calls; at higher concurrency, accumulated
context becomes cluttered, forcing the agent to make additional recovery-oriented calls. This dip-
rebound pattern mirrors the rise-fall trend in task success, suggesting that early efficiency gains from
shared context eventually give way to inefficiency as context becomes harder to disentangle.
