to 8, where our framework successfully detects and revises
hallucinations.
Results on Module Applicability
To assess the generalizability of our framework components,
we conduct module-level ablation experiments across dif-
ferent backbone LLMs. Specifically, we evaluate the perfor-
mance of thedetection module(Table 10) and therevision
module(Table 11) when integrated with various base mod-
els.
Detailed Prompt Examples Used in Experiments
We present the exact prompts used in our experiments, in-
cluding ourstructural-reasoning mechanism(Table 12),
which encourage more structured and faithful reasoning, as
well asbaseline prompt strategies(Table 13) adapted from
prior works for comparison.
Error Analysis
We analyze the failure cases in hallucination detection,
which can be broadly categorized into three types:
•Misunderstanding of Language:The model fails to
correctly interpret the question or the answer.
•Lack of Background Knowledge:The model is unable
to detect hallucinations when it lacks access to essential
domain or factual knowledge required for verification.
•Incorrect Reasoning:The model starts with a correct in-
terpretation or premise but makes logical mistakes during
the reasoning process, leading to incorrect conclusions.
In the following, we provide representative examples il-
lustrating each of the three identified error types.
