2. Related Work
Attribution graphs and replacement models.Attri-
bution graphs operationalize feature →logit pathways
in a local replacement model that linearizes the
residual stream through CLT or SAE features and
freezes attention (Ameisen et al., 2025; Lindsey et al.,
2025a). https://www.neuronpedia.org/ exposes these
graphs for selected models and displays per-feature cards
at scale (Neuronpedia, 2025; Lindsey et al., 2025c).
Circuit-tracer provides the open-source reference
implementation (Safety Research contributors, 2025) for
computing these graphs. Our work operates entirelydown-
streamof attribution: we do not propose a new attribution
method, but we do produce supernodes that Neuronpedia
tooling can render and pin. Our work proposes a clustering
technique that reduces the human labor required to produce
supernode labels.
Causal circuits with transcoders.Hanna & Ameisen
(2026) use cross-layer transcoder feature circuits to study
whether language models latently plan rhyming words,
structuring their analysis around two falsifiability condi-
tions (predict-token-before-emission, causally-affect-token).
Marks et al. (2025) apply sparse-feature circuit analysis to
subject–verb agreement; we contribute a domain-general
harness and a proposal on how to generate groupings of
attribution graphs on scale.
Causal interpretability and matched random controls.
Geiger et al. (2025) formalize three levels of interpretive
claim and emphasize thatdistinguishinglevels requires
intervention. Heap et al. (2025) show that SAE auto-
interpretability scores (Bills et al., 2023; Paulo et al., 2024)
and standard SAE reconstruction metrics can be similar for
randomly-initialized and trained transformers, highlighting
the need for metrics anchored to causal effect. Our matched-
random-control protocol (§3.8) is designed to address this
critique: any structural property a random feature set shares
with the labeled one (count, layer histogram, eligibility)
is held constant; only the concept alignment changes. We
also compare our intervention technique with substituting
the most influential features, showing that our grouping
is working not only by increasing the number of features
steered.
3. Method
3.1. Domains and seed prompts
We set four domains spanning two-hop factual recall in
different relational structures and answer templates. Each
domain has a fixed seed-prompt with three semantic fields
(input, intermediate, answer):
• USA: “capital of state containingX”; 50 entities, 2,450
non-identity swap pairs.
• Books: “character X appears in a book by”; 10 entities,
90 non-identity swap pairs.
• Products: “founder of the company that makes X”; 12
entities, 132 non-identity swap pairs.
• Paintings: “first name of the painter of X”; 10 entities,
90 non-identity swap pairs.
3.2. Setting and attribution-graph generation
We use Gemma-2-2B with the public CLT dictionary
(∼2.5M features over 26 layers; Hanna & Piotrowski, 2025;
2
Automatically Interpreting Attribution Graphs via Probe Prompting
Ameisen et al., 2025). Attribution graphs are generated
via the Neuronpedia API; exact API parameter names and
values are in Section B. The replacement model freezes
attention during graph computation.
3.3. Probe prompt generation
Given a prompt used to generate the attribution graph p
(e.g., “the capital of the state containing Dallas is”), we
use an LLM to generate a small set of concept-targeted
probes. First, we extract the K most salient concepts from
p (and, when available, from the model’s output), each
tagged with a short description. The second pass turns the
extracted concepts into probe prompts that share the seed’s
surface structure (same prepositions, same answer position,
comparable length) while varying the content entities. A
typical seed yields 4–5 probes. The full system prompt and
the concept-to-probe template are reproduced in Section B.
3.4. Cross-Prompt Activation Signatures (CPAS)
A peak on a single prompt is ambiguous: a feature that
activates on is in the seed could be a dictionary detector
for the copula, a context-specific feature, or a procedural
feature that promotes whatever output the model is about
to emit. The CPAS resolves the ambiguity by aggregating
peaks across deliberately varied probes (Figure 2).
For each (feature, probe prompt) pair we record a small set
of per-probe measures: at each token position we measure
the feature’s peak activation, how sparsely the feature fires
across the probe set, and a robust z-score and cosine simi-
larity of the feature’s activations relative to the seed prompt.
Aggregating across probes yields a compact per-feature
signature (the CPAS): a handful of summary numbers cap-
turing how consistent the peak token is across probes, how
many distinct tokens the feature peaks on, and the feature’s
confidence in its dominant role. Formal definitions and
raw-measure specifications are in Section B.
3.5. Functional vs. semantic tokens; target-token
mapping
Tokens are labeledfunctional(eg. copulas, articles, preposi-
tions) orsemantic(content-bearing). When a feature peaks
on a functional token, a ±7-token directional search iden-
tifies the nearest semantic peak (e.g., forward for articles;
backward for the possessive’s).
3.6. Four functional roles
Each feature is assigned one of four functional roles. The
four-role vocabulary is an empirical extension of the supern-
ode types that appear in circuit-tracing case studies (Lindsey
et al., 2025a; Ameisen et al., 2025); we found that these four
roles covered the vast majority of features we encountered
during pilot analyses, and we formalize them here as the
pipeline’s output types.
• Semantic-Dictionary (SEM-DICT).The feature
fires consistently on thesamesemantic token across
probes—a dictionary-like detector for a specific con-
cept (e.g., a feature that reliably peaks on “Texas”
whenever Texas is mentioned).
•Semantic-Concept (SEM-CONC).The feature peaks
on semantic tokens across a small family of related to-
kens rather than on a single one—a concept-level rather
than token-level detector, typical of middle layers.
• Relationship (REL).The feature does not concentrate
its activity on any particular token; it fires diffusely
across the probe, with a comparatively dense activation
pattern. These features appear to encode a relation or
context rather than a named entity.
• Say-X (SAY-X).The feature peaks on functional to-
kens (e.g., is, the) in predictable positions relative
to a semantic target, and sits in the later half of the
network. After the ±7-token directional search (Sec-
tion 3.5), the feature is named by thetargetsemantic
token that follows or precedes the functional peak.
The exact thresholds and priority ordering are in Section B.
A post-hoc clustering analysis of the per-feature CPAS met-
rics confirms that the four-role partition is best read as a
deliberatecoarse-grainingof a finer natural geometry; de-
tails in Section J.
3.7. Supernode formation and naming
Same-role same-name features form a supernode. Naming
is role-specific.
Semanticfeatures (both SEM-DICTand SEM-CONC) are
named by the semantic token at which they activate most
strongly: a feature that consistently peaks on “Texas” re-
ceives the nameTexas.
Say-Xfeatures are named by the semantic token they appear
to promote: e.g.,Say (Austin)for a feature peaking on is
immediately before “Austin”.
Relationshipfeatures are named by the highest-activation
semantic token . The format is(token) related, producing
names like(containing) relatedfor features that fire dif-
fusely on spatial-relationship phrases.
3.8. Causal validation of grouping
We test whether supernodes have an operational causal con-
sequence. For a source–target pair $(e A, e B)$, the inter-
vention asks: if we suppress the features labeled as related to
the source entity and amplify the features labeled as related
