hit, Phase 2 uses the fact that the steering effect typically has
a sharp onset: we compute the KL divergence of the steered
output against the unsteered baseline at each probed M, lo-
cate the interval in which KL rises most steeply, and binary-
refine inside that interval for 6 further steps, accepting a hit
at any refinement point. Full pseudocode in Section G.
4. Results
4.1. State capital case study
Lindsey et al. (2025a) use attribution graphs to investigate
two-step reasoning with the prompt “The capital of the state
containing Dallas is”, and we start from the same template.
The Gemma 2 CLT attribution graph for this prompt con-
tains 1,182 features at our cumulative-influence threshold.
Running these features through the probe-prompt pipeline
