Automatically Interpreting Attribution Graphs via Probe Prompting
A. Reproducibility Statement
To support the workshop’s emphasis on reproducibility and code/data access, we release the following anonymously for the
review period.
• Code.Repository at https://github.com/peppinob-ol/attribution-graph-probing (commit
hash and license inREADME.md).
• Datasets.The full 44,596-run swap dataset (graphs, prompts, activations, supernode groupings, swap dumps for all
five domains) at the same URL, with a per-domain manifest in Section R.
• Interactive demo.An anonymized HuggingFace Space at https://huggingface.co/spaces/Peppinob/
concept-swap-explorerthat showcases concept swaps across 82 entities in 4 domains.
• Per-domain CLI examples.Section R gives end-to-end commands for the USA, Books, Products, Paintings, Sounds
pipelines, including the exactτ,M, and seed used for each table and figure.
• Determinism.The pipeline is fully deterministic (sha256-seeded random controls; fixed temperature/seed/penalty for
generation; no random initialization); per-feature checkpoints permit resumption from partial runs.
• Hardware.A single L4 GPU is sufficient to reproduce CPAS for any single entity in 14–24 minutes (graph generation
+ activation measurement + grouping + subgraph), excluding the swap sweep; the full swap sweep is documented
per-domain in Section R.
Appendix
The appendix is organized to track the main paper: §B–§D give a detailed reference for the pipeline, its functional vocabulary,
and emblematic feature examples per role; §E collects worked steering case studies; §F–§H back up the field-additivity,
M-search, and steering-strength discussions of §4; §I gives the logit-shift regime taxonomy used throughout; §J validates the
four-role partition against the natural cluster geometry of the CPAS metric space; §K is the human-curated Dallas case study
with comparison with our pipeline and a fixed-K saturation control; §L–§M give per-domain labeled-vs-random and per-pair
influence-matched top-K results; §N details the observation that suppression-is-easy, steering-is-hard §O–§P back up the
cross-prompt and scaffold analyses; §Q discusses the weakest of our four domains (Paintings); §R is the reproducibility
manifest; §S is the glossary. Every appendix section is self-contained and cross-referenced from the main text.
B. Pipeline Reference
B.1. End-to-end pipeline
The pipeline takes an attribution graph for a seed prompt and returns a collection of concept-aligned supernodes together with
the data needed to run matched-control swap interventions. The pseudocode below summarizes the steps; the paragraphs
that follow describe each step in plain language.
Input: attribution graph G_p, seed prompt p, target logit y
Output: concept-aligned supernodes {S_i}, swap harness I
1. V_tau = select_features_by_cumulative_influence(G_p, tau=0.95)
2. C = llm_generate_concepts(p, K)
3. Probes = synthesize_probes(C, template_constraints)
4. for each feature f in V_tau, each probe q in Probes:
A[f, q] = measure_activation(f, q)
5. CPAS[f] = aggregate_cross_prompt(A[f, *])
6. for each feature f in V_tau:
role[f] = classify(CPAS[f], thresholds)
name[f] = assign_name(f, A, blacklist, target_token_rules)
7. Supernodes = group_by((role, name)), stability >= 0.6
8. for each swap pair (e_A, e_B):
I_lab = build_swap(S(e_A), S(e_B), M_ablate=-2, M_amplify=20)
I_rand = matched_random_control(I_lab)
run(I_lab); run(I_rand)
9. adaptive_M_search(missed_pairs)
10
Automatically Interpreting Attribution Graphs via Probe Prompting
Step 1 (feature selection).Attribution graphs from Neuronpedia are generated with a relatively permissive node-
inclusion threshold (nodeThreshold=0.8), a high edge-influence threshold ( edgeThreshold=0.85), and a cap
of maxFeatureNodes=5000. The graph typically contains 600–5,000 nodes; we then retain only the nodes whose
cumulative influence sums to at leastτ=0.95, which reduces each circuit to 200–700 features for batch experiments.
Step 2–3 (probe generation).The seed prompt is handed to a separate LLM together with a small specification that (a) lists
theconceptswe expect to find in the graph (entities, relations, answer tokens) and (b) constrains the probe templates to
be syntactically close to the seed (same prepositions, same answer position, same length). The LLM returns a handful of
concept-targeted probe prompts. Typical probe counts are 4–5 per seed.
Step 4–5 (activation measurement and CPAS).Each feature in Vτ is re-run on each probe and its activation measured with the
Neuronpedia API. The per-(feature, probe) record stores the peak token, the peak position in the probe, the activation density,
a sparsity ratio, a robust z-score, and a cosine similarity to the seed activation. These raw measures are then aggregated
across probes into a compact per-feature signature (the CPAS).
Step 6 (classification and naming).A strict-priority decision tree (§3.6; thresholds in Table 3) assigns each feature one
of four functional roles (Semantic-Dictionary, Semantic-Concept, Relationship, Say-X) or a fifth REVIEWbucket that is
excluded from downstream analysis. Naming is role-specific (§3.7).
Step 7 (supernode formation).Same-role same-name features are merged into a supernode; features whose role or name is
inconsistent across probes (<60%probe-level stability) are markedungroupedand excluded.
Step 8–9 (causal-validation harness).For every entity pair in the domain we build an additive entity-swap intervention (§3.8),
run it at Mablate=−2 / Mamplify=20 with attentionnotfrozen, and compare it to three deterministic matched-random-control
replicates (§3.8). Pairs that miss at the defaults enter adaptiveM-search (§G).
B.2. CPAS measures
The CPAS is the seven-number summary that the decision tree operates on. Table 2 gives the formal definitions and the
intuition for each field.
Field Definition and intuition
peak consistency main Fraction of probes whose peak token matches the feature’s modal peak. High (≥0.8 )
means the feature is a dictionary-like detector for one particular token.
n distinct peaks Number of distinct peak tokens acrossactiveprobes. = 1 for a Semantic-Dictionary
feature; larger for Semantic-Concept or Say-X.
share F/conf F Share of active probes whose peak falls on a functional token / classifier confidence on
that share. A feature withshare F≈1and highconf Fis a Say-X candidate.
func vs sem pct100·
␀
maxfunc −max sem

/max overall. Positive when the peak is on a functional token;
used to break ties between Say-X and semantic roles.
sparsity median Median of (peak−mean)/peak across active probes. Low values indicate a dense,
diffuse activation pattern characteristic of Relationship features.
conf S1−share F, i.e. confidence that the peak is on a semantic token.
Table 2.CPAS fields used by the classification rules in §3.6.
B.3. Decision-rule thresholds
The decision tree is a strict-priority cascade: features are evaluated against the rules in the order below, and the first matching
rule wins. Thresholds were iteratively refined on a small set of pilot circuits and then frozen before the 44,596-run sweep.
The layer-based asymmetry (Say-X requires layer ≥7 , Sem-Conc accepts layer ≤3 ) encodes a rough prior observed in
Gemma-2-2B: early layers are overwhelmingly semantic-content features; the ”Say X” behavior of functional-token features
promoting a specific answer is observed only in the later half of the network.
Dictionary subtype: strict and fallback.The semantic role is split internally into threesubtypesthat determine down-
stream naming. Rule 1 (peak consistency main≥0.80 and n distinct peaks≤1 ) produces the strictDictio-
narysubtype: stable single-token detectors. Rule 4 produces eitherConcept(the conf S or func vs sem pct branch)
or, when fired through its low-layer branch (layer≤3), theDictionary (fallback)subtype: early-layer semantic features
