G.4. Top-krescue is null
A second alternative is that the M=20 signal is distributed over too many features, and that concentrating amplification on
the top-k features by graph influence might yield targeted hits at high M. On products→facebook we ran the full
factorialk∈ {1,3,5,10,67}withM∈ {20,50,100,200}(six source entities, so 120 runs per cell). The result was0hits
for any k <67 at any M. Only the full 67-feature set achieves hits. A worked example: at k=1, M=20 the top-influence
feature for alibaba→facebook generates “the founder of Alibaba in 1999. . . ”, at k=3 the output is garbled token
sequences, at k=10 the first token is a programming-code token, and only at k=67 does the model correctly emit “Mark
Zuckerberg. . . ”. Graph influence correlatesnegativelywith stored activation in this domain (ρ=−0.362 , p=0.003): the
top-k by graph influence are the loudest but not the most informative features, and concentrating amplification on them
produces generic “question answering” output rather than the target answer.
G.5. Five failure modes surfaced by theM-sweep
The pairs that remain unrescued after adaptive search fall into five qualitatively different failure modes. Identifying the five
modes was itself a research output: the labels below are pointers to the investigation.
(1) Severe overshoot.A pair whose hit exists at small M but is destroyed at the default M=20. Rescuable by M reduction.
Example: oklahoma tulsa high-vsMax pairs, 37/42 pairs rescued at M=5 (88% rescue rate). At M=20 the first token
is typically a garbage token (AddTagHelper) followed by “Oklahoma, is Tulsa” (the city, not the capital); atM=5 the
first token is a comma and the continuation corrects to “Oklahoma City, and Tulsa is Oklahoma”.
(2) Field interference.A pair that hits on the intermediate+answer subset but not on the all-three-fields variant. Rescuable by
field-additivity search (the field-composition effect of §??).
(3) Feature-interaction noise.The label is correct but at high M distributed noise masks the target. Example:
indiana→arkansas produces tonode at M=20 and correctly produces Little Rock at M=5. A subset of
these are rescued byMreduction; the remainder are irreducible.
(4) Signal collapse.The target logit collapses below a pair-specific M threshold. Example:
vermont burlington→kansas wichita has vsMax 8.34 at M=20 but only 0.31 at M=5. For these
pairs the usefulMrange is narrow and lies above the default; no value ofMin{5,7,10,20}produces a hit.
21
Automatically Interpreting Attribution Graphs via Probe Prompting
(5) Feature specificity failure.The intervention correctly activates the answer concept but the wrong specific entity within
the concept. Example: vermont→kansas generates “Kansas is Hutchinson” (wrong Kansas city), not “Topeka”. Not
fixable by M adjustment: the features that were grouped under the Kansas supernode appear to encode “Kansas cities”
rather than “Topeka” specifically.
The coarse taxonomy is important for interpreting the aggregate recovery numbers. Modes (1)–(3) are rescued by adaptive
M-search; mode (4) is occasionally rescued; mode (5) is not.
H. KL Divergence as a Steering Diagnostic
Linearity of KL inM.Within the M∈[5,20] range, the per-pair KL curve between the steered and unsteered distributions
is close to linear: R2 >0.93 for 8 of the 10 USA pairs on which we estimated the linear fit. Table 6 lists the slope and
intercept of the linear fit for each probed pair; the slope varies by a factor of 5× across pairs, and is strongly predicted by the
pair’s ablate count (more source features ablated⇒steeper KL rise per unitM).
