Automatically Interpreting Attribution Graphs via Probe Prompting
J. Natural Cluster Geometry of CPAS Metrics
The decision tree (Table 3) partitions features into four functional roles using six axis-aligned threshold cuts. A natural
concern is whether these four labels reflect dense regions of the feature metric space or impose arbitrary cuts. We tested it
by re-aggregating per-feature metrics across all four datasets (≈8,000 deduplicated features) and running unsupervised
clustering with several methods.
Threshold sensitivity.Each of the four primary cuts was perturbed by ±10% on the manifest. Per-cut flip rates ranged
from 0.05% to 1.5%, and within tight neighborhoods of each threshold the local flip rate stayed below 4%. The simplest
“many points sit on arbitrary boundaries” reading is therefore inconsistent with the data.
Visual structure.We standardized the six metrics and projected them with t-SNE per dataset (Figure 23). Across all four
domains the four rule labels occupy spatially coherent regions: RELATIONSHIPforms a single low-sparsity arc, SAY-X a
tight late-layer cluster, and SEM-CONCoccupies the layer-shallow midband. The SEM-DICTbucket consistently splits into
two visually disconnected regions, an early hint that the rule’s semantic-fallback branch sweeps up two genuinely different
feature populations.
