K. Human-curated Dallas Case Study
The Dallas/Austin attribution graph is the only target circuit in our datasets that has a publicly-released human-curated
subgraph (the Neuronpedia graph gemma-fact-dallas-austin by user mh2parker). We use this single-circuit
asymmetry to run a focused case study: hold the target circuit fixed at texas dallas, run every non-Dallas USA state (49
sources) as the source side, and vary the target-side featurebagacross four conditions: human curation, our auto pipeline,
an unlabeled top-K-by-influence control, and a label-shuffled floor control. The headline comparison is ours-vs-human; the
top-K family is a control that asks whether pure influence ranking can substitute for either, and the shuffled-labels condition
is a sanity floor.
K.1. Setup
One target circuit (texas dallas, the Dallas/Austin prompt with 1,182 features at our cumulative-influence threshold),
49 non-Dallas USA source states (every entity from full swap human dallas.yml), Dallas always the target. Each
conditionis a different target-side bag for the Dallas circuit:
• ours: full canonical auto Dallas grouping (the eight concept-aligned supernodes of Figure 3, 458 classified features).
