Q. Paintings as the Weakest of the Four Domains
Paintings sits at the bottom of the four domains with a full-labeled Hit% of 4.4 and a vsMax gap of +1.38 (“Weak”). Three
structural factors contribute.
(a) The answer field is coarse.The answer is the painter’s first name— Claude, Pablo, Leonardo—tokens with many
non-painting associations in the general language model distribution. Amplifying features that peak on “Claude” in a Monet
circuit therefore moves the logit toward a cluster of non-painting “Claude” meanings.
(b) The painter supernode subsumes the first-name supernode.“Monet” as a string contains much of “Claude” as a concept
(the painter is the agent that the first name names), so the painter and first name supernodes overlap by construction.
This collapses the field-additivity structure: painter+first name is not a very different intervention from painter
alone.
(c) High error-node rates.Paintings has the highest error-node rate of any domain (17%; an error node is a node whose
attribution could not be resolved during graph generation), so a substantial portion of the circuit is simply not visible to the
grouping or the intervention. The maximum number of features a paintings intervention can operate on is therefore smaller
than in the other domains, and the signal-to-noise ratio is correspondingly worse.
Concretely, 89/90 pairs fail at the painter+first name variant. The only hit (La Grande Jatte → Water Lilies) already
had a baseline rank of 3 for “Claude”, so the intervention only had to move the logit by two positions. We therefore treat
Paintings as a Weak-strength supporting domain. The rank-perfect scaffold prediction (Table 18) isconsistentwith the
Paintings Weak verdict.
