3.2 INFORMATION-THEORETICFORMULATION OFCONTEXTUALGAIN
The ideal theoretical tool to answer our question isConditional Mutual Information (CMI). The CMI
I(T;E|S)measures the reduction in uncertainty about a target variableT(the next token) after
observing an extended contextE, given that a short contextSis already known (Cover & Thomas,
2006).
The CMI can be expressed in two well-known, equivalent forms. The first defines CMI as the
reduction in conditional entropy:
I(T;E|S) =H(T|S)−H(T|S, E)(1)
whereH(· | ·)is the conditional entropy. A second, equivalent formulation expresses the CMI as the
expected Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence between the predictive distributions with and without
the extended context:
I(T;E|S) =E p(s,e)
