An in-depth analysis of the refutations follows.
6.4. Refutation: In-Depth
Three data sets – TAC 2011 (multi-document over time), Cyber Threat Intelligence
(single document, specialist) and CrisisFACTS (multi-stream, closest to raw data) – were
analyzed for different types of refutations. The findings were as follows:
• Allowing 3 attributing sentences instead of 1 improved support significantly, but has
the potential to add sentences of lower quality.
• Refutations often differ from LLM hallucinations in a machine summary.
• The types of refutations reflect the underlying data. TAC 2011 and CrisisFACTS refu-
tations include timing errors, where attributing sentences refer to different periods of
time when facts differed, but Cyber Threat Intelligence did not. Attributing sentences
for Cyber Threat Intelligence refutations were more likely to be about related, rather
than directly relevant, topics for lower-ranked attributions. CrisisFACTS refutations
were of a variety of types and included issues with ambiguity and parsing.
• Parsing problems due to poorly formatted source text can lead to meaningless attribut-
ing sentences.
• Annotators may differ on what was considered a refutation.
Details follow, starting with a new refutation typology based on an existing typology
for factual errors in machine summaries from “Understanding Factuality in Abstractive
Summarization with FRANK: A Benchmark for Factuality Metrics” by Artidoro Pangoni,
Vidhisha Balachandran, and Yulia Tsetkov at NAACL 2021 [ 14]. We explore TAC 2011
with NLI and sentence embedding attribution methods on human summaries for Task 1
(1 attributing sentence with preceding and following sentences for context) and Task 2
(3 attributing sentences), followed by an analysis of all three data sets with the sentence
embedding attribution method on abstractive and hybrid machine summaries for Task 2.
6.4.1. Typology of Factual Errors (Refutation)
The typology of factual errors for abstractive summaries in [14] was modified for the
refutation analysis as follows:
• Semantic Frame Errors:
– Predicate Error (PredE) : The predicate in the summary statement is inconsistent
with the attribution(s).
–Entity Error (EntE): The primary arguments (or their attributes) in the summary
statement are inconsistent with the attribution(s).
– Circumstance error: (CircE) Additional information (such as location or time)
specifying the circumstance around a predicate in the summary statement is
inconsistent with the attribution(s).
• Content Verifiability Errors
– Out of Article Error (OutE): The summary statement contains information not
present in the attribution(s); this includes extrinsic hallucinations.
– Grammatical Error (GramE): The grammar mistakes in anattributionis so wrong
that it becomes meaningless.
• Additional Categories
– Others (OthE): Refutation errors that do not correspond to any of the above
categories.
–Not an Error (NE): There are no refutation errors.
Examples from [14], modified for refutations can be found in Table 4. The first 4 (PredE,
EntE, CircE, OutE) are examples ofsummarystatements where errors are highlighted in red.
The last (GramE) is an example where the grammar in theattributionis so wrong that it is
meaningless.
