A Theoretical Derivation and Analysis
We provide a theoretical motivation of the TiDPO-
DKL objective, demonstrating how it emerges from
a time-variant constrained optimization problem,
followed by an analysis of its gradient properties
and convergence guarantees.
A.1 Gradient Imbalance Analysis via Score
Dilution
Why does standard DPO fail to resolve temporal
conflicts in long-context scenarios? We attribute
this failure to the phenomenon ofScore Dilution,
recently formalized by Bansal et al. (2025).
In standard causal self-attention, the attention
weight αt,i assigned to a token i at step t is gov-
erned by the softmax function:
αt,i = exp(qt ·k i)Pt
j=1 exp(qt ·k j)
(9)
The denominator (partition function) acts as a
global normalizer. In long-context regimes where
the history length Nhist grows significantly larger
than the recent context Nrecent, the partition func-
tion accumulates contributions from a vast number
of historical “distractor” tokens.
The Dilution Mechanism.Even if individual his-
torical tokens have low relevance scores, their ag-
gregate probability mass dominates the denomina-
tor due to the sheer volume of Nhist. As proven by
Bansal et al. (2025), unless the logit margin of the
relevant recent token scales as Ω(logT) , its atten-
tion weight will asymptotically vanish. This creates
a diluted attention surface where the model’s effec-
tive receptive field is smoothed over the inertial
history.
Consequence for DPO Gradients.This forward-
pass dilution directly propagates to the backward
pass. Since the gradient magnitude with respect
to context tokens is modulated by their attention
weights (∇L ∝α), the optimization signal for the
critical conflict-resolving tokens (recent turn) is ex-
ponentially suppressed. The accumulated gradient
flow is thus dominated by the historical consistency
term: X
j∈History
