Ellipsoid Boundary Construction
For each known classk, we denote the region inside its el-
lipsoid boundary asE k ⊂R n. To construct this boundary,
we first compute the centroid of the ellipsoid and then learn
a matrix that encodes itsshape, including both its size and
orientation. Simultaneously, we construct an affine transfor-
mation that maps the ellipsoid to a ball, which is useful for
determining whether a point lies within the ellipsoid and for
calculating the proposed loss function.
Centroid ComputationFor each known classk, we com-
pute the ellipsoid centroidc k as the mean of the representa-
tions of samples belonging to classk:
ck = 1|Sk| Xzi∈Sk zi,(3)
whereS k ={z i |y i =k}denotes the representation set of
classk.
Shape Parameterization via Learnable MatrixIn this
section we parameterize an ellipsoid fEk with the center at
the origin, namely acentralized ellipsoid, as a pattern of the
shape (size and orientation) ofE k. ThenE k is defined by a
simple translation:
Ek = fEk +c k :={ˆx∈R n |ˆx=x+c k, x∈ fEk}.(4)
A centralized ellipsoid is commonly characterized bynor-
thogonal unit vectors{d i}ni=1 specifying its axis directions
and corresponding positive axis lengths{a i}ni=1 ⊂R +.
However, directly optimizing the orthogonal vectors{d i}
using gradient descent is intractable due to the rigid orthog-
onality constraints. To overcome this, we propose to learn
a matrixA k ∈R n×n that implicitly encodes both the axis
directions and scales and define the centralized ellipsoid as
fEk ={x∈R n | ∥Akx∥2 ≤∆ k},(5)
where∆ k = 1|Sk| Pzi∈Sk ||zi −c k||2 is the average distance
between the centroidc k and samples with labelkto enhance
the numerical stability among known classes with different
scales. This formulation eliminates the need for explicit or-
thogonality constraints while ensuring that fEk remains an
ellipsoid.
