categoriesY(containing both known and novel intents) inD 𝑢.
As depicted in Fig. 1, NID has two settings, i.e., unsupervised
and semi-supervised ones [20, 31, 41]:
Unsupervised NID. Under the unsupervised setting, there are no
labeled utterances available, i.e., D𝑙 =∅ . The goal is to cluster
Dtest =D𝑢 into𝐾distinct intent categories.
Semi-supervised NID. In semi-supervised NID tasks, models are
trained on training setDtrain and evaluated on abalancedtesting set
Dtest ={(𝑥 𝑖,𝑦𝑖)|𝑦𝑖∈Y} |D test|𝑖=1 , where the training set is composed
of both labeled and unlabeled utterances, i.e.,Dtrain =D𝑙∪D𝑢. In
particular, under this setting, the labeled data is limited, typically
with a labeled ratio |D𝑙||D train| ≤ 10%and aknown class ratio(KCR)
|Y𝑘|
𝐾 ≤ 75%. The trained models are expected to discriminate known
intents fromDtest, while mining for new intents in the rest of
utterances.
3.2 Canonical Cascaded Architecture
The majority of methods for NID follow a cascaded pipeline as
illustrated in Fig. 2, wherein the first stage focuses on mapping all
utterances to a semantic embedding space, i.e., encoding them as
text embeddings, while the second stage is a standard clustering
algorithm (e.g.,𝐾-Means [21]) applied to group these embeddings
into intent categories. In the literature, the first phase is often
referred to asintent learning. Apre-trained text encoder, denoted
as PTE(·) , is trained or fine-tuned depending on the specific NID
setting to produce the learned intent space. In the unsupervised
setting (D𝑙 =∅ ), the encoderPTE(·) is trained on the unlabeled data
D𝑢. In the semi-supervised setting, it is trained onDtrain =D𝑙∪D𝑢,
leveraging information from both known intents (Y𝑘) and unlabeled
data. After training, the final encoder maps each utterance𝑥𝑖 to a
