 15 
Model Scale Effects: Results reveal a critical pattern: our structured episodic approach particularly 
benefits smaller models. With GPT-4, our system achieved parity with traditional RAG (6-6-21), with 
most responses judged equivalent. We interpret this as GPT-4’s superior internal knowledge 
compensating for traditional RAG’s less structured context. The model’s extensive training on Van 
Gogh materials enables high-quality responses even from fragmented biographical chunks. However, 
with GPT-3.5, our system demonstrated clear superiority (15-8-10), nearly doubling win rate over 
traditional RAG. This advantage amplified further with GPT-3/Davinci-002 (15-6-12), showing that 
structured, first-person episodic memories with explicit metadata provide greater benefit as base model 
capability decreases. 
Implications: These results directly support our target deployment scenario. While large-scale models 
achieve acceptable performance with various RAG approaches, smaller models suitable for edge 
deployment, educational institutions, and resource-constrained environments show substantial 
improvement with our structured memory architecture. This pattern suggests our methodology’s primary 
value lies in democratizing character-based dialogue systems by enabling good performance from 
accessible models rather than requiring expensive, high-capability cloud APIs. 
