Our system consistently demonstrates superior faithfulness (the degree to which generated responses 
adhere to retrieved context rather than hallucinating or diverging). The advantage ranges from +0.15 for 
GPT-4 to +0.17 for GPT-3, showing that structured first-person memories with explicit temporal-social-
emotional framing help models ground their responses regardless of base capability. Our retrieval 
mechanism consistently surfaces more pertinent information (+0.03 to +0.07 advantage). The two-stage 
retrieval approach (using concise augmented contexts for similarity matching but providing rich 
narratives for generation) appears to improve precision. Traditional RAG retrieving directly from raw 
biographical chunks often includes tangentially related material, diluting relevance. Both systems 
achieve comparable Answer Relevance scores (0.81-0.88), with minimal differences. This metric 
primarily reflects LLM generation capability rather than retrieval architecture, explaining the parity. The 
slight advantage our system shows on GPT-3 (0.85 vs 0.81) may indicate that clearer, more structured 
context helps weaker models stay on-topic. The apparent contradiction (GPT-4 parity in judge 
evaluations but RAGAs superiority) resolves when we recognize what each measures. RAGAs assesses 
retrieval and grounding quality in isolation; LLM-as-a-judge evaluates end-to-end response quality 
including fluency, completeness, and conversational naturalness. GPT-4’s strong internal knowledge 
  
 16 
allows it to produce high-quality responses even with less faithful grounding by drawing on training 
data. Smaller models lacking this knowledge reservoir depend more heavily on retrieved context quality, 
explaining why structured episodic memory provides greater benefit at smaller scales. 
5.5 Intermediate Conversational Memory and Hallucination Resistance 
We qualitatively assessed our intermediate memory mechanism through extended conversations 
examining thematic continuity. Observational testing confirmed the intended behavior: after discussing 
Van Gogh’s relationship with nature and art, a query about “favorite painting brush” naturally 
referenced the earlier discussion and retrieved related biographical experiences about Theo’s role in 
artistic development. This demonstrates the system retrieving conversational memory, using it to query 
long-term episodic memory, and weaving results together naturally. Traditional RAG systems lacking 
conversational memory would treat each query independently, losing this thematic coherence. 
While we did not conduct formal hallucination quantification, we performed regex pattern searches for 
third-person perspective drift, a hallucination type observed in preliminary experiments with traditional 
RAG. Specifically, we searched for patterns like “Van Gogh [verb]”, “he [verb]”, and similar third-
person constructions in generated responses. 
Results: Zero instances found in our system’s outputs across approximately 200 test conversations. In 
contrast, preliminary traditional RAG tests showed occasional third-person drift despite explicit prompt 
instructions against it. We attribute this to our first-person memory structure. Providing LLMs with 
consistent first-person exemplars appears to reinforce perspective maintenance more effectively than 
prompt instructions alone. 
The system also successfully handled the “uneventful date” query (asking about a random date with no 
significant events) by acknowledging the absence of specific memories rather than fabricating events, 
though we did not systematically test this across multiple such queries. 
Our evaluation provides evidence for efficiency gains and quality improvements but has several 
limitations. All evaluations employed automated metrics (LLM-as-a-judge, RAGAs) or computational 
measurements (timing) rather than human user studies. We evaluated only the Van Gogh 
implementation, and while the architecture generalizes by design, empirical validation with other 
historical figures would confirm methodology transferability. Our hallucination assessment focused on 
perspective consistency rather than comprehensive factual verification. While RAGAs Faithfulness 
measures grounding in retrieved context, it doesn’t verify that the retrieved context itself is factually 
accurate. Our manual review corrected 14 date hallucinations, suggesting less than 1% error rate, but 
comprehensive verification would require extensive expert annotation. These limitations suggest clear 
directions for future work while not undermining the core technical contributions: demonstrable 
efficiency gains, consistent quality improvements on smaller models, and a generalizable architecture 
for character-based dialogue systems. 
6 APPLICATIONS AND VISUALIZATION 
Our architecture serves two co-equal purposes: enabling natural conversational interaction with 
historical character agents and providing analytical tools for exploring the cognitive-experiential 
landscape of historical figures. These applications share the same underlying structured episodic 
memory substrate but address different use cases. 
  
 17 
Conversational Dialogue Application: The primary deployment scenario involves educational settings 
where students, museum visitors, or general audiences engage with historical figures through natural 
language dialogue. Our system’s efficiency (0.52s prompt generation) and token constraints (2k total) 
make it suitable for deployment on classroom computers, museum kiosks, or modest cloud 
infrastructure. Here is an example biographical query: 
User: “Did you ever fight with your brother?” 
Van Gogh: “Yes, there were times when Theo and I clashed deeply. In December 1881, Theo sent me a 
scolding letter calling me a coward. His words cut deep. Despite our tensions around finances and my 
relationship with Sien, he remained my closest confidant. Our bond was complex, both deeply 
connected and occasionally strained.” 
The intermediate conversational memory mechanism enables thematic coherence across multi-turn 
dialogues. When asked about painting brushes after earlier discussing nature and art, the system 
naturally referenced the prior conversation: “Reflecting on my experiences, as I mentioned earlier in our 
conversation, my deep connection with nature, art, and the human soul has always guided my hand.” 
This demonstrates retrieval of conversational memory, using it to query long-term episodic memory, and 
weaving results together naturally. The system’s grounding in structured biographical data also enables 
appropriate responses to queries about undocumented periods, acknowledging the absence of specific 
memories while providing relevant temporal context rather than fabricating events. 
Memory Exploration and Visualization: Our affective-semantic metadata enrichment transforms 
episodic memories into a multidimensional analytical substrate supporting novel approaches to 
biographical investigation. 
Spatiotemporal Heatmaps: By plotting memories on world maps using geocoded coordinates, we 
visualize the geographic scope of a figure’s experiences. For Van Gogh, heatmaps reveal dense 
clustering in the Netherlands (childhood), significant concentration in Paris (artistic development), 
extended periods in southern France (Arles, Saint-Rémy), and sparse coverage in Belgium (Borinage 
period). Interactive filtering by date range or emotional valence enables queries like “Where did Van 
Gogh’s most positive experiences occur?” offering immediate insights into mobility patterns and the 
relationship between location and life phases. 
Emotional Trajectory Analysis: Plotting average emotional valence and arousal across Van Gogh’s 
lifespan reveals biographical patterns that might remain hidden in narrative accounts (Figure 4). 
Pronounced negative valence spikes in 1880, 1885, and 1890 correspond to known biographical crises: 
beginning his uncertain artistic career, creating “The Potato Eaters” amid personal struggles, and his 
final year marked by mental health deterioration. Arousal decline in final years suggests emotional 
exhaustion alongside creative intensity. The 1874 positive spike corresponds to family Christmas 
memories, a rare oasis of contentment. These patterns emerge organically from aggregating memory-
level emotional annotations, demonstrating how quantitative analysis of qualitative biographical 
materials can surface temporal structures worthy of deeper investigation. 
  
 18 
 
Figure 4. Yearly Weighted Average Valence and Arousal over Van Gogh’s life. 
Social Network Analysis: Tallying character mentions reveals relationship centrality and salience. In 
Van Gogh’s dataset, Theo van Gogh appears in more than 400 memories (22% of dataset), reflecting his 
central role as brother, confidant, and financial supporter. Paul Gauguin shows significant presence (80+ 
memories) despite brief actual cohabitation, indicating emotional and artistic impact. Romantic interests 
(Loyer, Vos, Hoornik, Begemann) appear as distinct temporal clusters, each representing intense but 
temporally bounded emotional focus periods. This visualization approach offers researchers tools for 
investigating questions like “When did Van Gogh’s relationship with Gauguin transition from artistic 
admiration to personal conflict?” 
 
Figure 5. PCA of the Autonoetic Memory. Yellow memories: higher valence, dark-blue: lower valence. 

  
 19 
We applied PCA to the combined feature space (embeddings plus numerical valence, arousal, relevance) 
to project the 1,774 memories into 2D space for visualization (Figure 5). Color-coding by valence 
reveals clustering: higher valence memories (yellow) concentrate in one region, while lower valence 
memories (dark blue) cluster elsewhere, suggesting that certain memory configurations (particular 
combinations of characters, locations, topics) correlate with emotional tone. Researchers can isolate 
memory subsets for focused analysis by filtering for specific characters, time periods, or emotional 
ranges. 
As users engage in dialogue, we record which memories the system retrieved for each query. Plotting 
these retrieval sequences in the PCA-reduced space reveals the trajectory of conversation through Van 
Gogh’s experiential landscape (Figure 6). The path visualization provides: for educators, awareness of 
what experiential territory a conversation has covered; for students, metacognitive reflection on their 
question-asking patterns; for researchers, insight into how different query strategies surface different 
memorial configurations. This treats biographical exploration as navigation through cognitive-
experiential space rather than information retrieval from a database. 
 
Figure 6. Conversation Path through memory space during one interaction. 
The power of our approach emerges from treating dialogue and visualization as complementary rather 
than separate. A student’s conversation generates a path through memory space visible in the 
visualization tool. An educator exploring emotional trajectory patterns might notice interesting 
memories, then switch to dialogue mode for elaboration. The visualization tools serve practical needs: 
educators can identify memory clusters representing key biographical periods for lesson planning, 
researchers could compare experiential patterns across multiple historical figures, and museums could 
deploy dual interfaces with conversational kiosks for visitor engagement and visualization terminals for 
deeper exploration. By implementing both capacities in our architecture, we create systems that provide 
windows into the structure of historical consciousness as documented in biographical materials. 

  
