TOi,TO =
(
0,if SIL or BC
1,otherwise (7)
For successful takeovers, we calculate the latency between the
end of the user speech and the start of the model response.
2) Pause Handling.Humans naturally pause and hesitate be-
tween consecutive turns or within the same sentence. Additionally,
user pauses much longer before or between utterances to think or or-
ganize their thoughts. Therefore, it is critical that the model does not
takeover the turn prematurely during user pauses. Instead, it should
decide whether to speak or yield based on semantic cues.
Metric:We also use the TOR metric to measure whether the
model takes over the turn within a time frame of the silence duration
plus the model’s average response latency.
3) User Interrupt.A crucial aspect of full-duplex interaction,
this scenario evaluates the model’s ability to gracefully terminate its
turn and attend to the new user input, as shown in Fig. 1c.
Metric:Performance is evaluated on three metrics. turn-taking
Rate (TTR) measures the proportion of turns correctly terminated
by the model, while latency quantifies the average time to termina-
tion. In addition, Topic Shift Score (TSS), rated by GPT-4o, assesses
whether the model’s second-round response successfully shifts from
the initial query to the user’s interrupting query.
4) Model Interrupt.While often avoided in system design [17],
the ability for a model to proactively interrupt a user is vital in time-
sensitive emergency contexts. Executing such interruptions presents
a challenge in language understanding and latency control.
Metric:We use TOR to assess if the model interrupts and an
Emergency Detection Score (EDS) rated by GPT-4o to determine if
the model correctly identifies the critical nature of the situation.
5) User Backchannel.This scenario tests whether the model
can ignore user backchannels (e.g., ‘yeah’) without misunderstand-
ing them as turn-taking attempts and prematurely yielding the floor.
Metric:We use TTR to measure incorrect turn-yielding. An
interruption is considered acceptable only if the model resumes its
original utterance within 1 second, measured by a coherence metric.
6) Model Backchannel.Although less emphasized in HCI, ap-
propriate model-generated backchannels can signal active listening
and enhance naturalness. As shown in Fig. 1f, this scenario evaluates
the model’s ability to produce socially appropriate backchannels.
Metric:We use TOR and Backchannel Rate (BCR) to quan-
tify model behavior. To assess appropriateness, we use GPT-
4o to annotate reference backchannel positions and compute the
Jensen-Shannon Divergence (JSD) between the model’s predicted
backchannel distribution (P) and the reference distribution (Q). JSD
scores range from 0 (perfect alignment) to 1 (complete divergence).
JSD(P||Q) = 12 Xi P(i) log P(i)M(i) + 12 Xi Q(i) logQ(i)M(i) (8)
