Abstract
Task-oriented dialogue system (TOD) completes particular task based on user
preferences across natural language interactions. Considering the impressive per-
formance of large language models (LLMs) in natural language processing (NLP)
tasks, most of the latest TODs are centered on LLMs. While proactive plan-
ning is crucial for task completion—the primary purpose of TODs— many
existing TODs overlook effective goal-aware planning, partly due to deficient
incorporating of intermediate conversation information essential for informed
decision-making. This paper presents a model for managing task-oriented conver-
sations, conceptualized centered on the information state approach to dialogue
management. The created model incorporates constructive intermediate informa-
tion in planing. Initially, predefined slots and textpart informational components
are created to model user preferences. Consequently, while investigating task-
oriented conversations, critical circumstances in interactions are identified, and
informational components corresponding to these circumstances are created.
Possible configurations for these informational components leads to limited infor-
mation states. Then dialogue moves, which indicate movement between these
information states and the procedures that must be performed in this movement,
are created. Exploiting these information states and dialogue moves, the update
strategy as a pivotal part of presented model is constructed which indicates
the dialogue moves in conversation. Leveraging in-context learning capabilities
of LLMs, the created model is implemented. In this model, database queries
are created centered on indicated predefined slots and the order of retrieved
entities is indicated centered on the text part. This mechanism enables passing
the whole corresponding entities to the user preferences in the order of con-
gruency. Evaluations exploiting the complete test conversations of MultiWOZ
2.2, with no more than a domain in a conversation, illustrate maximal inform,
and success. Compared with previous methods, the performance of the created
domain-independent model improves in terms of these task completion metrics.
Keywords:Task-Oriented Dialogue System, In-Context Learning, Dialogue
Management, Information State Approach, Update Strategy, Information State,
Dialogue Moves
1 Introduction
The primary purpose of a collaborative task-oriented dialogue system (TOD) is to
manage conversation and coach a user to complete task without any conflict of inter-
est with the user. The collaborative TOD leverages its whole potential to effectively
complete the task centered on user preferences. Management of conversations within
TOD involves updating the context centered on previous interactions, interpreting
user utterances within the context, interfacing with back-end infrastructure such as
databases, planners, and execution modules pertinent to the current task or domain,
and creating the later utterance [18, 27, 28].
Predominantly, many of the latest task-oriented dialogue systems (TODs) are created
centered on the dialogue state model. This frame-based model characterizes slots as
2
markers of particular pieces of predefined information that TODs intend to extract
from user utterances in the course of the interaction. The dialogue state model com-
prises natural language understanding (NLU), dialogue state tracker (DST), dialogue
policy (DP), and natural language generation (NLG) components.[28].
The considerable portion of processing in TODs is language processing. With
the prowess of the large language model (LLM) in mitigating linguistic chal-
lenges—particularly contextual understanding and language generation—LLMs are
incorporated in the majority of lately introduced TODs. In task-oriented conver-
sations, despite chitchat, the purpose is task completion, with each TOD-created
utterance in line with this end. While many previous LLM-based TODs fail to main-
tain this goal-oriented perspective, proactivity is a crucial prerequisite for creating
a goal-aware TOD. Employing the information of intermediate phases in the con-
versation, proactivity enables the TOD to consider the consequence of changes in
task completion and provide effective unprompted information ; to illustrate, if there
is no entity in the database that is congruent with user-indicated configuration of
predefined slots, a proactive TOD, considering the current configuration and entities
in the database, can create a clarifying question which enables the user to change
preferences slightly and complete the task. While different methods were employed in
previous works, the intermediate information they could integrate into the planning
process is commonly limited to the dialogue state, acts and database-retrieved infor-
mation. The lack of comprehensive constructive intermediate information in dialogue
state is a limitation of previous TODs and prevents proactivity[10, 18, 21].
The information state-based dialogue theory framework comprises informational
components, their formal representations, dialogue moves, update rules, and update
strategies. Contrasting with the dialogue-state model, the information-state approach
to dialogue management presents a wider perspective on modeling task-oriented
conversations. The dialogue-state model can be conceptualized as an instance of the
information-state model. In addition, while the dialogue-state model is in contrast to
plan-based methodologies, the information-state model enables an effective integra-
tion of these paradigms. [27].
In this paper, the challenges and defeats in the conversations of previous TODs
investigated to identify which missing intermediate information leads to error-prone
conditions. Instances of these challenging conditions might include user input errors in
predefined slots or conditions in which users reject the entities. Then, centered on this
investigation and inspired by the conceptualization of information state approach to
dialogue management, limited informational components corresponding to construc-
tive intermediate pieces of information in task-oriented conversations are created.
Initially, considering the limitation of creating a TOD centered on limited predefined
intents [9], for modeling user preferences, informational components of predefined slots
and text part are created, while the text part contains the constraint in preferences
beyond the predefined slots. Then, challenging conditions in conversations examined,
and constructive intermediate information, which employing it could mitigate that
challenge and informational components corresponding to them are created. The con-
figurations of created informational components leads to limited information states.
In order to construct a proactive TOD, not only creating informational components
3
are important but also constructing an effective management policy is crucial. Conse-
quently, dialogue moves corresponding to the possible movement between information
states are initiated and their corresponding procedures, including language process-
ing, database retrieval and changing informational component configurations that
will be performed in these movements, are established. Then centered on the possible
information states and created dialogue moves, the update strategy is constructed to
manage changes in conversation, indicating a dialogue move in each phase.
In the model presented in this paper, the task of TOD is decomposed into smaller
parts centered on critical points in the conversation, which contain constructive pieces
of intermediate information. Pre-training an LLM or fine-tuning a pre-trained LLM
are popular manners to create LLM-based TODs. To implement modularized TODs
models in this way, the cost of curating datasets and trainings is not negligible. With
considerable progress in instruction-tuned LLMs, leveraging in-context learning has
become common to employ the capabilities of LLMs out-of-the-box in conducting
particular tasks. While writing the effective prompt that clearly indicates the instruc-
tion is profound for in-context learning of LLMs, without updating LLM parameters
or imposing curating datasets, the cost of learning is minimal [18, 52].
The language proficiency of models centered on in-context learning of LLM is con-
siderable, but effective planning is commonly ignored in these models. Therefore,
methods including task decomposition and external planner-aided are presented
to improve the planning of these LLM-based models. [30]. The presented model is
implemented centered on in-context learning of LLMs inspired by task decomposition
and external planner-aided methods. As in-context learning enables injecting inter-
mediate information into established language processing subtasks and performing
them with low cost, it can be potentially infeasible if the implementation centered on
pre-training or fine-tuning LLMs.
The implemented method can be interpreted as a retrieval-augmented generation
(RAG) model, while the retrieval component is not centered on the vector database
but on a custom entity search model. The prompt in in-context learning is created by
integrating the concluded information of retrieval and the informational components
into the prompt template. In the presented model indicated in 3, the update strategy,
considering information state, infers dialogue move. Procedures corresponding to
dialogue move may include language processing, retrieving entity information from
the database, or configuring informational components. After performing procedures,
its product is passed to the update strategy. If the ToD utterance is prepared, it is
presented to user; if it is not created, the update strategy indicates dialogue move
considering the information state, then the procedures corresponding to that dialogue
move are performed, and its product is passed to update strategy. If the ToD utter-
ance is prepared, it is presented to the user. If the conversation is not completed, the
user passes another utterance to the model. The update strategy pursues processing.
This cycle continues until the conversation is ended.
4
Fig. 1Created Model
The performance of implemented TOD, centered on task completion metrics, is
evaluated and compared with previously created TODs in the collection of MultiWOZ
conversations, while each conversation is not in more than a domain. The inform of
the presented method is complete and considering exploitation of the custom retrieval
mechanism, there is no drop in success compared with inform, which is confirmed in
evaluations. The presented method improves the inform and susses of previous models
considerably.
The purpose of this paper is to improve task completion in task-oriented dialogue
systems through collaborative proactive planning, which integrates comprehensive
intermediate information into the dialogue management. These tasks commonly lead
to retrieving entity information from database. This paper creates a collaborative
model for management of task-oriented conversations that:
• Incorporates comprehensive intermediate information crucial for planning, enhanc-
ing task completion and reducing error-prone conditions.
• Leverages the language proficiency of large language models (LLMs) while is
proactive and task-aware in its planning.
• It is centered on the information-state approach to dialogue management, which
enables it to mitigate the limitations of dialogue-state-based models.
• It is centered on in-context learning of LLMs, inspired by task decomposition and
planner-aided methods to improve LLMs planning capabilities.
• It exploits an entity search mechanism that, after retrieving entities centered
on predefined slots, orders entities considering their congruence with the rest of
preferences.
In section 2, previous works and their comprehensive investigation are provided.
Ensuing this, section 3 introduces the presented model. A comprehensive break-
down of implementation, experiments and evaluations are then presented in section 4.
Eventually, conclusions are provided in section 5.
5
2 Previous Works
The informed management of interaction in TOD is a critical information science issue
[51]. Previous works introduce different models to create TODs. With the capabilities
of large language models and considerable language processing within TODs, most
of the lastly implemented TODs are LLM-based. The previous works in LLM-based
TODs in 2.1, and papers concentrating on proactivity and creating clarifying questions
in 2.2 are explored. The pivotal insights and challenges in previous works for creating
proactive collaborative TOD are investigated in 2.3.
2.1 LLM-based models
Initially, the models centered on pre-training or calibrating LLMs are investigated in
2.1.1. Then, the models centered on in-context learning of LLMs, which exploit LLMs
out-of-the-box without changing their parameters [52], are examined in 2.1.2.
2.1.1 Models Centered on Pre-training and Fine-tuning of LLMs
[4] endeavors to mitigate the expenses of creating TODs on a large scale and introduce
a benchmark for improving them. The costs incurred in creating TODs include label-
ing and providing domain-specific information and expertise. While language models
exhibit considerable prowess in creating utterances, their calibration for crafting utter-
ances tailored to the goals and domain within TODs is imperative. Consequently, their
paper presents an loss function encompassing belief state tracking and response gen-
eration subtasks.
Exploiting their created loss, they create and train an LLM-based model for task-
centered utterance creation. Their model emerges as a pre-trained language model
tailored explicitly for TODs. Customization of their presented model for individual
domains involves calibrating the model centered on created loss function, exploiting
pre-collected in-domain conversations. The process of collecting data for each domain
is expensive. To mitigate this challenge, their paper presents a multi-phase calibra-
tion method. Ensuing initial calibration with a pre-collected dataset, they continue
improving the model exploiting a machine teaching method until either performance
plateaus or certain indicated epochs have been completed.
TODs consist of modules for understanding, dialogue management, and utterance
creation. While, the conventional modular approach in constructing such systems com-
monly introduces propagation errors. [5] presents a method to model the sequence
probability, incorporating outputs from understanding, dialogue management, and
utterance creation phases leveraging the chain rule of probability. They consequently
train an LLM-based end-to-end model, leading to a unified language model for the
whole modules. Their model improves its prior works on the multiWOZ dataset.
While complexity of database queries and utterances with conversation capabilities
presents obstacles in pre-training an end-to-end TOD, the scarcity of training data
exacerbates these challenges. [12] presents a model that decomposes the dialogue sys-
tem into distinct knowledge base retrieval and generation components, which are
pre-trained independently. This method enables exploitation of KB-free corpora in
training the utterance creation component. In addition, their paper introduces a
6
data augmentation method to mitigate the scarcity of data for training the database
retrieval component, which enables effective calibration of their presented TOD.
While many previous methods to pre-training TODs have primarily emphasized under-
standing and creating utterances, [13] concentrates on enhancing dialogue policy to
improve pre-training language models in creating a TOD. They introduce a model
centered on UniLM [35] for pre-training of TODs, which predicts the dialogue policy
explicitly from labeled and unlabeled date, exploiting semi-supervised method. They
create UniDA, which is labeled with dialogue acts, and create an unlabeled UnDial cor-
pus, which is created through collecting certain dialogue corpora. The UniDA dataset
is meticulously crafted from online dialogue corpora, and to ensure quality, they
present a gating mechanism to choose only high-quality data points in pre-training.
Their presented model encompasses multiple objectives: response selection, response
generation, dialogue action prediction, and consistency regularization. Their pre-
training dataset is the union of UniDA and UnDial. Their presented objective for
labeled data is the cumulation of the response selection, response generation, dialogue
action prediction, and consistency regularization terms, while their objective for unla-
beled data is the cumulation of: response selection, response generation with multiply
of consistency regularization and gating score. Their presented objective for calibra-
tion depends on whether the training dataset is labeled or unlabeled. For a labeled
dataset, it is the cumulation of response selection, response generation, dialogue action
prediction terms, while for unlabeled datasets it only consists of response selection,
and response generation terms.
Pre-trained language models (PLMs) enhance the performance of TODs considerably.
While the devoid of grounding and lack of diversity are issues within TODs created
centered on pre-trained language models. paper [14] presents training objectives for
calibrating PLMs to mitigate these challenges. Their presented model creates utter-
ances, predicts beliefs, and preforms multiple subtasks.
While the parameters of belief state predictor and response predictor are common,
the training loss for belief prediction and utterance prediction differ slightly. Their
presented objective for belief prediction is centered on cross-entropy. While for utter-
ance prediction, the objective includes cross-entropy and unlikelihood loss terms, to
enhance diversity by penalizing repetition. They exploit a back-translation method to
create paraphrases, thereby overcoming the lack of diversity obstacle.
[15] concentrates on the inter-dependency of components within PLM-based TODs,
including error propagation, data annotation overhead, and inference latency chal-
lenges. To mitigate these issues, they present a unified model for whole subtasks
in task-oriented conversations, encompassing dialogue state tracking, dialogue policy
learning, and natural language understanding and creation. Their paper introduces a
pre-training model centered on a maximum likelihood objective to predict intended
text centered on the context and a task-specific prompt. They indicate that exploiting
this prompt inspired by the concept of in-context learning.
[16] investigates utterance-level training and evaluation in previously presented meth-
ods to create end-to-end TODs centered on PLMs, which cause not only missing of
the intermediate information, such as belief states, but also unrealistically center on
independent labeled information for subtasks. They model task-oriented conversations
7
in conversation level and present a method centered on calibrating a large PLM on
the whole context encompassing user utterance, belief state, database results, system
act, and system utterance. In their presented method, calibration leverages the lan-
guage modeling objective to optimize the probability of predicting the next word, and
they probe the evaluation centered on the created content instead of predefined cor-
rect information.
While task-bot is a conversational task assistant (CTA) which equips users with
information and enables them to complete the task independently, and is a little dif-
ferent from TOD, the components of them are not quite different [20]. [26] presents a
task-bot model that in its dialogue manager, after identifying the task, retrieves the
information from the corresponding databases. Exploiting this information, it updates
PlanGraph which introduced in their paper and contributes to effectively managing
complex tasks within task-bots. The embedded information in PlanGraph enables pro-
viding instructions. Throughout this process, certain filters are exploited to eliminate
unsafe and harmful instructions. The dialogue manager identifies whether a template-
based or neural creator, which is a question-answering module, will be exploited to
create the utterance to maintain a balance between precision and flexibility. They cre-
ate question-answering (QA) module through calibrating a pre-trained QA language
model.
2.1.2 Models Centered on In-Context Learning of LLMs
The critical role of language understanding and utterance creation tasks in task-
oriented dialogue management, as well as the effectiveness of LLMs in performing
these tasks, led to the creation of many LLM-based task-oriented dialogue managers.
InstructTODS [1] is an end-to-end TOD that leverages the capabilities of a large pre-
trained language model out-of-the-box. While it withholds calibrating the exploited
LLM, and eschews exploiting domain information or otologies, its performance in task
completion competes that of calibrated TODs. The presented model of InstructTODS
incorporates proxy belief states and action thoughts, enhancing its capability to inter-
act effectively with the knowledge base. The LLM in utterance generation module is
capable of incorporating not only the context but also entire database in its input.
The proxy belief state encapsulates the user’s intent until the current point in the
conversation.Action thoughts are constructed centered on information encapsulated in
the proxy belief state. Indeed, action thoughts work as indicators of the information
that must retrieved from the knowledge base. Utilizing this information and the pre-
defined templates, InstructTODS exploits the large language model as a code creator
to create queries, exploiting action thoughts as input. The retrieved information is
investigated upon querying and parsed by LLM to wrap-up the interaction and indi-
cate whether the action thought has been completed. If not, another action thought
is created, and the explained phases are iterated until the action thought is either
completed or the knowledge retriever indicates a lack of corresponding information in
the knowledge base. Then, the utterance creation module exploits the context and the
concluded information of the knowledge base query process to create the utterance. In
conditions which corresponding information is not in the knowledge base, the created
utterance prompts the user for information.
8
[2] presents a method for creating prompts in TODs. While leveraging LLMs in a box
manner for TOD creation is practical, prompt creation for these systems tackles certain
challenges. If creators construct a unified prompt to create utterances in the whole con-
versation turns, they must encompass constraints corresponding to every conversation
condition within this prompt, inevitably leading to lengthy prompts which probably
cause weak performance and hallucinations in creating utterances. While if creators
construct a compact prompt, it might leads to oversight of crucial constraints corre-
sponding with certain conversation conditions, undermining the TOD’s proficiency in
managing conversation. To mitigate this problem, [2] introduces a method to dynam-
ically create prompts at each conversation turn, considering the conversation context
leading up to that point.
In their presented method, a trainable slot generator component is incorporated, which
leverages context to identify the domain and slots within the belief state. The domain
and slot information, extracted through this component, are considered prior informa-
tion. Moreover, an adaptive prompt generator component exists within their presented
model. Leveraging prior information and context, this component constructs prompts
to enable the creation of utterances and the complete belief state. The prompts are
constructed to create the complete belief state, comprising constant and changing ele-
ments. The constant portion encompasses instructions for belief state extraction along
with illustrative examples, whereas the changing portion encompasses domain slots
and their corresponding candidate configurations. The prompts constructed for creat-
ing utterances also comprise constant and changing components. The constant portion
entails instructions for utterance creation, while the changing part involves matching
tokens with the corresponding entities.
[3] investigates the potential of exploiting instruction-tuned LLMs out-of-the-box in
creating TODs. They also evaluate LLMs’ performance in dialogue state tracking com-
pared to supervised task-specific models. Their evaluations indicate that while LLMs
may initially under-perform in dialogue state tracking, they effectively complete con-
versations if correct slots are provided. Their paper introduces a model for LLM-based
TOD. In their presented model, TOD can be implemented in a zero-shot or few-shot
manner. The dialogue state-tracking component comprises phases of prompting the
LLM to extract the domain, and then extracting slots-related information from user
input exploiting a created prompt. The extracted information then updates the con-
versation state, which is exploited to query the database. Then, a prompt is created
to produce utterances, incorporating information from the belief state, retrieved enti-
ties, context, and user utterance.
In the few-shot manner, they introduce another phase at the initiation of the pro-
cess. A context store is established by extracting correspondent conversations from
the training collection centered on their congruence with the current conversation.
Before prompt creation, items are retrieved from the context store; an item serves as
a positive example, while another is corrupted and exploited as a negative example.
They exploit certain instructed-tuned LLM models in zero-shot and few-shot man-
ners to investigate the effectiveness of them in implementing their presented model. In
addition, they exploit oracle belief states to evaluate the performance of their imple-
mented TOD while excluding dialogue state tracking errors. They exploit MultiWOZ
9
and schema-guided dataset in their experiments for evaluation purposes.
[11] mitigates the lack of conversation-level evaluation of dialogue systems constructed
on LLMs. To complete this gap, they exploit MultiWOZ and conduct a human eval-
uation to measure the performance of dialogue systems constructed on the model
presented in [3]. They are compared with other conversational systems including rule-
based and TOATOD [34], an end-to-end TOD. Conducted evaluations illustrate that
GPT4 performs comparably to rule-based systems and consistently outperforms other
implemented conversational systems.
LLMs perform competently in creating utterances within chat environments, but their
efficacy diminishes in complex conversations, such as medical or legal consultations. [6]
presents a model for proactive management of professional consultation conversations,
leveraging the capabilities of LLMs. They claim this model works as a more clever
and professional chatbots. The presented model comprises chat agent, topic manager,
topic enricher, and context manager modules. Their presented model characterizes the
topic as the primary concentration of each cycle in the conversation, and it charac-
terizes task as the particular mission which is deemed complete upon confronting the
whole predefined topics in the conversation.
In the topic evolution phase, the topic manager, working as primary module of their
model, processes input encompassing the user query, chat context, action list, and the
state of the topic stack. It then predicts the crucial action for topic evolution in the
current cycle. In this model, each action corresponds to a particular program to imple-
ment an instruction. Predefined actions in their presented model include creating a
topic, concluding the current topic, remaining within the current topic, and finalizing
the current topic. Execution of the command corresponding to the predicted action
enables topic transitions, including the maintenance of the topic stack which is a data
structure to maintain conversation states. Then, the topic enricher, by processing the
current topic and the output of the context manager, presents input for creating utter-
ances which is the last phase and is enabled by the chat agent module.
[9] examines the intent-based NLU approach, which is the conventional model for
creating industrial TODs comprising natural language understanding (NLU), dia-
logue management (DM), and natural language generation (NLG) components. In
this approach, NLU is modeled as a classification task with predetermined intents as
classes; consequently user utterances are categorized into their corresponding intents.
While working with a collection of limited predefined intents can pose challenges in
updating the intents. The essence of intents lies in their conversion to tasks, but not
every user utterance can be precisely mapped to tasks or predefined intents.
[9] presents an LLM-based model for industrial TODs, which considers the poten-
tials of in-context learning of LLMs, and mitigates the challenges of the intent-based
NLU approach. The principal components of their presented model include business
logic, dialogue understanding (DU), and conversation repair. In each conversation
cycle, DU, informed by the entire conversation context, converts user utterances into
a sequence of commands. The dialogue manager processes commands to update the
dialogue stack, which prorates the conversation state. If conversation repair is war-
ranted centered on the user utterance, the dialogue stack is updated centered on the
corresponding improving pattern.
10
[25] presents a collaborative model that mitigates the paramount challenge of consid-
ering different user populations within taskbots. This model contains a state graph
that prorates conversation and context to improve NLU. The presented model’s DM
is capable of creating clarifying questions to moderate misunderstandings in conversa-
tions. To create an inclusive multi-modal interface, they conduct collaborative efforts
with many communities, including the deaf and hard of hearing (DHH). In addition,
the NLG component integrates LLM-based and rule-based modules to diminish con-
cerns of efficiency and privacy in creating utterances.
The paper [54] concentrates on the limitations of LLM-based, particularly in-context
learning-based TODs. In those TODs, passive planning and non-iterative retrieval
mechanism are challenging; consequently, the paper creates ProTOD to improve
proactivity and task completion. ProTOD consists of an exploratory retrieval mech-
anism and a passive-to-proactive policy planner. The retrieval mechanism is centered
on chain-of-plan-execution to explore comprehensive corresponding information by
exploiting collection of tools. If policy considered as the list of actions, these actions
are not only influenced by context information but are also contingent on each other.
In planning it indicates a collection of mandatory actions as the initial policy; con-
sequently in the latter proactive phase, it integrates proactive actions like asking
clarification questions. Integration considering the contingency between actions is
multiphase process. in each phase, the model assesses if it is influential to integrate
proactive actions, then creates an integrated policy and corresponding utterance.
The challenges in modular models of creating TODs is investigated in paper [55]. In
includes error propagation, customization costs, transferability, fault tolerance. Their
paper presents a non-modular model which is centered on instruction template and
LLM. To decrease customization costs and improve transferability, exploiting this
instruction template in different issues is not complex. To improve adaptability in man-
agement of challenging conditions, there is not a lot of constraint in the instruction
template; consequently, it does not limit the processing and changes in conversation.
The instruction template initially indicates what the task category and TOD charac-
ter are. Then it contains the task information part, which explains the information
like what the task is and which are the corresponding tools. The last part corresponds
to the layout for passing information.
2.2 Pro-activity and the Creation of Clarifying Questions
Proactivity in a conversational system is the capability of managing a conversation by
taking the initiative, predicting and considering the consequences of changes before
implementing them, in completing purpose of the conversation. Proactive dialogue
systems are capable of coordinating complex interactions and managing conversations
toward a predetermined purpose [21]. While the LLMs enhance the capability of con-
versational agents to create utterances, LLMs are only trained to pursue instructions,
and a lack of goal awareness poses challenges in current LLM-based conversational
systems. [10] investigates LLM-based conversational agents and examines methods
that have been presented to enhance goal awareness in proactive, non-collaborative,
and multi-goal conversational systems. Proactivity is the most critical characteristic
of goal awareness in intelligent conversational systems. In TODs, proactivity enables
11
providing effective unprompted information. This capability is promising in cultivat-
ing enhanced collaboration between users and TODs toward the completion of tasks
[10].
[18] inspects the capabilities of LLM-based dialogue systems in managing proactive
conversations, including clarification, goal awareness, and non-collaboration conver-
sations, as proactivity is paramount for eliciting clarifying questions, coordinating
target-guided conversations, and managing non-collaborative exchanges. They present
proactive chain-of-thought (ProCoT) prompting method to improve proactivity of
LLM-based conversational systems through elevating their planning and taking ini-
tiative toward task completion. The ProCoT is conceptualized as p(t, a, r — D, C,
A), D indicates the task background, C implies the context, A indicates the potential
dialogue acts, and t explains the thought for the decision-making process concerning
predicted action (e.g., ambiguity analysis of a user utterance in clarification conversa-
tions). In comparison with proactive prompting p(a, r — D, C, A), ProCoT involves
a phase to check the effect of conducting predicted action on task completion. Their
evaluations indicate that ProCoT improves the performance of clarification and target-
guided conversations.
[23] concentrates on proactive information-seeking conversations and introduces the
proactive conversational question answering (PCQA) task, which combines conversa-
tional question answering (CQA) and clarification question generation (CQG). The
PCQA tasks are decomposed into identifying ambiguity in the user query, proactively
creating a clarifying question if the user query is identified as ambiguous, and answer-
ing the user query. Their paper introduces the PACIFIC dataset, crafted for PCQA
over contexts of table and text in the finance domain. In addition, they present the
UniPCQA multi-task learning model, which unifies whole subtasks of PCQA. While
the performance of UniPCQA exploiting PACIFIC is not week, it is lower than that
of experts, which indicates the essence of improving PCQA performance.
[24] concentrates on creating clarifying questions within an information-seeking con-
dition. They initially construct a clarification taxonomy by investigating Bing search
logs which contribute to creating templates. Their paper presents certain models
for creating clarification questions. [19] presents a method to integrate chitchat into
TODs, which enables providing non-inquired information to enhance their interaction,
interestingness, informativeness, and humanlikeness quality while maintaining task
completion efficiency. It introduces a methodology for dataset creation and implements
it to create a dataset with inserting chitchat for copious conversations of schema-
guided dialogue (SGD) and MultiWOZ task-oriented datasets. In their paper, certain
models are created. These models are trained on their created collection to produce
chitchat-augmented task-oriented conversations. While this integrated chitchat infor-
mation improved user experience, it probably contains limited effective information
for the user. In contrast, [22] provides a dataset to mitigate this issue, which considers
integrating knowledge grounded chitchat into the conversation.
2.3 Investigation of Previous Works
The performance of the last created LLMs leads to leveraging in-context learning of
LLMs instead of calibrating pre-trained language models in creating many TODs.
12
While the lack of a common baseline model for creating and comparing in-context
learning-based TODs is challenging. [8] concentrate on enabling the creation and
evaluation of multi-lingual LLMs-based TODs. Their implementations are centered
on TOD model comprising a dialogue state tracking, a database, and utterance cre-
ation components. Centered on this presumed model, their paper constructs instances
of calibration-based and in-context learning-based TODs and evaluates their perfor-
mance across multiple languages. In in-context learning-based TOD creation, it creates
prompts for implementing the initial and last components. Centered on their evalua-
tions , while the utterances created in the implemented in-context learning-based TOD
are more diverse and likable, there is a gap between possible processing exploiting only
in-context learning of LLMs and processing which is essential for creating TODs.
LLMs, trained on the corpus of internet, are capable of conducting utterance creation
and language understanding tasks centered on precise instructions across differ-
ent domains without imposing calibration. Challenges in task-oriented conversations
encompass understanding, creating utterances, and planning. Planning in LLMs is
challenging [30], while constituting a pivotal component of TODs. The primary pur-
pose of TODs is to complete a particular task—conventionally involving retrieving
entities from a database centered on the conversational exchange between the user
and the TOD [29]— which is challenging even with errorless language processing.
While LLMs exploiting in-context learning commonly excel in utterance creation and
language understanding, weak planning may lead to TODs creating utterances with
commendable BLEU [36] scores that lack informativeness for TOD users.
In terms of planning, considering planning as producing a list of actions, the methods
exploited in the LLM-based platforms to improve their planning can be categorized
into task decomposition, plan selection, external module, reflection and memory. Task
decomposition, centered on divide-and-conquer, decomposes the process into smaller
parts, which are easier to plan and prevent infeasible and inefficient planning. The
external module or external planer-aided planning method converts information that
must be provided to the planning into intermediate representation leveraging an LLM.
Then, an external planner is exploited to perform planning exploiting this information.
The equation 1 corresponds to this process. With the environment as E and the task
goal as g, Θ is the parameters of the LLM, P is the prompts, Φ indicates the external
planner module, and h is the instituted information. This integration enhances LLM
planning in terms of interpretability, conceptual completeness, and constancy. These
methods are interconnected more than mutually exclusive [30].
