3 Method
In TOD, the primary purpose is to complete particular tasks centered on preferences.
In these conversations, preferences illustrate the constraints of database queries.
14
In the presented model in this paper, considering the limitations of TOD models
centered on predefined slots and intents, preferences are modeled not only with
predefined slots but also with a text part which contains the constraints beyond the
predefined slots. This model enables to consider the whole preferences.
With these presumptions, it is possible to create conversational platform which pro-
cesses user inputs, retrieves database entities that are congruent to the predefined
slots of the preferences, and indicates the order of these entities centered on their
congruence to the text part of the preferences. The TOD then can conclude the
conversation by presenting the ordered entities. While, deviations from this path are
common, considering errors in preferences, limitations within the database entities,
or entity rejection.
If the user inputs, ”I am looking for a restaurant that serves canap in the east.”
and ”canap” is incorrect or out-of-domain input for TOD, it can create a clarifying
question, prompting the user to modify the incorrect or out-of-domain input. If the
user input is, ”can you help me find a restaurant that serves Italian food with a
moderate price range please”, and none of the user-indicated inputs are wrong or
out-of-domain, but there is no entity in the database that is congruent with this
combination of preferences. In this circumstance, instead of merely informing the
user that there is no congruent entity , the TOD can provide effective information
centered on the database entities and user preferences. It might inform the user of
entities in the database with predefined slots such as: ”price: cheap, food: Italian” or
”price: moderate, food: European.” In this way, it contributes to the modification of
input preferences collaboratively.
With concentration on changes in informative elements across different conver-
sation paths, many task-oriented conversations investigated. Consequently, centered
on conducted investigation of conversations, it is possible to create limited informa-
tional components which are constructive in planning, and their changes portray the
changes in conversation conditions, including but not limited to the predefined slots
and text part in preferences. These include informational components that portray
circumstances such as user input errors in predefined slots, and rejection of retrieved
entities. Consequently, these limited informational components lead to limited possi-
ble Information states. Considering movement between possible information states,
dialogue moves are created. The created informational components, and the dialogue
moves are listed in Figure 2.
Centered on information state approach to dialogue management, an update strategy
is constructed. Creating an update strategy which is broken down, centered on the
critical points of changing informational components enables explicitly exploiting
identified informational components in planning. With the conventional calibration-
based LLMs, it is impractical to calibrate each decomposed part of the model. While
in-context learning of LLMs—inspired by task decomposition and external planer-
aided planning methods —enables implementing this update strategy. The update
strategy in the presented model is close to the position of an external planner (Φ ) in
