4 Experiments
In this part, the information concerning the implementation of the presented model
and the experiments carried out is presented in 4.1, including evaluation metrics in
4.1 and the dataset in 4.1. Consequently, the explanation of the performance and
comparison with previous works is provided in 4.2.
4.1 Comprehensive Breakdown of Implementation and
Experiments
Evaluation Metrics
The evaluation of conversational systems can concentrate on their external behav-
ior, internal capability, or impact on users and the processes they are a part of. The
elements being evaluated are called constructs, which are operationalized to identify
measurement methods. The construct can be operationalized in multiple ways. [17]
23
investigates the constructs and their operationalizations for evaluating conversational
systems, particularly in customer service context. It categorizes the constructs into
intrinsic evaluation, and system-in-context classes.
The system-in-context construct is mainly created for TODs. It includes task success/-
effectiveness, usability, and user experience classes. Compared to intrinsic evaluation,
most system-in-context constructs are examined through human evaluation, except
for task completion, for which some mechanized evaluation methods exist [17].
The primary purpose of a TOD is task completion, which can be measured objectively
centered on the inform and success metrics. While human evaluation can be effective, it
is commonly time-consuming and complex considering potential biases and differences
in opinions. Therefore, in this paper, which concentrates primarily on task completion,
TODs are evaluated and compared centered on objective metrics of task completion.
Inform and success, which correspond to the task completion (task success) construct
of the system-in-context category, are the most common measures in previous TOD
works. Inform measures the percentage of conversations in which the entities provided
by the TOD do not contradict the current information state (dialogue state) and user
goals. The success rate measures the percentage of conversations in which the TOD
provides the whole user inquired information. In this paper, which concentrates on
improving task completion, the whole congruent entities are presented to the user,
including their entire information in the database in a table; therefore, evaluating
inform will be equivalent to evaluating success [1, 2, 4, 13, 14, 16].
Dataset
The MultiWOZ dataset contains task-oriented conversations, contributing to the
training and evaluation of TODs. To evaluate the implemented system, we exploited
the MultiWOZ 2.2 [33] dataset, which is divided into training, development, and test
subsets. In this paper, the test part of this dataset is exploited to investigate the
task completion capabilities of the implemented conversational system. While, since
the test part contains multi-domain conversations which are outside the extent of the
paper’s problem, conversations that cover more than a domain at a time are filtered
out. The exploited collection of conversations includes conversations in the domains
of hotel, train, attraction, and restaurants. In the exploited MultiWOZ dataset, there
is a schema that indicates the predefined slots, their characterizations, and possible
configurations for some of the predefined slots of each domain. Table 2 illustrates this
information for the restaurant domain.
In the exploited MultiWOZ dataset, there is a database table for each domain
that contains information about entities. If the possible configurations for prede-
fined slots are not indicated in the metadata, we extract them from the domain’s
corresponding database. The captions of predefined slots and the corresponding
columns in the database might differ; therefore, we mitigate these inconsistencies in
the pre-processing phase. Eventually, we compile the information for each domain
into a dictionary, which includes predefined slots, their characterization and possible
configurations, and the information of database entities. The information in this
dictionary corresponds to the informational components of the unchanged part of the
