4.2 Performance Examination
Paper [37] concentrates on the challenge of comparing TODs exploiting MultiWOZ
dataset. They implement the common evaluation metrics for TODs, with the input
parameter being the predictions made by the investigated TOD for conversations in
the test dataset of MultiWOZ. Predictions of previously created TODs are in the cor-
responding GitHub repository.
While in this paper, the predictions corresponding to conversations that are in more
than a domain, which are out of this paper’s problem, are eleminated from the pre-
dictions of previously created TODs. In this way, the predictions of the investigated
TODs in the test dataset were collected. Then, the evaluation method implemented
by [37] is performed with these predictions as input to calculate the inform and suc-
cess for previously created TODs in the test dataset.
The other metric for evaluating TODs is ’combined,’ which integrates inform, suc-
cess and BLEU. This is commonly calculated by cumulating the mean of inform and
success, to the BLEU. While the concentration in this paper is on task completion,
consequently the BLEU score is excluded from this combination. Instead, the mean of
the inform and success is calculated, indicated as ’updated combined,’ to compare the
performance with prior TODs in task completion. Table 3 and diagram 9 illustrate
the performance of presented model in this paper and previously created TODs.
Considering the explanation of the presented model’s capabilities in management,
even in certain problematic conversations in MultiWOZ 2.2, in whole conversations
within the test dataset, the implemented TOD provides complete information of enti-
ties that is congruent with the user preferences. In other words, not only are the
