5 Conclusion
In this paper, we created a model for collaborative and proactive management of
task-oriented conversations. Considering the limitations of the models from previ-
ous works, the presented model conceptualized centered on the information state
approach to dialogue management. This model incorporates intermediate informa-
tional components that are crucial for the proactive management of task-oriented
conversations. User preferences are modeled as the predefined slot and text part
informational components. By examining challenging conditions in task-oriented con-
versations, we identified other informational components whose exploitation could
33
mitigate error-prone conditions. These informational components leads to limited
possible information states. Consequently, dialogue moves created, and the update
strategy constructed. Inspired by task decomposition and external planner-aided plan-
ning methods, the model is implemented centered on in-context learning of LLMs.
The whole domain-dependent information is extracted in pre-processing, creating the
constant portion of the information state, and is exploited as input parameters for pro-
cedures. With this consideration, the presented model is independent of the domain.
The exploited entity retrieval mechanism enables presenting the whole congruent enti-
ties to user preferences in an orderly manner. The conducted experiments leveraging
the conversations of MultiWOZ test, without changing domain in a conversation, indi-
cated zero failures in task completion (i.e., maximal inform and success), illustrating
the effectiveness of the presented model in managing task-oriented conversations in
different domains. Compared to previously presented models, this model consider-
ably improves inform and success task completion metrics. It is possible to create an
instruction collection exploiting the conversations with the created TOD, which will
include intermediate information to create an instruction-tuned LLM as a proactive,
collaborative TOD.
