ment guidelines, criteria, and the expertise of the
evaluators. The results, presented in Table 2, indi-
cate that the generated conversations exhibit high
levels of personality relevance, effective bargain-
ing, and fairness in negotiation outcomes, while
consistently being rated as interesting, coherent,
human-like, and natural, underscoring the overall
high quality of the negotiation interactions. We
measure the inter-evaluator agreement using Fleiss
Kappa (κ) (Fleiss, 1971). The κ scores in range of
0.4 < κ <0.7 for all criteria indicate fair agree-
ment among evaluators (McHugh, 2012). We pro-
vide further dataset analysis in Appendix F.
