or offensive content, a reduction facilitated by the
LLM’s built-in toxicity mechanisms.
F.3 Comparison with Existing Datasets
We present a comparison of our proposed PACT
dataset with existing publicly available negotiation
dialogue datasets in Table 8. Notably, none of the
existing datasets leverages an argumentation-based
approach, which we introduce in PACT to enable
more rational and realistic negotiation dynamics.
Besides, PACT dataset exhibits an average of 21.43
turns per conversation, reflecting a more interactive
and conversational structure. It also stands as the
largest dataset dedicated exclusively to negotiation
scenarios within the tourism domain, further en-
hancing its relevance for specialized applications.
G Experimental Specifications
G.1 Task Setups Details
The details of 5 setups are as follows: (i) Pre-
trained Models (PMs) to assess LLMs’ inherent
capability across all sub-tasks; (ii) Pre-trained Mod-
els with Personality (PMswP) to examine LLMs’
ability to generate personality-driven responses
by leveraging the interlocutors’ personality as in-
put for RG task; (iii) Fine-tuning on a Human-
Human Negotiation Dialogue Dataset (Details are
Preprint Version Paper is accepted at EMNLP (Findings) 2025
given Appendix G.3) (FT-H2HNego) investigates
whether fine-tuning on general negotiation dia-
logues improves performance for PR, DAP, and
RG tasks; (iv) Fine-tuning on PACT (FT-Vanilla)
to evaluate the PACT’s effectiveness in improving
LLMs performance across all sub-tasks; and (v)
Fine-tuning on PACT with background knowledge,
personality, and dialog act information (FT-Ours)
to enhance LLMs’ ability to generate factually
grounded, personality-driven, and appropriate re-
sponses during negotiation. The prompt templates
for experiments with GPT-4.1-mini are given in
Table 16.
G.2 Response Generation Details
G.2.1 Retrieval-Augmented Generation
We design our response generation framework
based on the Retrieval-Augmented Generation
(RAG) model (Lewis et al., 2020) to integrate back-
ground knowledge into LLMs and mitigate the is-
sue of hallucination during RG task. We utilize
the curated database of travel packages to collect
the package details. To enable efficient retrieval,
we generate dense vector representations of these
details and the context using BERT-based embed-
dings (Devlin et al., 2019). Then, we retrieve the
top-most package details that closely align with
the dialogue context, ensuring that responses re-
main coherent and factually accurate. For selecting
top-most package details, we then compute the
cosine similarity between the dialogue context em-
beddings and the package embeddings stored in the
vector database to retrieve the package exhibiting
the highest similarity to the target context. After-
ward, we extract the available amenities, services,
and their corresponding prices from the retrieved
package and incorporate them into the generated re-
sponses, thereby ensuring the factual accuracy and
reducing the likelihood of exaggerated or mislead-
ing descriptions. If a requested amenity or service
is unavailable in the retrieved package, we first
check for similar alternatives within the same pack-
age. If no suitable options are found, the traveler
is informed about the unavailability and offered al-
ternative packages to ensure accurate and relevant
interaction.
Once the most relevant package is retrieved, we
extract the amenities, services, and their corre-
sponding prices to gather the necessary information
for response generation. If a requested amenity or
service is unavailable in the retrieved package, our
framework incorporates a fallback mechanism to
ensure relevant alternatives are identified. Specifi-
cally, we search within the same package for ameni-
ties or services that are conceptually similar, lever-
aging semantic similarity using cosine similarity
measure to identify the closest match. For creating
the embeddings of amenities and services, we again
employ BERT model. If no suitable alternatives
are found, the agent politely informs the traveler of
the unavailability and suggest other packages that
might meet the traveler’s requirements. This fall-
back mechanism further ensures that the response
remains accurate and relevant.
G.2.2 Multi-task Learning
Acquiring personality information and inferring
the future action to be taken from the dialogue
context are critical for providing personalized and
contextually relevant travel recommendations in
real-time. To ensure this, we propose to capture the
personality and dialog act information on-the-fly
from the dialogue context, which are then used aa
a signal for enhanced response generation. The
sub-tasks, PR, DAP, and RG tasks are inherently
interconnected, as they all require a deep under-
standing of the dialogue context. To leverage this
interdependence and ensure more accurate and con-
sistent response generation, we propose adopting
a multi-task learning (MTL) approach (Argyriou
et al., 2006) by training the PR, DAP, and RG mod-
els simultaneously. The rationale behind this multi-
task setup is rooted in the shared nature of the
dialogue context input. Each of these models pro-
cesses the same context and outputs a latent vector,
which captures key aspects of the conversation. By
training the models together, the model can learn
more robust and generalized features that enhance
its performance across all three sub-tasks.
G.3 Human-Human Negotiation Dialogue
Dataset Details
For the task setup - ‘Fine-tuning on a Human-
Human Negotiation Dialogue Dataset (FT-
H2Hnego)’, we use the Job Interview (JI) dataset
that consists of human-human negotiation dia-
logues in a job interview setting, where participants
assume the roles of recruiter and applicant to
negotiate distinct issues. The dialogues were
collected using Amazon Mechanical Turk (MTurk),
ensuring high-quality human-human negotiation
interactions. Each negotiation session required
workers to exchange at least six messages while
Preprint Version Paper is accepted at EMNLP (Findings) 2025
making up to three draft agreement proposals,
ensuring rich and structured negotiation discourse.
The dataset consists of 2,639 dialogues with
utterances annotated with one of the dialog acts,
namely greet, disagree, agree, inquire, propose,
and inform.
Since the dataset is not annotated with personal-
ity profiles, we annotate the dialogues in JI dataset
with Big-Five personality traits (McCrae and John,
1992) given its universality and wide applicability.
Based on the recent advancements in leveraging the
LLMs as annotators to significantly reduce anno-
tation costs and human effort (Gilardi et al., 2023;
He et al., 2023), we utilize the LLM - LLaMA-
3.1-8B (Touvron et al., 2023) due to its strong rea-
soning capability followed by a human-in-the-loop
approach for the interlocutors’ profile annotation in
the dataset. The entire annotation process unfolds
in three steps.
1. Manual annotation of dialogue samples: It
involves randomly sampling 100 dialogues
from the dataset and manually annotating the
dialogues with recruiter’s and applicant’s per-
sonality profile labels and the corresponding
exaplanations. This annotated dataset is re-
ferred to as ANN -JI, hereafter.
2. Few-shot prompting for personality profile
labeling: In this step, LLaMA-3.1-8B (Tou-
vron et al., 2023) is prompted in a few-shot
setting, using the manually annotated sam-
ples as few-shot demonstrations, to generate
personality profile labels. These labels are ac-
companied by natural language explanations
to provide insight into the model’s predictions.
The sample prompt is shown below.
Prompt template for personality profile
labeling
Task instruction: Given the dialogue, deter-
mine the recruiter’s and applicant’s personality
as one of the following - Openness, Consci-
entiousness, Extroversion, Agreeableness, and
Neuroticism. Then explain your prediction.
Few-shot demonstrations:
Dialogue:
Recruiter’s Personality Label:
Applicant’s Personality Label:
Explanation:
Target input:
Dialogue:
LLM output:
Recruiter’s Personality Label: <label1>
Applicant’s Personality Label: <label2>
Explanation: <explanation>
3. Manual Verification and Correction: It in-
volves verifying and re-annotating the dia-
logues by the human subjects to create gold-
standard datasets.
Annotation Guidelines. Each dialogue is anno-
tated by 3 annotators. The annotators are instructed
to analyze the overall interaction style of the inter-
locutors rather than focusing on individual utter-
ances. If a speaker exhibits multiple traits through-
out the conversation, the most dominant one should
be selected to capture the interlocutor’s general
personality rather than momentary shifts in behav-
ior. To enhance reliability, we employ a majority
voting mechanism to determine the final personal-
ity profile of both the applicant and the recruiter.
We observe a reliable multi-rater Kappa (McHugh,
2012) agreement ratios of 79.2% in Step 1 and
81.8% Step 3 among the annotators. It is noted
that dataset annotation involves the same human
evaluators as those involved in manual assessment
of PACT dataset quality (§3.3).
G.4 Implementation Details
We split the dataset into train, validation, and test
with the ratio of 7:1:2. All the results are com-
puted on the test set. We prompt the LLMs in
zero-shot setup. For conversation generation, we
adopt nucleus (Top-p) sampling (Holtzman et al.,
2019) with p = 0.95 and temperature T = 1.0.
We fine-tune the LLMs with LoRA adaption (Hu
et al., 2021) using 4*V100 GPUs, with batch size
of 2 per GPU, and learning rate of 5e−5 for three
epochs. The fine-tuning of all the LLMs are based
on the SFT trainer of Transformer Reinforcement
Learning (TRL)2 (von Werra et al., 2020). All the
implementation is done using the PyTorch3 frame-
work. The transformer-based models from Hug-
gingFace (Wolf et al., 2019) are utilized in all the
experiments.
G.5 Evaluation Metrics Details
Perplexity (PPL) (Papineni et al., 2002) evaluates
how well a model predicts a response. Word-
overlap-based metrics like BLEU-1/2 (B-1/2) (Pa-
pineni et al., 2002), METEOR (MET) (Banerjee
and Lavie, 2005), and ROUGE-L (R-L) (Lin, 2004)
compute the n-gram overlap between the ground-
truth response and the model’s generated response.
DISTINCT-1/2 (D-1/2) (Li et al., 2015) measures
2https://github.com/huggingface/trl
3https://pytorch.org/
Preprint Version Paper is accepted at EMNLP (Findings) 2025
the diversity of n-grams in the generated responses.
Embedding-based metrics such as BERTScore F1
(BS-F1)4 (Zhang et al., 2019) align the generated
response and the ground-truth response in latent
semantic space to assess the semantic similarity
between the gold response and the model’s gen-
erated response. BS-F1 computes word similarity
using contextual embeddings from the BERT (De-
vlin et al., 2019) model.
Response Length (R-LEN) calculates the length
of the generated responses, Personality Accuracy
(P-ACC) evaluates the alignment of the generated
responses with the specified personality profile, and
Dialog Act Accuracy (D-ACC) measures the con-
sistency of the generated responses with the desig-
nated dialog act. To evaluate P-ACC and D-ACC,
we fine-tune two distinct RoBERTa-base models
(Liu et al., 2019) on our dataset for 8 epochs.
The classifier used for computing P-ACC achieves
an accuracy of 61.57% and a macro-F1 score of
58.31%. Similarly, the classifier for D-ACC attains
an accuracy of 80.59% and a macro-F1 score of
79.22%.
FEQA (Durmus et al., 2020) and QuestEval (QE)
(Scialom et al., 2021) are QA-based metrics de-
signed to evaluate the faithfulness of generated
content. FEQA assesses consistency by extracting
answers from a source document using question-
answer pairs derived from the summary, with dis-
crepancies indicating unfaithfulness. It demon-
strates strong alignment with human judgments
and excels in evaluating highly abstractive sum-
maries. In this study, we adapt FEQA by concate-
nating the dialogue context and grounded pack-
age information to form the document and treating
the generated response as the summary. QE oper-
ates in both reference-dependent and reference-free
modes, with the former relying on input sources
and golden references. In this study, the reference-
dependent mode is used, employing the same
source construction method as FEQA. Both FEQA5
and QE6 metrics are computed using their respec-
tive official libraries with default settings.
