Our observations indicate that integrating backa-
ground package knowledge together with person-
ality and dialog act information generates more
factual, rational and convincing responses aligned
with the personality and dialog act during nego-
tiation. For instance, in the first sample, traveler
expresses concern about the price of a travel pack-
age and seeks a reduction. The FT- Vanilla mod-
els (LLaMA-2-chat, Vicuna) reinforce the price
but fail to explore alternatives, making their re-
sponses appear rigid and less engaging. Addition-
ally, these models sometimes introduce minor in-
consistencies or fail to align completely with the
negotiation context. In contrast, FT-Ours models
not only recognize the traveler’s budget constraints
but also proactively propose factual modifications,
such as adjusting accommodations or tweaking the
itinerary. The responses remain grounded in the
provided package information, ensuring that no hal-
lucinated details are introduced while maintaining
persuasiveness, flexibility, and user engagement.
In the second sample, traveler requests the removal
of a package component to lower the overall cost.
Here, the ground-truth agent provides a price ad-
justment while maintaining the overall value of the
package. The FT-Vanillamodels largely mirror this
response but occasionally generate exaggerated or
incorrect details, such as an arbitrary price increase.
However, the FT-Ours models demonstrate a more
adaptive negotiation action while maintaining fac-
tual correctness. They suggest reasonable itinerary
adjustments to better match the traveler’s budget
while ensuring quality retention, all without intro-
ducing fabricated details.
Overall, the analysis demonstrates that FT-Ours
consistently outperforms other setups by gener-
ating responses that are both effective and factu-
ally grounded. By leveraging background knowl-
edge, personality profiles, and dialog act cues, FT-
Ours models enhance negotiation adaptability, im-
prove persuasiveness, and foster greater context-
awareness while minimizing hallucinations. These
findings underscore the importance of incorporat-
ing package knowledge, personality, and dialog act
information into LLMs for more effective, truthful,
and human-like interactions.
Moreover, we conduct human evaluation in a
multi-turn setup for the best-performing Vicuna
model under FT-Ours setup. The human evalua-
tion is conducted with the help of the same four
human evaluators. Before interacting with the sys-
tem, the evaluators receive a detailed briefing on
the travel packages used in the dataset, including
associated aspects, amenities, and service descrip-
tions, followed by the different personality profiles.
Evaluators are instructed to select a particular travel
package and one profile each from argumentation,
preference, and buying style profile sets, and initi-
ate conversations with the system based on a given
travel package and express their preferences ac-
cording to the selected personality profiles.
Each evaluator engages in 5 interactions, ensur-
ing that every session features a different travel
package with a distinct combination of attributes
and system responses. To ensure meaningful di-
alogue, each interaction spans at least 8 conver-
sational turns. This setup results in a total of 20
human-evaluated dialogues. After completing all
interactions, evaluators rate each dialogue along
six dimensions: PR, BE, OF, F, C, E, Faith, and
Hal. using a 4-point Likert scale. We obtain the
following results: PR: 3.05, BE: 3.92, OF: 3.94, F:
3.01, C: 3.87, E: 3.85, Faith.: 79.16, Hal.: 20.84.
These high ratings across all metrics demonstrate
that Vicuna, under the FT-Ours setup, consistently
generates personality-relevant responses, facilitates
effective bargaining, and ensures fairness in negoti-
ation outcomes. Besides, responses are consistently
rated as fluent, coherent, natural, and faithful, un-
derscoring the overall high quality of the generated
negotiation interactions. A sample interaction is
given in Table 18.
