5.2. Medical Communication Quality
Table 2 presents HealthBench overall scores (per-
dimension breakdown in Table 7, Appendix).RE-
CAPimproves overall quality at every scale, with
gains of 8% for Llama-3.1-8B, 11% for OSS-20B,
and 18% for OSS-120B. The largest gains occur
in Completeness (23% for OSS-20B) and Commu-
nication Quality (27% for OSS-20B), suggesting
emotional reasoning helps models address both
medical and emotional dimensions of patient con-
cerns.
However,RECAPexhibits systematic trade-
offs. Accuracy and Instruction Following decrease
across all scales, reflecting prioritization of com-
prehensive, emotionally tuned responses over lit-
eral precision. Increased standard deviations (e.g.,
Llama-3.1-8Bfrom0.0067to0.0235)indicatemore
variable, scenario-specific responses rather than
formulaic outputs. These accuracy differences do
not reflect knowledge limitations, asRECAPuses
the same underlying models, but rather a shift to-
ward emotional appropriateness in response gen-
eration. This surfaces a style-versus-precision ten-
sion common to empathy-forward systems, miti-
gable through verification techniques such as self-
refinement.
5.3. Clinical Evaluation Results
Single-Turn EvaluationRECAPachieved an
overall score of 3.41 (±0.32), significantly outper-
forming Zero-Shot (2.95,p < .001, d=0.98), Per-
sona (2.94,p < .001, d=1.02), and Emotion Prim-
ing (2.93, p < .001, d=1.08). As shown in Fig-
ure 4a,RECAPconsistently outperforms all base-
lines across evaluation dimensions.
The largest improvements emerged in empathy-
related dimensions, specifically Supportiveness
(+0.67, d=1.37) and Emotional Recognition (+0.59,
d=1.34), both representing large effect sizes. Fac-
tual Completeness showed more modest improve-
ment(+0.29, d=0.85),suggestingRECAP’sprimary
advantage lies in emotional rather than informa-
tional quality. All conditions scored lowest on Emo-
tionalRecognition(range2.54–3.18),indicatingthis
dimension remains challenging for LLMs.
Scenario-level win rates (Figure 6, Appendix)
showRECAPwins 88% against Persona, 84%
against Emotion Priming, and 76% against Zero-
Shot.RECAPachieved the highest rating across
allthreebaselinesin17of25scenarios(68%),with
46% of responses rated 4–5 compared to 22–29%
for baselines.
Multi-Turn EvaluationRECAPmaintained its ad-
vantage with an overall score of 3.77 (±0.29) ver-
sus Zero-Shot’s 3.71 (±0.29). Figure 4b shows
RECAPoutperforms Zero-Shot across all four di-
mensions, with the strongest gains on Personaliza-
tion (3.95 vs 3.80,d=0.70) and greater consistency
(SD=0.16 vs 0.26). The narrower gap compared
to single-turn settings indicates that extended con-
text provides additional grounding, thoughRECAP
continues yielding benefits in adapting to individual
patientneeds. Scenario-levelcomparisonsshowed
RECAPwinning 5 of 10 scenarios (50%), with 1
tie and 4 Zero-Shot wins, and achieving slightly
higher-quality ratings (76% rated 4–5 vs 72% for
Zero-Shot).
Qualitative AnalysisVADER sentiment analysis
(Hutto and Gilbert, 2014) of annotator comments
showedRECAPreceivedsignificantlymorepositive
sentiment, with 68% positive versus 28–56% for
baselines and mean compound scores of +0.142
versus-0.259to -0.006(Cohen’s d=0.56vs. pooled
baselines).RECAPhadzeroscenariosratedbelow
2.5, compared to 1–3 for baselines, and achieved
high ratings (≥3.5) in 44% of scenarios versus 8–
12%forbaselines(Figure7,Appendix). Annotators
characterizedRECAPresponses as “emotionally
cognizant, supportive and complete,” while criticiz-
ing baselines as “robotic” and “medically correct
but emotionally inadequate.”
