shifts” (see Appendix B.1 for examples) where the
user appears to initiate a new request but is in fact
continuing to refine a previous one. In such cases,
Advanced may incorrectly interpret these as true
shifts, leading to inaccurate rewrites.
These results underscore the pivotal role of input
formulation in determining plan quality, showing
a well-guided rewriter can convey the appropriate
amount of information to the downstream planner,
neither omitting critical details nor overwhelming
its reasoning. At the same time, they expose the
limitations of purely prompt-based approaches, mo-
tivating our subsequent experiments with a trained
rewriter presented in Section 5.5.
Structural and Semantic Comparisons:As
