vor certain positions (Wang et al. 2024a; Zheng et al. 2024).
Therefore, traditional evaluation requires assessing both or-
ders of response pairs, effectively doubling inference cost.
Experiments demonstrate thatSageLMoffers more robust
evaluations, exhibiting strong position consistency under re-
sponse order reversal. On theSpeechFeedbacktest dataset,
SageLMachieves consistency rates of 84.95% and 96.10%
on the semantic and acoustic dimensions respectively, con-
firming both its robustness and evaluation efficiency.
Model Performance of Difference Response LengthAs
shown in Figure 5, our analysis of the 728-sample test set
indicates that SageLM’s agreement with human annotations
slightly decreases as the combined length of the evaluated
response pair increases (pairs>70s are excluded due to
scarcity). To corroborate this finding, we conducted a sup-
plementary analysis on a larger dataset, sampling 100 ex-
amples from distinct intervals of combined response length.
Experiment results show a similar slight decline in agree-
ment between model’s evaluations and GPT-4o annotations,
thoughthe agreement rate remained high at about 80%.
General vs. Task-specific Models in Evaluation Tasks
Task-specific models for judgment is necessary in both text
and speech response modality. Firstly, Whisper + PandaLM-
7B and Whisper + Qwen2.5-32B exhibit comparable perfor-
mance, suggesting thattask-specific evaluation modelscan
match the performance of general-purpose models with sev-
eral times more parameters. Secondly, All baseline models
showed significant improvements in their ability to evalu-
ate speech responses after two-stage post-training, reaching
a usable level with high agreement with human preference.
