Out-of-Distribution Generalization on Real S2S LLMs
OutputsTo assess the generalization ofSageLMtoun-
seen datasetandreal S2S LLMs output distribution, we
evaluate it on AlpacaEval followed by V oiceBench (Chen
et al. 2024c), comparing Kimi-Audio with Qwen2.5-omni.
As shown in Table 3,SageLMsurpasses the Whisper +
GPT-4o pipeline by 18.67% in agreement and 27.21% in ac-
curacy, validating its capability to evaluate within the true
generative distribution of S2S LLMs.
To better understand this gap betweenSageLMand cas-
caded baseline, we further analyzed the evaluation results
produced by Whisper in combination with GPT-4o. As illus-
trated in the case study in Appendix V, Whisper introduced
substantial transcription errors for Kimi-Audio responses
due to speaking rate and prosody, which consequently bi-
ased GPT-4o toward favoring Qwen2.5-Omni. In contrast,
SageLMand human evaluators were able to accurately cap-
ture the semantic content of the responses and make correct
judgments. This finding highlights the issue of error propa-
gation in cascaded systems and underscores the necessity of
an end-to-end model for speech judgement.
Model Performance of Difference Response Pair Cut-
off LengthSageLM’s 60s comparison window suffices for
most evaluation settings. On the out-of-domain AlpacaE-
val set, we measured speech response durations from five
S2S LLMs as shown in Fig. 6. Although SageLM truncates
both compared audios to 60s, responses from SLAM-Omni,
Llama-Omni and the upper quartile for Qwen2.5-Omni are
all below 60 s. The 60s limit also covers the vast majority of
longer generations from GLM-4-V oice and Kimi-Audio.
To further assess performance on long speech responses,
