Conclusions
In this work, we investigate evaluation methods for spoken
dialogue models and proposeSageLM, motivated by two
main challenges: (1) human-annotated evaluation is costly
and difficult to scale, and (2) cascaded ASR-to-text-LLM
pipelines suffer from error propagation and loss of acoustic
information. To address these issues, we construct Speech-
Feedback, a dataset comprising both semantic and acoustic
evaluations of speech responses. Preliminary experiments
demonstrate that supervised fine-tuning of speech-to-text
LLMs using interpretable feedback outperforms both label-
only SFT and GRPO, even under a significant generation-
verification mismatch in the evaluation task. To mitigate
the scarcity of acoustic supervision, we adopt a two-stage
SFT strategy to separately enhance semantic and acoustic
evaluative capabilities. Results show thatSageLMachieves
82.79% agreement with human judgments, surpassing cas-
caded and SLM-based baselines by at least 7.42% and
26.20%, respectively.
Future Work
In this paper, we introducedSageLM, an end-to-end frame-
work for spoken dialogue evaluation. Despite its promising
results, our work has several limitations. Addressing these
challenges presents valuable opportunities for advancing the
spoken dialogue community. We highlight the following po-
tential directions for future research:
Multi-turn Dialogue:ExtendingSageLMto multi-turn
conversations will require sophisticated mechanisms for
managing and incorporating spoken dialogue history, which
remains a significant design challenge.
Multilingual Evaluation:ExpandingSageLM’s capabil-
ities to multilingual contexts is hindered by two primary fac-
tors: the scarcity of datasets and the limitations of current
TTS technologies, particularly in realistically synthesizing
code-switched utterances.
Full-duplex Interaction:Evaluating full-duplex conver-
sations (Arora et al. 2025; Ge et al. 2025) introduces sub-
stantial difficulties. Beyond the persistent issues of data
scarcity and error propagation, this domain presents new
challenges, such as dual-stream (user and model) speech un-
derstanding and multi-speaker scenarios.
