Appendix
I. Semantic Preference Annotations
Dataset Filtering Pipeline
To adapt this data for voice-centric interaction and evalua-
tion, we apply a multi-stage filtering pipeline.
First, we excluded instructions related tomath and code
using Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct (Yang et al. 2024a), as these
domains typically involve highly structured content (e.g.,
formulas, SQL, HTML, LaTeX, Python, C++) that pose
challenges for voice modality due to their reliance on pre-
cise formatting and extended context.
Second, since UltraFeedback includesmultilingualin-
structions (e.g., translation tasks), and current TTS models
struggle with code-switching across languages, we applied
a language detection tool3 to remove such instances.
Third, dialogue data often contains special characters
(e.g.,\n) that disrupt natural sentence boundaries and
prosodic phrasing in speech. We replaced these with ap-
propriate punctuation to improve speech segmentation.
Then, six open-source TTS models and one commercial
TTS model were used to synthesize the filtered seman-
tic composition data, namely CosyV oice (Du et al. 2024a),
CosyV oice2 (Du et al. 2024b), SparkTTS (Wang et al.
2025b), ChatTTS4, F5-TTS (Chen et al. 2024b), Index-TTS
(Deng et al. 2025), and gpt-4o-mini-tts5.
Furthermore, we filtered out samples with highWhisper
ASR WER, as these typically indicate degraded TTS quality
or the presence of residual mathematical, programming, or
multilingual content. Specifically, the thresholdθ W ERis set
to 0.2 when the word countlof the transcribed text is less
than 400 and linearly decreases to 0 aslincreases to 600 ,
