• Mini-Omni2 [29]: Builds on Mini-Omni by adding a cross-
modal encoder that fuses audio embeddings with dialogue
history, enabling duplex (simultaneous receive/send) capa-
bility.
• Freeze-Omni [30] : Freezes a pretrained ASR encoder and
TTS decoder, then trains a lightweight bridge to map speech
embeddings into the decoder, enabling speech-to-speech
dialogue using only text–speech paired data.
• GLM-4-V oice [31]: Built on GLM-4, this two-stage system
first generates text tokens, then produces speech codec
tokens; an alignment predictor reduces idle time, though
overall latency remains higher than streaming approaches.
• Moshi [32]: Employs a global-local Transformer and multi-
layer neural codec: it predicts time-aligned text tokens as
a prefix, then generates residual codec codes in parallel
streams for user and system, removing explicit turn markers
and jointly modeling semantics and acoustics.
• dGSLM [33]: An early end-to-end system trained on paired
audio that uses self- and cross-attention to map input
waveforms directly to outputs, achieving natural interactivity
without an LLM component.
These models span LLM-centric streaming (LLaMA-
Omni, GLM-4-V oice), codec-based autoregressive (Mini-
Omni, Mini-Omni2), and purely speech-driven (Freeze-Omni,
dGSLM, Moshi) approaches. Their performance on emotional
prompts highlights how each handles affect in single-turn (and
multi-turn, where applicable) dialogue.
