2.3. Pre-Processing
The V AP model for this experiment is trained using a three-
channel audio (i.e., one channel for each speaker) with a 16 kHz
sampling rate, and a 16-bit bit depth. We did not incorporate
any form of diarization since each participant had their own pin
microphone. The V AP model evaluates the audio and voice ac-
tivity information in 20 s intervals. We included the start time
and end time for each voice activity segment for all three speak-
ers for each 20 s interval. Since the model predicts the future
600 ms of voice activity, we included voice activity information
up to 20.6 s for each interval. The V AP model was trained on the
raw audio and voice activity information for each 20 s interval.
Figure 3:Triadic VAP model architecture.
2.4. Model Architecture
The triadic V AP model architecture is shown in Figure 3. Simi-
lar to other V AP studies, we used a pre-trained contrastive pre-
dictive coding (CPC) 2 encoder to extract features from the raw
audio. The CPC was pre-trained on the LibriSpeech dataset
[18] using the methods described in [19]. The CPC output is
fed to a one-layer self-attention Transformer [20] that indepen-
dently evaluates each audio channel. The outputs from the self-
attention Transformer are inputted into a 64-dimension three-
layer multi-channel cross-attention Transformer which encodes
interactive information from the three channels. Next, we con-
catenate the outputs from the Transformers and perform mul-
titask learning. The primary task is V AP which predicts future
voice activity of the three participants, encoded in Figure 1. The
second task is voice activity detection (V AD) which detects cur-
rent voice activity for the three participants to help stabilize the
V AP training [12]. Overall, the triadic V AP model architecture
is largely the same as the architecture used for dyadic V AP. The
dyadic cross-attention was extended to a multi-channel cross at-
tention Transformer designed to handle triadic data.
2.5. Model Training
The V AP model uses the previous voice activity of 20 s and the
accompanying waveform as input to predict the future voice ac-
tivity of 0.6 s using a 50 Hz frame rate (i.e., frame predictions
are made every 20 ms). We split the data using 80% for train-
ing, 10% for validation, and 10% for testing with a random seed
set for reproducibility. The data was split so that data from a
specific triad was not used in multiple splits. The model was
trained for 10 epochs with a batch size of 8 and a weight decay
of 0.001. We used the AdamW optimizer with a learning rate
of 3.64×10−4. Cross-entropy loss was used as the loss function,
comparing ground truth labels and predicted voice activity dur-
ing training. The test set was evaluated using the model that
produced the smallest loss on the validation split. The model
was implemented using PyTorch [21] and based on code from
previous work [12].
3. Results
3.1. Loss
We evaluated three models: 1) a model trained only on the spon-
taneous speech from the TEIDAN corpus, 2) a model trained
only on the attentive listening part of the TEIDAN corpus, and
2https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.03748
3) a model that was trained on both spontaneous and attentive
listening. Table 3 contains the test loss for the three models. The
model with both spontaneous speech and attentive listening per-
formed best while the attentive listening only model performed
the worst.
