The high task success and low concept error rate
of LUSTER are therefore best interpreted as a ben-
efit of the lexicalised end-to-end design and the
representational capacity of the LLM backbone,
rather than as a direct consequence of affective
modelling alone. By reasoning directly over en-
tity values, semantic actions, and database infor-
mation in lexical form, LUSTER can reduce infor-
mation loss between action selection and surface
realisation. The effect of affective optimisation is
isolated more directly in the ablation study, where
LUSTER is compared against LUSTER-base and
intermediate reward configurations under matched
architecture and training conditions.
5.2 Ablation Study
We conduct an ablation study to analyse the contri-
bution of emotion modelling and RL within LUS-
TER configurations.
As shown in Table 2, adding emotion modelling
via SL (Base + EM) yields a modest sentiment im-
provement together with lower concept error. This
suggests that user emotion and system conduct la-
bels can provide useful conditioning signals for re-
sponse quality and semantic grounding, although
the latter RL variants show that this effect is not
automatic under all optimisation objectives.
Adding RL with a sentiment reward (Base + EM
+ RL (Sent.)) substantially improves sentiment,
achieving the highest sentiment score among the
ablation settings, but reduces task success and in-
creases concept error. This suggests that optimis-
ing affective reward alone can misalign with task
