Optimising only task success (Base + EM +
RL (Succ.)) significantly improves success while
slightly increasing sentiment, but concept error re-
mains higher than in the supervised settings. This
suggests that RL can shift the model away from
supervised response patterns, improving dialogue-
level goal completion while making surface reali-
sation slightly less reliable.
When sentiment and success rewards are jointly
optimised (LUSTER), the model achieves the best
overall success rate while maintaining improved
sentiment and keeping concept error close to the
non-emotional baseline. This suggests that the two
rewards play complementary roles: sentiment re-
ward encourages responses that elicit more posi-
tive user affect, while success reward constrains
optimisation toward task completion. Balancing
these objectives improves task success and affec-
tive interaction quality while maintaining compa-
rable semantic reliability.
5.3 LUSTER with Different LLM Backbones
We evaluate several backbone families: Llama-
3 (Grattafiori et al., 2024), Qwen-3 (Team, 2026),
Phi-3/3.5 (Abdin et al., 2024), and GPT-2 (Rad-
ford et al., 2019). Models with at least 3B param-
eters are fine-tuned using LoRA (Hu et al., 2022).
5.3.1 Supervised Learning
We configure all models to include emotion-
modelling intermediate tasks and train them with
SL. We aim to examine scaling behaviour and
identify a strong backbone for subsequent RL.
Figure 4a and 4b visualise the trend of each metric
against model size.
Task successgenerally improves with model
size across modern LLM families, although gains
diminish at larger scales. This motivates our
choice of Llama-3.2-3B for the main experiments,
as it provides a balanced trade-off between perfor-
mance and computational cost.
Sentimentshows a more nuanced trend. For
Llama, Qwen, and Phi, sentiment tends to improve
with scale, suggesting that larger models capture
affective cues and generate emotionally appropri-
ate responses better. In contrast, GPT-2 exhibits a
non-monotonic trend: sentiment declines at inter-
mediate scales before partially recovering at 1.5B.
One plausible explanation is that smaller GPT-2
models rely heavily on surface-level heuristics or
templated responses that may appear emotionally
consistent, whereas mid-sized variants lack suffi-
cient representational capacity to robustly model
both task semantics and emotional nuance. Only
at larger scales does GPT-2 regain enough capacity
to stabilise emotional behaviour, although it still
underperforms compared to modern LLMs.
Notably, the divergence between success and
sentiment trends highlights that sentiment is not
strictly correlated with task success. This suggests
that emotion constitutes an additional optimisation
dimension, emphasising the need to explicitly in-
corporate emotional objectives into dialogue sys-
tem training rather than assuming they will emerge
from the optimisation for task success.
5.3.2 Reinforcement Learning
We further investigate whether RL can improve a
weaker backbone by applying the same RL train-
ing procedure to the worst-performing model in
our backbone study, namely Phi-3.5-mini-instruct
(4B parameters). This experiment examines
whether a model with relatively poor performance
after supervised learning can be improved through
RL, and whether the effects of emotion modelling
and RL remain consistent across backbones. Ac-
cordingly, we repeat the same ablation setting used
in Section 5.2.
