5.4 Interactive Evaluation with Real Users
5.4.1 Evaluation Set-ups and Human Ratings
We conduct a human evaluation on Amazon Me-
chanical Turk using the DialCrowd toolkit (Huynh
et al., 2022) to compare LUSTER, LUSTER-base,
and EmoLoop. Participants are presented with
randomly generated single- or multi-domain goals
specifying entity constraints and required informa-
tion. After they interact with each system to com-
plete the assigned task, they have to provide rat-
ings based on (1) perceived task success and (2)
post-dialogue self-reported user sentiment. Per-
ceived task success is measured as a binary an-
swer on whether the system has provided all infor-
mation, while the self-reported user sentiment is
rated on a 5-point Likert scale ranging from very
negative to very positive (see Appendix A.6). Af-
ter excluding low-quality samples using heuristic
criteria, including dialogue length and linguistic
diversity, we retained 86, 98, and 99 valid ratings
for EmoLoop, LUSTER-base, and LUSTER, re-
spectively.
We also note a clear difference in interaction
style between human participants and the user
simulator. In the paid human study, users tended
to issue much shorter and more concise requests
aimed at completing the assigned goal efficiently,
whereas the simulator more often produced fuller,
more explicit utterances (see Appendix B.1). This
distinction is important for interpreting the qual-
itative differences observed in system behaviour
under human versus simulated evaluation.
