First, LUSTER produces substantially more clar-
ification behaviour than LUSTER-base, suggest-
ing that affect-aware optimisation encourages the
system to ask for missing constraints or recover
uncertain dialogue states rather than proceeding
immediately. Second, apology and repair in-
crease slightly. This indicates that the improve-
ment is not simply a matter of apologising more
frequently; rather, LUSTER more often combines
affective acknowledgement with task-oriented re-
covery. Third, LUSTER leads to more warm clo-
sures with the user towards the end of the inter-
action, which is consistent with the higher propor-
tion of satisfied users and the improvement in post-
dialogue sentiment. Behavioural examples (over-
apology, failure-triggered frustration, repair after
confusion, and over-clarification) can be found in
Appendix B.2.
