3.2. Model alignment
RLHF has been shown effective in aligning LLM with hu-
man preferences (Ziegler et al., 2019). In RLHF, a reward
function is learned from human feedback on model outputs,
and the model is iteratively optimized to produce responses
that maximize this reward. While effective, RLHF can be
computationally costly due to the need for training a reward
model and performing repeated reward queries. The opti-
mization process via commonly used algorithms such as
3
Aligning Spoken Dialogue Models from User Interactions
proximal policy optimization (PPO) (Schulman et al., 2017)
is also often complex and unstable. This issue becomes
more pronounced when aligning spoken dialogue models,
where the assessment of speech outputs typically requires ei-
ther human listening, intractable at scale, or calling an ASR
to transcribe the generations for assessment throughout the
training process, inducing a significantly heavier pipeline.
We leverage offline alignment methods (e.g. Direct Pref-
erence Optimization (DPO) (Rafailov et al., 2024b) and
its variants, such as SimPO (Meng et al., 2024) and APO
(D’Oosterlinck et al., 2025)), an alternative formulation
that simplifies the alignment process by directly optimiz-
ing the model parameters using preference data, removing
the need for an explicit reward model. Noting the prefer-
ence dataset D = {(x(i), y(i)w , y(i)l )}Ni=1, where yw is the
preferred response over yl given an input context x, the
training objective is formulated as a classification loss:
