Figure 2 shows the results for the cases where we generated and cross-evaluated
the GPT models. The diagonal is used to determine that GPT-4 is better suited to
the task of identifying the target relation given the expanded responses. The confu-
sion matrices for the case of GPT-3.5 are more sparse with certain biases towards
certain relations. This may also be caused by an overlapping issue concerning the
definitions of the commonsense relations. On the other hand, the cross-confusion
matrices give us information about the quality of the sentences generated by GPT-
3.5 and GPT-4. Taking into consideration Figures 2b and 2d we see how, despite
having been generated by each of the models, the results are not very different and
maintain a certain similarity in the values of the diagonal.
5 Conclusions and Future Work
In this study, we introduce a listwise zero-shot generation and reranking approach
powered by open-source large language models. Our initial experiments show the
viability of using LLMs for automatic generation and evaluation of commonsense
8 Estecha-Garitagoitia et al.
dialogue turns. Both GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 generations were found very good (being
GPT-3.5 a suitable option in case of considering costs), while for evaluation GPT-4
performed the best. In future work, we plan to increase the number of dialogues
and perform manual annotations to better detect which commonsense attributes are
better reflected in the expanded turns. Finally, we also plan to fine-tune a small
generative model that can be used to automatically extract the attributes in chitchat
dialogues.
