1) Training data:Besides the two datasets curated in
Section II-C, we also include the V oiceAssistant 5 data for
training. We leverage the zero-shot TTS to generate the answer
speech for the V oiceAssistant dataset. The statistics of the three
datasets are shown in Table II.
2) Model Configuration:We use the open-source Phi4-
MM6 speech encoder as our audio encoder. For the language
model, For the language model, we adopt Phi4-MM 3.8B as
the base model and further scale it up to 7B. The speech
tokenizer7, audio token vocoder (including both the flow
matching model and neural vocoder) are directly adopted from
CosyV oice 2 [18]8 without modification.
3) Training details:In line with the configuration described
in the Phi4-MM paper [17], we incorporate a Low-Rank
Adaptation (LoRA) [19] module with a rank of 320. Our
experiments are conducted using two base language models,
with 3.8 billion and 7 billion parameters, respectively. When
equipped with LoRA, the total number of trainable parameters
becomes 460 million and 707 million for the 3.8B and 7B
