3. Method
We begin our problem formulation with a single-turn spoken di-
alog system, where X and Y represent the user’s and system’s
speech feature sequences. Traditional cascaded spoken dia-
logue systems employ separate modules for sub-tasks, as shown
in Fig. 1a. These systems typically consist of an ASR model,
which optimizes P (Sasr|X) to produce the ASR transcript ˆSasr;
a text response generation model based on LLM which opti-
mizes P (Sres| ˆSasr) to estimate the text response ˆSres; and a
TTS model, which optimizes P (Y | ˆSres, Xspk), where Xspk is
the TTS speaker prompt. Since each module is separately op-
timized, the system suffers from error propagation. To address
this, E2E spoken dialogue systems [17, 19] maximize the pos-
terior distribution P (Y |X, Xspk) as shown in Fig. 1b. While
these systems offer fully E2E differentiable training, they lack
structured reasoning, leading to less coherent responses and in-
creased training data requirements.
3.1. CoT formulation of E2E Spoken Dialog Systems
Inspired by the strengths of both schools of thought, our method
aims to perform CoT training by explicitly incorporating the
prediction of ASR transcript Sasr and text response Sres within
the E2E spoken dialogue formulation. By regarding theSasr and
Sres as a probabilistic variable, we can theoretically incorporate
them into the posterior distributionP (Y |X, Xspk) (§ 3) via sum
rule. We further use Viterbi approximation and conditional in-
dependence (C.I.) assumption to get:
P (Y |X, Xspk) ≈ P (Y |X, Xspk, ˆSres, ˆSasr)
where ˆSres = arg max
Sres
p(Sres|X,Xspk, ˆSasr)
where ˆSasr = arg max
