Compared to standard E2E systems that model
P (Y |X, Xspk), our CoT model preserves E2E differen-
tiability while improving semantic coherence and speech
quality through multi-stage reasoning, conditioning on in-
termediate outputs ˆSasr and ˆSres. Compared to TTS in the
cascaded system that model P (Y | ˆSres, Xspk), our approach
reduces error propagation and improves expressive speech
synthesis by conditioning on input speech X and ASR
transcript ˆSasr to generate final speech output. We apply the
proposed CoT training as a post-training strategy on an open
SpeechLM [22] that has been pre-trained on ASR, TTS, Speech
Continuation, and TextLM tasks. By aligning each reasoning
stage with the SpeechLM’s pre-training tasks—namely ASR,
text LM, and TTS — our approach achieves faster convergence
and improved training data efficiency.
3.2. Text and Audio Joint Tokenizer
Our CoT training procedure utilizes autoregressive generation
of a joint text/audio token, as defined in [22]. First, speech fea-
ture sequence X, text S, and special token⟨token⟩ are tokenized
(Tok(·)) to the following discrete representation:
X = Tok(X), S = Tok(S), ⟨token⟩ = Tok(⟨token⟩), (2)
where the underline indicates the tokenized value. X =
(x1, x2, ...,) is a sequence of M-dimensional discrete audio
codec and its t-th vector is represented as xt = [x1t , ..., xMt ]⊤.
x1t is reserved for a speech semantic token [33]. Similarly, S
is composed of j-th discrete vector sj = [sj, ∅, ...,∅]⊤, where
∅ pads null values from the 2nd to Mth dimension, as text to-
kens are one-dimensional. ⟨token⟩ is also converted similarly to
text tokens. All tokens belong to ashared discrete vector space,
