5.1.3 Evaluating Dialogue Systems
With the user simulator model fixed, we can now
systematically evaluate dialogue system realisation
strategies, and the model realising them. How do
different dialogue system configurations, varying
in model size, prompting strategy, and architectural
design, compare in terms of task success?
The clem:todd framework streamlines ex-
perimentation across models and configurations.
We assess systems using a wide range of
LLMs—spanning 1B to 70B parameters and model
families such as Qwen, LLaMA, and GPT. Our
objective is to identify how architectural choices
impact performance, and to what extent model size
influences outcomes.
As mentioned in Section 3, we evaluated three
different dialogue systems and the results are re-
ported in Table 3 based on standard metrics for
task-oriented dialogue (Budzianowski et al., 2018):
Inform (I), and Booking Accuracy (B) (Xu et al.,
2024), with Booking Accuracy used as the primary
indicator of end-to-end task success.
At smaller scales (1B–3B), all architectures per-
form poorly (Booking Rate ∼ 0.05 ), highlighting
the limitations of small models for complex dia-
