5 EXPERIMENTS
5.1 Experimental Setup
We follow a similar experimental setup as the previous Validation
Experiments in Section 3.3.3, except for the following differences:
5.1.1 Baselines. We consider comparing two main categories of
methods: (1) Prompt-based methods, including Standard Prompt
(where the LLM directly acts as the system without considering
dialogue strategy), Proactive [8] (where the LLM first selects a
policy and then generates a response accordingly), ProCoT [8]
(where the LLM uses Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning to gener-
ate the next policy), ICL-AIF [12] (where another LLM acts as a
coach, analyzing the dialogue state and guiding the system on how
to respond following), and GDP-Zero [49] (where MCTS is con-
ducted to help search the dialogue policy); and (2) Planner-based
methods, including PPDPP [9] (where RoBERTa is trained as a
planner to select policies) and TRIP [52] (which uses the LLM to
infer the user’s mental states and next actions, providing insights
